server side)\n- `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds` — end-to-end request latency\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds` — **queue wait time** (time the request spends in the queue before being scheduled)\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds` — inference time\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` — prefill time (in some versions)\n- `vllm:request_decode_time_seconds` — decode time\n- `vllm:num_requests_waiting` — number of requests waiting in the queue\n- `vllm:num_requests_running` — number of requests running\n\nActually, let me recall the precise vLLM metrics. In newer vLLM versions:\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds` — Histogram of time spent in the waiting queue\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds` — Histogram of inference time\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` — Histogram of prefill time (this may be behind a flag)\n- `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds` — TTFT histogram\n\nKey insight: **queue time + prefill time ≈ TTFT** (roughly). That is, if we scrape the metrics endpoint and can extract the queue time and prefill time histograms, we can separate the two.\n\nBut wait — the metrics endpoint exposes histograms, which are aggregate (bucketed counts and sums), not per-request values. So how do we separate per-request queue time from prefill time?\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. The task says:\n> Feed the client's parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time, and assert that the two columns separate them.\n\nSo the test is about the **parser**. The client has a parser that takes metrics data (with queue time and processing/prefill time) and produces two separate columns. The test feeds this parser a fixture with known values and asserts the separation.\n\nSo the key deliverable is a **parser function** that takes in a scraped metrics payload and extracts queue wait and prefill/processing time as two separate numbers.\n\nLet me think about the actual design. The client:\n1. For each request, measures client-side TTFT (timestamping first token arrival relative to request start).\n2. Scrapes the server's `/metrics` endpoint before and after (or around) the batch and computes the delta in the queue-time and prefill-time histograms to derive server-reported queue wait and prompt processing.\n\nActually, in vLLM, per-request metrics are tricky. Let me reconsider whether vLLM exposes per-request queue/prefill breakdown.\n\nActually, vLLM's OpenAI-compatible API doesn't directly return per-request queue and prefill breakdowns. But the `/metrics` endpoint exposes histograms. To get per-request values you need to take deltas between scrapes.\n\nBut for a bench harness, the practical approach is:\n- Scrape `/metrics` before the arm's requests and after, compute the delta in the sum of the queue-time histogram and the sum of the prefill/inference-time histogram, and divide by the number of requests to get an average.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the scope of the task. The acceptance criteria are:\n```\npython3 -m py_compile bench/fn-stream-client.py && bash -n bench/run-matrix.sh && python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v\n```\n\nSo all that matters for acceptance is:\n1. The client compiles (py_compile).\n2. The matrix script is syntactically valid (bash -n).\n3. The unit tests pass (unittest tests.test_bench_client).\n\nAnd claim 6.2 in the spec: \"The bench client separates queue wait from prefill → the client unit test with injected queuing passes in the test suite.\"\n\nSo the core thing that will be graded is **the unit test with injected queuing**. The test feeds the parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time and asserts that the two columns separate them.\n\nSo I need:\n1. `bench/fn-stream-client.py` — the client. It must have a parser function that takes metrics (with queue time and prefill time) and produces two separate columns. It must be importable by the test.\n2. `bench/run-matrix.sh` — the matrix script. Counterbalanced A-B-B-A, three loads per arm, medians, depth series 0/10240/102400, speculative on/off arms, results under results/, bench.json receipt.\n3. `tests/test_bench_client.py` — the unit test. Feed the parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time, assert separation.\n\nNow, the steering comment adds hardening requirements:\n1. The bench matrix MUST record concurrency (number of in-flight sequences) per arm on the output rows.\n2. If any token fingerprint diverges between runs of the same arm, the harness must byte-compare a serial (concurrency=1) replay of that arm before trusting its numbers — flag the arm as suspect rather than averaging across the divergence.\n\nLet me think about the design more carefully.\n\n**Client design (`bench/fn-stream-client.py`):**\n\nThe client should:\n1. Send streaming completion requests to an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.\n2. Measure per-request client-side TTFT: timestamp request start and timestamp the first token.\n3. Scrape the server's `/metrics` endpoint.\n4. Parse the metrics to extract queue wait time and prefill (prompt processing) time as separate quantities.\n5. Emit CSV with a header comment explaining the column semantics.\n6. Compute a sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token IDs.\n\nThe critical requirement is that the \"prefill\" column must not be a duplicate of the \"ttft\" column. Prefill must come from a separate source (server metrics), not from the client-side TTFT.\n\nLet me think about how vLLM exposes queue time and prefill time in metrics. Let me look up actual vLLM metrics names.\n\nActually, I recall that vLLM (as of ~0.5+) exposes these per-request timing metrics as histograms:\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds` — time spent waiting in the queue\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds` — time for inference\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` — prefill time (may not exist in all versions)\n- `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds` — TTFT\n\nAnd:\n- `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds` — end-to-end\n\nThe relationship: e2e ≈ queue + prefill + decode. And TTFT ≈ queue + prefill (roughly).\n\nHmm, but these are histograms. For a bench harness, we'd take deltas.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The point of the task is really the *separation* concept, not perfect fidelity to vLLM's actual metrics format. The test feeds a *fixture* with known queue time and processing time. So I have freedom to design the fixture format and the parser, as long as:\n- The parser produces two columns: one for queue wait, one for prefill/processing.\n- The two columns are derived from *different* sources (not the same value twice).\n- The test can inject known values and verify separation.\n\nBut at the same time, I want the client to be a genuine, honest client that would work against real vLLM. So the parser should parse Prometheus exposition format from `/metrics` and extract queue-time and prefill-time histograms.\n\nLet me design it as follows:\n\n**Metrics scraping:** The client scrapes `/metrics` before sending the requests (baseline) and after they complete. It parses both scrapes and computes per-request deltas:\n- Queue wait = (sum_after(queue histogram) - sum_before(queue histogram)) / num_new_requests\n- Prefill/processing = (sum_after(prefill histogram) - sum_before(prefill histogram)) / num_new_requests\n\nHmm, but this gets complicated because we have to match up which requests are \"new.\" Let me simplify.\n\nActually — a cleaner approach for the bench: scrape metrics before each request (or batch) and after, and attribute the deltas.\n\nBut honestly, for the acceptance criteria, what matters is that the parser function exists and correctly separates two injected values. Let me make the parser robust and well-designed while keeping the core testable.\n\nLet me reconsider. Perhaps the cleanest design is:\n\nThe client's core parse function parses a Prometheus metrics payload (text in exposition format) and returns a structured snapshot: for each named histogram, the `_sum` and `_count`. Then a separate function computes the delta between two snapshots to derive per-request queue and prefill times.\n\nThe test can then:\n1. Construct two fixture payloads (before/after) with known `_sum` values for the queue-time histogram and the prefill-time histogram.\n2. Call the parser / delta function.\n3. Assert that the queue column and the prefill column have the expected distinct values.\n\nThis cleanly tests \"the two columns separate them.\"\n\nActually wait, let me re-read the task wording again very carefully:\n\n> tests/test_bench_client.py: feed the client's parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time, and assert that the two columns separate them.\n\nSo: feed the parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time. Assert that the two columns separate them.\n\nSo the fixture has a known queue time and a known processing time, and the parser should produce two columns (queue, processing) with those two distinct values. The test asserts that the queue column == known queue time and the processing column == known processing time, and that they're different from each other (and, importantly, that the processing column is not equal to the ttft / first-token column).\n\nAnd spec claim 6.2 says \"the client unit test with injected queuing.\" So the fixture injects queuing.\n\nOK, here's my plan for the parser design:\n\nThe client scrapes `/metrics` and parses it into a `MetricsSnapshot` object keyed by metric name -> {sum, count, buckets}. Then a function `split_first_token(before, after, n_requests)` computes:\n- `queue_wait_s` = delta_sum(queue_metric) / delta_count(queue_metric) (or / n_requests)\n- `prefill_s` = delta_sum(prefill_metric) / delta_count(prefill_metric)\n\nAnd the crucial invariant: `prefill_s` is derived from the prefill metric, NOT from the ttft series. And the ttft series is measured client-side.\n\nLet me also think about this: the reference defect was `prefill_mean_s = statistics.mean(ttfts)`. Our version must not do that. Our `prefill_mean_s` (or whatever we call it) must come from the server metrics' prefill histogram delta, while `ttft_mean_s` comes from the client-side first-token timestamps. And we also emit `queue_wait_mean_s` from the queue histogram delta.\n\nSo three distinct numbers:\n- `ttft_mean_s` — client-side, first-token minus request-start.\n- `queue_wait_mean_s` — server-side, from queue-time histogram delta.\n- `prefill_mean_s` — server-side, from prefill-time (prompt processing) histogram delta.\n\nAnd the invariant to test: `queue_wait_mean_s != prefill_mean_s` (they're separate), and `prefill_mean_s != ttft_mean_s` (prefill is not a duplicate of ttft).\n\nOK, let me design the metrics parsing. vLLM's `/metrics` is in Prometheus exposition format. Example:\n\n```\n# HELP vllm:request_queue_time_seconds Histogram of time spent in the queue\n# TYPE vllm:request_queue_time_seconds histogram\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_bucket{le=\"0.001\"} 0.0\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_bucket{le=\"0.01\"} 1.0\n...\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_sum 12.5\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_count 40.0\n```\n\nSo I need to parse `_sum` and `_count` lines.\n\nMetric names in vLLM: let me recall precisely. Actually, I should be careful about exact metric names. Let me think about what vLLM actually exposes.\n\nFrom vLLM source (`vllm/engine/metrics.py`), the stats logger exposes:\n- `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`\n- `vllm:time_per_output_token_seconds`\n- `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_prompt_tokens`\n- `vllm:request_generation_tokens`\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` (in newer versions)\n- `vllm:request_decode_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_success_total`\n- `vllm:num_requests_waiting`\n- `vllm:num_requests_running`\n- `vllm:num_requests_swapped`\n\nActually, the exact set depends on the vLLM version. This campaign uses vLLM 0.25.1 (forked), so let's check what metrics that version exposes. But we can't actually run the server here. For robustness, I'll parse whatever histogram is present, keyed by name, with configurable metric names.\n\nTo be safe and honest, I'll:\n1. Define the metric names we expect as constants, with fallbacks.\n2. The parser extracts `_sum` and `_count` for any metric name.\n3. The \"split\" logic computes queue wait from the queue-time metric and prefill from the prefill-time metric. If the prefill-time metric is absent, we can fall back to inference time minus decode time, but that gets complicated.\n\nActually, for the purposes of this task and the test, let me make the parser general (parse any histogram sum/count) and have the split logic reference specific metric names. The test will inject a fixture with those specific metric names.\n\nLet me reconsider: should I make the client robust to the case where the metrics endpoint doesn't expose a prefill breakdown? Spec says the client \"scrapes the server's metrics endpoint to separate scheduler queue wait from prompt processing.\" So the design assumes the metrics endpoint provides enough information to separate them. vLLM does expose `request_queue_time_seconds`. For prompt processing, in newer vLLM there's `request_prefill_time_seconds`.\n\nLet me verify: does vLLM 0.25.x expose `request_prefill_time_seconds`? I think recent vLLM has, behind `--otlp-traces-endpoint` or in metrics... Actually, let me reconsider.\n\nHmm, I recall vLLM's metrics have these \"request timing\" histograms, controlled by a config. Let me not over-think the exact upstream name; I'll make it configurable and document clearly. The key deliverable is the separation and the test.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole approach once more. There might be a simpler, cleaner interpretation that better matches \"feed the parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time.\"\n\nPerhaps the fixture is not a Prometheus payload but a structured representation. But the task says \"scrapes the server's metrics endpoint,\" so the natural format is Prometheus exposition text. The test fixture would be a sample of Prometheus text with known `_sum` values.\n\nLet me go with the Prometheus exposition format for the fixture. That's the most honest and matches \"scrapes the server's metrics endpoint.\"\n\nOK, let me design the code.\n\n### bench/fn-stream-client.py\n\nLet me structure it:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"fn-stream-client.py — honest streaming benchmark client for flashnext.\n\n... docstring explaining the separation doctrine ...\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport csv\nimport hashlib\nimport io\nimport json\nimport sys\nimport time\nimport urllib.request\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional\n\n# --- Prometheus metrics parsing ---\n\n@dataclass\nclass HistogramReading:\n    total_sum: float\n    count: float\n\n@dataclass\nclass MetricsSnapshot:\n    histograms: Dict[str, HistogramReading]\n    ...\n\ndef parse_prometheus(text: str) -> MetricsSnapshot:\n    \"\"\"Parse Prometheus exposition text into histogram sum/count readings.\"\"\"\n    ...\n\ndef scrape(url) -> MetricsSnapshot:\n    ...\n\n# Metric names\nQUEUE_METRIC = \"vllm:request_queue_time_seconds\"\nPREFILL_METRIC = \"vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds\"\nTTFT_METRIC = \"vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds\"\n\ndef split_first_token(before, after, num_requests) -> (queue_wait, prefill):\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, but I need to be careful: the number of requests for averaging. If I take the delta of `_sum` and `_count`, the average is delta_sum / delta_count. That's cleaner than dividing by num_requests (which might differ from delta_count if there's pre-existing load). Let me use delta_sum / delta_count.\n\nBut the test fixture: the test injects known queue time and processing time. If I use delta_sum / delta_count, the test needs to set up a before and after snapshot. Let me think about what's cleanest for the test.\n\nOption A: The parser function takes a single metrics payload that already contains per-request queue and prefill (as if the server reports per-request). But Prometheus doesn't work that way.\n\nOption B: The split function takes before and after snapshots. The test constructs two payloads.\n\nOption C: The parser has a lower-level function that, given a snapshot, returns the `_sum` and `_count` for a named metric, and a higher-level `per_request_mean(snapshot, name)` or a delta-based one.\n\nLet me make it testable at multiple levels:\n- `parse_prometheus(text) -> MetricsSnapshot` — parses text.\n- `MetricsSnapshot.hist(name) -> Optional[HistogramReading]` — gets a reading.\n- `split_first_token(before: MetricsSnapshot, after: MetricsSnapshot) -> FirstTokenSplit` — computes queue_wait_s and prefill_s from deltas.\n\nThe test will:\n1. Build a `before` payload with queue_sum=0, queue_count=0, prefill_sum=0, prefill_count=0.\n2. Build an `after` payload with queue_sum = KNOWN_QUEUE_TOTAL, queue_count = N, prefill_sum = KNOWN_PREFILL_TOTAL, prefill_count = N.\n3. Call split, and assert that queue_wait_s ≈ KNOWN_QUEUE_TOTAL/N and prefill_s ≈ KNOWN_PREFILL_TOTAL/N, and that they differ.\n\nThat's clean. Let me also make sure the test asserts the key anti-defect property: that prefill is not equal to ttft. But ttft is measured client-side, not from metrics. So maybe the test should also assert that the prefill column is not derived from the ttft series.\n\nHmm, to really nail down the \"does not duplicate first-token\" property, I could have the test also construct a scenario where ttft (client-side) is a known value different from prefill. But ttft is measured during an actual request, which requires a live server. In a unit test, I can't easily run a live request.\n\nAlternative: the test could verify the *structural* property — that the prefill column and the ttft column come from different code paths / sources. But that's hard to assert directly.\n\nLet me focus on what's cleanly testable: the separation of queue and prefill (the two server-side columns), and that the prefill parser reads from the prefill metric (not the ttft metric). I can assert that if I inject different values into the prefill metric and the ttft metric, the prefill column reflects the prefill metric and NOT the ttft metric. That directly proves \"prefill is not a duplicate of first-token.\"\n\nSo the test:\n- Fixture with:\n  - queue metric: sum = Q_TOTAL, count = N\n  - prefill metric: sum = P_TOTAL, count = N\n  - ttft metric: sum = T_TOTAL, count = N (T_TOTAL != P_TOTAL)\n- Assert:\n  - queue_wait == Q_TOTAL / N\n  - prefill == P_TOTAL / N\n  - prefill != T_TOTAL / N  (proving prefill is not read from ttft)\n  - prefill != queue_wait (proving separation)\n\nThat's a strong, honest test.\n\nActually, to keep it simple and focused, let me have `split_first_token` return a dataclass with `queue_wait_s`, `prefill_s`, and also `queue_n`, `prefill_n`. The test asserts on those.\n\nLet me also reconsider: do I want the client to actually perform HTTP requests and SSE parsing? For py_compile and a focused unit test, the HTTP parts just need to be syntactically valid and importable. But to be a genuine harness, it should do real streaming. Let me implement a real streaming client with urllib, so it's actually usable, while keeping the parsing logic isolated and unit-testable.\n\nBut SSE streaming with urllib is a bit awkward. Let me implement it cleanly. Actually, I could also support a non-streaming mode. But the task says \"streaming client\" and \"per-request time-to-first-token.\" So streaming (SSE) is the way.\n\nLet me implement:\n- `stream_completion(url, payload, ...)`: opens a POST with stream=True, reads SSE lines, timestamps the first token, collects token IDs, computes sha256 fingerprint.\n\nFor SSE parsing: read lines, look for `data: {...}` lines, parse JSON, extract token text and id. Timestamp first non-empty token.\n\nFor token IDs: OpenAI completions streaming returns `choices[0].logprobs` if requested, or `choices[0].token_id`? Actually, the completions API (non-chat) returns `choices[].text`. To get token IDs, we can request `logprobs` or use `echo`. Hmm. Actually, vLLM's completions API in streaming mode returns chunks with `choices[0].text`. Token IDs are not directly in the standard response unless we request logprobs.\n\nBut the task says \"sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token IDs.\" So we need token IDs. vLLM's `/v1/completions` supports returning `prompt_token_ids` in some modes, and logprobs give token info. Actually, there's a cleaner way: vLLM supports `logprobs` in completions, which gives per-token info. But that's per-token logprobs, not raw IDs.\n\nHmm, actually, vLLM's OpenAI completions API has a non-standard field. Let me recall. When you set `logprobs=k`, each chunk's choice has `logprobs: {tokens: [...], token_logprobs: [...], top_logprobs: [...], text_offset: [...]}`. The `tokens` list has token *strings*, not IDs.\n\nAlternatively, vLLM returns `prompt_token_ids` if you... no.\n\nActually, there's a simpler interpretation: \"sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token IDs\" — we can fingerprint the completion deterministically. If we can't easily get raw token IDs from the OpenAI API, we can fingerprint the concatenated token text (or the logprobs tokens). But the task explicitly says \"token IDs.\"\n\nLet me reconsider. vLLM's OpenAI-compatible completions endpoint does actually support returning token IDs in some configurations. Actually, I recall vLLM has an extra field. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the cleanest is: request with `logprobs=1` and `echo=False`, and use the returned token strings from logprobs as a proxy for the token sequence, then fingerprint that. But the task says token IDs specifically.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the client should hit vLLM's native `/generate` or use the `prompt_token_ids` input. Actually, for fingerprinting purposes, what matters is a deterministic hash of the completion. Let me design it so the fingerprint is computed over the token IDs if available, else over the token text, and document clearly.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider — maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me look at whether there's a reference in the repo for how token IDs are obtained. Let me check the container/host tooling and any existing bench-like scripts.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The repo doesn't have bench/ yet. Let me look at what other scripts exist, especially scripts/receipts-verify.py (style reference) and existing tests, to match the house style. Let me also check host/ and scripts/.\n\nLet me look at those now.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I now have the full picture of the reference defect (§4.4: `prefill_mean_s` duplicated `ttft_mean_s`). Let me study the house style: the cited style reference, existing tests, and surrounding tooling.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_cf583753df124541871b8603","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/scripts/receipts-verify.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":22151,"output":5899,"cacheRead":13312,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":5787,"totalTokens":41362,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990059209,"responseId":"chatcmpl-ac943386-c42a-44b6-b8e6-2df178dc6ad9","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
