{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"This is extremely useful. Now I understand the house style and conventions:\n\n1. API endpoint: `FN_API = http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT`, completions at `/v1/completions`, metrics presumably at `/metrics`.\n2. Model name: `FN_SERVED_NAME` (default \"flashnext\").\n3. Streaming SSE parsing pattern: read lines starting with `data:`, parse JSON, `stream_options: {\"include_usage\": True}` to get usage including prompt_tokens and completion_tokens.\n4. Timestamps with `time.time()` or `time.perf_counter()`.\n5. Receipts: `{\"step\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\"}` JSON.\n\nImportantly, vLLM's `/metrics` endpoint. Let me confirm: in run-tp2.sh the metrics are probed from the OS (sysfs), not from vLLM's /metrics. But my client needs to scrape vLLM's `/metrics` endpoint. vLLM exposes Prometheus metrics at `/metrics` by default (unless disabled). Good.\n\nNow, for token IDs: note that in run-tp2.sh's fidelity step, they request `logprobs: 5` and get `choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"]` — that's a list of token strings, not IDs. So to get token IDs, we'd need a different approach.\n\nHmm, but the task says \"sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token ids\". Let me think about how vLLM can return token IDs.\n\nOption 1: vLLM's `/v1/completions` doesn't return token IDs directly in the standard OpenAI response. But it can if... Actually, vLLM has an extension. Let me think.\n\nActually — there's a cleaner way. vLLM supports `prompt_token_ids` as input. And for output, if we want token IDs, we can use the `logprobs` field which returns token strings but not IDs.\n\nWait, actually vLLM DOES return token IDs in some cases. Let me recall the OpenAI completions response format from vLLM. The standard `CompletionResponse` has `choices: [{text, index, logprobs, finish_reason, stop_reason}]`. No token_ids.\n\nBut vLLM has a feature: if you set `echo` or use the raw engine, you get token IDs. Actually the cleanest: vLLM's serving completions supports a `logprobs` returning tokens as strings.\n\nHmm, but actually — I recall that newer vLLM versions added `prompt_token_ids` to the request and there might be token_ids in... Let me not rely on uncertain API details.\n\nLet me reconsider. The fingerprint needs to be over \"token ids\". Given the constraints and that this is a bench harness that will run against real vLLM, I should get token IDs reliably. \n\nThe most robust approach in vLLM: request with `logprobs` set, and the response includes `logprobs.tokens` (strings) and `logprobs.text_offset`. But not IDs.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether vLLM returns token IDs anywhere. \n\nThere's actually a vLLM-specific feature: the `/v1/completions` endpoint, when given `extra_body` or... no.\n\nLet me think about the deterministic fingerprint requirement from the steering. The steering says: \"if any token fingerprint diverges across runs of the same arm, the harness must byte-compare a serial replay\". So the fingerprint needs to be a deterministic function of the completion that would be stable across runs when the engine is deterministic (temperature=0, greedy). And it should catch QSA-gather wrong-output divergences.\n\nFor fingerprinting, what matters is:\n1. It's deterministic for identical outputs.\n2. It changes when the output changes.\n3. It's based on token IDs ideally.\n\nGiven vLLM returns token *strings* via logprobs, and those are deterministic for greedy decoding, fingerprinting over the token strings (or the completion text) would work for divergence detection. But the task explicitly says \"token ids\".\n\nLet me look at whether vLLM provides token IDs. Actually, I now recall: vLLM's `CompletionResponseStreamChoice` and `CompletionResponseChoice` do NOT include token_ids in the OpenAI-compatible layer. BUT — vLLM has a way to get them.\n\nActually, the cleanest robust method: use `logprobs` and hash the `tokens` list from logprobs. But that's token strings.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider — maybe I should request the raw token IDs through a different mechanism. Actually, you know what, let me reconsider the whole fingerprinting approach to be robust:\n\nI'll compute the fingerprint over the token IDs if the server provides them (some vLLM builds/configs include them), and fall back to the token strings from logprobs, and document clearly. But to be safe and match \"token ids\", let me see if there's a way.\n\nActually — I just recalled. vLLM DOES support returning `token_ids` if you send `prompt_token_ids` in the request OR through the `CompletionResponse`. Let me check... No.\n\nLet me take yet another approach. Let me look at what vLLM actually exposes. Since I can't run vLLM here, let me be pragmatic: I'll design the fingerprint to hash the completion's token sequence. To get token IDs, I'll request `logprobs` and also try to use the `completion_tokens` usage. But the most reliable \"token id\" source in vLLM's OpenAI API is actually...\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider. There IS a clean way to get token IDs from vLLM: set `\"logprobs\": 1` and the response's `logprobs` dict contains `\"tokens\"` (the token strings). But there's also, in some versions, a way via `echo`.\n\nOK you know what, I think the pragmatic and honest approach is:\n- Request with `logprobs` enabled.\n- Collect the token sequence. vLLM returns token strings in `logprobs.tokens`.\n- BUT the task wants token IDs. Let me check if vLLM returns them.\n\nLet me actually search my memory harder: In vLLM, the OpenAI completions response `CompletionResponseChoice` has these fields: `index`, `text`, `logprobs`, `finish_reason`, `stop_reason`, and in newer versions `prompt_token_ids` is NOT there. \n\nHmm. Actually, I found it: vLLM has a feature where if you pass `\"extra_body\": {\"return_token_ids\": true}` ... no that's not real either.\n\nLet me just be pragmatic and design it so that:\n1. We request `logprobs` (so we get per-token data