fine:\n- QUEUE_METRIC = \"vllm:request_queue_time_seconds\"\n- PREFILL_METRIC = \"vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds\"\n\nBut does vLLM 0.25.x expose `request_prefill_time_seconds`? Let me think. Actually, I'm not 100% sure. Let me make the client robust: it parses ALL histograms and looks for queue and prefill metrics by name, with a fallback chain. And I'll make the names configurable via constants. For the unit test, I control the fixture so I'll use the names the parser expects.\n\nActually, let me reconsider what vLLM exposes. Let me recall vLLM's metrics more carefully. From vLLM `vllm/engine/metrics.py` (StatLogger), the histograms created:\n- `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds`  ← this exists in newer vLLM (0.6+)\n- `vllm:request_decode_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`\n- `vllm:time_per_output_token_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_prompt_tokens`\n- `vllm:request_generation_tokens`\n- `vllm:request_success_total`\n\nYes, I'm fairly confident `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` and `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds` exist in modern vLLM. Good. And the relationship: \n- queue + prefill ≈ TTFT (server-side)\n- inference = prefill + decode (roughly)\n\nSo separating queue from prefill: both are exposed as separate histograms. \n\nLet me also handle the case where prefill isn't directly exposed but inference and decode are: prefill = inference - decode. But that's more fragile. I'll prefer direct prefill metric, fall back to inference-decode. Actually, let me keep it simpler and rely on the direct metrics, but make the parser generic enough. I'll implement a fallback: if prefill metric absent, try inference minus decode.\n\nHmm, let me not overengineer. I'll implement:\n- `QUEUE_METRICS = (\"vllm:request_queue_time_seconds\",)` \n- `PREFILL_METRICS = (\"vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds\",)` with fallback computed from inference - decode if absent.\n\nActually, to keep the unit test clean and the code honest, I'll define the primary metric names and parse those. Let me keep a small fallback for robustness but keep the main path clear.\n\nLet me now also reconsider: the CSV header comment must describe column semantics explicitly. Something like:\n```\n# fn-stream-client CSV — column semantics:\n#   arm            : bench arm label (spec-off / spec-on)\n#   load           : load index within the arm (1..loads_per_arm)\n#   depth_tokens   : target prompt depth in tokens\n#   concurrency    : in-flight sequences for this row\n#   request_index  : per-request index within the load\n#   ttft_s         : CLIENT-side time-to-first-token (request start -> first token)\n#   queue_wait_s   : SERVER-reported scheduler queue wait (metrics delta), NOT derived from ttft\n#   prefill_s      : SERVER-reported prompt processing (metrics delta), NOT derived from ttft\n#   decode_s       : client-side total minus ttft\n#   total_s        : client-side total request time\n#   prompt_tokens  : server-reported prompt token count\n#   completion_tokens : server-reported completion token count\n#   fingerprint    : sha256 of the completion's token ids\n# NOTE: prefill_s is a measurement from the server metrics endpoint, NOT a\n#       duplicate of ttft_s (contrast: reference harness vllm-stream-client.py\n#       shipped prefill_mean_s == ttft_mean_s, evidence/nix-strix-halo.md §4.4).\n```\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me design the parser for testability. I'll structure:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass HistReading:\n    total: float  # _sum\n    count: float  # _count\n\ndef parse_metrics(text) -> dict[str, HistReading]:\n    # returns {metric_name: HistReading} for histograms (sum+count)\n\ndef scrape_metrics(url) -> dict[str, HistReading]:\n    # HTTP GET url, parse\n\ndef first_token_split(before, after, queue_metric=..., prefill_metric=...) -> dict:\n    # returns {\"queue_wait_s\": ..., \"prefill_s\": ..., ...}\n```\n\nWait, but for the test, the fixture should have \"known queue and processing times\". Let me make the split function take before/after snapshots and compute per-request means. The test builds before (empty/zero) and after (with known sums/counts) snapshots, calls split, and asserts.\n\nLet me reconsider the exact metric parsing. Prometheus exposition:\n```\n# TYPE vllm:request_queue_time_seconds histogram\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_bucket{le=\"0.5\"} 3.0\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_sum 42.5\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_count 10.0\n```\n\nI need to parse `_sum` and `_count` lines. Note metric names contain colons. The line format is `<name>{labels} <value>` or `<name> <value>`. For `_sum` and `_count`, there are no labels typically. So I parse lines, and for lines matching `^(<metric>_sum)\\s+(<float>)`, record.\n\nLet me write parse_metrics to return a dict keyed by the base histogram name (strip `_sum`/`_count`/`_bucket`) → HistReading(total, count).\n\nLet me write:\n```python\ndef parse_metrics(text):\n    readings = {}\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        line = line.strip()\n        if not line or line.startswith(\"#\"):\n            continue\n        parts = line.split()\n        if len(parts) < 2:\n            continue\n        name_field, value = parts[0], parts[-1]\n        # strip labels\n        name = name_field.split(\"{\", 1)[0]\n        try:\n            val = float(value)\n        except ValueError:\n            continue\n        if name.endswith(\"_sum\"):\n            base = name[:-4]\n            readings.setdefault(base, HistReading(0.0, 0.0)).total = val\n        elif name.endswith(\"_count\"):\n            base = name[:-6]\n            readings.setdefault(base, HistReading(0.0, 0.0)).count = val\n        # ignore _bucket and others for our purposes\n    return readings\n```\n\nWait, `_count` suffix is 6 chars (\"_count\"). Yes. `_sum` is 4. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: some metrics are counters, not histograms, e.g. `vllm:num_requests_running` (gauge) or `vllm:request_success_total` (counter). Those don't have `_sum`/`_count`. We only care about histogram sum/count. Fine.\n\nAlso, metric names: vLLM uses `vllm:` prefix. In Prometheus exposition, colons are valid in metric names. Good.\n\nNow the split function:\n```python\ndef first_token_split(before, after, num_requests=None,\n                      queue_metric=QUEUE_METRIC, prefill_metric=PREFILL_METRIC):\n    def delta_mean(metric):\n        b = before.get(metric); a = after.get(metric)\n        if a is None:\n            return None, 0\n        dsum = a.total - (b.total if b else 0.0)\n        dcount = a.count - (b.count if b else 0.0)\n        if dcount <= 0:\n            return None, 0\n        return dsum / dcount, int(dcount)\n    queue_wait, q_n = delta_mean(queue_metric)\n    prefill, p_n = delta_mean(prefill_metric)\n    return SplitResult(queue_wait_s=queue_wait, prefill_s=prefill, ...)\n```\n\nHmm wait, but what if before is None (first scrape)? Let me make before optional (treat as zero). Actually the client always scrapes before and after. For the test, I'll pass both.\n\nNow, an important honesty point: the queue_wait and prefill computed this way are *means over all requests that completed between the two scrapes*, not necessarily just our batch. If the server is otherwise idle (bench is the only client), then the delta is exactly our batch. That's the bench assumption. I'll document that.\n\nLet me reconsider whether dividing by Δcount is right. Δcount = number of requests that had that metric recorded between scrapes = our batch size (assuming idle). Δsum = total queue time for those. So mean = Δsum/Δcount. Good. And I'll also record the observed Δcount so the matrix can sanity-check it equals the expected request count.\n\nNow, the fingerprint. Let me design token-id extraction.\n\nFor streaming completions with logprobs, vLLM chunks look like:\n```json\n{\"id\":\"...\",\"object\":\"text_completion\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"text\":\" tok\",\"logprobs\":{\"tokens\":[\" tok\"],\"token_logprobs\":[-0.1],\"top_logprobs\":[...],\"text_offset\":[...]},\"finish_reason\":null}]}\n```\n\nSo `choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"]` gives token strings per chunk. But NOT ids.\n\nTo get token ids, I recall vLLM actually does include them now. Let me think... Actually, in vLLM, when you request completions, you can get token IDs by... hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. There's actually a clean way: vLLM's `CompletionResponse` in some versions includes `prompt_token_ids` in the choice. But completion token ids... \n\nOK, I'll make a pragmatic decision that's honest: The fingerprint is computed over the completion's token sequence. I'll get token identifiers from the response with a priority:\n1. If the chunk choice carries integer token ids (key `token_ids` or similar), use them.\n2. Otherwise, use the logprobs `tokens` strings.\n3. Otherwise, use the raw text.\n\nAnd I'll compute sha256 over a canonical serialization. I'll document that under greedy decoding this is deterministic and catches the QSA divergence.\n\nBut the task literally says \"token ids\". Let me try harder to get real token ids. \n\nActually — I just realized there's a clean and reliable way to get token IDs from vLLM: request `echo=False` and `logprobs`, then the response's logprobs contains tokens. But for IDs, vLLM's OpenAI layer... \n\nYou know, let me look at this from a different angle. vLLM's `/v1/completions` request accepts `prompt_token_ids` (list of ints) as an alternative to `prompt` (string). And I recall that recent vLLM added `CompletionResponseChoice.prompt_token_ids`. For completion tokens, there isn't a standard field.\n\nGiven the difficulty, and that the fingerprint's PURPOSE is divergence detection (per steering), I'll make the fingerprint robust and deterministic using whatever token-level signal the server provides, preferring integer ids. I'll write the extraction to look for ids and fall back to logprobs tokens. This is honest engineering: the code TRIES to use token ids, documents the fallback.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe I'm wrong and vLLM DOES return token ids. Let me just write the client to handle a `token_id` field in the chunk if present, and I'll make the fingerprint function work on a list of ints (the canonical case). For the fallback, I'll tokenize by logprobs tokens. \n\nHmm, I want to get this right. Let me think about whether there's a deterministic token-id source.\n\nAlternative clean approach: Use vLLM's `/tokenize` endpoint to get prompt token ids, and for completion, request with `logprobs=1` and `echo`... no.\n\nActually — the cleanest reliable source of completion token IDs in vLLM is to use the NON-streaming response with `logprobs` — but even there, logprobs gives token strings.\n\nWait, I recall now: vLLM's logprobs in the OpenAI completions response, the `LogProbs` model has `tokens: List[str]`, `token_logprobs`, `top_logprobs`, `text_offset`. No ids. BUT, there was a feature added where you can get content including token ids via... the `CompletionLogProbs`... no.\n\nOK. Decision: I'll make the fingerprint from the completion token sequence, using integer token IDs when available and logprobs token strings otherwise. I'll structure the code so the \"token ids\" path is primary and clearly documented, with the string fallback. For the bench's divergence-detection purpose, both work. This is the honest, robust choice. I'll note in a comment that vLLM's OpenAI completions layer reports per-token strings (not ids) unless the fork adds an ids field, so we hash whichever token-level identifier the server returns, which is deterministic under greedy sampling.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider — actually I want to double check by considering that the campaign's vLLM fork might expose token ids. But I can't know. Let me just be robust.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the fingerprint source ONE more time and settle it with the most robust vLLM-compatible method:\n\nI'll request completions with `\"logprobs\": 1` and `\"echo\": false`. From the streaming (or final) response, I collect `logprobs.tokens` (list of token strings). To turn this into a stable fingerprint equivalent to token ids, I hash the sequence. Since tokenization is deterministic, identical completions yield identical token sequences and identical fingerprints; QSA divergence changes tokens → changes fingerprint. \n\nBut to honor \"token ids\" literally, I'll ALSO check for `token_id`/`token_ids` fields. And I'll build the fingerprint over ints when present.\n\nLet me just write it robustly and move on. I've spent enough analysis.\n\nLet me now also think: the fingerprint is \"of every completion's token ids\" — so each completion (each request) gets one fingerprint. In the CSV, each request row has its fingerprint. Good.\n\nNow let me think about concurrency and the barrier. I'll use threading with a barrier:\n```python\nbarrier = threading.Barrier(concurrency)\ndef worker(i):\n    barrier.wait()\n    ... run request, measure ...\n```\nWith `--requests` total and `--concurrency` workers, I'll dispatch requests to a thread pool of `concurrency` workers, all released by a barrier at the start. Actually, if requests > concurrency, the barrier model is more complex. Let me keep it simple: the barrier synchronizes the first `concurrency` tasks. Actually, let me use a simpler model: launch `concurrency` threads, each pulls from a queue of `requests` items. The barrier releases all threads simultaneously for the first wave. Subsequent requests per thread run back-to-back. This measures sustained concurrency.\n\nActually, for a clean \"in-flight = concurrency\" measurement, I'll launch exactly `concurrency` threads, each running `requests/concurrency` sequential requests (or distribute). The barrier ensures they start together. That gives concurrency in-flight = concurrency.\n\nLet me keep the concurrency handling straightforward and well-documented.\n\nLet me now also handle depth: I need to synthesize prompts of ~depth tokens. Like run-tp2.sh, I'll build from a fixed paragraph, estimate chars/token, pad, and verify via usage prompt_tokens, re-pad once if undershot. For depth=0, use a minimal single-token-ish prompt (but can't be truly 0; I'll use the smallest reasonable and note the actual prompt_tokens). Actually \"depth 0\" probably means no long context — a short baseline prompt. I'll use a minimal prompt and record actual prompt_tokens. The row's depth_tokens column records the *target* depth, and prompt_tokens records actual.\n\nNow let me also reconsider the \"prefill number is a measurement, not a proxy\" requirement from deliveredBehaviors: \"A client whose prefill number is a measurement, not a proxy\". This confirms prefill must come from the server metrics (a real measurement), not be a duplicate of ttft. Good, my design does this.\n\nAnd \"A matrix whose every number carries its protocol\" — each row/number carries its protocol metadata (arm, depth, concurrency, load, protocol/spec config). So the CSV and bench.json must carry the full protocol context for every number. I'll make sure each row has arm/load/depth/concurrency/spec config, and bench.json carries the full protocol.\n\nAlright. Let me also check the worklist to see if there's more detail on this task and the receipt schema, and check tests/test_instruments.py and test_host_tooling.py for style. Also check contracts/trace.schema.json maybe not needed. Let me look at the worklist task entry and one more test.\n\nLet me read the worklist and test_host_tooling.py.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me check the worklist task entry, an existing test for style, and the cluster-down/preflight interfaces.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_9e967aa5b6c3415e9809ee48","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\necho \"=== worklist bench-harness task ===\"\npython3 -c \"import json;wl=json.load(open('silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json'));[print(json.dumps(t,indent=1)) for t in wl['tasks'] if t['id']=='bench-harness']\"\necho \"=== all task ids ===\"\npython3 -c \"import json;wl=json.load(open('silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json'));[print(t['id'],'|',t.get('kind'),'|',t.get('conflictDomains')) for t in wl['tasks']]\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":10096,"output":5830,"cacheRead":50688,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":5619,"totalTokens":66614,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990359663,"responseId":"chatcmpl-3bda04d5-a31e-471f-9787-54e0c41007cf","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
