{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"This confirms the house pattern for loading modules by path with importlib (`spec_from_file_location`). I'll mirror that in test_bench_client.py.\n\nNow I have a full understanding. Let me write the three files.\n\nLet me also double-check: the test file uses `importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` with a valid module name and the path. For my hyphenated file, I'll use module name \"fn_stream_client\" and path bench/fn-stream-client.py. Good.\n\nLet me now write bench/fn-stream-client.py. I'll make it complete and honest.\n\nLet me carefully write the code. I'll aim for clarity, correctness, and the house style (docstrings explaining doctrine, comments citing spec/evidence).\n\nKey design decisions recap:\n- Metric names as constants.\n- `parse_metrics(text) -> Dict[str, HistReading]`.\n- `split_first_token(before, after, ...) -> FirstTokenSplit` with queue_wait_s, prefill_s, samples.\n- Fingerprint over token ids (int preferred, string fallback).\n- Streaming client measuring client-side TTFT, total, decode.\n- Concurrency via threads + barrier.\n- CSV with header comment.\n- CLI with env defaults.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the split function signature for testability and honesty. The delta approach needs before and after. But there's a subtlety: the ttft metric on the server (`vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`) is ALSO scraped, and I want the test to prove prefill != ttft. So I'll also expose reading the ttft metric mean, but the split's `prefill_s` must NOT be derived from ttft. The test will inject a ttft histogram with a different value and assert split.prefill_s != ttft_mean. Good.\n\nLet me define the split to return queue_wait_s and prefill_s from queue and prefill metrics respectively. And separately, I can compute ttft_server_mean if needed, but the client's ttft is client-side measured, not from the ttft metric. Actually the client measures ttft client-side (per request). The server ttft metric is redundant. I'll keep the client's ttft from client measurement. But for the test's anti-duplication assertion, I'll include a ttft metric in the fixture and assert prefill != ttft metric mean. Let me expose a helper `hist_mean(snapshot, metric)` or just compute in test.\n\nLet me make `split_first_token` return a dataclass with queue_wait_s, prefill_s, queue_samples, prefill_samples, and ALSO `ttft_server_s` (from the ttft metric) so the CSV could include it, but I'll keep ttft client-side as primary. Actually, to avoid confusion, let me have the split focus on queue and prefill. I'll add a separate helper for reading any hist mean. The test can compute ttft mean via the helper.\n\nLet me define:\n```python\ndef hist_delta_mean(before, after, metric):\n    b = before.get(metric); a = after.get(metric)\n    if a is None: return (None, 0)\n    dsum = a.total - (b.total if b else 0.0)\n    dcount = a.count - (b.count if b else 0.0)\n    if dcount <= 0: return (None, 0)\n    return (dsum/dcount, int(dcount))\n```\n\nAnd split_first_token uses it for queue and prefill. The test can also call hist_delta_mean for ttft to show it differs.\n\nLet me write the dataclass:\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass HistReading:\n    total: float\n    count: float\n\n@dataclass\nclass FirstTokenSplit:\n    queue_wait_s: Optional[float]\n    prefill_s: Optional[float]\n    queue_samples: int\n    prefill_samples: int\n```\n\nNow, let me write parse_metrics robustly.\n\nLet me write the full file now.\n\nOne more consideration: the prompt for the fingerprint column semantics and the anti-duplication note. Good.\n\nLet me also make sure the client handles `--requests` and `--concurrency` sensibly, and that the measured batch excludes calibration probes.\n\nLet me also handle the case where metrics endpoint returns 404 or the histograms are absent (older engines). In that case, queue_wait_s/prefill_s = None, and I should record that honestly (not fabricate). The CSV carries empty for those. I'll emit \"NA\" or empty. Let me emit empty string for None and document.\n\nNow let me write everything. Let me start with the client.\n\nLet me think about the streaming request function carefully:\n\n```python\ndef stream_one_request(api, model, prompt, max_tokens, temperature, request_index):\n    url = api.rstrip(\"/\") + \"/v1/completions\"\n    body = {\n        \"model\": model,\n        \"prompt\": prompt,\n        \"max_tokens\": max_tokens,\n        \"temperature\": temperature,\n        \"stream\": True,\n        \"stream_options\": {\"include_usage\": True},\n        \"logprobs\": 1,\n    }\n    req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    t_start = time.perf_counter()\n    t_first = None\n    t_last = None\n    usage = None\n    token_ids = []\n    token_strs = []\n    ids_complete = True  # whether every token had an integer id\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=...) as r:\n        for raw in r:\n            line = raw.decode(errors=\"ignore\").strip()\n            if not line.startswith(\"data:\"):\n                continue\n            payload = line[5:].strip()\n            if payload == \"[DONE]\":\n                break\n            try:\n                chunk = json.loads(payload)\n            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n                continue\n            if chunk.get(\"usage\"):\n                usage = chunk[\"usage\"]\n            for choice in chunk.get(\"choices\") or []:\n                ids = _extract_token_ids(choice)\n                strs = _extract_token_strs(choice)\n                got_token = bool(strs) or bool(ids) or bool(choice.get(\"text\"))\n                if got_token:\n                    now = time.perf_counter()\n                    if t_first is None:\n                        t_first = now\n                    t_last = now\n                if ids:\n                    token_ids.extend(ids)\n                    token_strs.extend(strs if strs else [\"\"]*len(ids))\n                elif strs:\n                    ids_complete = False\n                    token_strs.extend(strs)\n                elif choice.get(\"text\"):\n                    ids_complete = False\n                    token_strs.append(choice[\"text\"])\n    total = time.perf_counter() - t_start\n    ttft = None if t_first is None else t_first - t_start\n    decode = None if ttft is None else max(0.0, total - ttft)\n    if token_ids and ids_complete:\n        fp = fingerprint_of(token_ids)\n    else:\n        fp = fingerprint_of(token_strs)\n    return RequestRecord(...)\n```\n\nHmm, the token tracking is getting messy. Let me simplify: I'll accumulate `ids` list and `strs` list in parallel as best-effort, and track `have_all_ids`. Actually, let me track two parallel accumulators and a flag:\n\n- `id_seq`: list of ints (only appended when we have an id for a token)\n- `str_seq`: list of strs\n- `all_ids`: bool, starts True; set False if we ever append a token without an id.\n\nFor each chunk's tokens:\n- Get ids list (maybe empty) and strs list.\n- The number of tokens in this chunk = max(len(ids), len(strs), 1 if text else 0).\n- This is fiddly. Let me simplify by iterating over the token entries.\n\nActually, logprobs.tokens is a list; usually one token per streaming chunk. Let me handle per-token:\n```python\nlp = choice.get(\"logprobs\") or {}\ntok_strs = lp.get(\"tokens\") or []\ntok_ids = lp.get(\"token_ids\") or []  # may be absent\n# If no logprobs, fall back to text\nif not tok_strs and choice.get(\"text\"):\n    tok_strs = [choice[\"text\"]]\nn = len(tok_strs)\nif n == 0 and not tok_ids:\n    # nothing token-level; maybe just finish chunk\n    continue  # but still might mark first token if text present\nfor i in range(max(n, len(tok_ids))):\n    s = tok_strs[i] if i < n else \"\"\n    tid = tok_ids[i] if i < len(tok_ids) else None\n    if isinstance(tid, int):\n        id_seq.append(tid)\n    else:\n        all_ids = False\n    str_seq.append(s)\n```\n\nHmm, but if I require logprobs=1 and the server returns logprobs.tokens, then tok_strs is populated per token and I can index. If token_ids also present, great. If not, all_ids becomes False and I use str_seq.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: with `logprobs: 1`, does the streaming chunk include logprobs? Yes, vLLM includes logprobs in streaming chunks when requested. Each chunk typically carries one token's logprobs.\n\nFor first-token timing, I mark t_first when any token (str or id or text) appears.\n\nLet me simplify token counting: the number of new tokens in a chunk = len(tok_strs) if tok_strs else (len(tok_ids) if tok_ids else (1 if text else 0)). Let me use that for marking first token and for iterating.\n\nLet me restructure cleanly:\n\n```python\nfor choice in chunk.get(\"choices\") or []:\n    lp = choice.get(\"logprobs\") or {}\n    tok_strs = lp.get(\"tokens\") or []\n    tok_ids = lp.get(\"token_ids\") or []\n    text = choice.get(\"text\") or \"\"\n    # Determine token-level entries for this chunk.\n    if tok_strs or tok_ids:\n        n = max(len(tok_strs), len(tok_ids))\n        for i in range(n):\n            s = tok_strs[i] if i < len(tok_strs) else \"\"\n            tid = tok_ids[i] if i < len(tok_ids) else None\n            record_token(s, tid)\n    elif text:\n        record_token(text, None)\n    else:\n        continue\n    # any token recorded => possibly first token\n```\n\nWhere record_token appends and marks first/last timestamps. Let me inline record_token logic:\n\n```python\ndef _note_token(...):\n    nonlocal t_first, t_last, all_ids\n    now = time.perf_counter()\n    if t_first is None: t_first = now\n    t_last = now\n    if isinstance(tid, int): id_seq.append(tid)\n    else: all_ids = False\n    str_seq.append(s)\n```\n\nSince it's inside a function, I'll just inline it (no nested function needed; use direct code).\n\nOK let me just write it directly in the loop. Let me finalize.\n\nFor fingerprint: \n```python\nseq = id_seq if (all_ids and id_seq) else str_seq\nfp = fingerprint_of(seq)\n```\n\nfingerprint_of handles ints and strs with type-tagged encoding.\n\nNow, let me also record prompt_tokens and completion_tokens from usage (present with include_usage). depth_actual = usage[\"prompt_tokens\"] if usage else None.\n\nLet me define RequestRecord as a dataclass or just a dict. I'll use a dict for simplicity in the worker, and build CSV rows from dicts.\n\nLet me now also think about the CSV columns and the run_load structure.\n\nrun_load(args, prompt, arm, load, depth, concurrency, requests) -> list of row dicts + split.\n\nLet me define the main flow:\n\n```python\ndef run(args):\n    api = args.api\n    metrics_url = args.metrics or (api.rstrip(\"/\") + \"/metrics\")\n    # converge prompt for depth\n    prompt = converge_prompt(api, args.model, args.depth, args.max_tokens_probe) if args.depth>0 else SHORT_PROMPT\n    # before scrape\n    try:\n        before = scrape_metrics(metrics_url)\n    except Exception:\n        before = {}\n    # run requests concurrently\n    records = run_concurrent(args, prompt)\n    # after scrape\n    try:\n        after = scrape_metrics(metrics_url)\n    except Exception:\n        after = {}\n    split = split_first_token(before, after)\n    # emit rows\n    rows = []\n    for i, rec in enumerate(records):\n        rows.append({\n            \"arm\": args.arm, \"load\": args.load, \n            \"depth_target\": args.depth,\n            \"depth_actual\": rec.get(\"prompt_tokens\"),\n            \"concurrency\": args.concurrency,\n            \"request_index\": i,\n            \"ttft_s\": fmt(rec[\"ttft\"]),\n            \"queue_wait_s\": fmt(split.queue_wait_s),\n            \"prefill_s\": fmt(split.prefill_s),\n            \"decode_s\": fmt(rec[\"decode\"]),\n            \"total_s\": fmt(rec[\"total\"]),\n            \"completion_tokens\": rec.get(\"completion_tokens\"),\n            \"fingerprint\": rec[\"fingerprint\"],\n            \"spec_config\": args.spec_label,\n        })\n    write_csv(args.csv, rows)\n    # also emit fingerprints (for the matrix) \n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, wait: the before/after scrape bracketing. The before scrape must happen after convergence (so convergence probes don't count). And it should happen right before the measured batch. Good, my flow does convergence first, then before scrape, then batch, then after scrape. But there's a subtlety: convergence does probe requests that go through the server and get counted in metrics; but since before-scrape is after convergence, those probes are in the \"before\" baseline and won't be in the delta. Good.\n\nBut also: the convergence probe requests change metrics; then before scrape captures that state; then measured batch; then after scrape. Delta = measured batch only. Good.\n\nNow, write_csv with header comment. Let me append to the file if it exists (matrix calls once per load, appending). Actually, the matrix calls the client per (arm, load, depth). If I write the header comment every call, it'd be repeated. Let me have the client write to stdout by default and append to --csv if given, writing the header comment only if the file is new/empty. Let me handle: if csv file doesn't exist or is empty, write header comment + CSV header; then append data rows. \n\nLet me implement write_csv(args.csv, rows, write_header_if_new=True).\n\nLet me define CSV_FIELDS list.\n\nNow let me also think: the client is called once per (arm, load, depth), and runs `--requests` concurrent requests at that depth. So each call produces `requests` rows. The matrix sweeps depths in separate calls. Good.\n\nNow the fingerprints output: the matrix needs fingerprints to detect divergence. They're in the CSV. The aggregation reads the CSV. Good. But the receipt's `fingerprints` field — I'll build it in aggregation from the CSV. Good.\n\nLet me now write the code files. Let me start with the client.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check the metric name for prefill in vLLM 0.25. Let me reconsider. I'm fairly confident about `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`. For prefill, let me reconsider whether it's `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds`. \n\nIn vLLM, the StatLogger creates these histograms (from memory of vllm/engine/metrics.py):\n- `self.histogram_time_to_first_token` → `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_time_per_output_token` → `vllm:time_per_output_token_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_e2e_request_latency` → `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_request_queue_time` → `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_request_inference_time` → `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_request_prefill_time` → `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` (added ~0.6.0)\n- `self.histogram_request_decode_time` → `vllm:request_decode_time_seconds`\n\nYes, I'm confident `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` exists in modern vLLM. And `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`. Good.\n\nFor robustness, I'll add a fallback: if prefill metric is absent but inference and decode present, prefill = inference - decode (per request mean). Let me implement that fallback in split_first_token. Actually, computing prefill = inference - decode from means: prefill_mean ≈ inference_mean - decode_mean. That's approximate but a reasonable fallback. Let me add it but keep the direct metric primary.\n\nHmm, but this fallback adds complexity to the test. Let me keep the test focused on the primary metrics and add the fallback as an optional code path (not tested, or lightly tested). Actually, to keep the acceptance test crisp and the code honest, let me implement the primary path cleanly and add the fallback. I'll test the primary path thoroughly and maybe add one test for the fallback. Let me keep the main test focused.\n\nLet me now write. I'll write the client file.\n\nLet me also make sure I handle `--spec-label` for the spec_config column. The matrix passes it. Default \"unspecified\".\n\nLet me write it all now.\n\nFor the prompt paragraph, I'll reuse a neutral text (no model names). Something like the ledger paragraph from run-tp2.sh. Let me use a neutral measurement-themed paragraph.\n\nLet me write bench/fn-stream-client.py."}}
