{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Both embedded Python blocks compile. Note: the inline Python in Phase C (the small `python3 -c` inside the serial replay) is a separate thing. Let's verify that too. Actually, the Phase C inline `python3 -c '...'` is a single-quoted string inside bash. Let's extract that and verify its syntax.\n\nLet's check the inline python inside Phase C:\n```python\nimport json, sys\nrec = json.loads(sys.stdin.read())\nfps = rec.get(\"fingerprints\", [])\ndistinct = sorted(set(fps))\nprint(json.dumps({...}))\n```\nLet's pull that out and compile it too. That's the one wrapped in `python3 -c '...'` right after `printf '%s\\n' \"$serial_json\" |`. Let's verify it.\n\nActually, let's write it out and compile it. But more simply: let's build a synthetic end-to-end test of Phase B and Phase D using the extracted blocks and fake data, and verify the whole reduction works (including suspect flagging).\n\nWhat to create:\n1. A synthetic matrix.csv with comment headers + rows covering two arms, three depths, three loads, and one divergent cell (spec-on/10240, whose fingerprints differ per load).\n2. Run block 0 (Phase B) → medians.csv, divergent.txt.\n3. Verify that divergent.txt contains spec-on/10240 (or, since it uses hyphens, \"spec-on\\t10240\").\n4. Create synthetic summaries jsonl and serial jsonl.\n5. Run block 1 (Phase D) → bench.json. Verify its structure, and that suspect flagging and receipts-verify's _bench gate (loads_per_arm>=3, counterbalanced true) are satisfied.\n\nLet's do this. Need to set the environment variables that the blocks read.\n\nEnvironment variables for block 0: MATRIX_CSV, MEDIANS_CSV, DIVERGENT_CSV.\nEnvironment variables for block 1: BENCH_DIR, RECEIPTS, LOADS_PER_ARM, CONCURRENCY, SCHEDULE_DESC, DEPTH_LIST, ARM_LIST, SUMMARIES_JSONL, SERIAL_JSONL, DIVERGENT_TXT.\n\nWait, block 1 reads os.environ[\"DIVERGENT_TXT\"] but doesn't actually use it? Let me check. Block 1 reads the env vars: bench_dir, receipts, loads_per_arm, concurrency, schedule, depths, arms, SUMMARIES_JSONL, SERIAL_JSONL. It doesn't read DIVERGENT_TXT (good, it derives divergent from the fingerprints set). But in run-matrix.sh, DIVERGENT_TXT is exported. Block 1 doesn't need it. OK.\n\nLet's build the synthetic test.\n\nThe rows of matrix.csv need CSV_FIELDS columns:\narm, load, depth_target, depth_actual, concurrency, request_index, ttft_s, queue_wait_s, prefill_s, decode_s, total_s, completion_tokens, fingerprint, spec_config\n\nWhat to create:\n- spec-off, loads 1..3, depths 0/10240/102400, consistent fingerprint per (arm,depth).\n- spec-on, loads 1..3, depths 0/10240/102400, fingerprint for depth 10240 differs per load (divergent), others consistent.\n\nLet's generate it in python.\n\nActually, to keep the tests focused, let's generate a small matrix: arms spec-off and spec-on; depths 0 and 10240 (subset); loads 1..3; 2 requests each. spec-on/10240 will be divergent.\n\nLet's write the generator, run block 0 and block 1, and verify.\n\nAlso let's test that Phase D's bench.json passes the receipts-verify _bench gate. The receipts-verify _bench checks data.loads_per_arm>=3 and data.counterbalanced==true. My bench.json sets those. But receipts-verify also requires step/status/ts and status != fail. My bench.json has step=\"bench\", status=\"pass\", ts. Good. However, receipts-verify reads results/receipts/*.json — in the synthetic test I'll point RECEIPTS to a temp dir so as not to contaminate the real results. Then verify separately.\n\nLet's build it."}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":1,"content":"Both embedded blocks compile. Let me also validate the inline Phase-C python and then run an end-to-end synthetic test of Phase B (median/divergence) and Phase D (receipt) with fake data.\n\n"}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":2,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_b75e9a8d82d04edd87c45624","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\n# Extract + compile the inline Phase-C python (the `python3 -c '...'` in serial replay)\npython3 - <<'EXTRACT'\nimport re\nsrc = open(\"bench/run-matrix.sh\").read()\nm = re.search(r\"python3 -c '(.*?)' >> \\\"\\$SERIAL_JSONL\\\"\", src, flags=re.S)\ncode = m.group(1)\nopen(\"/tmp/rm_phase_c.py\",\"w\").write(code)\nimport py_compile\npy_compile.compile(\"/tmp/rm_phase_c.py\", doraise=True)\nprint(\"Phase-C inline python: py_compile OK\")\nprint(code)\nEXTRACT"}}}}
