{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The depth convergence works: depth_target=64, depth_actual=71 (within the 2% / 64-token tolerance — the mock's len//4 tokenization is crude, so 71 vs 64 is within the `max(64, depth*0.02)=64` tolerance, so it converged). The split columns still correct (queue=0.5, prefill=0.1).\n\nEverything works end-to-end. \n\nNow let me clean up the /tmp artifacts (not strictly necessary, they're in /tmp).\n\nLet me now do a final review of the files for correctness and completeness against the task requirements:\n\nTask requirements checklist:\n1. ✅ bench/fn-stream-client.py — measures per-request TTFT client-side AND scrapes server metrics to separate queue wait from prompt processing. Prefill is NOT a duplicate of ttft. Emits CSV with explicit column semantics in header comment. sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token ids.\n2. ✅ bench/run-matrix.sh — arms interleaved A-B-B-A, three loads per arm, medians reported, depth series 0/10240/102400, speculative on/off arms, results under results/, bench.json receipt {loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}.\n3. ✅ tests/test_bench_client.py — feeds parser fixture with known queue and processing times, asserts two columns separate them.\n4. ✅ Boundaries: only bench/ and tests/ touched.\n5. ✅ Steering §1: concurrency recorded per arm/row.\n6. ✅ Steering §2: fingerprint divergence → serial replay (concurrency=1) byte-compare → flag suspect, don't average over divergence.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- ✅ python3 -m py_compile bench/fn-stream-client.py\n- ✅ bash -n bench/run-matrix.sh\n- ✅ python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v\n\nLet me double-check a few things:\n\n1. The task says \"results committed under results/\". My run-matrix.sh writes to results/bench/ and results/receipts/bench.json at runtime. I'm not committing fake results (correct — no fabricated numbers). But let me reconsider: does the checkpoint task (cp-bench) expect results to be present? The results are produced when run-matrix.sh runs overnight. My commit is just the harness. That's correct per the campaign model (run-matrix.sh runs, then cp-bench commits results).\n\n2. Let me reconsider whether I should add a `.gitkeep` or similar under results/bench/ so the directory structure is clear. But that would touch results/ (outside my conflictDomains). The brief says every path my commit touches must be inside bench/ or tests/. So I should NOT commit anything under results/. run-matrix.sh does `mkdir -p` at runtime. Good. I'll leave results/ alone.\n\nWait — but let me reconsider. Is there a risk that the brief's conflictDomains [\"bench\",\"tests\"] means I can ONLY write under those, and writing results at runtime is fine (not a commit)? Yes. My git commit will only add bench/ and tests/ files. Runtime writes to results/ are not part of my commit. Good.\n\n3. Let me verify git status shows only bench/ and tests/ changes before committing.\n\n4. Let me reconsider the worklist note: the repo-invariants test checks worklist hygiene (no model names, etc.), but that's about the worklist file which I don't touch. My bench files don't contain model family names (I use \"flashnext\" as the served name, which is the project identity, not a banned model family name; the banned list is qwen/llama/deepseek/claude/opus). Good. Let me double check my files don't contain any banned tokens. Let me grep.\n\nActually, test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene only checks the worklist file, not bench/. But to be safe and consistent with F.5, let me make sure my bench files don't contain model family names. Let me grep bench/ and tests/test_bench_client.py for the banned tokens.\n\n5. Let me reconsider: the GPL scan (test_no_gpl_text_in_tree) scans the whole tree including bench/. My files don't contain \"GNU GENERAL PUBLIC LICENSE\". Good.\n\n6. The secret scan (test_no_secret_material) scans for private keys, hf_, ghp_, AKIA. My files don't have those. Good.\n\nLet me run the full test suite one more time to be 100% sure, and grep for banned tokens.\n\nLet me also reconsider whether `bench/run-matrix.sh` might be picked up by any test's syntax scan. test_scripts_are_syntax_clean scans scripts/*.sh and host/**/*.sh — not bench/. So run-matrix.sh isn't scanned by that test, but it's bash -n clean anyway. Good.\n\nLet me also make sure I didn't leave any stray files. Let me check git status.\n\nLet me reconsider one more time the counterbalanced schedule and whether \"A-B-B-A\" is satisfied. My schedule for LOADS_PER_ARM=3: \n- i=1 (odd): spec_off, spec_on → A B\n- i=2 (even): spec_on, spec_off → B A\n- i=3 (odd): spec_off, spec_on → A B\nSequence: A B B A A B. Positions 1-4 = A B B A. ✅ Literally contains A-B-B-A. Counterbalanced (leading arm alternates). Each arm 3 loads. Good.\n\nNow, one concern: the receipt's `schedule` field shows \"spec_off:1 spec_on:1 spec_on:2 spec_off:2 spec_off:3 spec_on:3\" which matches A B B A A B. Good.\n\nLet me also double-check: the matrix measures depths in the inner loop, so for each (arm,load) in the schedule, it runs all 3 depths. So the actual execution is: (A,1,d0),(A,1,d10240),(A,1,d102400),(B,1,d0),... wait no. Let me re-read my loop:\n\n```bash\nfor idx in \"${!SCHED_ARM[@]}\"; do\n  arm=\"${SCHED_ARM[$idx]}\"\n  load=\"${SCHED_LOAD[$idx]}\"\n  serve_arm \"$arm\"\n  for depth in \"${DEPTHS[@]}\"; do\n    measure_cell \"$arm\" \"$load\" \"$depth\"\n  done\ndone\n```\n\nSo for schedule entry (A,1), it serves arm A once, then measures depths 0, 10240, 102400 for load 1. Then (B,1): serve arm B, measure depths for load 1. Then (B,2): serve arm B (no restart since same arm), measure depths for load 2. Then (A,2): serve arm A (restart), measure depths. Etc.\n\nSo serve restarts happen at arm transitions: A→B (restart), B→B (no), B→A (restart), A→A (no), A→B (restart). 3 restarts. Reasonable.\n\nGood. The counterbalancing is at the (arm,load) level, interleaving arms. Within each (arm,load), all depths run. This is a valid counterbalanced design.\n\nNow let me reconsider: is measuring all 3 depths within one serve-arm session OK for counterbalancing? The depth series within a load shares the same serve session. Time-drift across the whole schedule is handled by interleaving arms. Good enough and matches \"arms interleaved A-B-B-A\".\n\nAlright. Let me also reconsider whether the client handles the case where `--requests` isn't evenly divisible by concurrency. run_concurrent slices via `chunks[idx % concurrency]`. For requests=8, concurrency=4: idx0→c0,1→c1,2→c2,3→c3,4→c0,5→c1,6→c2,7→c3. Each worker 2 requests. Good. Barrier size = concurrency+1 = 5. All 4 workers + main wait, release. Good.\n\nWhat if requests < concurrency? e.g. requests=2, concurrency=4. chunks = [[0],[1],[],[]]. Workers with empty chunks still call barrier.wait(). Barrier size = 5. All 4 workers (including 2 with empty chunks) + main = 5 parties → release. Empty-chunk workers return immediately. Good, no deadlock. \n\nWhat if concurrency=1? chunks=[[0,1,...]]. barrier size 2. 1 worker + main. Release. Good.\n\nAlright, the concurrency handling is robust.\n\nLet me also double check: `barrier.wait()` in worker is wrapped in try/except BrokenBarrierError. If main never releases (shouldn't happen), workers would hang. But main always calls barrier.wait() after starting threads. Good.\n\nOne more: in run_concurrent, if a request errors, it's recorded but results[idx] stays None. `done = [r for r in results if r is not None]`. If all error, raise. Otherwise return done (partial). The CSV gets `len(rows)` rows = len(done). The stdout JSON \"requests\" = len(rows). Good.\n\nNow, let me reconsider a potential issue: the client's `stream_one_request` marks t_first when any token appears. But the first SSE chunk might be a usage-only chunk (empty choices) — no, usage comes at the end. The first token chunk has choices with logprobs. Good. But what about the very first chunk being a \"prefill\" chunk with empty text? In vLLM streaming, the first chunk typically has the first token. My mock sends token chunks directly. Good.\n\nAlso, `first_token` timing: I set t_first when n>0 (at least one token). For the first chunk, that's correct.\n\nLet me also verify the fingerprint in the integration test used token_ids (ints [101,102,103]) since the mock provided token_ids and all_ids stayed True. The fingerprint \"9052d9...\" is over [101,102,103]. Good — it used the token ids path.\n\nAlright, I'm confident the implementation is correct and complete. Let me do final housekeeping:\n1. grep for banned model names in my new files.\n2. Final full test suite run.\n3. git status to confirm only bench/ and tests/ changed.\n4. Commit on the branch.\n\nLet me also reconsider the commit. The brief says \"Commit the complete result on the assigned branch. Do not push, merge.\" So I commit on tally-work/flashnext-70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness. I should NOT push or merge. Let me commit with a clear message.\n\nLet me check git status first, then grep, then run tests, then commit.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should run the py_compile which creates __pycache__. py_compile bench/fn-stream-client.py creates bench/__pycache__/fn-stream-client.cpython-314.pyc. That's gitignored (.gitignore). Let me check .gitignore covers __pycache__ and *.pyc. The test test_no_bytecode_in_the_overlay checks that tracked files aren't .pyc. Since __pycache__ is gitignored, it won't be tracked. Let me verify .gitignore.\n\nLet me check .gitignore and clean up any __pycache__ I created (to be tidy). Actually, let me check what's gitignored.\n\nLet me run the housekeeping checks.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"End-to-end works: depth convergence (64→71 actual, within tolerance), correct split, and the fingerprint used the real token-id path. Let me do final housekeeping: check `.gitignore`, scan my new files for banned tokens, and confirm the git diff stays inside `bench/` and `tests/`.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3a68528256c94793a6c1470b","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\necho \"=== .gitignore ===\"; cat .gitignore\necho \"=== banned model-family tokens in new files (F.5) ===\"\ngrep -rniE 'qwen|llama|deepseek|claude|opus|mistral|gemma' bench/ tests/test_bench_client.py && echo \"FOUND banned token\" || echo \"clean: no banned model-family names\"\necho \"=== GPL / secret self-scan on new files ===\"\ngrep -rn 'GNU GENERAL' bench/ tests/test_bench_client.py || echo \"clean: no GPL text\"\ngrep -rnE 'BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY|hf_[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}|ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}|AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}' bench/ tests/test_bench_client.py || echo \"clean: no secret-like material\"\necho \"=== git status (should be only bench/ + tests/) ===\"\ngit status --short\necho \"=== cleanup any __pycache__ created by py_compile ===\"\nfind bench tests -name '__pycache__' -type d"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2392,"output":2904,"cacheRead":126720,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2526,"totalTokens":132016,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991630713,"responseId":"chatcmpl-f4b4a635-d228-493a-9376-8fc0a2bc357c","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3a68528256c94793a6c1470b","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== .gitignore ===\n__pycache__/\n*.pyc\n.direnv/\nresult\n.engine-overlay/\nresult-engine\nresults/logs/\n=== banned model-family tokens in new files (F.5) ===\nclean: no banned model-family names\n=== GPL / secret self-scan on new files ===\nclean: no GPL text\nclean: no secret-like material\n=== git status (should be only bench/ + tests/) ===\n?? bench/\n?? tests/test_bench_client.py\n=== cleanup any __pycache__ created by p