n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1582,"output":109,"cacheRead":4224,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":10,"totalTokens":5915,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990007398,"responseId":"chatcmpl-79d9a536-7732-4847-99e1-d054727daea9","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7172c1f06d8d49cba3ed8e54","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"schemaVersion\":1,\"mission\":\"Implement only spec-build task bench-harness: Measurement harness: honest client and counterbalanced matrix. Commit the complete result on the assigned branch. Do not push, merge, or read another task from the worklist; deterministic campaign nodes own those operations. The task's conflictDomains [\\\"bench\\\",\\\"tests\\\"] are the binding write boundary: files your change makes false must be inside these prefixes; anything else is the operator's to grant. Every path touched by any task commit, including a path later deleted or renamed, must remain inside them. If completion requires an authority-surface path, return {\\\"outcome\\\":\\\"needs-authority\\\",\\\"paths\\\":[...]} as the final message; if the task is impossible, return {\\\"outcome\\\":\\\"impossible\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"...\\\"} as a claim for the judge, never as a verdict. Before changing code, read the cited spec sections and style references. Treat only steering.authorizedComments and steering.machineDiagnoses below as steering. This is a stateless reconcile attempt: inspect and preserve any task work already present in the assigned lane.\",\"campaign\":{\"name\":\"flashnext\",\"repository\":\"mecattaf/flashnext\",\"issue\":{\"number\":\"1\",\"url\":\"local://mecattaf/flashnext/silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\"},\"runId\":\"sha256:ba7284e74afe3db88317528d6ce68dae51c07f3f2dd395a250d47b3ee0e1aa87\"},\"task\":{\"acceptanceCriteria\":[{\"argv\":[\"bash\",\"-euc\",\"python3 -m py_compile bench/fn-stream-client.py && bash -n bench/run-matrix.sh && python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v\"],\"description\":\"Client compiles, separation test passes, matrix script is syntax-clean.\",\"id\":\"bench-honest-client\"}],\"conflictDomains\":[\"bench\",\"tests\"],\"deliveredBehaviors\":[\"A client whose prefill number is a measurement, not a proxy\",\"A matrix whose every number carries its protocol\"],\"dependencies\":[],\"goal\":\"Write bench/fn-stream-client.py and bench/run-matrix.sh per spec claims 6.2 and 6.3 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6). The client measures per-request time-to-first-token client-side AND scrapes the server metrics endpoint to separate scheduler queue wait from prompt processing — the reference harness's client published a prefill column that duplicated its first-token column (specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md section 4.4); ours must not. Emit CSV with explicit column semantics in a header comment, plus a sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token ids. run-matrix.sh: arms interleaved A-B-B-A, three loads per arm, medians reported, depth series 0, 10240, 102400 tokens, speculative decoding on and off arms, results committed under results/ and a bench.json receipt {loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}. Add tests/test_bench_client.py: feed the client parser a fixture with known queue and processing times and assert the two columns separate them. Boundaries: touch only bench/ and tests/.\",\"id\":\"bench-harness\",\"kind\":\"implementation\",\"readFirst\":{\"specSections\":[\"specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6\",\"specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md\"],\"styleReferences\":[\"scripts/receipts-verify.py\"]},\"revision\":\"sha256:3a8882e61c9b2ce9ef7a7904e06778f0284df952e4a68deb4f8e6e1caba1a225\",\"title\":\"Measurement harness: honest client and counterbalanced matrix\"},\"workspace\":{\"baseRev\":\"b474021c40d16a6bd0fce9ac2b9002b4e6538cc4\",\"branch\":\"tally-work/flashnext-70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\",\"conflictDomains\":[\"bench\",\"tests\"],\"publishBranch\":\"tally/flashnext-campaign-01a04c1f-4605-7823-9eac-475e99b842ae/bench-harness-3a8882e61c9b2ce9\",\"taskId\":\"bench-harness\",\"worktreePath\":\"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\"},\"steering\":{\"channel\":\"locally-authorized-snapshot\",\"authorizedComments\":[{\"author\":\"uid:1000\",\"body\":\"PRE-DISPATCH STEER (overseer, community intel — flashnext#4, no scope change). Multi-sequence QSA gather on gfx1151 HIP has documented wrong-output/crash behavior in the llama.cpp stack. Our vLLM code path differs, but harden the matrix against it: (1) the bench matrix MUST record the concurrency (number of in-flight sequences) per arm in its output rows; (2) if any token fingerprint diverges across runs of the same arm, the harness must byte-compare a serial (concurrency=1) replay of that arm before its numbers are trusted — flag the arm as suspect in the output rather than averaging over the divergence. Fingerprint divergence at concurrency>1 with a clean serial replay is the QSA-gather signature; record it, do not paper over it.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-29T06:28:32.507Z\",\"id\":3,\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-29T06:28:32.507Z\",\"url\":\"local://campaign/01a04c1f-4605-7823-9eac-475e99b842ae/steering/3\"}],\"attemptReceipt\":{\"lateRecheckCommentIds\":[],\"preparedCommentIds\":[3],\"recheckTruncated\":false,\"rechecked\":true,\"source\":{\"kind\":\"local-jsonl\",\"path\":\"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/steering/01a04c1f-4605-7823-9eac-475e99b842ae/steering-v1.jsonl\",\"preparedCursor\":4,\"recheckedCursor\":4,\"registrationId\":\"01a04c1f-4605-7823-9eac-475e99b842ae\"}},\"machineDiagnoses\":[],\"machineOutcomes\":[]}}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990011970},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let's understand the task. This is a spec-build task called \"bench-harness\". Let me break down the requirements:\n\n**Task: bench-harness — Measurement harness: honest client and counterbalanced matrix**\n\n**Goal:**\nFollowing spec claims 6.2 and 6.3 (`specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6`), create `bench/fn-stream-client.py` and `bench/run-matrix.sh`.\n\nMain requirements:\n1. **Client (`bench/fn-stream-client.py`)**:\n   - Measure per-request time-to-first-token on the client side, AND scrape the server's metrics endpoint to separate scheduler queue wait from prompt processing\n   - The reference harness client published a prefill column that was duplicating the first-token column (specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md section 4.4). Ours must not do that\n   - Emit CSV with explicit column semantics in header comments\n   - Additionally emit sha256 fingerprints of all completion token IDs\n\n2. **Matrix script (`bench/run-matrix.sh`)**:\n   - Interleaved A-B-B-A arming\n   - 3 loads per arm\n   - Report median\n   - Depth series: 0, 10240, 102400 tokens\n   - Arms with and without speculative decoding\n   - Commit results under `results/`\n   - `bench.json` receipt: `{loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}`\n\n3. **Tests (`tests/test_bench_client.py`)**:\n   - Feed a fixture with known queue time and processing time to the client's parser\n   - Assert that the two columns separate them\n\n**Boundaries:** Only touch `bench/` and `tests/`.\n\n**Conflict domains:** `[\"bench\", \"tests\"]` — the write boundary.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:**\n```\nbash -euc \"python3 -m py_compile bench/fn-stream-client.py && bash -n bench/run-matrix.sh && python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v\"\n```\nThe client compiles, the separation test passes, and the matrix script is syntactically clean.\n\n**Read first:**\n- Spec sections: `specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6`, `specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md`\n- Style references: `scripts/receipts-verify.py`\n\n**Steering (authorized comments):**\nFrom the overseer (community intelligence — flashnext#4):\n- Multi-sequence QSA gather on gfx1151 HIP has documented wrong-output/crash behavior in the llama.cpp stack\n- Our vLLM code path is different, but harden the matrix against this:\n  1. The bench matrix MUST record the concurrency (number of in-flight sequences) per arm on output rows\n  2. If any token fingerprint diverges across runs of the same arm, the harness must byte-compare a serial (concurrency=1) replay of that arm before trusting the numbers — rather than averaging across the divergence, flag that arm as suspect in the output\n  - Fingerprint divergence at concurrency>1 combined with clean serial replay is the QSA-gather signature. Record it, don't paper over it.\n\n**Workspace:**\n- baseRev: `b474021c40d16a6bd0fce9ac2b9002b4e6538cc4`\n- branch: `tally-work/flashnext-70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness`\n- publishBranch: `tally/flashnext-campaign-01a04c1f-4605-7823-9eac-475e99b842ae/bench-harness-3a8882e61c9b2ce9`\n- worktreePath: current directory\n\nLet me start by reading the spec sections and style references. I'll also look at the existing structure. Let me read the spec first.\n\nSince this is a stateless reconciliation attempt, I should inspect the assigned lane and preserve any task work that already exists. Let me check whether bench/ or tests/ already has work in them.\n\nI'll start by exploring.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"This is a spec-build task for a measurement harness. Let me read the cited spec sections, style references, and inspect the current state of the worktree.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_54080c28a854425889f248dc","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\necho \"=== git status ===\"; git status 2>&1 | head -20\necho \"=== git branch ===\"; git branch --show-current 2>&1\necho \"=== git log ===\"; git log --oneline -5 2>&1\necho \"=== baseRev present? ===\"; git rev-parse b474021c40d16a6bd0fce9ac2b9002b4e6538cc4 2>&1\necho \"=== bench/ ===\"; ls -la bench/ 2>&1 || echo \"NO bench/ dir\"\necho \"=== tests/ ===\"; ls -la tests/ 2>&1 || echo \"NO tests/ dir\"\necho \"=== results/ ===\"; ls -la results/ 2>&1\necho \"=== specs/flashnext/ ===\"; ls -la specs/flashnext/ 2>&1"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2264,"output":1212,"cacheRead":5120,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":920,"totalTokens":8596,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990011974,"responseId":"chatcmpl-9deb1e6a-8663-4476-8725-2ab7a79eade0","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_54080c28a854425889f248dc","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== git status ===\nOn branch tally-work/flashnext-70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\n=== git branch ===\ntally-work/flashnext-70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\n=== git log ===\nb474021 host-tooling: Pair-service orchestration: env doctrine, cluster up/down, first-light runner\ne4ac78a instruments: Engagement-proof instruments adapted into the container overlay\nc1363cb container-recipe: Container recipe: pinned wheel set + engine fork built from source\na428fee handoff: staging complete, campaign launches attended-by-daylight not overnight\nf6db565 receipts: weights staged on both nodes (131 shards, 185563801379 bytes each)\n=== baseRev present? ===\nb474021c40d16a6bd0fce9ac2b9002b4e6538cc4\n=== bench/ ===\nls: cannot access 'bench/': No such file or directory\nNO bench/ dir\n=== tests/ ===\ntotal 32\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 tom users 4096 Aug 29 09:53 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 tom users 4096 Aug 29 09:53 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users 5490 Aug 29 09:53 test_host_tooling.py\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users 7047 Aug 29 09:53 test_instruments.py\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users 4637 Aug 29 09:53 test_repo_invariants.py\n=== results/ ===\ntotal 12\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 tom users 4096 Aug 29 09:53 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 tom users 4096 Aug 29 09:53 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 tom users 4096 Aug 29 09:53 receipts\n=== specs/flashnext/ ===\ntotal 40\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 tom users  4096 Aug 29 09:53 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 tom users  4096 Aug 29 09:53 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 tom users  4096 Aug 29 09:53 contracts\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 tom users  4096 Aug 29 09:53 evidence\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users 16899 Aug 29 09:53 spec.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users  1729 Aug 29 09:53 trace.json\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990035431},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The branch is correct, and the working tree is clean. `bench/` doesn't exist yet, and `tests/` doesn't have `test_bench_client.py` yet. In other words, there's no existing task work that should be preserved, so I need to build it from scratch.\n\nLet me read the spec. First, the sections referenced in the spec: `specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6` and `specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md`. Also the style reference `scripts/receipts-verify.py`.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Branch is correct, tree clean, no existing `bench/` or `test_bench_client.py`. I need to build from scratch. Let me read the cited spec sections and style references.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_607743bd980c4dc4bcd7582a","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/specs/flashnext/spec.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2135,"output":223,"cacheRead":7168,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":118,"totalTokens":9526,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990035435,"responseId":"chatcmpl-13c348d3-869f-4217-b7ad-b35002aa801f","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_607743bd980c4dc4bcd7582a","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# flashnext — the vendor FP8 release at tensor-parallel two on the coordinator/worker pair\n\nStatus: ratified 2026-08-28\nGoverns: silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\nConsumers: the flashnext worklist readFirst anchors; the S1 boundary sitting; the S3 morning review\nSupersedes: none\n\n## Outcome\n\nToday the pair serves nothing of this class: the stock engine refuses the FP8\nmixture layer on this GPU generation, the platform tree cannot load the FP8\nlookup table, and no container, units, or benchmarks exist for the pair. After\nthis campaign the estate carries a fork whose two patches admit the mixture\npath and port the disk-backed table onto the platform tree, a container built\nfrom that fork at an exactly pinned wheel set, host tooling that stands the\ntwo-node service on both rails with the wire as control, staged weights on both\nnodes, and a witnessed measurement record. Overnight checkpoints prove first\nlight on a proxy checkpoint, first light on the pair, warmed-decode residency,\na fidelity baseline, and counterbalanced benchmarks, each leaving a receipt the\nladder verifies. The morning operator arrives to a serving pair with numbers,\nor to a typed blocker naming its upstream cause with a drafted issue — never to\na silent skip.\n\n## Vocabulary\n\n- pair — the hosts coordinator and worker, two identical 128 GB unified-memory\n  desktops on the same fleet substrate.\n- rails — the interfaces thunderbolt0 and thunderbolt1, the inference plane,\n  socket transport only; one rail is cabled TB5 and one TB3, both trained at\n  40 Gb/s in the measured record, re-read at first light.\n- wire — the interface enp191s0, the control plane: ssh, orchestration,\n  staging.\n- workload checkpoint (NEW) — the vendor FP8 release at upstream revision\n  970c569adaca6b35532111fd6b27351b2baefe50; node-side artifact directory\n  /var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8; full naming lives in README.md and the\n  evidence, never in this file or the worklist.\n- engram table — the 51.2-billion-parameter hash-lookup embedding inside the\n  workload checkpoint, read once per token at one layer.\n- fork (NEW) — the engine fork published as github mecattaf/vllm branch\n  flashnext; base commit 8e4e036a311604800334989485b4ee23925956da, the\n  table pull request's head, which already carries the model-support pull\n  request's model code.\n- admission patch (NEW) — the fork change that admits the block-FP8 mixture\n  scheme on this GPU and adds the in-kernel upcast, switch FN_FP8_MOE.\n- table port (NEW) — the fork change that wires the mmap table path and the\n  FP8 embedding stack into the platform tree the pair runs.\n- mmap path (NEW) — the disk-backed table mechanism behind VLLM_PLE_MMAP:\n  page-cache faults serve gathers, zero table bytes GPU-resident.\n- proxy checkpoint (NEW) — a small synthetic checkpoint with the same\n  architecture identifier and expert format, for single-node first light\n  before the real weights are touched.\n- engine build (NEW) — the fork made runnable per ruling P4: the container\n  lane tonight, the nix graduation lane once the patch set freezes; the build\n  receipt records which lane served.\n- container (NEW) — the image flashnext:dev built by container/build.sh from\n  the fork at stable vendor wheels, with a bind-mounted cached build\n  directory for incremental iteration.\n- pair service (NEW) — the orchestration under host/ standing both ranks:\n  container up on both nodes, distributed head and worker, then the serve\n  process at tensor-parallel two.\n- receipt (NEW) — one JSON record under results/receipts written by an\n  overnight step; the ladder gate validates every receipt against its bounds.\n- bench matrix (NEW) — the counterbalanced measurement protocol of ruling P12.\n- fidelity baseline (NEW) — reference losses and frontier logits captured at\n  first light, the yardstick for every later change.\n- morning ledger (NEW) — docs/MORNING.md, rendered overnight: receipts, open\n  items, promotion checklist, blocker entries.\n- RDMA package (NEW) — the operator-attended fast-path bring-up prepared under\n  host/rdma: pinned out-of-tree module fetch (never vendored), the single-rail\n  contract, the coordinated-reboot checklist, and the socket-versus-verbs\n  measurement protocol.\n\n## Rulings\n\n| id | decision | ruling |\n|---|---|---|\n| P1 | identity and timing | identity flashnext; the worklist and every gate id below are authored at this S1 sitting; the Governs line resolves before ratification |\n| P2 | workload naming | model-family names stay out of spec and worklist bytes (lint L16); the workload is named by revision and by the node-side artifact directory; full names live in README.md and evidence |\n| P3 | fork base | the table pull request's head is the base — it carries the model-support pull request's model code; the model-support branch's later head commit is CI-only and is not taken |\n| P4 | engine substrate | the container lane serves the night: stable-index torch 2.13.0+rocm7.14.0 wheels — the fork's exact pin, published on the vendor stable channel — with the fork built from source in an image whose build directory is bind-mounted with compiler caching, so a one-line patch iteration recompiles only what changed; the nix derivation is the graduation lane, prepared tonight as wiring plus audit but never tonight's critical path — a moving patch set is not a thing to nixify, and the nix substrate charges a full recompile for every one-line change; the build receipt names the lane |\n| P5 | admission shape | the admission extends the upcast mechanism of upstream pull request 52970 to the fused-mixture oracle; refusal stays loud; FN_FP8_MOE=0 restores the stock refusal; no silent fallback is added |\n| P6 | cherry-picks | upstream pull requests 46012, 40963, 51511, 46110 enter the fork with Cherry-picked-from trailers; 44331, 46186, 46676 receive no task in this campaign and are recorded in IMPORTS.md for the four-bit lane |\n| P7 | transport | socket transport on both rails is the overnight transport of record, both rails named to the collective library; the RDMA package is prepared overnight but brought up only operator-attended, on one rail only, and only after a committed socket-transport benchmark is banked — the kernel half is entirely unpaid on this fleet, the worker's deploy path must move off the fast rail before any kernel change, the reboot order is worker first and never both at once, and adoption comes only on a counterbalanced win |\n| P8 | weights | staged library-to-node by scripts/stage-weights.sh with a per-file digest manifest; the fleet catalog row is a morning operator act prepared under handoff/; runtime hub downloads stay forbidden |\n| P9 | unattended overnight | GPU checkpoints run unattended; a wedge needing physical presence ends the campaign with the blocker typed in the morning ledger; no overnight reboot of either node |\n| P10 | first light mode | the table path's own guard demands piecewise capture with the mmap operation as a split boundary and refuses plain eager — first light runs that sanctioned mode; full-graph capture is morning work |\n| P11 | residency verdict | read after 50 warmed decode tokens on both ranks, never at load; GTT, RSS, and table page-cache residency recorded; pass bound 80 GiB per rank with zero table bytes GPU-resident |\n| P12 | bench protocol | three loads per arm, interleaved arms, medians, token fingerprints; depth series 0, 10240, 102400; speculative on and off arms; rows committed under results/ |\n| P13 | promotion | a morning human act; nothing overnight changes any fleet roster or default |\n| P14 | oracle provenance | the gate ids below are created in the governing worklist at this sitting; heavy overnight proofs bind through receipts validated by the receipts gate, and the trace joins each claim to its producing checkpoint |\n| P15 | compute routing | campaign adapter pi with the model unset — the host catalog answers; the fork engineering is estate bootstrap authored before arming and reviewed in-session |\n| P16 | blocker protocol | an unresolvable upstream defect ends as drafted issue text under handoff/upstream-issues plus a morning-ledger entry, never as a silent skip |\n\n## Claims\n\n### R1 — provenance and the public face\nWhy: every downstream judgment leans on the evidence corpus, and the repository is public from birth; a dangling citation is a fabricated fact.\n1.1 the seven sweep dossiers land under `specs/flashnext/evidence/` → the tests suite counts at least 7 (given) dossiers. [gate: repo-tests]\n1.2 `IMPORTS.md` names every external artifact with source, revision or pull-request id, license, and role → the notices file covers the same set, asserted by the tests suite. [gate: repo-tests]\n1.3 `README.md` states the project, the topology, and the evidence trail → the tests suite's secret scan passes on the published tree. [gate: repo-tests]\n\n### R2 — the fork\nWhy: the fork is the only path this class of workload can take on this GPU; its content must be provable from the estate, not narrated.\n2.1 BELIEVE:IMPORTS.md — the fork base is the table pull request's head commit 8e4e036a311604800334989485b4ee23925956da → the fork-verify gate confirms base ancestry on the published fork branch. [gate: fork-verify]\n2.2 the admission patch lands on the fork → the verify script finds the FN_FP8_MOE switch and the upcast plumbing in the fused-mixture layer tree. [gate: fork-verify]\n2.3 the table port lands on the fork → the verify script finds the mmap import, the FP8 dequant, and the relocated mmap module in the platform tree. [gate: fork-verify]\n2.4 the four ruling P6 cherry-picks land on the fork → the verify script finds each signature. [gate: fork-verify]\n2.5 every fork commit past the base is mirrored under `patches/` → the verify script counts mirrored patches equal to fork commits. [gate: fork-verify]\n\n### R3 — the container\nWhy: the pinned wheel set and the fork meet for the first time inside the image; the smoke step answers the cheap questions before any weight byte moves.\n3.1 the engine build lands through the ruling P4 container lane → a build receipt records status pass with the lane, the resolved torch and triton versions, and the wall-clock. [gate: receipts-verify]\n3.2 `scripts/run-smoke.sh` runs against the image → a smoke receipt records the GPU architecture string, a finite fp8 storage cast, the registered architecture identifier, the aperture, and the admission verdict. [gate: receipts-verify]\n\n### R4 — engine proof\nWhy: nobody anywhere has run this workload on this GPU at tensor-parallel two; each step below converts one unknown into a receipt.\n4.1 the proxy checkpoint serves on one node with the mmap path engaged → a proxy receipt records finite output and a clean shutdown. [gate: receipts-verify]\n4.2 the pair service reaches first light eager over both rails → a tp2 receipt records a greedy 300 (given)-token completion byte-identical across two (given) runs and the per-rail link speeds. [gate: receipts-verify]\n4.3 residency is read per ruling P11 → a residency receipt lands inside the per-rank bound with zero engram table bytes GPU-resident. [gate: receipts-verify]\n4.4 the fidelity baseline lands → reference losses and frontier logits are stored under `results/` beside their receipt. [gate: receipts-verify]\n4.5 context rises to 262144 (given) → a context receipt records decode within 10 (given) percent of the short-context figure. [gate: receipts-verify]\n\n### R5 — weights\nWhy: the pair serves the workload checkpoint from its own drives; staging is verified movement, not trust.\n5.1 `scripts/stage-weights.sh` places the workload checkpoint on both nodes → weight receipts record at least 131 (given) shards per node with byte totals matching the library source. [gate: receipts-verify]\n5.2 the fleet catalog patch is prepared under `handoff/` → the receipts gate parses it as a well-formed patch when present. [gate: receipts-verify]\n\n### R6 — service and measurement\nWhy: a number without its protocol is an anecdote; the harness and its client are graded before any number is quoted.\n6.1 the pair-service tooling lands under `host/` → unit files carry a stop-post teardown and no boot-time install, asserted by the tests suite. [gate: repo-tests]\n6.2 the bench client separates queue wait from prefill → a client unit test with injected queueing passes in the tests suite. [gate: repo-tests]\n6.3 the bench matrix runs per ruling P12 → a bench receipt records three (given) loads per arm, counterbalanced, with token fingerprints, and rows committed under `results/`. [gate: receipts-verify]\n6.4 the RDMA package lands per ruling P7 → the tests suite's package assertions pass: syntax-clean scripts, pinned module fetch, no unattended install path. [gate: repo-tests]\n\n### R7 — the morning and the blocker protocol\nWhy: promotion is a human act and the campaign's failure mode is typed, never silent.\n7.1 the morning ledger renders overnight → the operator reads receipts, open items, and the promotion checklist in one file. [HUMAN-ATTENDED]\n7.2 an overnight step fails for an upstream cause → drafted issue text lands under `handoff/upstream-issues/` and the morning ledger names the blocker. [HUMAN-ATTENDED]\n7.3 the morning operator reviews receipts and ledger at a keyboard → a promotion or blocker disposition is recorded, the catalog patch applied on promotion. [HUMAN-ATTENDED]\n7.4 the operator brings the RDMA package up attended, one rail, per its checklist → a socket-versus-verbs comparison lands under `results/` before any transport adoption. [HUMAN-ATTENDED]\n\n## Unchanged\n\nU.1 the GTT page ceiling stays 33554432 (given) pages on both nodes → the smoke receipt records that value. [gate: receipts-verify]\nU.2 the latency hold and its tripwire stay armed on both nodes → the tp2 receipt records held sub-budget round trips on both ends. [gate: receipts-verify]\nU.3 runtime hub downloads stay forbidden → the tests suite rejects any worklist bytes carrying a hub-download flag. [gate: repo-tests]\n\n## Unknowns\n\nUNKNOWN-1 whether the fused-mixture block-FP8 kernel is numerically sound on this GPU once admitted — drained by claims 4.1 and 4.4.\nUNKNOWN-2 whether fp8 storage plus the widening cast works on the pinned torch build — drained by claim 3.2.\nUNKNOWN-3 whether the collective library crosses the pair at tensor-parallel two over socket transport — drained by claim 4.2.\nUNKNOWN-4 the fork's runtime behavior on the ruling P4 wheel set — drained by claims 3.1 and 4.1.\nUNKNOWN-5 speculative-decode acceptance on real prompts and the draft-length optimum — recorded by the bench receipt; tuning is morning work.\nUNKNOWN-6 the wall-clock of the first full container engine build on this machine — measured by the build receipt; iteration after it rides the cached build directory.\nUNKNOWN-7 whether the pinned packaging expression satisfies every override signature and literal against the fork tree — the graduation-lane question, drained by the substrate-compat audit; it blocks nothing tonight.\nDECISION-1 does the container carry the aiter library? proposed: no — the admission path is self-contained and the aiter half of the pattern donor stays out (given)\n\n## Stages\n\n### S1 — bootstrap\nThis sitting: estate skeleton, evidence corpus, fork engineering, worklist\nauthored, both repositories published. Claims R1, R2; rulings P1 to P16.\n\n### S2 — overnight\nOrder: weights staging beside container build, then smoke, then proxy, then\npair first light, then residency and fidelity, then the bench matrix; the\nservice and measurement tooling lands ahead of the checkpoints that call it.\nClaims R3, R4, R5, R6; rulings P4 to P12.\n\n### S3 — morning\nOrder: ledger review, disposition, catalog patch on promotion, then the\noptimization menu in the morning ledger. Claims R7; ruling P13.\n\n## Forbidden\n\nF.1 Never load an unsigned kernel module or bring RDMA up unattended, and never on both rails.\nF.2 Do not import the upstream tuning module into any host closure.\nF.3 Do not add keys to the worklist schema.\nF.4 Never write under specs/flashnext/ from any lane.\nF.5 Do not put model-family names in spec or worklist bytes.\nF.6 Do not download weights at runtime.\nF.7 Never decide the residency verdict from load-time readings.\nF.8 Do not commit a benchmark number from a single uncounterbalanced run.\nF.9 Do not export an environment default the engine reads through an is-set probe.\nF.10 Do not vendor GPL code into the estate.\nF.11 Do not pull any wheel outside the two sanctioned sources — the realized substrate in the store and the vendor stable index.\nF.12 Never record an overnight step as skipped without a typed blocker in the morning ledger.\nF.13 Do not reboot either node overnight.\nF.14 Do not open any upstream pull request or issue from a lane.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990041238},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is quite an extensive spec. Let me focus on claim R6, the core of this task:\n\n**R6 — Services and Measurement**\n> Reason: numbers without a protocol are just anecdotes. The harness and its clients are evaluated before any numbers get cited.\n\n- 6.1 Pair-service tooling goes under `host/` → the unit files get stop-post teardown and no install-at-boot asserted by the test suite. [gate: repo-tests] — Not my task (this is host tooling).\n- 6.2 The bench client separates queue wait from prefill → a client unit test with queueing injected passes within the test suite. [gate: repo-tests] — **My task.**\n- 6.3 The bench matrix runs per verdict P12 → a bench receipt records three (specified) loads per arm, is counterbalanced, carries token fingerprints, and the rows are committed under `results/`. [gate: receipts-verify] — **My task.**\n- 6.4 RDMA packages… — Not my task.\n\n**Verdict P12 — Bench Protocol:**\n> Three loads per arm, interleaved arms, median, token fingerprints; depth series 0, 10240, 102400; arms with and without speculative; rows committed under `results/`.\n\nAlright, let me read the evidence file `specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md`, specifically section 4.4. That's where it's documented that the reference harness's client published a prefill column that duplicates the first-token column.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me read the key evidence file, especially section 4.4 documenting the reference harness's flaw, plus the style reference and the existing test patterns.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_655a3b149b154d81a0887c11","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5194,"output":439,"cacheRead":8448,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":336,"totalTokens":14081,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990041244,"responseId":"chatcmpl-a3367aca-c386-40aa-a20a-afa16c4c60f1","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_655a3b149b154d81a0887c11","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Dossier: `nix-strix-halo` (hellas-ai) as a flashnext flake INPUT\n\n**Target tree:** `/home/tom/Downloads/nix-strix-halo`\n**Read session date:** 2026-08-28\n**Grading:** `[S]` read from source · `[M]` measured (command output) · `[CL]` claimed by docs, unverified\n\n**Provenance pins (all [M], `git -C /home/tom/Downloads/nix-strix-halo …`):**\n\n```\nremote:  origin  https://github.com/hellas-ai/nix-strix-halo.git\nHEAD:    f0f2048ff842749b363ea3562a98fb0a04bb2e61  2026-08-18 10:03:06 +0200\n         \"npu-exporter: add AMD XDNA NPU Prometheus exporter (#162)\"\n```\n\nEvery line pin below is against this working tree at this commit. The tree is\nclean of local modifications for the files cited.\n\n---\n\n## 0. LICENSE VERIFICATION — the gating question\n\n**[M]** Two independent checks, both negative:\n\n```\n$ ls -la /home/tom/Downloads/nix-strix-halo | grep -iE 'licen|copying|legal'\n(no output, exit 1)\n\n$ find /home/tom/Downloads/nix-strix-halo -iname '*licen*' -o -iname 'COPYING*' -not -path './.git/*'\n(no output)\n```\n\n**[M]** Full file inventory (`find … -type f -not -path '*/.git/*'`) enumerated\nevery file in the tree. There is **no `LICENSE`, `LICENSE.md`, `COPYING`,\n`COPYRIGHT`, or `NOTICE` file anywhere**, at any depth.\n\n**[S]** `README.md` was grepped for `license|LICENSE` — **zero matches**. There is\nno license statement in prose either.\n\n**[S]** Individual `.nix` files carry no SPDX headers. The only license-adjacent\nmetadata is per-package `meta.maintainers` (e.g.\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:357` — `maintainers = with lib.maintainers; [ georgewhewell ]`),\nwhich is attribution, not a grant.\n\n### Consequence for the campaign\n\n| Use | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Declare as a flake input; consume `packages` / `overlays` / `nixosModules` / `lib` from the store path | **Fine.** No copying occurs; Nix fetches from the public repo. |\n| `import \"${inputs.nix-strix-halo}/overlays/therock-vllm.nix\" { … }` — importing a path *inside the input's store realisation* | **Fine.** Still input use, not vendoring. This is the escape hatch that matters (see §3). |\n| Copy any `.nix` file, patch, or `lib/bench/*.sh|*.py` into the flashnext tree | **Not allowed.** No LICENSE = all rights reserved by default. |\n| Copy the *shape* of an expression from memory after reading it | **Treat as copying.** Design around the input, do not transcribe. |\n\n**Practical rule for §3–§6:** every mechanism recommended below is expressed as\n*override / follows / import-from-store*, never as \"lift their expression.\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. FULL FLAKE SURFACE\n\n### 1.1 Inputs\n\n**[M]** `flake.lock` root node lists **48 inputs**. **[S]** `flake.nix:4–253`\ndeclares them. Structurally they are three groups:\n\n**Group A — real flakes (2):**\n\n| Input | `flake.nix` line | URL | Locked rev | Locked date [M] |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `nixpkgs` | `flake.nix:5` | `github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable` | `61b7c44c4073` | **2026-07-18** |\n| `nix-ai-tools` | `flake.nix:7–10` | `github:numtide/nix-ai-tools`, `inputs.nixpkgs.follows = \"nixpkgs\"` (`flake.nix:9`) | `26b51974319a` | 2026-07-19 |\n| `thunderbolt-ibverbs` | `flake.nix:57–60` | `github:hellas-ai/thunderbolt-ibverbs`, `inputs.nixpkgs.follows = \"nixpkgs\"` (`flake.nix:59`) | `76ba39b630a7` | 2026-06-30 |\n\n(Three, counting `thunderbolt-ibverbs`. It is the only input whose *outputs* —\noverlay + nixosModule + packages — are consumed; see `flake.nix:292`, `516`, `531`, `305`.)\n\n**Group B — hand-maintained source inputs, `flake = false` (13):**\n\n| Input | line | URL | Locked rev | Locked date [M] |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `ec-su-axb35` | `flake.nix:12–15` | `github:cmetz/ec-su_axb35-linux` | `7a9f372edcaa` | 2026-07-11 |\n| **`llama-cpp-master`** | **`flake.nix:17–20`** | `github:ggml-org/llama.cpp` (no ref → HEAD) | **`571d0d540df0`** | **2026-07-18** |\n| `llama-cpp-spacemit` | `flake.nix:22–25` | `github:spacemit-com/llama.cpp/v0.1.5` | `86d4ff230bdf` | 2026-07-04 |\n| `spine-triton` | `flake.nix:27–30` | `git+…spine-triton.git?ref=refs/tags/0.5.5&submodules=1` | `c62b11c5ea99` | 2026-06-04 |\n| `fastflowlm` | `flake.nix:32–35` | `github:FastFlowLM/FastFlowLM` | `fd371409897d` | 2026-07-17 |\n| `ds4` | `flake.nix:37–40` | `github:antirez/ds4` | `80ebbc396aee` | 2026-06-17 |\n| `ds4-hip` | `flake.nix:42–45` | `github:ejpir/ds4-hip/rocm-upstream-shape-cyberneurova` | `3490c2e46c91` | 2026-06-04 |\n| **`vllm-src`** | **`flake.nix:47–50`** | **`github:vllm-project/vllm/v0.25.1`** | **`752a3a504485`** | **2026-07-12** |\n| `mlx-src` | `flake.nix:52–55` | `github:NripeshN/mlx/rocm-support` | `0dadb703d773` | 2026-07-16 |\n| `xrt-src` | `flake.nix:62–65` | `git+…Xilinx/XRT?ref=XRT-2.21&submodules=1` | `8661761775a2` | 2026-06-04 |\n| `xdna-driver-src` | `flake.nix:67–70` | `git+…amd/xdna-driver?ref=1.7&submodules=1` | `0fb464f6e043` | 2026-06-04 |\n\n**Group C — machine-generated TheRock source tree (33 inputs), `flake = false`:**\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:72–73`:\n\n```nix\n# BEGIN generated TheRock source inputs\n# Generated by pkgs/therock/scripts/update-source-tree.py; do not edit by hand.\n```\n\nEvery input named `therock-src-7-15-gfx1151-*`. Root at `flake.nix:74–77`:\n`github:ROCm/TheRock/1de3171d00f6de55e9ed517dc6ca6e825d1e4b55`, locked\n**2026-07-18** [M]. The other 32 are submodule pins (rocm-libraries, rocm-systems,\nllvm-project, HIPIFY, perfetto, abseil, pybind11, …) — several extremely old\n(pybind11 2022-03-31, gulrak/filesystem 2023-03-05) because they are ROCm's own\nvendored third-party pins, not staleness introduced here.\n\n**[S]** `.github/dependabot.yml:14–19` explicitly *ignores* these:\n\n```yaml\n    ignore:\n      # TheRock source inputs are generated from pkgs/therock/sources/rocm-source.json\n      # and must be bumped atomically by pkgs/therock/scripts/update-source-tree.py.\n      - dependency-name: \"therock-src-*\"\n      # GitHub Dependabot's Nix updater does not currently support private inputs.\n      - dependency-name: \"thunderbolt-ibverbs\"\n```\n\n> **On the campaign record's \"stale 2026-07-18 llama-cpp pin\":** **CONFIRMED [M]**.\n> `llama-cpp-master` locks `571d0d540df0` at **2026-07-18**, 41 days before today.\n> Note the record's framing is slightly off in one respect: 2026-07-18 is not a\n> `llama-cpp`-specific staleness — it is the *whole lock's* watermark. `nixpkgs`\n> and the TheRock root are locked to the **same day**.\n\n### 1.2 Outputs\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:254–1119`. Systems: `flake.nix:262–264` —\n`linuxSystems = [ \"x86_64-linux\" ]`, `darwinSystems = [ \"aarch64-darwin\" ]`.\n\n| Output | line | Contents |\n|---|---|---|\n| `lib` | `466–500` | `defaultTherockSources`, `mkLiveIsoConfiguration`, `mkRocmTarget`, `rocmProviders`/`pythonProviders`/`pythonProviderStubs`, `therockTargets`, `bench`, and the four overlay builders **`mkRocmOverlay` (480), `mkPythonOverlay` (489), `mkPkgsOverlay` (490), `mkMtuneOverlay` (499)** |\n| `overlays` | `502–510` | `default` (`503–505`, `composeManyExtensions (mkOverlays { rocmTarget = defaultRocmTarget; })`), `python` (`506–508`), `spacemitK3` (`509`) |\n| `nixosModules` | `513–532` | `default`, `rpc-server`, `benchmark-executor`, `benchmark-runner`, `ec-su-axb35`, `fastflowlm`, `smu-exporter`, `npu-exporter`, `ryzenadj`, **`tuning` (530)**, `thunderbolt-ibverbs` |\n| `nixosConfigurations` | `534–536` | `live-iso` only |\n| `darwinModules` | `538–540` | `benchmark-executor` only |\n| `legacyPackages` | `545–553` | one pkgs set per `rocmTarget` (`gfx1010/1030/1036/1103/1151`) |\n| `benchmarks` | `555` | `perSystem mkBenchmarkSuites` (from `lib/bench`) |\n| `packages` | `557–757` | see below |\n| `apps` | `759–893` | wrappers over packages |\n| `checks` | `895–1083` | see below |\n| `devShells` | `1085–1111` | `default` only |\n| `formatter` | `1112` | `pkgs.nixfmt-tree` |\n\n**Notable packages [S]:** the Linux list is `flake.nix:617–645` (`vllm-rocm` at\n`642`), plus `flake.nix:712–715`:\n\n```nix\n\"strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench-${s}\" = pkgs.callPackage ./pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench {\n  vllmPackage = vllmPairBenchEnv;\n  packageSuffix = s;\n};\n```\n\nwhere `s = defaultRocmTarget.packageSuffix` (`flake.nix:561`) = `gfx1151`\n(`pkgs/therock/targets.nix:42,54`). So the concrete attr is\n`packages.x86_64-linux.strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench-gfx1151`.\n\n**`checks` [S]** (`x86_64-linux` only, guarded at `flake.nix:897`):\n`deadnix` (1058), `statix` (1059), `nixfmt` (1060–1062),\n`cuda-host-driver-runtime` (1063), `package-surface` (1064),\n`vllm-pair-bench-dry-run` (1065–1080).\n\nThe last is worth flagging for the campaign — **[S]** `flake.nix:1076–1079`:\n\n```\nbash -n ${./lib/bench/vllm-transport-matrix.sh}\npython3 -m py_compile ${./lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py}\nstrix-halo-vllm-pair-bench-ci --scenario qwen-peak --dry-run | tee \"$out\"\ngrep -q \"dry-run: not invoking\" \"$out\"\n```\n\nIt is a **syntax check plus a dry-run**, built against *fake* vLLM/RDMA/GCC\nstubs (`flake.nix:1021–1049`, comment at `1018–1020`: \"so CI validates the\nwrapper, matrix script, and client without the heavy ROCm closure or a real\ntwo-host lab\"). **No CI anywhere executes the harness against a real engine.**\nThis is the reason the §4 metrology bug survived: the only automated coverage is\n`py_compile`.\n\n### 1.3 How a downstream flake points `vllm-src` at a FORK — exact mechanism\n\nThere are **three** viable mechanisms; only the first two are license-clean and\nlow-friction. Facts first, then the ranking.\n\n**Facts establishing the wiring [S]:**\n\n`flake.nix:365–371` — the *only* place `vllm-src` is consumed:\n\n```nix\n(import ./overlays/therock-vllm.nix {\n  inherit lib therockPythonConfig;\n  target = rocmTarget;\n  vllmSrc = inputs.vllm-src;\n  vllmVersion = \"0.25.1\";\n  enabled = enableTherockVllm;\n})\n```\n\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:22–24` — the src is decorated before use:\n\n```nix\nvllmSrcWithTag = vllmSrc // {\n  tag = vllmSrc.tag or \"v${vllmVersion}\";\n};\n```\n\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:242–251` — src and version are substituted onto\nnixpkgs' vllm:\n\n```nix\n(py.vllm.override {\n  rocmSupport = true;\n  cudaSupport = false;\n  gpuTargets = vllmGpuTargets;\n  rocmPackages = therockRocmPackages;\n  inherit (py) amdsmi;\n}).overridePythonAttrs\n  (old: {\n    version = vllmVersion;\n    src = vllmSrcWithTag;\n```\n\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:363–365` — what the overlay actually publishes:\n\n```nix\nlib.optionalAttrs hasTherockVllmInputs {\n  \"vllm-rocm-therock-${s}\" = vllmTherock;\n}\n```\n\n`overlays/pkgs.nix:332` — the unsuffixed alias, resolved through `final`:\n\n```nix\n      vllm-rocm = final.\"vllm-rocm-therock-${suffix}\";\n```\n\nThe `final.` reference is the load-bearing detail: because the alias is a\nfixpoint reference, **a later overlay that redefines\n`vllm-rocm-therock-gfx1151` is automatically picked up by `vllm-rocm`.**\n\n---\n\n**Mechanism A — `follows` on the flake input (cleanest; recommended primary).**\n\nBecause `vllm-src` is a *top-level* input (`flake.nix:47–50`) and is threaded\nstraight through at `flake.nix:368`, a standard input override reaches it:\n\n```nix\ninputs.vllm-fork = {\n  url = \"github:<org>/vllm/<branch-with-PR53896+54129+patches>\";\n  flake = false;\n};\ninputs.nix-strix-halo.url = \"github:hellas-ai/nix-strix-halo\";\ninputs.nix-strix-halo.inputs.vllm-src.follows = \"vllm-fork\";\n```\n\n- **Reaches:** `src` of `vllm-rocm-therock-gfx1151`, hence `vllm-rocm`, hence\n  the `vllmPairBenchEnv` symlinkJoin (`flake.nix:685–691`) that feeds the bench\n  driver. One override moves the whole chain. **[S]**\n- **Does NOT reach:** the version string. `vllmVersion = \"0.25.1\"` is a **string\n  literal at `flake.nix:369`**, not an input. It survives into\n  `version = vllmVersion` (`therock-vllm.nix:250`) and\n  `VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE = vllmVersion` (`therock-vllm.nix:296`). The fork will\n  therefore *report itself as 0.25.1* regardless of what it actually is. **[S]**\n- **Second-order gotcha [S]:** `therock-vllm.nix:23` computes\n  `tag = vllmSrc.tag or \"v${vllmVersion}\"`. A GitHub-tag input carries a `tag`\n  attr; a branch/rev-pinned fork generally does not, so the fallback stamps\n  `v0.25.1` onto the fork's src attrset. Whether nixpkgs' vllm expression\n  *consumes* `src.tag` at the pinned nixpkgs rev is **UNDETERMINED** (see §3\n  caveat) — but `VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE` at `:296` is the belt to that\n  suspenders, so the practical risk is cosmetic version reporting, not a build\n  break.\n- **Verdict:** use this, and additionally pin the reported version via\n  Mechanism C so telemetry does not lie about what is running.\n\n**Mechanism B — re-import the overlay from the input's store path (full control).**\n\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix` is a plain function of\n`{ lib, target, vllmSrc, vllmVersion, therockPythonConfig ? …, enabled ? true }`\n(`therock-vllm.nix:1–8`). Downstream can call it with its own arguments:\n\n```nix\nnixpkgs.overlays = [\n  nix-strix-halo.overlays.default\n  (import \"${nix-strix-halo}/overlays/therock-vllm.nix\" {\n    inherit (nixpkgs) lib;\n    target  = nix-strix-halo.lib.therockTargets.rocmTargetsBySuffix.gfx1151;\n    vllmSrc = inputs.vllm-fork;\n    vllmVersion = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\";   # honest version\n  })\n];\n```\n\n- Gets **both** `src` and `version` right. **[S]** — signature at `:1–8`,\n  target accessor at `flake.nix:473` (`therockTargets = therockTargetConfig`)\n  and `pkgs/therock/targets.nix:4,41,57`.\n- Later overlay wins for `vllm-rocm-therock-gfx1151`; `vllm-rocm` follows via\n  the `final.` alias at `overlays/pkgs.nix:332`. **[S]**\n- **License-clean**: `\"${nix-strix-halo}/overlays/therock-vllm.nix\"` is a path\n  into the fetched input, evaluated in place. Nothing is copied into the\n  flashnext tree.\n- **Cost:** couples flashnext to that file's internal argument names. If\n  upstream renames `vllmSrc`, evaluation breaks loudly (good) rather than\n  silently.\n\n**Mechanism C — `overridePythonAttrs` on the finished package (patch injection).**\n\n```nix\n(final: prev: {\n  vllm-rocm = prev.vllm-rocm.overridePythonAttrs (old: {\n    version = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\";\n    src = inputs.vllm-fork;\n    patches = (old.patches or [ ]) ++ [ ./patches/pr53896.patch ];\n    env = (old.env or { }) // { VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\"; };\n  });\n})\n```\n\nThis is the answer to \"can we inject `.patch` files without copying their nix\ncode\" — **yes**, see §3.\n\n**What is NOT available [S]:** there is no `overlays.therockVllm` and no\n`lib.mkTherockVllmOverlay`.\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'mkTherockVllmOverlay\\|therock-vllm' --include='*.nix' . | grep -v ./.git\nflake.nix:365:          (import ./overlays/therock-vllm.nix {\n```\n\n**One match, the internal call site.** Contrast with `lib` at `flake.nix:480–499`,\nwhich *does* export `mkRocmOverlay`/`mkPythonOverlay`/`mkPkgsOverlay`/`mkMtuneOverlay`.\n`README.md:184–185` [CL] confirms the intended composition set and likewise omits\nvLLM: *\"For non-default providers compose your own overlays with `lib.mkRocmOverlay`,\n`lib.mkPythonOverlay`, and `lib.mkPkgsOverlay`.\"* **The vLLM overlay is the one\npiece of the stack with no supported parameterisation point.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. `overlays/therock-*.nix` — ROCm version and Python dependency assembly\n\n### 2.1 Which TheRock / ROCm version\n\n**[S]** `pkgs/therock/sources/rocm.json:2–9`:\n\n```json\n  \"linux\": {\n    \"gfx1151\": {\n      \"url\": \"https://rocm.nightlies.amd.com/tarball-multi-arch/therock-dist-linux-gfx1151-7.15.0a20260719.tar.gz\",\n      \"hash\": \"sha256-04mWViYQujFAy+mbptQ8djGHgLFlmaQHFTfTW8LJ/MY=\",\n      \"version\": \"7.15.0a20260719\",\n```\n\n**ROCm `7.15.0a20260719` — an AMD *nightly*, not a release.** `gfx1030` at\n`rocm.json:10–16` pins the same nightly date.\n\n**[S]** `pkgs/therock/sources/rocm-source.json` (for the from-source path) pins\n`https://github.com/ROCm/TheRock.git`, `ref refs/heads/main`,\n`rev 1de3171d00f6de55e9ed517dc6ca6e825d1e4b55`, `\"version\": \"7.15\"`.\n\nTwo overlay files, trivially related:\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-rocm.nix` is **one line**:\n\n```nix\nargs: import ../pkgs/therock args\n```\n\n(`wc -l` = 1 [M]. A pure re-export; the substance is `pkgs/therock/default.nix`.)\n\n### 2.2 How Python deps for vLLM are assembled — **wheels, not source**\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-python.nix:19–24` installs a\n`pythonPackagesExtensions` entry, gated on interpreter version at `:22`\n(`lib.versions.majorMinor pyprev.python.version == pythonConfig.pythonVersion`),\nand binds `wheels = final.\"therock-python-wheels-${s}\"` at `:24`.\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-python.nix:53–61` — the substitution, the core fact:\n\n```nix\n          torch = wheels;\n          torchvision = wheels;\n          triton = wheels;\n          triton-no-cuda = wheels;\n          torchaudio = wheels;\n          rocm = wheels;\n          \"rocm-sdk-core\" = wheels;\n          \"rocm-sdk-devel\" = wheels;\n          \"rocm-sdk-libraries-${s}\" = wheels;\n```\n\n**torch / torchvision / torchaudio / triton are AMD-published prebuilt wheels**,\nall pointing at one derivation. **[S]**\n`pkgs/therock/sources/python-wheels.json` gives the URLs and hashes:\n\n- `torch-2.11.0+rocm7.15.0a20260719-cp313-cp313-linux_x86_64.whl`\n- `torchvision-0.26.0+rocm7.15.0a20260719-cp313-cp313-linux_x86_64.whl`\n- `torchaudio-2.11.0+rocm7.15.0a20260719-cp313-cp313-linux_x86_64.whl`\n- `triton-3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.15.0a20260719-cp313-…`\n- index: `https://rocm.nightlies.amd.com/whl-multi-arch/`, `\"pythonTag\": \"cp313\"`\n\n**[S]** `pkgs/therock/python-config.nix:4` — `pythonVersion = \"3.13\"`, giving\n`packagesAttr = \"python313Packages\"` (`:11`). **The entire stack is pinned to\nCPython 3.13.** Consistent with the `cp313` wheel tags.\n\n**vLLM itself is the exception — it is built from source.** `overlays/therock-vllm.nix:321`:\n\n```nix\n        requiredSystemFeatures = (old.requiredSystemFeatures or [ ]) ++ [ \"big-parallel\" ];\n```\n\nwith the comment at `:317–320`: *\"vllm-rocm compiles a few hundred HIP kernels\nthrough the TheRock toolchain — heavy enough to need a big-parallel builder.\"*\nSo: **prebuilt wheels for the torch/triton substrate, from-source HIP compile\nfor vLLM's own kernels.** This is the correct shape for the campaign (a fork's\nkernel changes will actually be compiled), but it means every fork bump pays a\nseveral-hundred-kernel HIP build.\n\nAdditional dependency surgery in the same overlay, all **[S]**:\n\n- `therock-vllm.nix:28–40` — `mistral-common` force-pinned to `1.11.2` from\n  PyPI, `doCheck = false` (`:39`).\n- `therock-vllm.nix:41–46` — Triton *kernels* fetched **separately** from\n  `triton-lang/triton` rev `0263a6a6203cf27c441c57a6c808ea87ffb8f654`, surfaced\n  via `TRITON_KERNELS_SRC_DIR` (`:299`). Note this rev matches the\n  `+git0263a6a6` in the triton wheel tag — deliberately coherent.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:47–60` — a `withSetuptools80` shim swapping `setuptools` for\n  `py.setuptools_80` in grpcio-tools / setuptools-scm / setuptools-rust.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:63–90` — `therockRocmPackages`: **26 ROCm component attrs\n  (`clr`, `hipblaslt`, `rccl`, `composable_kernel`, …) all aliased to the single\n  `sdk` derivation** (`:15–20`), which is `therock-rocm-gfx1151` with\n  `localGpuTargets`/`gpuTargets` forced to the target's build targets.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:92–114` — **`dropVllmDependencyNames`, 22 entries** stripped\n  from vLLM's dependency closure: `amd-quark`, `apache-tvm-ffi`, `bitsandbytes`,\n  `conch-triton-kernels`, `datasets`, `fastsafetensors`, `mistral-common`,\n  `mistral_common`, `mistralai`, `opencv-python-headless`, `outlines`, `peft`,\n  `pyarrow`, `pytest-asyncio`, `runai-model-streamer`, `runai_model_streamer`,\n  `tensorizer`, `tilelang`, `timm`, `torchcodec`, `xformers`.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:126–136` — `unsupportedFeatureReasons`, 9 features hard-refused\n  with explanations. **Directly relevant to the campaign:** `aiter` is refused\n  (`:127`, *\"AITer needs a separately packaged ROCm aiter build and upstream only\n  enables it on MI300-class targets\"*) and `rixl` is refused (`:132`, *\"RIXL needs\n  separate ROCm RIXL/UCX/RDMA packaging and is only used by KV-transfer/disaggregated\n  serving paths\"*). Enabling either trips the assertion at `:239–241`.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:234–238` — three hard asserts: `tritonSupport`,\n  `tritonKernelsSupport`, and `otelSupport` **must all be true**.\n\n### 2.3 What would need to change for ROCm 10\n\n**No single knob.** Five coordinated edits, all **[S]** on file structure:\n\n1. **`pkgs/therock/sources/rocm.json`** — `url`/`hash`/`version` per target.\n   Regenerated by `pkgs/therock/scripts/update-rocm.py`; **[S]** its docstring\n   (`:3–8`) says *\"The TheRock nightly index is intentionally kept out of Nix\n   evaluation. Run this script manually when bumping the opt-in preview SDK,\n   review the diff, then commit the updated JSON.\"* `BASE_URL` at `:20` is\n   `https://rocm.nightlies.amd.com/tarball-multi-arch/`. **A ROCm 10 tarball must\n   exist at that nightly index under a matching target slug** (`TARGET_SLUGS`,\n   `:21–28`) or the script has nothing to find.\n2. **`pkgs/therock/sources/python-wheels.json`** — the torch/triton/vision/audio\n   wheel set must be *republished by AMD for ROCm 10 at cp313*. If AMD moves the\n   nightly wheels to cp314, `python-config.nix:4` must change too, which\n   re-resolves `python313Packages` → `python314Packages` across the whole\n   overlay stack (`therock-vllm.nix:21`, `flake.nix:689`, …).\n3. **`pkgs/therock/sources/rocm-source.json` + the 33 generated `flake.nix`\n   inputs (`flake.nix:74–~250`)** — regenerated *atomically* by\n   `pkgs/therock/scripts/update-source-tree.py` (per the banner at\n   `flake.nix:72–73` and the dependabot ignore at `.github/dependabot.yml:17`).\n   Hand-editing these is explicitly out of bounds.\n4. **`pkgs/therock/rocm-modules/*`** — ~60 module dirs carrying **~40 patches**\n   against ROCm internals (`therock/rocm-from-source/patches/*.patch`,\n   `rocm-modules/*/*.patch|*.diff`). A major-version ROCm bump is exactly where\n   these rot.\n5. **`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:269`** — `substituteInPlace pyproject.toml\n   --replace-fail '\"torch == 2.11.0\"' '\"torch\"'`. **`--replace-fail` means this\n   aborts the build if the exact string is absent.** A ROCm 10 wheel set will\n   ship a different torch version and vLLM will pin differently; this line is a\n   guaranteed break. The same brittleness applies to every other\n   `--replace-fail` in `postPatch` (`:259`, `:263`, `:268`, `:271`, `:279`, `:288`).\n\n**Assessment [S-derived]:** ROCm 10 is a *fork-and-maintain* undertaking against\nthis repo, not a config change. The campaign should assume the pinned ROCm\n7.15.0a20260719 nightly for its horizon and treat ROCm 10 as out of scope.\n\n---\n\n## 3. THE vLLM PACKAGE EXPRESSION\n\n### 3.1 How it is built\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-vllm.nix:242–248` — it is **not** a fresh expression.\nIt takes **nixpkgs' `python313Packages.vllm`**, applies `.override` for the ROCm\nknobs, then `.overridePythonAttrs` for everything else:\n\n```nix\n    (py.vllm.override {\n      rocmSupport = true;\n      cudaSupport = false;\n      gpuTargets = vllmGpuTargets;\n      rocmPackages = therockRocmPackages;\n      inherit (py) amdsmi;\n    }).overridePythonAttrs\n      (old: {\n        version = vllmVersion;\n        src = vllmSrcWithTag;\n```\n\n`vllmGpuTargets = target.buildTargets` (`:16`). For gfx1151,\n`pkgs/therock/targets.nix:41–52` sets `packageSuffix = \"gfx1151\"`,\n`hsaOverride = \"11.5.1\"`, and `rocmGpuTargets = [ \"gfx1151\" \"gfx90a\" ]` with the\ncomment at `:46–47` that gfx90a is *\"a composable_kernel build workaround\"*.\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:685–691` — how the bench driver gets a vLLM *with Ray*, and\nthe reasoning:\n\n```nix\n          # Two-host vLLM transport-matrix driver. The driver only needs the\n          # vLLM closure to expose both `vllm` and `ray`, so join the default\n          # ROCm vLLM package with ray rather than baking ray into vllm-rocm.\n          vllmPairBenchEnv = pkgs.symlinkJoin {\n            name = \"vllm-rocm-${s}-ray-env\";\n            paths = [\n              pkgs.vllm-rocm\n              pkgs.${therockPythonConfig.packagesAttr}.ray\n            ];\n          };\n```\n\n**This is a `symlinkJoin`, not a shared Python environment.** Ray and vLLM are\ntwo independently-built closures whose `bin/` and `lib/python3.13/site-packages/`\ntrees are symlink-merged. It works because both resolve their own deps through\ntheir own wrappers — but it is a fragile seam if the campaign needs Ray and vLLM\nto agree on a shared transitive dep version.\n\n### 3.2 Default version\n\n**`0.25.1`**, from **two independent places that must agree**:\n\n- **[S]** `flake.nix:48` — `url = \"github:vllm-project/vllm/v0.25.1\"` (the source)\n- **[S]** `flake.nix:369` — `vllmVersion = \"0.25.1\"` (the string)\n\nThese are **not coupled**. Nothing asserts the src tag matches the version\nstring. Overriding one via `follows` silently desynchronises them (§1.3\nMechanism A).\n\nConsumed at `therock-vllm.nix:250` (`version`), `:296`\n(`VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE`), and `:23` (`tag` fallback).\n\n> **[CL] Doc/source contradiction:** `README.md:22` states\n> `| `vllm-rocm` | source-built vLLM 0.23 against TheRock |`. The flake says\n> **0.25.1**. The README table is stale by two minor versions. **Trust\n> `flake.nix:48,369`; do not quote the README.**\n\n### 3.3 Patches applied\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-vllm.nix:253–255` — the overlay **adds zero patches**.\nIt only *subtracts* one:\n\n```nix\n        patches = builtins.filter (\n          patch: !(lib.hasSuffix \"0006-drop-rocm-extra-reqs.patch\" (toString patch))\n        ) (old.patches or [ ]);\n```\n\nSo the applied set is *nixpkgs' vllm patch list, minus `0006`*.\n\n**[S-local, indicative — see caveat]** Reading the **local system nixpkgs**\n(`/nix/store/llgwlxshmy0ifvxh7f8wq53vk5x7vd13-source`, `.version` = `26.11`),\n`pkgs/development/python-modules/vllm/default.nix:348–353`:\n\n```nix\n  patches = [\n    ./0002-setup.py-nix-support-respect-cmakeFlags.patch\n    ./0003-propagate-pythonpath.patch\n    ./0005-drop-intel-reqs.patch\n    ./0006-drop-rocm-extra-reqs.patch\n  ];\n```\n\nand `:338` — `version = \"0.16.0\"`.\n\n> **CAVEAT (important):** that store path is **this machine's system nixpkgs, not\n> the flake's pinned `61b7c44c4073` (2026-07-18)**. The exact patch list at the\n> pinned rev is **UNDETERMINED**. What would settle it: `nix flake archive` /\n> `nix build .#…vllm.src` against the pinned rev, or reading\n> `pkgs/development/python-modules/vllm/default.nix` at `61b7c44c4073`. I did not\n> fetch it (analysis-only, no builds). The *filter target* — a file literally\n> named `0006-drop-rocm-extra-reqs.patch` — is present in both, so the mechanism\n> is sound; only the exact surviving list is uncertain.\n\n**[S-derived] The structural hazard the campaign must price in:** nixpkgs' vllm\nis **0.16.0**; the overlay swaps in src **0.25.1** — a **nine-minor-version\njump** — while *inheriting 0.16-era patches*. Those three patches must still\napply cleanly to a 0.25.1 tree. They evidently do today (the repo builds), but a\nfork moving further from 0.25.1 raises the odds of a patch-apply failure that\nwill read as a confusing \"hunk FAILED\" in a file nobody in the campaign wrote.\n\n**[S] `postPatch` is REPLACED, not appended** — `therock-vllm.nix:257`:\n\n```nix\n        postPatch = ''\n```\n\nPlain assignment. Not `(old.postPatch or \"\") + …`. Comparing against local\nnixpkgs `:356+` (`rm vllm/third_party/pynvml.py`, the same two\n`substituteInPlace` calls), the overlay's block at `:258–266` **re-implements\nnixpkgs' postPatch verbatim** and then adds four more edits (`:268–292`). This is\ndeliberate supersession, but it means **any downstream `overridePythonAttrs` that\nassigns `postPatch` rather than appending will silently drop all of it** —\nincluding the `registry.py` env fix at `:288–291`, whose comment (`:284–287`)\nwarns it is load-bearing: *\"Upstream replaces its entire environment with\nPYTHONPATH, which strips the TheRock wheel runtime's LD_LIBRARY_PATH and makes\ntorch fail to load libstdc++ before an architecture can even be inspected.\"*\n\n### 3.4 Is a patch-overlay list exposed? — **NO, but injection works anyway**\n\n**[S]** `mkVllmTherock`'s complete signature, `therock-vllm.nix:138–156`:\n\n```nix\n  mkVllmTherock =\n    {\n      aiterSupport ? false,\n      audioSupport ? true,\n      benchSupport ? true,\n      fastsafetensorsSupport ? false,\n      flashinferSupport ? false,\n      grpcSupport ? false,\n      helionSupport ? false,\n      instanttensorSupport ? false,\n      otelSupport ? true,\n      rixlSupport ? false,\n      runaiSupport ? false,\n      tensorizerSupport ? false,\n      tritonSupport ? true,\n      tritonKernelsSupport ? true,\n      videoSupport ? false,\n      zenSupport ? false,\n    }:\n```\n\n**Sixteen parameters, all booleans, all feature toggles.** No `extraPatches`, no\n`patches`, no `src`, no `version`, no `postPatch` hook.\n\n**[S]** `therock-vllm.nix:361`:\n\n```nix\n  vllmTherock = lib.makeOverridable mkVllmTherock { };\n```\n\nSo `pkgs.vllm-rocm.override { … }` accepts **only those 16 booleans** — and 9 of\nthem are hard-refused by the assertion at `:239–241`.\n\n**ANSWER TO THE CAMPAIGN'S QUESTION — yes, `.patch` files can be injected\nwithout copying any of their Nix code**, via `overridePythonAttrs` on the\nfinished derivation. The result of `mkVllmTherock` is an ordinary\n`buildPythonPackage` derivation, so:\n\n```nix\n(final: prev: {\n  vllm-rocm = prev.vllm-rocm.overridePythonAttrs (old: {\n    src      = inputs.vllm-fork;\n    version  = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\";\n    patches  = (old.patches or [ ]) ++ [\n      ./patches/pr-53896.patch\n      ./patches/pr-54129.patch\n    ];\n    postPatch = (old.postPatch or \"\") + ''\n      # append only — never assign, see §3.3\n    '';\n    env = (old.env or { }) // { VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\"; };\n  });\n})\n```\n\nThree rules for the campaign, each grounded above:\n\n1. **Always `(old.patches or [ ]) ++ …`** — assignment discards the inherited\n   nixpkgs patches the build depends on.\n2. **Always `(old.postPatch or \"\") + …`** — `therock-vllm.nix:257` assigns, and\n   `:288–291` is load-bearing.\n3. **Set `VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE` alongside `version`** — `therock-vllm.nix:296`\n   is what the running server reports. Changing `version` alone leaves the\n   process announcing 0.25.1.\n\n**Recommended combination:** Mechanism A (`follows`, for the fork src, so the\nwhole chain including `vllmPairBenchEnv` moves) **+** Mechanism C\n(`overridePythonAttrs`, for `.patch` files and an honest version). Mechanism B\nis the fallback if the campaign needs a genuinely different `target`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. `strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench` + `modules/benchmark-*.nix`\n\n### 4.1 What the two-host TP=2 harness actually does\n\n**[S]** `pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench/default.nix:25` — it is a\n`writeShellApplication`, i.e. **a bash script, not a NixOS service**. Runtime\ninputs at `:28–33`: `coreutils`, `openssh`, `rsync`, `nixVersions.stable`.\n\nFlow, all **[S]**:\n\n1. Parse `--scenario` + overrides (`:84–100`).\n2. Select the scenario's model/transport/concurrency block (`:102–158`).\n3. Preflight six required paths (`:166–175`) — `$GCC_PREFIX/bin/gcc`,\n   `$RDMA/lib`, `$VLLM_ENV/bin/vllm`, `$VLLM_ENV/bin/ray`, and both bench\n   scripts. Missing → `exit 2`.\n4. Emit a ~40-line `REMOTE_ENV` heredoc (`:199–242`).\n5. `--dry-run` short-circuits at `:248–251` printing `(dry-run: not invoking)` —\n   the exact string CI greps for (`flake.nix:1079`).\n6. **`nix copy --no-check-sigs --to \"ssh://$host\"`** the four closures to *both*\n   hosts (`:263–267`).\n7. `scp` the env file to the master, then\n   **`ssh \"$MASTER\" \"set -a; . …/env; set +a; exec bash $bench_script_q\"`** (`:279`).\n8. `rsync` results back (`:283`).\n\n**The actual TP=2 topology lives in `lib/bench/vllm-transport-matrix.sh`** (691\nlines [M]). **[S]** `:568–606`:\n\n- `transport == \"solo\"` (`:568–578`): plain single-host `vllm serve` with\n  `NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1`.\n- otherwise (`:579–605`), the pair path:\n  - **`:586–587`** — `ray start --head --port=6379 --node-ip-address=\"$head\" --num-gpus=1 --include-dashboard=false --disable-usage-stats` on the **coordinator**.\n  - **`:588–589`** — one `ssh -n` invocation starting `ray start --address=$head:6379 --node-ip-address=$worker --num-gpus=1` on the **worker**.\n  - **`:590`** — `sleep 2`. A fixed sleep, not a readiness probe.\n  - **`:592–605`** — `vllm serve \"$model\" --tensor-parallel-size 2 --distributed-executor-backend ray --host 0.0.0.0 --port \"$PORT\"`.\n\nSo: **the \"pair / coordinator-worker over TCP rail\" topology is a Ray cluster\nstood up by SSH from a shell script.** Each node advertises `--num-gpus=1`; vLLM\nshards TP=2 across them via the Ray executor backend.\n\n**[S]** `:626–641` — the client is then invoked against **`http://127.0.0.1:$PORT/v1/completions`**,\ni.e. the coordinator's own loopback.\n\n**[S]** `:619–624` — the four transports:\n\n```\n    lan_tcp)    socket_ifname=\"$LAN_IFNAME/$REMOTE_LAN_IFNAME\" ;;\n    usb4_rdma)  socket_ifname=\"$LAN_IFNAME/$REMOTE_LAN_IFNAME\"; rdma_hca=$(resolve_usb4_hca) ;;\n    tb_tcp)     socket_ifname=\"$TB_IFNAME/$REMOTE_TB_IFNAME\" ;;\n    tb_rxe)     socket_ifname=\"$TB_IFNAME/$REMOTE_TB_IFNAME\"; rdma_hca=$RXE_HCA ;;\n```\n\n### 4.2 Config surface\n\n**[S] CLI flags** — `default.nix:86–97`: `--scenario`, `--master`, `--worker`,\n`--usb4-hca`, `--out`, `--dry-run`, `--extra-transports`, `--vllm-env`,\n`--rdma-prefix`, `--gcc-prefix`, `--bench-dir`, `-h|--help`.\n\n**[S] Nix-level args** — `default.nix:1–15`: `vllmPackage` and `packageSuffix`\nare required; **`vllmTransportMatrix` (`:13`) and `vllmStreamClient` (`:14`) are\noverridable path arguments**, defaulting to `../../lib/bench/*`. **This is the\nclean seam for §4.4** — the campaign can `callPackage` the driver with its own\ncorrected client, no forking of the Nix expression.\n\n**[S] Hardcoded defaults that are hellas lab facts, not ours** — `default.nix:39–41`:\n\n```\n    MASTER=\"grw@strix-1.lan.satanic.link\"\n    WORKER=\"grw@strix-2.lan.satanic.link\"\n    USB4_HCA=\"usb4_rdma0,usb4_rdma1,usb4_rdma5,usb4_rdma6\"\n```\n\nand `:204–205` — `LAN_IFNAME=br0.lan`, `TB_IFNAME=thunderbolt0`. `--master` /\n`--worker` / `--usb4-hca` are overridable; **`LAN_IFNAME` and `TB_IFNAME` are\nnot exposed as flags** — they are baked into the `REMOTE_ENV` heredoc. Changing\nthem requires either `--bench-dir` with a modified script or setting them in the\nenvironment ahead of the matrix script (which reads `${VAR:-default}`).\n\n**[S] Five scenarios** (`default.nix:102–158`): `qwen-peak`,\n`llama-tp2-win`, `qwen35-122b-awq-capacity`, `qwen35-122b-awq-prime`,\n`minimax-m27-awq-strix-2h`. The last (`:141–153`) is the interesting one for a\nlarge-model campaign: `MAX_MODEL_LEN=196608`, `MAX_NUM_SEQS=2`,\n`MAX_NUM_BATCHED_TOKENS=20480`, `--gpu-memory-utilization 0.92`, and\n`SCENARIO_EXTRA_ENV` (`:151`) setting `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER=0`,\n`TORCHDYNAMO_DISABLE=1`, `VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM=0`, `RAY_CGRAPH_get_timeout=1800`, etc.\n\n**[S] Baseline `REMOTE_ENV`** (`default.nix:223–239`) fixes NCCL/RCCL and Ray\nknobs: `NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX=1`, `NCCL_NET_MERGE_LEVEL=LOC`,\n`NCCL_MIN_NCHANNELS=4`/`NCCL_MAX_NCHANNELS=4`,\n`NCCL_IB_QPS_PER_CONNECTION=1`, `NCCL_IB_SPLIT_DATA_ON_QPS=0`,\n`CUDA_/HIP_/ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0`, the three\n`RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_*_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1`, and\n`VLLM_USE_RAY_V2_EXECUTOR_BACKEND=0`.\n\n**[S]** `vllm-transport-matrix.sh:188–243` declares **`TUNE_VARS`, 55 names**\n(NCCL/RCCL chunk & channel sizing, `HSA_NO_SCRATCH_RECLAIM`, `HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT`,\nGDRCopy, the `VLLM_USE_RAY_*` family). **[S]** `:244–248` — these are\n**pass-through only**:\n\n```bash\nfor name in \"${TUNE_VARS[@]}\"; do\n  if [[ -v \"$name\" ]]; then\n    tune_env+=(\"$name=${!name}\")\n  fi\ndone\n```\n\nOnly forwarded if *already set* in the caller's environment; the script sets no\ndefaults for them. Good news for the campaign: **55 tuning knobs are reachable\nfrom outside without touching their code.**\n\n### 4.3 `modules/benchmark-*.nix` — NOT serving infrastructure\n\n**[S]** `modules/benchmark-executor.nix` (56 lines [M]) configures **Nix\ndistributed builds**: `nix.distributedBuilds = true`, `nix.buildMachines`,\n`settings.builders-use-substitutes` (`:44–48`), plus\n`programs.ssh.knownHosts` (`:50–54`). Line 1 comment: *\"Shared module for\nmachines that submit benchmark builds to remote runners.\"*\n\n**[S]** `modules/benchmark-runner.nix` (351 lines [M]) is the receiving side:\n`nix.settings.system-features` / `extra-sandbox-paths` / `sandbox = \"relaxed\"`\n(`:308–312`), a models tmpfiles rule (`:314–316`), a device-ACL activation\nscript (`:323–325`), and udev `setfacl` rules exposing `/dev/kfd`, `/dev/dri/*`,\n`/dev/infiniband/*`, `/dev/accel/*`, nvidia nodes to the build sandbox\n(`:327–349`). Two assertions at `:292–306` (IOMMU-off requirement; NPUs\nincompatible with IOMMU-off).\n\n**These modules run benchmarks *as Nix derivations in a build sandbox on one\nhost*.** They have nothing to do with the two-host Ray pair, which is\nSSH-orchestrated outside Nix entirely.\n\n### 4.4 `vllm-stream-client.py:297` — VERIFY AND RE-PIN\n\n**Campaign claim:** *\"assigns `prefill_mean_s` from the TTFT series, so the\nshipped harness cannot separate prefill from queueing.\"*\n\n**VERDICT: CONFIRMED, and the line number is still exactly 297.**\n\n**[M] File identity at time of reading:**\n\n```\n$ md5sum lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py\naadc50c1c653b8cc58571313ba1fe070\n\n$ wc -l -c lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py\n  374 11903\n```\n\n**[M] Last modification:** `git log -1 -- lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py` →\n`8db2c08  2026-06-12 09:03:59 +0200  \"bench: add Strix Halo two-host vLLM pair benchmark (#79)\"`.\n**The file has been touched exactly once, at introduction.** Line 297 has not\nmoved since the campaign recorded it, and there is no upstream fix in flight.\n\n**[S] The defect, `lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py:294–297`:**\n\n```python\n            \"ttft_mean_s\": fmt(statistics.mean(ttfts) if ttfts else None),\n            \"ttft_p50_s\": fmt(percentile(ttfts, 50)),\n            \"ttft_p95_s\": fmt(percentile(ttfts, 95)),\n            \"prefill_mean_s\": fmt(statistics.mean(ttfts) if ttfts else None),\n```\n\nLine 297 is **character-identical to line 294**. `prefill_mean_s` is not a\nmeasurement — it is a duplicate of `ttft_mean_s` under a different column name.\nBoth are `statistics.mean(ttfts)` over the same list built at `:254–257`.\n\n**[S] Why the number cannot be a prefill time — the measurement itself,\n`:144–145` and `:174–175`:**\n\n```python\n    barrier.wait()\n    start = time.perf_counter()\n...\n                        if first_token is None:\n                            first_token = time.perf_counter()\n```\n\nand `:182–183`:\n\n```python\n        ttft = None if first_token is None else first_token - start\n        decode = None if ttft is None else max(0.0, duration - ttft)\n```\n\n`start` is stamped **client-side immediately after a barrier release** (`:144`),\ni.e. when N concurrent workers are simultaneously unleashed. `first_token` is\nstamped when the **first non-empty SSE `text` chunk** arrives (`:171–176`). The\ninterval therefore contains: client-side request construction, network RTT,\n**vLLM scheduler queue wait**, prompt prefill, the first decode step, and SSE\nframing/flush. At `--concurrency 256` (`default.nix:106,113`) the queue term\ndominates and is not separable.\n\n**[S] The authors document this — the module docstring, `:4–7`:**\n\n```\nThis measures what the OpenAI-compatible completions API exposes directly:\ntime-to-first-token and total request time. In the CSV, prefill time is the\nTTFT proxy: it includes queueing plus prompt prefill plus the first decode\nstep. Decode time is total time after TTFT.\n```\n\n**Refined characterisation for the campaign record:** this is not a *bug* in the\nsense of an accident — it is a **knowingly-shipped proxy with a misleading\ncolumn name**. `prefill_mean_s` appears in `FIELDS` at `:52`, adjacent to the\nthree `ttft_*` columns at `:49–51`, so a CSV consumer reasonably reads it as an\nindependent measurement. It is not. **Any campaign conclusion that reads\n`prefill_mean_s` as prefill time is invalid at concurrency > 1.**\n\n**[S] Corroborating absence:** grepping the file for `prefill|ttft|queue`\nreturns **no** reference to vLLM's `/metrics` endpoint,\n`vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`, or any scheduler-queue histogram. The client\nnever queries server-side telemetry. Separating prefill from queueing requires\nscraping `/metrics` (or `--collect-detailed-traces`), which this harness does\nnot do.\n\n**Fix path that avoids copying their code [S]:** `default.nix:14` exposes\n`vllmStreamClient ? ../../lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py` as a package\nargument. The campaign can write its own client and `callPackage` the driver\nwith `vllmStreamClient = ./flashnext-stream-client.py;` — a clean parameter\noverride, no forking of the `.nix`, no copying of the `.py`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. THE FORBIDDEN TUNING MODULE\n\n**Identified: `nixosModules.tuning` → `modules/tuning.nix`.**\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:530` — `tuning = import ./modules/tuning.nix;`\n\n**[M]** The file is **33 lines**. **[S]** In full:\n\n```nix\n{ pkgs, ... }:\n{\n  boot = {\n    kernelParams = [\n      # GTT: 80GB (leaves ~30GB system RAM for OS/apps)\n      \"ttm.pages_limit=20971520\"\n    ];\n    tmp.useTmpfs = true;\n  };\n\n  hardware.firmware = [ pkgs.strix-halo-mes-firmware ];\n\n  services.tuned = {\n    enable = true;\n    profiles = {\n      strix-halo = {\n        main = {\n          include = \"accelerator-performance\";\n        };\n      };\n    };\n  };\n\n  systemd.services.tuned-set-profile = {\n    description = \"Set TuneD profile\";\n    after = [ \"tuned.service\" ];\n    wantedBy = [ \"multi-user.target\" ];\n    serviceConfig = {\n      Type = \"oneshot\";\n      ExecStart = \"${pkgs.tuned}/bin/tuned-adm profile accelerator-performance\";\n    };\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Why it is forbidden — four independent collisions\n\n**1. GTT sizing, unconditionally, with no option. [S] `modules/tuning.nix:4–7`.**\n\n`ttm.pages_limit=20971520` — **[M]** `20971520 × 4096 = 85,899,345,920 bytes =\nexactly 80 GiB`, matching the comment at `:5`. This is a bare\n`boot.kernelParams` **list append with no `mkDefault`, no `mkOption`, no\n`enable` flag.** NixOS merges `boot.kernelParams` by concatenation, so importing\nthis alongside a dotfiles module that also sets a GTT limit yields **two\n`ttm.pages_limit=` tokens on the same kernel command line**. The kernel takes the\nlast occurrence; ordering is a function of module merge order, i.e.\n**effectively nondeterministic from the operator's point of view.** No\nevaluation error will warn you — the build succeeds and the machine boots with\nwhichever value won. *This is precisely the \"would fight the dotfiles values\"\nfailure the Forbidden list anticipates, and it fails silently.*\n\n**[M] Repo-wide scan — this is the ONLY GTT/TTM setter in the tree:**\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'pages_limit\\|gttsize\\|ttm\\.\\|amdttm\\|GTT' --include='*.nix' --include='*.md' .\npkgs/amdtop/default.nix:39:      accel class. Reports per-core CPU history, VRAM/GTT pools, memory\nmodules/tuning.nix:5:      # GTT: 80GB (leaves ~30GB system RAM for OS/apps)\nmodules/tuning.nix:6:      \"ttm.pages_limit=20971520\"\n```\n\n`amdtop` is a description string. **`modules/tuning.nix:6` is the single\nsubstantive hit.** So the forbidden surface is small and precisely located — but\nit is also *unavoidable*: there is no way to import the module and decline the\nGTT parameter.\n\n> **Refinement worth recording:** the module sets **`ttm.pages_limit`**, not\n> `amdgpu.gttsize` and not `amdttm.pages_limit`. If the flashnext dotfiles set\n> GTT via a *different* knob, the two will not textually collide — they will\n> both apply, and the effective limit becomes the more restrictive of the two,\n> which is harder to diagnose than a duplicate. Worth confirming which knob the\n> dotfiles use before assuming \"no overlap = safe.\"\n\n**2. The 80 GiB figure encodes a 128 GB host. [S] `:5`** — *\"leaves ~30GB system\nRAM for OS/apps\"* implies 110 GB usable ≈ a 128 GB Strix Halo. On a differently\nconfigured host the number is simply wrong, and wrong in the direction that\nstarves the OS.\n\n**3. TuneD is enabled globally and forced with a oneshot. [S] `:13–32`.**\n`services.tuned.enable = true` (`:14`) plus a `systemd.services.tuned-set-profile`\nunit (`:24–32`) that runs `tuned-adm profile accelerator-performance` at every\nboot, `wantedBy = multi-user.target`. This **overrides any power/CPU-governor\npolicy** the dotfiles establish, at boot, every boot. If the dotfiles also\ndeclare `services.tuned`, that is a genuine option-merge conflict — this one at\nleast fails loudly.\n\n**4. Firmware injection. [S] `:11`** —\n`hardware.firmware = [ pkgs.strix-halo-mes-firmware ];`. Pulls an out-of-tree\nMES firmware blob into `/run/current-system/firmware`, changing GPU\ninitialisation. Also list-merged, also unconditional.\n\n### The trap: the module is *advertised* as safe to import\n\n**[S]** `README.md:196` — *\"`nixosModules.ec-su-axb35`, `ryzenadj`, `tuning` —\nStrix Halo hardware modules\"*, listed without caveat.\n\n**[S]** `examples/configuration.nix:17–25` — the shipped example **imports it**:\n\n```nix\n  imports = [\n    \"${modulesPath}/installer/cd-dvd/installation-cd-base.nix\"\n\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.default\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.tuning\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.ec-su-axb35\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.rpc-server\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.fastflowlm\n  ];\n```\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:459–462` — that file *is* `nixosConfigurations.live-iso`.\n\n**So the upstream-blessed pattern is exactly what the Forbidden list prohibits.**\nAnyone following the README or copying the example into a host closure imports\nthe GTT override by accident. **The Forbidden-list entry is well-founded and\nshould be enforced mechanically, not by convention** — e.g. an assertion in the\nflashnext host modules that `boot.kernelParams` contains at most one\n`ttm.pages_limit=` token.\n\n**Safe alternative [S]:** `nixosModules.default` (`flake.nix:514–521`) is inert\nwith respect to tuning — it only imports the thunderbolt-ibverbs module and\nappends `self.overlays.default` to `nixpkgs.overlays`. **That is the only module\nthe campaign needs to get `pkgs.vllm-rocm`.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. SERVING MODULES — is the pair topology expressible?\n\n### 6.1 What serving modules exist\n\n**[M] Decisive negative:**\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'vllm' modules/\nNO MATCH in modules/\n```\n\n**There is no vLLM NixOS module of any kind — no systemd unit, no container, no\noption namespace.** The string `vllm` does not appear anywhere under `modules/`.\n\n**[S]** The complete `nixosModules` set (`flake.nix:513–532`), classified:\n\n| Module | Kind | Serving? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `default` (`514–521`) | overlay application only | no |\n| `rpc-server` (`522`) | **systemd units — llama.cpp `rpc-server`** | yes, llama.cpp only |\n| `benchmark-executor` (`523`) | Nix distributed-build client | no |\n| `benchmark-runner` (`524`) | Nix builder capabilities + udev ACLs | no |\n| `ec-su-axb35` (`525`) | EC driver | no |\n| `fastflowlm` (`526`) | **systemd unit — FastFlowLM NPU server** | yes, NPU only |\n| `smu-exporter` (`527`) | Prometheus exporter | no |\n| `npu-exporter` (`528`) | Prometheus exporter | no |\n| `ryzenadj` (`529`) | power tuning | no |\n| `tuning` (`530`) | **FORBIDDEN — §5** | no |\n| `thunderbolt-ibverbs` (`531`) | re-export from the input | no |\n\n**Only two modules manage an inference server, and neither is vLLM:**\n\n- **[S]** `modules/rpc-server.nix` — `options.services.llama-cpp-rpc-servers`\n  (`:114–117`), `systemd.services = mapAttrs' …` (`:159–162`), unit description\n  *\"llama.cpp RPC server (${name})\"* (`:162`), `ExecStart` at `:170`. Options at\n  `:20–106`: `package`, `threads`, `device`, `host`, `port`, `enableCache`,\n  `cacheDirectory`, `extraArgs`, `environment`, `restart`, `user`, `group`,\n  `openFirewall`. **This is llama.cpp's RPC worker protocol — a completely\n  different distribution mechanism from vLLM+Ray.** Not reusable.\n- **[S]** `modules/fastflowlm.nix` — `mkEnableOption \"FastFlowLM NPU inference\n  server\"` (`:23`), `systemd.services.fastflowlm` (`:187`), `ExecStart` (`:202`),\n  `port` (`:59`), `openFirewall` (`:148`),\n  `networking.firewall.allowedTCPPorts` (`:270`). XDNA2 NPU, OpenAI-compatible.\n  **Structurally the closest precedent for a vLLM unit, but for different\n  hardware and a different binary.**\n\n### 6.2 Is the coordinator/worker pair expressible with them?\n\n**No. [S-derived]** The pair topology exists **only** as imperative shell:\n\n- Ray head start — `lib/bench/vllm-transport-matrix.sh:586–587`\n- Ray worker start **over `ssh`** — `:588–589`\n- `sleep 2` as the sole synchronisation — `:590`\n- `vllm serve --tensor-parallel-size 2 --distributed-executor-backend ray` — `:598–599`\n- teardown — `:162` (`ssh … \"$VLLM_ENV/bin/ray stop --force\"`)\n\nNothing in `nixosModules` models a Ray head, a Ray worker, a TP degree, a rail\nselection, or a coordinator/worker role. `benchmark-runner` and\n`benchmark-executor` (§4.3) look superficially like fleet modules but configure\n**Nix remote builders**, not runtime services.\n\n### 6.3 Conclusion for the campaign\n\n**flashnext must write its own systemd units.** Concretely, the repo gives:\n\n| Need | From nix-strix-halo | Campaign writes |\n|---|---|---|\n| ROCm vLLM package | **`pkgs.vllm-rocm`** via `nixosModules.default` [S `flake.nix:514–521`, `overlays/pkgs.rocm:332`] | — |\n| Ray in the same closure | pattern only — `symlinkJoin` [S `flake.nix:685–691`] | own env (a real `python.withPackages` is more robust than the symlinkJoin) |\n| Thunderbolt/USB4 RDMA rail | **`nixosModules.thunderbolt-ibverbs`** [S `flake.nix:531`], `pkgs.rdma-core-usb4` [S `flake.nix:320`] | — |\n| Ray head unit | nothing | **own unit** |\n| Ray worker unit | nothing | **own unit** (with a real readiness gate, not `sleep 2`) |\n| `vllm serve` TP=2 unit | nothing | **own unit** |\n| NCCL/RCCL env | **values readable** from `default.nix:223–239` and the 55-name `TUNE_VARS` at `vllm-transport-matrix.sh:188–243` [S] | own `serviceConfig.Environment` |\n| GTT / TuneD | **`nixosModules.tuning` — FORBIDDEN (§5)** | own, in dotfiles |\n\nGood news: the units are straightforward, and the *hard-won empirical content* —\nwhich NCCL knobs matter on this fabric — is plain data in files we may read and\nwhose **values** we may use. Reading a number and setting it in our own unit is\nnot copying their code.\n\n---\n\n## 7. FLAKE.LOCK AGES AND BUILDABILITY\n\n### 7.1 Age of each input\n\n**[M]** Computed from `flake.lock` `lastModified` against today (2026-08-28):\n\n| Input | Locked | Age |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nixpkgs` | **2026-07-18** | **41 days** |\n| `vllm-src` | **2026-07-12** | **47 days** |\n| `llama-cpp-master` | 2026-07-18 | 41 days |\n| `therock-src-…-root` (TheRock) | 2026-07-18 | 41 days |\n| `nix-ai-tools` | 2026-07-19 | 40 days |\n| `thunderbolt-ibverbs` | 2026-06-30 | 59 days |\n| `mlx-src` | 2026-07-16 | 43 days |\n| `fastflowlm` | 2026-07-17 | 42 days |\n| `ec-su-axb35` | 2026-07-11 | 48 days |\n| `llama-cpp-spacemit` | 2026-07-04 | 55 days |\n| `ds4` | 2026-06-17 | 72 days |\n| `ds4-hip` | 2026-06-04 | 85 days |\n| `xrt-src` / `xdna-driver-src` / `spine-triton` | 2026-06-04 | 85 days |\n\n[Showing lines 1-1150 of 1303 (50.0KB limit). Use offset=1151 to continue.]"}],"details":{"truncation":{"content":"# Dossier: `nix-strix-halo` (hellas-ai) as a flashnext flake INPUT\n\n**Target tree:** `/home/tom/Downloads/nix-strix-halo`\n**Read session date:** 2026-08-28\n**Grading:** `[S]` read from source · `[M]` measured (command output) · `[CL]` claimed by docs, unverified\n\n**Provenance pins (all [M], `git -C /home/tom/Downloads/nix-strix-halo …`):**\n\n```\nremote:  origin  https://github.com/hellas-ai/nix-strix-halo.git\nHEAD:    f0f2048ff842749b363ea3562a98fb0a04bb2e61  2026-08-18 10:03:06 +0200\n         \"npu-exporter: add AMD XDNA NPU Prometheus exporter (#162)\"\n```\n\nEvery line pin below is against this working tree at this commit. The tree is\nclean of local modifications for the files cited.\n\n---\n\n## 0. LICENSE VERIFICATION — the gating question\n\n**[M]** Two independent checks, both negative:\n\n```\n$ ls -la /home/tom/Downloads/nix-strix-halo | grep -iE 'licen|copying|legal'\n(no output, exit 1)\n\n$ find /home/tom/Downloads/nix-strix-halo -iname '*licen*' -o -iname 'COPYING*' -not -path './.git/*'\n(no output)\n```\n\n**[M]** Full file inventory (`find … -type f -not -path '*/.git/*'`) enumerated\nevery file in the tree. There is **no `LICENSE`, `LICENSE.md`, `COPYING`,\n`COPYRIGHT`, or `NOTICE` file anywhere**, at any depth.\n\n**[S]** `README.md` was grepped for `license|LICENSE` — **zero matches**. There is\nno license statement in prose either.\n\n**[S]** Individual `.nix` files carry no SPDX headers. The only license-adjacent\nmetadata is per-package `meta.maintainers` (e.g.\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:357` — `maintainers = with lib.maintainers; [ georgewhewell ]`),\nwhich is attribution, not a grant.\n\n### Consequence for the campaign\n\n| Use | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Declare as a flake input; consume `packages` / `overlays` / `nixosModules` / `lib` from the store path | **Fine.** No copying occurs; Nix fetches from the public repo. |\n| `import \"${inputs.nix-strix-halo}/overlays/therock-vllm.nix\" { … }` — importing a path *inside the input's store realisation* | **Fine.** Still input use, not vendoring. This is the escape hatch that matters (see §3). |\n| Copy any `.nix` file, patch, or `lib/bench/*.sh|*.py` into the flashnext tree | **Not allowed.** No LICENSE = all rights reserved by default. |\n| Copy the *shape* of an expression from memory after reading it | **Treat as copying.** Design around the input, do not transcribe. |\n\n**Practical rule for §3–§6:** every mechanism recommended below is expressed as\n*override / follows / import-from-store*, never as \"lift their expression.\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. FULL FLAKE SURFACE\n\n### 1.1 Inputs\n\n**[M]** `flake.lock` root node lists **48 inputs**. **[S]** `flake.nix:4–253`\ndeclares them. Structurally they are three groups:\n\n**Group A — real flakes (2):**\n\n| Input | `flake.nix` line | URL | Locked rev | Locked date [M] |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `nixpkgs` | `flake.nix:5` | `github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable` | `61b7c44c4073` | **2026-07-18** |\n| `nix-ai-tools` | `flake.nix:7–10` | `github:numtide/nix-ai-tools`, `inputs.nixpkgs.follows = \"nixpkgs\"` (`flake.nix:9`) | `26b51974319a` | 2026-07-19 |\n| `thunderbolt-ibverbs` | `flake.nix:57–60` | `github:hellas-ai/thunderbolt-ibverbs`, `inputs.nixpkgs.follows = \"nixpkgs\"` (`flake.nix:59`) | `76ba39b630a7` | 2026-06-30 |\n\n(Three, counting `thunderbolt-ibverbs`. It is the only input whose *outputs* —\noverlay + nixosModule + packages — are consumed; see `flake.nix:292`, `516`, `531`, `305`.)\n\n**Group B — hand-maintained source inputs, `flake = false` (13):**\n\n| Input | line | URL | Locked rev | Locked date [M] |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `ec-su-axb35` | `flake.nix:12–15` | `github:cmetz/ec-su_axb35-linux` | `7a9f372edcaa` | 2026-07-11 |\n| **`llama-cpp-master`** | **`flake.nix:17–20`** | `github:ggml-org/llama.cpp` (no ref → HEAD) | **`571d0d540df0`** | **2026-07-18** |\n| `llama-cpp-spacemit` | `flake.nix:22–25` | `github:spacemit-com/llama.cpp/v0.1.5` | `86d4ff230bdf` | 2026-07-04 |\n| `spine-triton` | `flake.nix:27–30` | `git+…spine-triton.git?ref=refs/tags/0.5.5&submodules=1` | `c62b11c5ea99` | 2026-06-04 |\n| `fastflowlm` | `flake.nix:32–35` | `github:FastFlowLM/FastFlowLM` | `fd371409897d` | 2026-07-17 |\n| `ds4` | `flake.nix:37–40` | `github:antirez/ds4` | `80ebbc396aee` | 2026-06-17 |\n| `ds4-hip` | `flake.nix:42–45` | `github:ejpir/ds4-hip/rocm-upstream-shape-cyberneurova` | `3490c2e46c91` | 2026-06-04 |\n| **`vllm-src`** | **`flake.nix:47–50`** | **`github:vllm-project/vllm/v0.25.1`** | **`752a3a504485`** | **2026-07-12** |\n| `mlx-src` | `flake.nix:52–55` | `github:NripeshN/mlx/rocm-support` | `0dadb703d773` | 2026-07-16 |\n| `xrt-src` | `flake.nix:62–65` | `git+…Xilinx/XRT?ref=XRT-2.21&submodules=1` | `8661761775a2` | 2026-06-04 |\n| `xdna-driver-src` | `flake.nix:67–70` | `git+…amd/xdna-driver?ref=1.7&submodules=1` | `0fb464f6e043` | 2026-06-04 |\n\n**Group C — machine-generated TheRock source tree (33 inputs), `flake = false`:**\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:72–73`:\n\n```nix\n# BEGIN generated TheRock source inputs\n# Generated by pkgs/therock/scripts/update-source-tree.py; do not edit by hand.\n```\n\nEvery input named `therock-src-7-15-gfx1151-*`. Root at `flake.nix:74–77`:\n`github:ROCm/TheRock/1de3171d00f6de55e9ed517dc6ca6e825d1e4b55`, locked\n**2026-07-18** [M]. The other 32 are submodule pins (rocm-libraries, rocm-systems,\nllvm-project, HIPIFY, perfetto, abseil, pybind11, …) — several extremely old\n(pybind11 2022-03-31, gulrak/filesystem 2023-03-05) because they are ROCm's own\nvendored third-party pins, not staleness introduced here.\n\n**[S]** `.github/dependabot.yml:14–19` explicitly *ignores* these:\n\n```yaml\n    ignore:\n      # TheRock source inputs are generated from pkgs/therock/sources/rocm-source.json\n      # and must be bumped atomically by pkgs/therock/scripts/update-source-tree.py.\n      - dependency-name: \"therock-src-*\"\n      # GitHub Dependabot's Nix updater does not currently support private inputs.\n      - dependency-name: \"thunderbolt-ibverbs\"\n```\n\n> **On the campaign record's \"stale 2026-07-18 llama-cpp pin\":** **CONFIRMED [M]**.\n> `llama-cpp-master` locks `571d0d540df0` at **2026-07-18**, 41 days before today.\n> Note the record's framing is slightly off in one respect: 2026-07-18 is not a\n> `llama-cpp`-specific staleness — it is the *whole lock's* watermark. `nixpkgs`\n> and the TheRock root are locked to the **same day**.\n\n### 1.2 Outputs\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:254–1119`. Systems: `flake.nix:262–264` —\n`linuxSystems = [ \"x86_64-linux\" ]`, `darwinSystems = [ \"aarch64-darwin\" ]`.\n\n| Output | line | Contents |\n|---|---|---|\n| `lib` | `466–500` | `defaultTherockSources`, `mkLiveIsoConfiguration`, `mkRocmTarget`, `rocmProviders`/`pythonProviders`/`pythonProviderStubs`, `therockTargets`, `bench`, and the four overlay builders **`mkRocmOverlay` (480), `mkPythonOverlay` (489), `mkPkgsOverlay` (490), `mkMtuneOverlay` (499)** |\n| `overlays` | `502–510` | `default` (`503–505`, `composeManyExtensions (mkOverlays { rocmTarget = defaultRocmTarget; })`), `python` (`506–508`), `spacemitK3` (`509`) |\n| `nixosModules` | `513–532` | `default`, `rpc-server`, `benchmark-executor`, `benchmark-runner`, `ec-su-axb35`, `fastflowlm`, `smu-exporter`, `npu-exporter`, `ryzenadj`, **`tuning` (530)**, `thunderbolt-ibverbs` |\n| `nixosConfigurations` | `534–536` | `live-iso` only |\n| `darwinModules` | `538–540` | `benchmark-executor` only |\n| `legacyPackages` | `545–553` | one pkgs set per `rocmTarget` (`gfx1010/1030/1036/1103/1151`) |\n| `benchmarks` | `555` | `perSystem mkBenchmarkSuites` (from `lib/bench`) |\n| `packages` | `557–757` | see below |\n| `apps` | `759–893` | wrappers over packages |\n| `checks` | `895–1083` | see below |\n| `devShells` | `1085–1111` | `default` only |\n| `formatter` | `1112` | `pkgs.nixfmt-tree` |\n\n**Notable packages [S]:** the Linux list is `flake.nix:617–645` (`vllm-rocm` at\n`642`), plus `flake.nix:712–715`:\n\n```nix\n\"strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench-${s}\" = pkgs.callPackage ./pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench {\n  vllmPackage = vllmPairBenchEnv;\n  packageSuffix = s;\n};\n```\n\nwhere `s = defaultRocmTarget.packageSuffix` (`flake.nix:561`) = `gfx1151`\n(`pkgs/therock/targets.nix:42,54`). So the concrete attr is\n`packages.x86_64-linux.strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench-gfx1151`.\n\n**`checks` [S]** (`x86_64-linux` only, guarded at `flake.nix:897`):\n`deadnix` (1058), `statix` (1059), `nixfmt` (1060–1062),\n`cuda-host-driver-runtime` (1063), `package-surface` (1064),\n`vllm-pair-bench-dry-run` (1065–1080).\n\nThe last is worth flagging for the campaign — **[S]** `flake.nix:1076–1079`:\n\n```\nbash -n ${./lib/bench/vllm-transport-matrix.sh}\npython3 -m py_compile ${./lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py}\nstrix-halo-vllm-pair-bench-ci --scenario qwen-peak --dry-run | tee \"$out\"\ngrep -q \"dry-run: not invoking\" \"$out\"\n```\n\nIt is a **syntax check plus a dry-run**, built against *fake* vLLM/RDMA/GCC\nstubs (`flake.nix:1021–1049`, comment at `1018–1020`: \"so CI validates the\nwrapper, matrix script, and client without the heavy ROCm closure or a real\ntwo-host lab\"). **No CI anywhere executes the harness against a real engine.**\nThis is the reason the §4 metrology bug survived: the only automated coverage is\n`py_compile`.\n\n### 1.3 How a downstream flake points `vllm-src` at a FORK — exact mechanism\n\nThere are **three** viable mechanisms; only the first two are license-clean and\nlow-friction. Facts first, then the ranking.\n\n**Facts establishing the wiring [S]:**\n\n`flake.nix:365–371` — the *only* place `vllm-src` is consumed:\n\n```nix\n(import ./overlays/therock-vllm.nix {\n  inherit lib therockPythonConfig;\n  target = rocmTarget;\n  vllmSrc = inputs.vllm-src;\n  vllmVersion = \"0.25.1\";\n  enabled = enableTherockVllm;\n})\n```\n\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:22–24` — the src is decorated before use:\n\n```nix\nvllmSrcWithTag = vllmSrc // {\n  tag = vllmSrc.tag or \"v${vllmVersion}\";\n};\n```\n\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:242–251` — src and version are substituted onto\nnixpkgs' vllm:\n\n```nix\n(py.vllm.override {\n  rocmSupport = true;\n  cudaSupport = false;\n  gpuTargets = vllmGpuTargets;\n  rocmPackages = therockRocmPackages;\n  inherit (py) amdsmi;\n}).overridePythonAttrs\n  (old: {\n    version = vllmVersion;\n    src = vllmSrcWithTag;\n```\n\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:363–365` — what the overlay actually publishes:\n\n```nix\nlib.optionalAttrs hasTherockVllmInputs {\n  \"vllm-rocm-therock-${s}\" = vllmTherock;\n}\n```\n\n`overlays/pkgs.nix:332` — the unsuffixed alias, resolved through `final`:\n\n```nix\n      vllm-rocm = final.\"vllm-rocm-therock-${suffix}\";\n```\n\nThe `final.` reference is the load-bearing detail: because the alias is a\nfixpoint reference, **a later overlay that redefines\n`vllm-rocm-therock-gfx1151` is automatically picked up by `vllm-rocm`.**\n\n---\n\n**Mechanism A — `follows` on the flake input (cleanest; recommended primary).**\n\nBecause `vllm-src` is a *top-level* input (`flake.nix:47–50`) and is threaded\nstraight through at `flake.nix:368`, a standard input override reaches it:\n\n```nix\ninputs.vllm-fork = {\n  url = \"github:<org>/vllm/<branch-with-PR53896+54129+patches>\";\n  flake = false;\n};\ninputs.nix-strix-halo.url = \"github:hellas-ai/nix-strix-halo\";\ninputs.nix-strix-halo.inputs.vllm-src.follows = \"vllm-fork\";\n```\n\n- **Reaches:** `src` of `vllm-rocm-therock-gfx1151`, hence `vllm-rocm`, hence\n  the `vllmPairBenchEnv` symlinkJoin (`flake.nix:685–691`) that feeds the bench\n  driver. One override moves the whole chain. **[S]**\n- **Does NOT reach:** the version string. `vllmVersion = \"0.25.1\"` is a **string\n  literal at `flake.nix:369`**, not an input. It survives into\n  `version = vllmVersion` (`therock-vllm.nix:250`) and\n  `VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE = vllmVersion` (`therock-vllm.nix:296`). The fork will\n  therefore *report itself as 0.25.1* regardless of what it actually is. **[S]**\n- **Second-order gotcha [S]:** `therock-vllm.nix:23` computes\n  `tag = vllmSrc.tag or \"v${vllmVersion}\"`. A GitHub-tag input carries a `tag`\n  attr; a branch/rev-pinned fork generally does not, so the fallback stamps\n  `v0.25.1` onto the fork's src attrset. Whether nixpkgs' vllm expression\n  *consumes* `src.tag` at the pinned nixpkgs rev is **UNDETERMINED** (see §3\n  caveat) — but `VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE` at `:296` is the belt to that\n  suspenders, so the practical risk is cosmetic version reporting, not a build\n  break.\n- **Verdict:** use this, and additionally pin the reported version via\n  Mechanism C so telemetry does not lie about what is running.\n\n**Mechanism B — re-import the overlay from the input's store path (full control).**\n\n`overlays/therock-vllm.nix` is a plain function of\n`{ lib, target, vllmSrc, vllmVersion, therockPythonConfig ? …, enabled ? true }`\n(`therock-vllm.nix:1–8`). Downstream can call it with its own arguments:\n\n```nix\nnixpkgs.overlays = [\n  nix-strix-halo.overlays.default\n  (import \"${nix-strix-halo}/overlays/therock-vllm.nix\" {\n    inherit (nixpkgs) lib;\n    target  = nix-strix-halo.lib.therockTargets.rocmTargetsBySuffix.gfx1151;\n    vllmSrc = inputs.vllm-fork;\n    vllmVersion = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\";   # honest version\n  })\n];\n```\n\n- Gets **both** `src` and `version` right. **[S]** — signature at `:1–8`,\n  target accessor at `flake.nix:473` (`therockTargets = therockTargetConfig`)\n  and `pkgs/therock/targets.nix:4,41,57`.\n- Later overlay wins for `vllm-rocm-therock-gfx1151`; `vllm-rocm` follows via\n  the `final.` alias at `overlays/pkgs.nix:332`. **[S]**\n- **License-clean**: `\"${nix-strix-halo}/overlays/therock-vllm.nix\"` is a path\n  into the fetched input, evaluated in place. Nothing is copied into the\n  flashnext tree.\n- **Cost:** couples flashnext to that file's internal argument names. If\n  upstream renames `vllmSrc`, evaluation breaks loudly (good) rather than\n  silently.\n\n**Mechanism C — `overridePythonAttrs` on the finished package (patch injection).**\n\n```nix\n(final: prev: {\n  vllm-rocm = prev.vllm-rocm.overridePythonAttrs (old: {\n    version = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\";\n    src = inputs.vllm-fork;\n    patches = (old.patches or [ ]) ++ [ ./patches/pr53896.patch ];\n    env = (old.env or { }) // { VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\"; };\n  });\n})\n```\n\nThis is the answer to \"can we inject `.patch` files without copying their nix\ncode\" — **yes**, see §3.\n\n**What is NOT available [S]:** there is no `overlays.therockVllm` and no\n`lib.mkTherockVllmOverlay`.\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'mkTherockVllmOverlay\\|therock-vllm' --include='*.nix' . | grep -v ./.git\nflake.nix:365:          (import ./overlays/therock-vllm.nix {\n```\n\n**One match, the internal call site.** Contrast with `lib` at `flake.nix:480–499`,\nwhich *does* export `mkRocmOverlay`/`mkPythonOverlay`/`mkPkgsOverlay`/`mkMtuneOverlay`.\n`README.md:184–185` [CL] confirms the intended composition set and likewise omits\nvLLM: *\"For non-default providers compose your own overlays with `lib.mkRocmOverlay`,\n`lib.mkPythonOverlay`, and `lib.mkPkgsOverlay`.\"* **The vLLM overlay is the one\npiece of the stack with no supported parameterisation point.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. `overlays/therock-*.nix` — ROCm version and Python dependency assembly\n\n### 2.1 Which TheRock / ROCm version\n\n**[S]** `pkgs/therock/sources/rocm.json:2–9`:\n\n```json\n  \"linux\": {\n    \"gfx1151\": {\n      \"url\": \"https://rocm.nightlies.amd.com/tarball-multi-arch/therock-dist-linux-gfx1151-7.15.0a20260719.tar.gz\",\n      \"hash\": \"sha256-04mWViYQujFAy+mbptQ8djGHgLFlmaQHFTfTW8LJ/MY=\",\n      \"version\": \"7.15.0a20260719\",\n```\n\n**ROCm `7.15.0a20260719` — an AMD *nightly*, not a release.** `gfx1030` at\n`rocm.json:10–16` pins the same nightly date.\n\n**[S]** `pkgs/therock/sources/rocm-source.json` (for the from-source path) pins\n`https://github.com/ROCm/TheRock.git`, `ref refs/heads/main`,\n`rev 1de3171d00f6de55e9ed517dc6ca6e825d1e4b55`, `\"version\": \"7.15\"`.\n\nTwo overlay files, trivially related:\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-rocm.nix` is **one line**:\n\n```nix\nargs: import ../pkgs/therock args\n```\n\n(`wc -l` = 1 [M]. A pure re-export; the substance is `pkgs/therock/default.nix`.)\n\n### 2.2 How Python deps for vLLM are assembled — **wheels, not source**\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-python.nix:19–24` installs a\n`pythonPackagesExtensions` entry, gated on interpreter version at `:22`\n(`lib.versions.majorMinor pyprev.python.version == pythonConfig.pythonVersion`),\nand binds `wheels = final.\"therock-python-wheels-${s}\"` at `:24`.\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-python.nix:53–61` — the substitution, the core fact:\n\n```nix\n          torch = wheels;\n          torchvision = wheels;\n          triton = wheels;\n          triton-no-cuda = wheels;\n          torchaudio = wheels;\n          rocm = wheels;\n          \"rocm-sdk-core\" = wheels;\n          \"rocm-sdk-devel\" = wheels;\n          \"rocm-sdk-libraries-${s}\" = wheels;\n```\n\n**torch / torchvision / torchaudio / triton are AMD-published prebuilt wheels**,\nall pointing at one derivation. **[S]**\n`pkgs/therock/sources/python-wheels.json` gives the URLs and hashes:\n\n- `torch-2.11.0+rocm7.15.0a20260719-cp313-cp313-linux_x86_64.whl`\n- `torchvision-0.26.0+rocm7.15.0a20260719-cp313-cp313-linux_x86_64.whl`\n- `torchaudio-2.11.0+rocm7.15.0a20260719-cp313-cp313-linux_x86_64.whl`\n- `triton-3.7.1+git0263a6a6.rocm7.15.0a20260719-cp313-…`\n- index: `https://rocm.nightlies.amd.com/whl-multi-arch/`, `\"pythonTag\": \"cp313\"`\n\n**[S]** `pkgs/therock/python-config.nix:4` — `pythonVersion = \"3.13\"`, giving\n`packagesAttr = \"python313Packages\"` (`:11`). **The entire stack is pinned to\nCPython 3.13.** Consistent with the `cp313` wheel tags.\n\n**vLLM itself is the exception — it is built from source.** `overlays/therock-vllm.nix:321`:\n\n```nix\n        requiredSystemFeatures = (old.requiredSystemFeatures or [ ]) ++ [ \"big-parallel\" ];\n```\n\nwith the comment at `:317–320`: *\"vllm-rocm compiles a few hundred HIP kernels\nthrough the TheRock toolchain — heavy enough to need a big-parallel builder.\"*\nSo: **prebuilt wheels for the torch/triton substrate, from-source HIP compile\nfor vLLM's own kernels.** This is the correct shape for the campaign (a fork's\nkernel changes will actually be compiled), but it means every fork bump pays a\nseveral-hundred-kernel HIP build.\n\nAdditional dependency surgery in the same overlay, all **[S]**:\n\n- `therock-vllm.nix:28–40` — `mistral-common` force-pinned to `1.11.2` from\n  PyPI, `doCheck = false` (`:39`).\n- `therock-vllm.nix:41–46` — Triton *kernels* fetched **separately** from\n  `triton-lang/triton` rev `0263a6a6203cf27c441c57a6c808ea87ffb8f654`, surfaced\n  via `TRITON_KERNELS_SRC_DIR` (`:299`). Note this rev matches the\n  `+git0263a6a6` in the triton wheel tag — deliberately coherent.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:47–60` — a `withSetuptools80` shim swapping `setuptools` for\n  `py.setuptools_80` in grpcio-tools / setuptools-scm / setuptools-rust.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:63–90` — `therockRocmPackages`: **26 ROCm component attrs\n  (`clr`, `hipblaslt`, `rccl`, `composable_kernel`, …) all aliased to the single\n  `sdk` derivation** (`:15–20`), which is `therock-rocm-gfx1151` with\n  `localGpuTargets`/`gpuTargets` forced to the target's build targets.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:92–114` — **`dropVllmDependencyNames`, 22 entries** stripped\n  from vLLM's dependency closure: `amd-quark`, `apache-tvm-ffi`, `bitsandbytes`,\n  `conch-triton-kernels`, `datasets`, `fastsafetensors`, `mistral-common`,\n  `mistral_common`, `mistralai`, `opencv-python-headless`, `outlines`, `peft`,\n  `pyarrow`, `pytest-asyncio`, `runai-model-streamer`, `runai_model_streamer`,\n  `tensorizer`, `tilelang`, `timm`, `torchcodec`, `xformers`.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:126–136` — `unsupportedFeatureReasons`, 9 features hard-refused\n  with explanations. **Directly relevant to the campaign:** `aiter` is refused\n  (`:127`, *\"AITer needs a separately packaged ROCm aiter build and upstream only\n  enables it on MI300-class targets\"*) and `rixl` is refused (`:132`, *\"RIXL needs\n  separate ROCm RIXL/UCX/RDMA packaging and is only used by KV-transfer/disaggregated\n  serving paths\"*). Enabling either trips the assertion at `:239–241`.\n- `therock-vllm.nix:234–238` — three hard asserts: `tritonSupport`,\n  `tritonKernelsSupport`, and `otelSupport` **must all be true**.\n\n### 2.3 What would need to change for ROCm 10\n\n**No single knob.** Five coordinated edits, all **[S]** on file structure:\n\n1. **`pkgs/therock/sources/rocm.json`** — `url`/`hash`/`version` per target.\n   Regenerated by `pkgs/therock/scripts/update-rocm.py`; **[S]** its docstring\n   (`:3–8`) says *\"The TheRock nightly index is intentionally kept out of Nix\n   evaluation. Run this script manually when bumping the opt-in preview SDK,\n   review the diff, then commit the updated JSON.\"* `BASE_URL` at `:20` is\n   `https://rocm.nightlies.amd.com/tarball-multi-arch/`. **A ROCm 10 tarball must\n   exist at that nightly index under a matching target slug** (`TARGET_SLUGS`,\n   `:21–28`) or the script has nothing to find.\n2. **`pkgs/therock/sources/python-wheels.json`** — the torch/triton/vision/audio\n   wheel set must be *republished by AMD for ROCm 10 at cp313*. If AMD moves the\n   nightly wheels to cp314, `python-config.nix:4` must change too, which\n   re-resolves `python313Packages` → `python314Packages` across the whole\n   overlay stack (`therock-vllm.nix:21`, `flake.nix:689`, …).\n3. **`pkgs/therock/sources/rocm-source.json` + the 33 generated `flake.nix`\n   inputs (`flake.nix:74–~250`)** — regenerated *atomically* by\n   `pkgs/therock/scripts/update-source-tree.py` (per the banner at\n   `flake.nix:72–73` and the dependabot ignore at `.github/dependabot.yml:17`).\n   Hand-editing these is explicitly out of bounds.\n4. **`pkgs/therock/rocm-modules/*`** — ~60 module dirs carrying **~40 patches**\n   against ROCm internals (`therock/rocm-from-source/patches/*.patch`,\n   `rocm-modules/*/*.patch|*.diff`). A major-version ROCm bump is exactly where\n   these rot.\n5. **`overlays/therock-vllm.nix:269`** — `substituteInPlace pyproject.toml\n   --replace-fail '\"torch == 2.11.0\"' '\"torch\"'`. **`--replace-fail` means this\n   aborts the build if the exact string is absent.** A ROCm 10 wheel set will\n   ship a different torch version and vLLM will pin differently; this line is a\n   guaranteed break. The same brittleness applies to every other\n   `--replace-fail` in `postPatch` (`:259`, `:263`, `:268`, `:271`, `:279`, `:288`).\n\n**Assessment [S-derived]:** ROCm 10 is a *fork-and-maintain* undertaking against\nthis repo, not a config change. The campaign should assume the pinned ROCm\n7.15.0a20260719 nightly for its horizon and treat ROCm 10 as out of scope.\n\n---\n\n## 3. THE vLLM PACKAGE EXPRESSION\n\n### 3.1 How it is built\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-vllm.nix:242–248` — it is **not** a fresh expression.\nIt takes **nixpkgs' `python313Packages.vllm`**, applies `.override` for the ROCm\nknobs, then `.overridePythonAttrs` for everything else:\n\n```nix\n    (py.vllm.override {\n      rocmSupport = true;\n      cudaSupport = false;\n      gpuTargets = vllmGpuTargets;\n      rocmPackages = therockRocmPackages;\n      inherit (py) amdsmi;\n    }).overridePythonAttrs\n      (old: {\n        version = vllmVersion;\n        src = vllmSrcWithTag;\n```\n\n`vllmGpuTargets = target.buildTargets` (`:16`). For gfx1151,\n`pkgs/therock/targets.nix:41–52` sets `packageSuffix = \"gfx1151\"`,\n`hsaOverride = \"11.5.1\"`, and `rocmGpuTargets = [ \"gfx1151\" \"gfx90a\" ]` with the\ncomment at `:46–47` that gfx90a is *\"a composable_kernel build workaround\"*.\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:685–691` — how the bench driver gets a vLLM *with Ray*, and\nthe reasoning:\n\n```nix\n          # Two-host vLLM transport-matrix driver. The driver only needs the\n          # vLLM closure to expose both `vllm` and `ray`, so join the default\n          # ROCm vLLM package with ray rather than baking ray into vllm-rocm.\n          vllmPairBenchEnv = pkgs.symlinkJoin {\n            name = \"vllm-rocm-${s}-ray-env\";\n            paths = [\n              pkgs.vllm-rocm\n              pkgs.${therockPythonConfig.packagesAttr}.ray\n            ];\n          };\n```\n\n**This is a `symlinkJoin`, not a shared Python environment.** Ray and vLLM are\ntwo independently-built closures whose `bin/` and `lib/python3.13/site-packages/`\ntrees are symlink-merged. It works because both resolve their own deps through\ntheir own wrappers — but it is a fragile seam if the campaign needs Ray and vLLM\nto agree on a shared transitive dep version.\n\n### 3.2 Default version\n\n**`0.25.1`**, from **two independent places that must agree**:\n\n- **[S]** `flake.nix:48` — `url = \"github:vllm-project/vllm/v0.25.1\"` (the source)\n- **[S]** `flake.nix:369` — `vllmVersion = \"0.25.1\"` (the string)\n\nThese are **not coupled**. Nothing asserts the src tag matches the version\nstring. Overriding one via `follows` silently desynchronises them (§1.3\nMechanism A).\n\nConsumed at `therock-vllm.nix:250` (`version`), `:296`\n(`VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE`), and `:23` (`tag` fallback).\n\n> **[CL] Doc/source contradiction:** `README.md:22` states\n> `| `vllm-rocm` | source-built vLLM 0.23 against TheRock |`. The flake says\n> **0.25.1**. The README table is stale by two minor versions. **Trust\n> `flake.nix:48,369`; do not quote the README.**\n\n### 3.3 Patches applied\n\n**[S]** `overlays/therock-vllm.nix:253–255` — the overlay **adds zero patches**.\nIt only *subtracts* one:\n\n```nix\n        patches = builtins.filter (\n          patch: !(lib.hasSuffix \"0006-drop-rocm-extra-reqs.patch\" (toString patch))\n        ) (old.patches or [ ]);\n```\n\nSo the applied set is *nixpkgs' vllm patch list, minus `0006`*.\n\n**[S-local, indicative — see caveat]** Reading the **local system nixpkgs**\n(`/nix/store/llgwlxshmy0ifvxh7f8wq53vk5x7vd13-source`, `.version` = `26.11`),\n`pkgs/development/python-modules/vllm/default.nix:348–353`:\n\n```nix\n  patches = [\n    ./0002-setup.py-nix-support-respect-cmakeFlags.patch\n    ./0003-propagate-pythonpath.patch\n    ./0005-drop-intel-reqs.patch\n    ./0006-drop-rocm-extra-reqs.patch\n  ];\n```\n\nand `:338` — `version = \"0.16.0\"`.\n\n> **CAVEAT (important):** that store path is **this machine's system nixpkgs, not\n> the flake's pinned `61b7c44c4073` (2026-07-18)**. The exact patch list at the\n> pinned rev is **UNDETERMINED**. What would settle it: `nix flake archive` /\n> `nix build .#…vllm.src` against the pinned rev, or reading\n> `pkgs/development/python-modules/vllm/default.nix` at `61b7c44c4073`. I did not\n> fetch it (analysis-only, no builds). The *filter target* — a file literally\n> named `0006-drop-rocm-extra-reqs.patch` — is present in both, so the mechanism\n> is sound; only the exact surviving list is uncertain.\n\n**[S-derived] The structural hazard the campaign must price in:** nixpkgs' vllm\nis **0.16.0**; the overlay swaps in src **0.25.1** — a **nine-minor-version\njump** — while *inheriting 0.16-era patches*. Those three patches must still\napply cleanly to a 0.25.1 tree. They evidently do today (the repo builds), but a\nfork moving further from 0.25.1 raises the odds of a patch-apply failure that\nwill read as a confusing \"hunk FAILED\" in a file nobody in the campaign wrote.\n\n**[S] `postPatch` is REPLACED, not appended** — `therock-vllm.nix:257`:\n\n```nix\n        postPatch = ''\n```\n\nPlain assignment. Not `(old.postPatch or \"\") + …`. Comparing against local\nnixpkgs `:356+` (`rm vllm/third_party/pynvml.py`, the same two\n`substituteInPlace` calls), the overlay's block at `:258–266` **re-implements\nnixpkgs' postPatch verbatim** and then adds four more edits (`:268–292`). This is\ndeliberate supersession, but it means **any downstream `overridePythonAttrs` that\nassigns `postPatch` rather than appending will silently drop all of it** —\nincluding the `registry.py` env fix at `:288–291`, whose comment (`:284–287`)\nwarns it is load-bearing: *\"Upstream replaces its entire environment with\nPYTHONPATH, which strips the TheRock wheel runtime's LD_LIBRARY_PATH and makes\ntorch fail to load libstdc++ before an architecture can even be inspected.\"*\n\n### 3.4 Is a patch-overlay list exposed? — **NO, but injection works anyway**\n\n**[S]** `mkVllmTherock`'s complete signature, `therock-vllm.nix:138–156`:\n\n```nix\n  mkVllmTherock =\n    {\n      aiterSupport ? false,\n      audioSupport ? true,\n      benchSupport ? true,\n      fastsafetensorsSupport ? false,\n      flashinferSupport ? false,\n      grpcSupport ? false,\n      helionSupport ? false,\n      instanttensorSupport ? false,\n      otelSupport ? true,\n      rixlSupport ? false,\n      runaiSupport ? false,\n      tensorizerSupport ? false,\n      tritonSupport ? true,\n      tritonKernelsSupport ? true,\n      videoSupport ? false,\n      zenSupport ? false,\n    }:\n```\n\n**Sixteen parameters, all booleans, all feature toggles.** No `extraPatches`, no\n`patches`, no `src`, no `version`, no `postPatch` hook.\n\n**[S]** `therock-vllm.nix:361`:\n\n```nix\n  vllmTherock = lib.makeOverridable mkVllmTherock { };\n```\n\nSo `pkgs.vllm-rocm.override { … }` accepts **only those 16 booleans** — and 9 of\nthem are hard-refused by the assertion at `:239–241`.\n\n**ANSWER TO THE CAMPAIGN'S QUESTION — yes, `.patch` files can be injected\nwithout copying any of their Nix code**, via `overridePythonAttrs` on the\nfinished derivation. The result of `mkVllmTherock` is an ordinary\n`buildPythonPackage` derivation, so:\n\n```nix\n(final: prev: {\n  vllm-rocm = prev.vllm-rocm.overridePythonAttrs (old: {\n    src      = inputs.vllm-fork;\n    version  = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\";\n    patches  = (old.patches or [ ]) ++ [\n      ./patches/pr-53896.patch\n      ./patches/pr-54129.patch\n    ];\n    postPatch = (old.postPatch or \"\") + ''\n      # append only — never assign, see §3.3\n    '';\n    env = (old.env or { }) // { VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE = \"0.26.0.dev-flashnext\"; };\n  });\n})\n```\n\nThree rules for the campaign, each grounded above:\n\n1. **Always `(old.patches or [ ]) ++ …`** — assignment discards the inherited\n   nixpkgs patches the build depends on.\n2. **Always `(old.postPatch or \"\") + …`** — `therock-vllm.nix:257` assigns, and\n   `:288–291` is load-bearing.\n3. **Set `VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE` alongside `version`** — `therock-vllm.nix:296`\n   is what the running server reports. Changing `version` alone leaves the\n   process announcing 0.25.1.\n\n**Recommended combination:** Mechanism A (`follows`, for the fork src, so the\nwhole chain including `vllmPairBenchEnv` moves) **+** Mechanism C\n(`overridePythonAttrs`, for `.patch` files and an honest version). Mechanism B\nis the fallback if the campaign needs a genuinely different `target`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. `strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench` + `modules/benchmark-*.nix`\n\n### 4.1 What the two-host TP=2 harness actually does\n\n**[S]** `pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench/default.nix:25` — it is a\n`writeShellApplication`, i.e. **a bash script, not a NixOS service**. Runtime\ninputs at `:28–33`: `coreutils`, `openssh`, `rsync`, `nixVersions.stable`.\n\nFlow, all **[S]**:\n\n1. Parse `--scenario` + overrides (`:84–100`).\n2. Select the scenario's model/transport/concurrency block (`:102–158`).\n3. Preflight six required paths (`:166–175`) — `$GCC_PREFIX/bin/gcc`,\n   `$RDMA/lib`, `$VLLM_ENV/bin/vllm`, `$VLLM_ENV/bin/ray`, and both bench\n   scripts. Missing → `exit 2`.\n4. Emit a ~40-line `REMOTE_ENV` heredoc (`:199–242`).\n5. `--dry-run` short-circuits at `:248–251` printing `(dry-run: not invoking)` —\n   the exact string CI greps for (`flake.nix:1079`).\n6. **`nix copy --no-check-sigs --to \"ssh://$host\"`** the four closures to *both*\n   hosts (`:263–267`).\n7. `scp` the env file to the master, then\n   **`ssh \"$MASTER\" \"set -a; . …/env; set +a; exec bash $bench_script_q\"`** (`:279`).\n8. `rsync` results back (`:283`).\n\n**The actual TP=2 topology lives in `lib/bench/vllm-transport-matrix.sh`** (691\nlines [M]). **[S]** `:568–606`:\n\n- `transport == \"solo\"` (`:568–578`): plain single-host `vllm serve` with\n  `NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1`.\n- otherwise (`:579–605`), the pair path:\n  - **`:586–587`** — `ray start --head --port=6379 --node-ip-address=\"$head\" --num-gpus=1 --include-dashboard=false --disable-usage-stats` on the **coordinator**.\n  - **`:588–589`** — one `ssh -n` invocation starting `ray start --address=$head:6379 --node-ip-address=$worker --num-gpus=1` on the **worker**.\n  - **`:590`** — `sleep 2`. A fixed sleep, not a readiness probe.\n  - **`:592–605`** — `vllm serve \"$model\" --tensor-parallel-size 2 --distributed-executor-backend ray --host 0.0.0.0 --port \"$PORT\"`.\n\nSo: **the \"pair / coordinator-worker over TCP rail\" topology is a Ray cluster\nstood up by SSH from a shell script.** Each node advertises `--num-gpus=1`; vLLM\nshards TP=2 across them via the Ray executor backend.\n\n**[S]** `:626–641` — the client is then invoked against **`http://127.0.0.1:$PORT/v1/completions`**,\ni.e. the coordinator's own loopback.\n\n**[S]** `:619–624` — the four transports:\n\n```\n    lan_tcp)    socket_ifname=\"$LAN_IFNAME/$REMOTE_LAN_IFNAME\" ;;\n    usb4_rdma)  socket_ifname=\"$LAN_IFNAME/$REMOTE_LAN_IFNAME\"; rdma_hca=$(resolve_usb4_hca) ;;\n    tb_tcp)     socket_ifname=\"$TB_IFNAME/$REMOTE_TB_IFNAME\" ;;\n    tb_rxe)     socket_ifname=\"$TB_IFNAME/$REMOTE_TB_IFNAME\"; rdma_hca=$RXE_HCA ;;\n```\n\n### 4.2 Config surface\n\n**[S] CLI flags** — `default.nix:86–97`: `--scenario`, `--master`, `--worker`,\n`--usb4-hca`, `--out`, `--dry-run`, `--extra-transports`, `--vllm-env`,\n`--rdma-prefix`, `--gcc-prefix`, `--bench-dir`, `-h|--help`.\n\n**[S] Nix-level args** — `default.nix:1–15`: `vllmPackage` and `packageSuffix`\nare required; **`vllmTransportMatrix` (`:13`) and `vllmStreamClient` (`:14`) are\noverridable path arguments**, defaulting to `../../lib/bench/*`. **This is the\nclean seam for §4.4** — the campaign can `callPackage` the driver with its own\ncorrected client, no forking of the Nix expression.\n\n**[S] Hardcoded defaults that are hellas lab facts, not ours** — `default.nix:39–41`:\n\n```\n    MASTER=\"grw@strix-1.lan.satanic.link\"\n    WORKER=\"grw@strix-2.lan.satanic.link\"\n    USB4_HCA=\"usb4_rdma0,usb4_rdma1,usb4_rdma5,usb4_rdma6\"\n```\n\nand `:204–205` — `LAN_IFNAME=br0.lan`, `TB_IFNAME=thunderbolt0`. `--master` /\n`--worker` / `--usb4-hca` are overridable; **`LAN_IFNAME` and `TB_IFNAME` are\nnot exposed as flags** — they are baked into the `REMOTE_ENV` heredoc. Changing\nthem requires either `--bench-dir` with a modified script or setting them in the\nenvironment ahead of the matrix script (which reads `${VAR:-default}`).\n\n**[S] Five scenarios** (`default.nix:102–158`): `qwen-peak`,\n`llama-tp2-win`, `qwen35-122b-awq-capacity`, `qwen35-122b-awq-prime`,\n`minimax-m27-awq-strix-2h`. The last (`:141–153`) is the interesting one for a\nlarge-model campaign: `MAX_MODEL_LEN=196608`, `MAX_NUM_SEQS=2`,\n`MAX_NUM_BATCHED_TOKENS=20480`, `--gpu-memory-utilization 0.92`, and\n`SCENARIO_EXTRA_ENV` (`:151`) setting `VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER=0`,\n`TORCHDYNAMO_DISABLE=1`, `VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM=0`, `RAY_CGRAPH_get_timeout=1800`, etc.\n\n**[S] Baseline `REMOTE_ENV`** (`default.nix:223–239`) fixes NCCL/RCCL and Ray\nknobs: `NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX=1`, `NCCL_NET_MERGE_LEVEL=LOC`,\n`NCCL_MIN_NCHANNELS=4`/`NCCL_MAX_NCHANNELS=4`,\n`NCCL_IB_QPS_PER_CONNECTION=1`, `NCCL_IB_SPLIT_DATA_ON_QPS=0`,\n`CUDA_/HIP_/ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0`, the three\n`RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_*_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1`, and\n`VLLM_USE_RAY_V2_EXECUTOR_BACKEND=0`.\n\n**[S]** `vllm-transport-matrix.sh:188–243` declares **`TUNE_VARS`, 55 names**\n(NCCL/RCCL chunk & channel sizing, `HSA_NO_SCRATCH_RECLAIM`, `HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT`,\nGDRCopy, the `VLLM_USE_RAY_*` family). **[S]** `:244–248` — these are\n**pass-through only**:\n\n```bash\nfor name in \"${TUNE_VARS[@]}\"; do\n  if [[ -v \"$name\" ]]; then\n    tune_env+=(\"$name=${!name}\")\n  fi\ndone\n```\n\nOnly forwarded if *already set* in the caller's environment; the script sets no\ndefaults for them. Good news for the campaign: **55 tuning knobs are reachable\nfrom outside without touching their code.**\n\n### 4.3 `modules/benchmark-*.nix` — NOT serving infrastructure\n\n**[S]** `modules/benchmark-executor.nix` (56 lines [M]) configures **Nix\ndistributed builds**: `nix.distributedBuilds = true`, `nix.buildMachines`,\n`settings.builders-use-substitutes` (`:44–48`), plus\n`programs.ssh.knownHosts` (`:50–54`). Line 1 comment: *\"Shared module for\nmachines that submit benchmark builds to remote runners.\"*\n\n**[S]** `modules/benchmark-runner.nix` (351 lines [M]) is the receiving side:\n`nix.settings.system-features` / `extra-sandbox-paths` / `sandbox = \"relaxed\"`\n(`:308–312`), a models tmpfiles rule (`:314–316`), a device-ACL activation\nscript (`:323–325`), and udev `setfacl` rules exposing `/dev/kfd`, `/dev/dri/*`,\n`/dev/infiniband/*`, `/dev/accel/*`, nvidia nodes to the build sandbox\n(`:327–349`). Two assertions at `:292–306` (IOMMU-off requirement; NPUs\nincompatible with IOMMU-off).\n\n**These modules run benchmarks *as Nix derivations in a build sandbox on one\nhost*.** They have nothing to do with the two-host Ray pair, which is\nSSH-orchestrated outside Nix entirely.\n\n### 4.4 `vllm-stream-client.py:297` — VERIFY AND RE-PIN\n\n**Campaign claim:** *\"assigns `prefill_mean_s` from the TTFT series, so the\nshipped harness cannot separate prefill from queueing.\"*\n\n**VERDICT: CONFIRMED, and the line number is still exactly 297.**\n\n**[M] File identity at time of reading:**\n\n```\n$ md5sum lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py\naadc50c1c653b8cc58571313ba1fe070\n\n$ wc -l -c lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py\n  374 11903\n```\n\n**[M] Last modification:** `git log -1 -- lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py` →\n`8db2c08  2026-06-12 09:03:59 +0200  \"bench: add Strix Halo two-host vLLM pair benchmark (#79)\"`.\n**The file has been touched exactly once, at introduction.** Line 297 has not\nmoved since the campaign recorded it, and there is no upstream fix in flight.\n\n**[S] The defect, `lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py:294–297`:**\n\n```python\n            \"ttft_mean_s\": fmt(statistics.mean(ttfts) if ttfts else None),\n            \"ttft_p50_s\": fmt(percentile(ttfts, 50)),\n            \"ttft_p95_s\": fmt(percentile(ttfts, 95)),\n            \"prefill_mean_s\": fmt(statistics.mean(ttfts) if ttfts else None),\n```\n\nLine 297 is **character-identical to line 294**. `prefill_mean_s` is not a\nmeasurement — it is a duplicate of `ttft_mean_s` under a different column name.\nBoth are `statistics.mean(ttfts)` over the same list built at `:254–257`.\n\n**[S] Why the number cannot be a prefill time — the measurement itself,\n`:144–145` and `:174–175`:**\n\n```python\n    barrier.wait()\n    start = time.perf_counter()\n...\n                        if first_token is None:\n                            first_token = time.perf_counter()\n```\n\nand `:182–183`:\n\n```python\n        ttft = None if first_token is None else first_token - start\n        decode = None if ttft is None else max(0.0, duration - ttft)\n```\n\n`start` is stamped **client-side immediately after a barrier release** (`:144`),\ni.e. when N concurrent workers are simultaneously unleashed. `first_token` is\nstamped when the **first non-empty SSE `text` chunk** arrives (`:171–176`). The\ninterval therefore contains: client-side request construction, network RTT,\n**vLLM scheduler queue wait**, prompt prefill, the first decode step, and SSE\nframing/flush. At `--concurrency 256` (`default.nix:106,113`) the queue term\ndominates and is not separable.\n\n**[S] The authors document this — the module docstring, `:4–7`:**\n\n```\nThis measures what the OpenAI-compatible completions API exposes directly:\ntime-to-first-token and total request time. In the CSV, prefill time is the\nTTFT proxy: it includes queueing plus prompt prefill plus the first decode\nstep. Decode time is total time after TTFT.\n```\n\n**Refined characterisation for the campaign record:** this is not a *bug* in the\nsense of an accident — it is a **knowingly-shipped proxy with a misleading\ncolumn name**. `prefill_mean_s` appears in `FIELDS` at `:52`, adjacent to the\nthree `ttft_*` columns at `:49–51`, so a CSV consumer reasonably reads it as an\nindependent measurement. It is not. **Any campaign conclusion that reads\n`prefill_mean_s` as prefill time is invalid at concurrency > 1.**\n\n**[S] Corroborating absence:** grepping the file for `prefill|ttft|queue`\nreturns **no** reference to vLLM's `/metrics` endpoint,\n`vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`, or any scheduler-queue histogram. The client\nnever queries server-side telemetry. Separating prefill from queueing requires\nscraping `/metrics` (or `--collect-detailed-traces`), which this harness does\nnot do.\n\n**Fix path that avoids copying their code [S]:** `default.nix:14` exposes\n`vllmStreamClient ? ../../lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py` as a package\nargument. The campaign can write its own client and `callPackage` the driver\nwith `vllmStreamClient = ./flashnext-stream-client.py;` — a clean parameter\noverride, no forking of the `.nix`, no copying of the `.py`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. THE FORBIDDEN TUNING MODULE\n\n**Identified: `nixosModules.tuning` → `modules/tuning.nix`.**\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:530` — `tuning = import ./modules/tuning.nix;`\n\n**[M]** The file is **33 lines**. **[S]** In full:\n\n```nix\n{ pkgs, ... }:\n{\n  boot = {\n    kernelParams = [\n      # GTT: 80GB (leaves ~30GB system RAM for OS/apps)\n      \"ttm.pages_limit=20971520\"\n    ];\n    tmp.useTmpfs = true;\n  };\n\n  hardware.firmware = [ pkgs.strix-halo-mes-firmware ];\n\n  services.tuned = {\n    enable = true;\n    profiles = {\n      strix-halo = {\n        main = {\n          include = \"accelerator-performance\";\n        };\n      };\n    };\n  };\n\n  systemd.services.tuned-set-profile = {\n    description = \"Set TuneD profile\";\n    after = [ \"tuned.service\" ];\n    wantedBy = [ \"multi-user.target\" ];\n    serviceConfig = {\n      Type = \"oneshot\";\n      ExecStart = \"${pkgs.tuned}/bin/tuned-adm profile accelerator-performance\";\n    };\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Why it is forbidden — four independent collisions\n\n**1. GTT sizing, unconditionally, with no option. [S] `modules/tuning.nix:4–7`.**\n\n`ttm.pages_limit=20971520` — **[M]** `20971520 × 4096 = 85,899,345,920 bytes =\nexactly 80 GiB`, matching the comment at `:5`. This is a bare\n`boot.kernelParams` **list append with no `mkDefault`, no `mkOption`, no\n`enable` flag.** NixOS merges `boot.kernelParams` by concatenation, so importing\nthis alongside a dotfiles module that also sets a GTT limit yields **two\n`ttm.pages_limit=` tokens on the same kernel command line**. The kernel takes the\nlast occurrence; ordering is a function of module merge order, i.e.\n**effectively nondeterministic from the operator's point of view.** No\nevaluation error will warn you — the build succeeds and the machine boots with\nwhichever value won. *This is precisely the \"would fight the dotfiles values\"\nfailure the Forbidden list anticipates, and it fails silently.*\n\n**[M] Repo-wide scan — this is the ONLY GTT/TTM setter in the tree:**\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'pages_limit\\|gttsize\\|ttm\\.\\|amdttm\\|GTT' --include='*.nix' --include='*.md' .\npkgs/amdtop/default.nix:39:      accel class. Reports per-core CPU history, VRAM/GTT pools, memory\nmodules/tuning.nix:5:      # GTT: 80GB (leaves ~30GB system RAM for OS/apps)\nmodules/tuning.nix:6:      \"ttm.pages_limit=20971520\"\n```\n\n`amdtop` is a description string. **`modules/tuning.nix:6` is the single\nsubstantive hit.** So the forbidden surface is small and precisely located — but\nit is also *unavoidable*: there is no way to import the module and decline the\nGTT parameter.\n\n> **Refinement worth recording:** the module sets **`ttm.pages_limit`**, not\n> `amdgpu.gttsize` and not `amdttm.pages_limit`. If the flashnext dotfiles set\n> GTT via a *different* knob, the two will not textually collide — they will\n> both apply, and the effective limit becomes the more restrictive of the two,\n> which is harder to diagnose than a duplicate. Worth confirming which knob the\n> dotfiles use before assuming \"no overlap = safe.\"\n\n**2. The 80 GiB figure encodes a 128 GB host. [S] `:5`** — *\"leaves ~30GB system\nRAM for OS/apps\"* implies 110 GB usable ≈ a 128 GB Strix Halo. On a differently\nconfigured host the number is simply wrong, and wrong in the direction that\nstarves the OS.\n\n**3. TuneD is enabled globally and forced with a oneshot. [S] `:13–32`.**\n`services.tuned.enable = true` (`:14`) plus a `systemd.services.tuned-set-profile`\nunit (`:24–32`) that runs `tuned-adm profile accelerator-performance` at every\nboot, `wantedBy = multi-user.target`. This **overrides any power/CPU-governor\npolicy** the dotfiles establish, at boot, every boot. If the dotfiles also\ndeclare `services.tuned`, that is a genuine option-merge conflict — this one at\nleast fails loudly.\n\n**4. Firmware injection. [S] `:11`** —\n`hardware.firmware = [ pkgs.strix-halo-mes-firmware ];`. Pulls an out-of-tree\nMES firmware blob into `/run/current-system/firmware`, changing GPU\ninitialisation. Also list-merged, also unconditional.\n\n### The trap: the module is *advertised* as safe to import\n\n**[S]** `README.md:196` — *\"`nixosModules.ec-su-axb35`, `ryzenadj`, `tuning` —\nStrix Halo hardware modules\"*, listed without caveat.\n\n**[S]** `examples/configuration.nix:17–25` — the shipped example **imports it**:\n\n```nix\n  imports = [\n    \"${modulesPath}/installer/cd-dvd/installation-cd-base.nix\"\n\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.default\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.tuning\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.ec-su-axb35\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.rpc-server\n    inputs.self.nixosModules.fastflowlm\n  ];\n```\n\n**[S]** `flake.nix:459–462` — that file *is* `nixosConfigurations.live-iso`.\n\n**So the upstream-blessed pattern is exactly what the Forbidden list prohibits.**\nAnyone following the README or copying the example into a host closure imports\nthe GTT override by accident. **The Forbidden-list entry is well-founded and\nshould be enforced mechanically, not by convention** — e.g. an assertion in the\nflashnext host modules that `boot.kernelParams` contains at most one\n`ttm.pages_limit=` token.\n\n**Safe alternative [S]:** `nixosModules.default` (`flake.nix:514–521`) is inert\nwith respect to tuning — it only imports the thunderbolt-ibverbs module and\nappends `self.overlays.default` to `nixpkgs.overlays`. **That is the only module\nthe campaign needs to get `pkgs.vllm-rocm`.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. SERVING MODULES — is the pair topology expressible?\n\n### 6.1 What serving modules exist\n\n**[M] Decisive negative:**\n\n```\n$ grep -rn 'vllm' modules/\nNO MATCH in modules/\n```\n\n**There is no vLLM NixOS module of any kind — no systemd unit, no container, no\noption namespace.** The string `vllm` does not appear anywhere under `modules/`.\n\n**[S]** The complete `nixosModules` set (`flake.nix:513–532`), classified:\n\n| Module | Kind | Serving? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `default` (`514–521`) | overlay application only | no |\n| `rpc-server` (`522`) | **systemd units — llama.cpp `rpc-server`** | yes, llama.cpp only |\n| `benchmark-executor` (`523`) | Nix distributed-build client | no |\n| `benchmark-runner` (`524`) | Nix builder capabilities + udev ACLs | no |\n| `ec-su-axb35` (`525`) | EC driver | no |\n| `fastflowlm` (`526`) | **systemd unit — FastFlowLM NPU server** | yes, NPU only |\n| `smu-exporter` (`527`) | Prometheus exporter | no |\n| `npu-exporter` (`528`) | Prometheus exporter | no |\n| `ryzenadj` (`529`) | power tuning | no |\n| `tuning` (`530`) | **FORBIDDEN — §5** | no |\n| `thunderbolt-ibverbs` (`531`) | re-export from the input | no |\n\n**Only two modules manage an inference server, and neither is vLLM:**\n\n- **[S]** `modules/rpc-server.nix` — `options.services.llama-cpp-rpc-servers`\n  (`:114–117`), `systemd.services = mapAttrs' …` (`:159–162`), unit description\n  *\"llama.cpp RPC server (${name})\"* (`:162`), `ExecStart` at `:170`. Options at\n  `:20–106`: `package`, `threads`, `device`, `host`, `port`, `enableCache`,\n  `cacheDirectory`, `extraArgs`, `environment`, `restart`, `user`, `group`,\n  `openFirewall`. **This is llama.cpp's RPC worker protocol — a completely\n  different distribution mechanism from vLLM+Ray.** Not reusable.\n- **[S]** `modules/fastflowlm.nix` — `mkEnableOption \"FastFlowLM NPU inference\n  server\"` (`:23`), `systemd.services.fastflowlm` (`:187`), `ExecStart` (`:202`),\n  `port` (`:59`), `openFirewall` (`:148`),\n  `networking.firewall.allowedTCPPorts` (`:270`). XDNA2 NPU, OpenAI-compatible.\n  **Structurally the closest precedent for a vLLM unit, but for different\n  hardware and a different binary.**\n\n### 6.2 Is the coordinator/worker pair expressible with them?\n\n**No. [S-derived]** The pair topology exists **only** as imperative shell:\n\n- Ray head start — `lib/bench/vllm-transport-matrix.sh:586–587`\n- Ray worker start **over `ssh`** — `:588–589`\n- `sleep 2` as the sole synchronisation — `:590`\n- `vllm serve --tensor-parallel-size 2 --distributed-executor-backend ray` — `:598–599`\n- teardown — `:162` (`ssh … \"$VLLM_ENV/bin/ray stop --force\"`)\n\nNothing in `nixosModules` models a Ray head, a Ray worker, a TP degree, a rail\nselection, or a coordinator/worker role. `benchmark-runner` and\n`benchmark-executor` (§4.3) look superficially like fleet modules but configure\n**Nix remote builders**, not runtime services.\n\n### 6.3 Conclusion for the campaign\n\n**flashnext must write its own systemd units.** Concretely, the repo gives:\n\n| Need | From nix-strix-halo | Campaign writes |\n|---|---|---|\n| ROCm vLLM package | **`pkgs.vllm-rocm`** via `nixosModules.default` [S `flake.nix:514–521`, `overlays/pkgs.rocm:332`] | — |\n| Ray in the same closure | pattern only — `symlinkJoin` [S `flake.nix:685–691`] | own env (a real `python.withPackages` is more robust than the symlinkJoin) |\n| Thunderbolt/USB4 RDMA rail | **`nixosModules.thunderbolt-ibverbs`** [S `flake.nix:531`], `pkgs.rdma-core-usb4` [S `flake.nix:320`] | — |\n| Ray head unit | nothing | **own unit** |\n| Ray worker unit | nothing | **own unit** (with a real readiness gate, not `sleep 2`) |\n| `vllm serve` TP=2 unit | nothing | **own unit** |\n| NCCL/RCCL env | **values readable** from `default.nix:223–239` and the 55-name `TUNE_VARS` at `vllm-transport-matrix.sh:188–243` [S] | own `serviceConfig.Environment` |\n| GTT / TuneD | **`nixosModules.tuning` — FORBIDDEN (§5)** | own, in dotfiles |\n\nGood news: the units are straightforward, and the *hard-won empirical content* —\nwhich NCCL knobs matter on this fabric — is plain data in files we may read and\nwhose **values** we may use. Reading a number and setting it in our own unit is\nnot copying their code.\n\n---\n\n## 7. FLAKE.LOCK AGES AND BUILDABILITY\n\n### 7.1 Age of each input\n\n**[M]** Computed from `flake.lock` `lastModified` against today (2026-08-28):\n\n| Input | Locked | Age |\n|---|---|---|\n| `nixpkgs` | **2026-07-18** | **41 days** |\n| `vllm-src` | **2026-07-12** | **47 days** |\n| `llama-cpp-master` | 2026-07-18 | 41 days |\n| `therock-src-…-root` (TheRock) | 2026-07-18 | 41 days |\n| `nix-ai-tools` | 2026-07-19 | 40 days |\n| `thunderbolt-ibverbs` | 2026-06-30 | 59 days |\n| `mlx-src` | 2026-07-16 | 43 days |\n| `fastflowlm` | 2026-07-17 | 42 days |\n| `ec-su-axb35` | 2026-07-11 | 48 days |\n| `llama-cpp-spacemit` | 2026-07-04 | 55 days |\n| `ds4` | 2026-06-17 | 72 days |\n| `ds4-hip` | 2026-06-04 | 85 days |\n| `xrt-src` / `xdna-driver-src` / `spine-triton` | 2026-06-04 | 85 days |","truncated":true,"truncatedBy":"bytes","totalLines":1302,"totalBytes":60364,"outputLines":1150,"outputBytes":51196,"lastLinePartial":false,"firstLineExceedsLimit":false,"maxLines":2000,"maxBytes":51200}},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990052260},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to read the rest of this file, especially section 4.4 (already read) and other related stuff. But first, let me read the rest of this file (offset=1151).\n\nAlso, I should look at scripts/receipts-verify.py for the style reference, and read the existing test files to understand the test patterns. Furthermore, I should check what the bench harness is expected to do.\n\nLet me finish reading this evidence file first.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me read the rest of the evidence file.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4ffe2bfb76fe446f98f7f48f","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md","offset":1151}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":19254,"output":197,"cacheRead":12672,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":99,"totalTokens":32123,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990052266,"responseId":"chatcmpl-fa064d67-5991-4973-9173-747e4eef15a0","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4ffe2bfb76fe446f98f7f48f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"| `therock-src-…rocm-libraries` | 2026-07-14 | 45 days |\n| `therock-src-…rocm-systems` | 2026-07-16 | 43 days |\n| `therock-src-…amd-llvm` | 2026-07-15 | 44 days |\n| ROCm vendored third-party (~20 nodes) | 2022-03-31 … 2026-06 | up to **1611 days** — ROCm's own pins, not local staleness |\n\nTransitive (via `nix-ai-tools`/`thunderbolt-ibverbs`): `flake-parts` 2026-07-01,\n`treefmt-nix` 2026-07-18, `bun2nix` 2026-06-29, `systems` 2023-04-09.\n\nAlso note **[S]**: `lib/hydra/benchmark/` contains a **second, separate flake\nwith its own `flake.lock`** (`lib/hydra/benchmark/flake.nix`, `flake.lock`), also\ncovered by dependabot (`.github/dependabot.yml:6`). Not on the campaign's\ncritical path, but it means \"the lock\" is ambiguous — there are two.\n\n### 7.2 The finding the age table hides\n\n**[M] The lock is 4 weeks behind the code in the same repo:**\n\n```\n$ git log -1 --format='%ci %s' -- flake.lock\n2026-07-21 17:51:58 +0200 rocm: update Strix Halo inference stack to 7.15 (#148)\n\n$ git log -1 --format='%H %ci %s'\nf0f2048… 2026-08-18 10:03:06 +0200 npu-exporter: add AMD XDNA NPU Prometheus exporter (#162)\n```\n\n**`flake.lock` has not been touched since 2026-07-21 — 38 days ago — while HEAD\nadvanced to 2026-08-18.** Everything merged in those four weeks\n(`npu-exporter` #162, `amdtop` #161, `smu-exporter` #156, `mlir-aie` #158,\n`gfx1030` #151 — **[M]** from `git log --oneline -5`) landed **without a lock\nbump**, i.e. was only ever evaluated against the July-18 nixpkgs.\n\nThis matters more than the raw 41 days: **[S]** `.github/dependabot.yml:7–8`\nschedules `interval: \"daily\"` nix input updates. Daily automation that has\nproduced **zero lock commits in 38 days** means either the automation is failing\nor its PRs are not being merged. Either way, **the lock is not being\ncontinuously validated**, and the newest ~28 days of code has never been\ngreen against anything but a stale pin.\n\n**[M] Churn of the files the campaign depends on** — these are *not* moving:\n\n| File | Last touched |\n|---|---|\n| `overlays/therock-vllm.nix` | 2026-07-21 (`12e2ed2`, #148) |\n| `pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench/default.nix` | 2026-06-12 (`8db2c08`, #79) |\n| `lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py` | 2026-06-12 (`8db2c08`, #79) |\n| `modules/tuning.nix` | 2026-06-03 (`109038d`, a *nixpkgs bump* commit) |\n\n**Interpretation:** the vLLM/bench surface is **stable and unmaintained**, not\nstable-because-mature. `tuning.nix` last changed only as collateral in a\ndependency bump. The campaign should expect **no upstream fix** for §4.4 and\nshould not plan around one.\n\n### 7.3 Does it build against current nixpkgs?\n\n**UNDETERMINED — and deliberately so.** I ran **no builds and no evaluations**\nper the analysis-only constraint. What I can and cannot say:\n\n**Can say [S/M]:**\n- The flake is *self-consistent*: it pins its own `nixpkgs` at `61b7c44c4073`\n  (2026-07-18) and, used **without** a `follows`, evaluates against that pin.\n  For a downstream consumer this is the safe mode.\n- The `vllm-rocm` build chain is **maximally sensitive to the nixpkgs pin**,\n  because it does not define vLLM — it *mutates nixpkgs'* (`therock-vllm.nix:242`,\n  `py.vllm.override`). Concretely it assumes nixpkgs provides:\n  - a `python313Packages.vllm` accepting `.override { rocmSupport, cudaSupport,\n    gpuTargets, rocmPackages, amdsmi }` [S `:242–247`];\n  - a patch file literally named `0006-drop-rocm-extra-reqs.patch` in that\n    package's `patches` [S `:253–255`] — if nixpkgs renames or drops it, the\n    filter silently becomes a no-op and the ROCm-extra-reqs patch is re-applied\n    to a 0.25.1 tree it was not written for;\n  - `py.setuptools_80`, `py.outlines-core`, `py.amdsmi`, `py.watchfiles`, … [S `:214–231`];\n  - a `pyproject.toml` containing the exact literal `\"torch == 2.11.0\"` [S `:269`,\n    `--replace-fail`].\n- Every `--replace-fail` in `postPatch` (`:259, 263, 268, 271, 279, 288`) is a\n  **hard abort** on a missed match. This is good engineering (fails loudly) and\n  simultaneously the reason `inputs.nix-strix-halo.inputs.nixpkgs.follows =\n  \"nixpkgs\"` is **high-risk**: a newer nixpkgs whose vllm has moved past 0.16.0\n  will very likely break one of them.\n- **[S-local, indicative]** the local system nixpkgs (26.11) ships\n  `vllm` **0.16.0** with the four patches listed in §3.3, `0006` present. If the\n  pinned 2026-07-18 nixpkgs is close to that, the mechanism holds today.\n\n**Cannot say:** whether a *current* (late-August 2026) nixpkgs still satisfies\nall of the above. **What would settle it:** a `nix flake check --no-build` /\n`nix eval .#packages.x86_64-linux.vllm-rocm.drvPath` with\n`--override-input nixpkgs github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable`. Evaluation alone\n(no realisation) would surface the `.override` signature and attribute-existence\nfailures; the `--replace-fail` breakages only appear at build time.\n\n**Recommendation:** **do not `follows` nixpkgs into this input.** Let it keep its\nown 2026-07-18 nixpkgs. Accept the resulting closure duplication — it buys a\npackage that actually evaluates, and it is the configuration upstream last\ntested.\n\n---\n\n## 8. SUMMARY FOR THE CONSUMPTION PLAN\n\n1. **License: no LICENSE file exists** [M, two searches + full file inventory +\n   README grep]. Flake-input use only. Never copy `.nix`, `.patch`, `.sh`, or\n   `.py` into the flashnext tree. Importing a path *inside the fetched input*\n   (`\"${input}/overlays/therock-vllm.nix\"`) is input use and is fine.\n2. **Fork the vLLM src via `inputs.nix-strix-halo.inputs.vllm-src.follows`**\n   [S `flake.nix:47–50`, `:368`]. It reaches `vllm-rocm` and the bench env in one\n   move. **It does not change the version string** — `vllmVersion = \"0.25.1\"` is a\n   literal at `flake.nix:369` feeding `VLLM_VERSION_OVERRIDE`\n   [S `therock-vllm.nix:296`].\n3. **No patch list is exposed.** `mkVllmTherock` takes 16 booleans and nothing\n   else [S `therock-vllm.nix:138–156`]; `lib` exports four overlay builders and\n   no vLLM one [S `flake.nix:480–499`; `grep` → 1 internal hit].\n   **Inject `.patch` files via `overridePythonAttrs` with `(old.patches or []) ++ …`**,\n   and **append** to `postPatch` — never assign, because `therock-vllm.nix:257`\n   assigns and `:288–291` is load-bearing.\n4. **ROCm is `7.15.0a20260719`, an AMD nightly** [S `rocm.json:4–6`]. torch 2.11.0\n   / triton 3.7.1 / cp313 arrive as **prebuilt AMD wheels** [S\n   `therock-python.nix:53–61`, `python-wheels.json`, `python-config.nix:4`];\n   **only vLLM is compiled from source** [S `therock-vllm.nix:321`]. ROCm 10 is a\n   five-file coordinated fork, not a config change — and\n   `therock-vllm.nix:269`'s `--replace-fail '\"torch == 2.11.0\"'` guarantees a break.\n5. **`vllm-stream-client.py:297` — CONFIRMED at current bytes, line number\n   unchanged.** md5 `aadc50c1c653b8cc58571313ba1fe070`, 374 lines / 11903 bytes [M].\n   Line 297 is character-identical to line 294; both are `statistics.mean(ttfts)`.\n   The docstring at `:4–7` admits the proxy. The file has been modified exactly\n   once, at introduction (2026-06-12) [M] — **expect no upstream fix.** Replace\n   the client through the existing `vllmStreamClient` package argument\n   [S `pkgs/strix-halo-vllm-pair-bench/default.nix:14`].\n6. **Forbidden module = `nixosModules.tuning` = `modules/tuning.nix`, 33 lines.**\n   It appends `ttm.pages_limit=20971520` (80 GiB) to `boot.kernelParams` with\n   **no option and no `mkDefault`** [S `:4–7`] — the only GTT setter in the repo\n   [M, repo-wide grep] — plus forces TuneD `accelerator-performance` at every\n   boot [S `:13–32`] and injects MES firmware [S `:11`]. Duplicate kernel\n   parameters **merge silently**; the collision will not error. Compounding the\n   trap: `README.md:196` lists it without caveat and\n   `examples/configuration.nix:21` imports it. **Enforce the prohibition with an\n   assertion, not a convention.** Use `nixosModules.default` [S `flake.nix:514–521`],\n   which is tuning-inert.\n7. **No vLLM serving module exists** — `grep -rn 'vllm' modules/` returns\n   **nothing** [M]. The coordinator/worker Ray pair lives only in\n   `lib/bench/vllm-transport-matrix.sh:586–599` as SSH-driven shell, with a\n   `sleep 2` for synchronisation [S `:590`]. **flashnext must write its own\n   systemd units**; it may freely reuse the empirically-tuned NCCL/RCCL *values*\n   [S `default.nix:223–239`; `vllm-transport-matrix.sh:188–243`].\n8. **Lock staleness: 41 days on nixpkgs/TheRock, 47 on vllm-src — but the\n   sharper fact is that `flake.lock` has not moved since 2026-07-21 while HEAD\n   advanced to 2026-08-18** [M], despite `dependabot.yml:7–8` scheduling daily\n   updates. ~28 days of merged code has never been evaluated against a fresh\n   pin. **Do not `follows` nixpkgs into this input**: `vllm-rocm` is built by\n   mutating *nixpkgs'* vllm [S `therock-vllm.nix:242`] and depends on a patch\n   filename [S `:253–255`] and six `--replace-fail` literals [S `:259–292`].\n   Buildability against a *current* nixpkgs is **UNDETERMINED** — no builds were\n   run; `nix eval .#packages.x86_64-linux.vllm-rocm.drvPath\n   --override-input nixpkgs …` would settle the evaluation half.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990059204},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now the full context is in place. Key points from section 4.4 of the evidence file:\n\n**Reference harness's defect (nix-strix-halo's `vllm-stream-client.py`):**\n- Line 297: `prefill_mean_s = statistics.mean(ttfts)` — this duplicates `ttft_mean_s` (line 294). So the \"prefill\" column is a duplicate of the \"time-to-first-token\" column, not a separate measurement.\n- TTFT is measured client-side: `start` is stamped right after barrier release, and `first_token` is stamped when the first SSE text chunk arrives. This interval includes: request construction, network RTT, **vLLM scheduler queue wait**, prompt prefill, the first decode step, and SSE framing.\n- So prefill is not separable from queue wait with client-side measurement alone.\n\n**Our fix:** The client must separate queue wait from prefill. The evidence says: \"separating prefill from queuing requires scraping `/metrics` (or `--collect-detailed-traces`), but this harness doesn't do that.\"\n\nSo our client must scrape the server's metrics endpoint in order to separate scheduler queue wait from prompt processing.\n\nThe task description says:\n> The client must scrape the server's metrics endpoint in addition to measuring per-request client-side time-to-first-token, in order to separate scheduler queue wait from prompt processing — the reference harness's client was emitting a prefill column that duplicated its first-token column (specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md section 4.4), but ours must not do that. Emit a CSV with explicit column semantics stated in a header comment, plus a sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token IDs.\n\nLet me think about how vLLM's /metrics endpoint actually works.\n\n**vLLM's metrics endpoint:**\nvLLM exposes Prometheus metrics at `/metrics`. Relevant metrics:\n- `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds` — a histogram of TTFT (this is still a client-observed TTFT measured on the server side)\n- `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds` — end-to-end request latency\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds` — **queue wait time** (time the request spends in the queue before being scheduled)\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds` — inference time\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` — prefill time (in some versions)\n- `vllm:request_decode_time_seconds` — decode time\n- `vllm:num_requests_waiting` — number of requests waiting in the queue\n- `vllm:num_requests_running` — number of requests running\n\nActually, let me recall the precise vLLM metrics. In newer vLLM versions:\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds` — Histogram of time spent in the waiting queue\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds` — Histogram of inference time\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` — Histogram of prefill time (this may be behind a flag)\n- `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds` — TTFT histogram\n\nKey insight: **queue time + prefill time ≈ TTFT** (roughly). That is, if we scrape the metrics endpoint and can extract the queue time and prefill time histograms, we can separate the two.\n\nBut wait — the metrics endpoint exposes histograms, which are aggregate (bucketed counts and sums), not per-request values. So how do we separate per-request queue time from prefill time?\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. The task says:\n> Feed the client's parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time, and assert that the two columns separate them.\n\nSo the test is about the **parser**. The client has a parser that takes metrics data (with queue time and processing/prefill time) and produces two separate columns. The test feeds this parser a fixture with known values and asserts the separation.\n\nSo the key deliverable is a **parser function** that takes in a scraped metrics payload and extracts queue wait and prefill/processing time as two separate numbers.\n\nLet me think about the actual design. The client:\n1. For each request, measures client-side TTFT (timestamping first token arrival relative to request start).\n2. Scrapes the server's `/metrics` endpoint before and after (or around) the batch and computes the delta in the queue-time and prefill-time histograms to derive server-reported queue wait and prompt processing.\n\nActually, in vLLM, per-request metrics are tricky. Let me reconsider whether vLLM exposes per-request queue/prefill breakdown.\n\nActually, vLLM's OpenAI-compatible API doesn't directly return per-request queue and prefill breakdowns. But the `/metrics` endpoint exposes histograms. To get per-request values you need to take deltas between scrapes.\n\nBut for a bench harness, the practical approach is:\n- Scrape `/metrics` before the arm's requests and after, compute the delta in the sum of the queue-time histogram and the sum of the prefill/inference-time histogram, and divide by the number of requests to get an average.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the scope of the task. The acceptance criteria are:\n```\npython3 -m py_compile bench/fn-stream-client.py && bash -n bench/run-matrix.sh && python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v\n```\n\nSo all that matters for acceptance is:\n1. The client compiles (py_compile).\n2. The matrix script is syntactically valid (bash -n).\n3. The unit tests pass (unittest tests.test_bench_client).\n\nAnd claim 6.2 in the spec: \"The bench client separates queue wait from prefill → the client unit test with injected queuing passes in the test suite.\"\n\nSo the core thing that will be graded is **the unit test with injected queuing**. The test feeds the parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time and asserts that the two columns separate them.\n\nSo I need:\n1. `bench/fn-stream-client.py` — the client. It must have a parser function that takes metrics (with queue time and prefill time) and produces two separate columns. It must be importable by the test.\n2. `bench/run-matrix.sh` — the matrix script. Counterbalanced A-B-B-A, three loads per arm, medians, depth series 0/10240/102400, speculative on/off arms, results under results/, bench.json receipt.\n3. `tests/test_bench_client.py` — the unit test. Feed the parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time, assert separation.\n\nNow, the steering comment adds hardening requirements:\n1. The bench matrix MUST record concurrency (number of in-flight sequences) per arm on the output rows.\n2. If any token fingerprint diverges between runs of the same arm, the harness must byte-compare a serial (concurrency=1) replay of that arm before trusting its numbers — flag the arm as suspect rather than averaging across the divergence.\n\nLet me think about the design more carefully.\n\n**Client design (`bench/fn-stream-client.py`):**\n\nThe client should:\n1. Send streaming completion requests to an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.\n2. Measure per-request client-side TTFT: timestamp request start and timestamp the first token.\n3. Scrape the server's `/metrics` endpoint.\n4. Parse the metrics to extract queue wait time and prefill (prompt processing) time as separate quantities.\n5. Emit CSV with a header comment explaining the column semantics.\n6. Compute a sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token IDs.\n\nThe critical requirement is that the \"prefill\" column must not be a duplicate of the \"ttft\" column. Prefill must come from a separate source (server metrics), not from the client-side TTFT.\n\nLet me think about how vLLM exposes queue time and prefill time in metrics. Let me look up actual vLLM metrics names.\n\nActually, I recall that vLLM (as of ~0.5+) exposes these per-request timing metrics as histograms:\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds` — time spent waiting in the queue\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds` — time for inference\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` — prefill time (may not exist in all versions)\n- `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds` — TTFT\n\nAnd:\n- `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds` — end-to-end\n\nThe relationship: e2e ≈ queue + prefill + decode. And TTFT ≈ queue + prefill (roughly).\n\nHmm, but these are histograms. For a bench harness, we'd take deltas.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The point of the task is really the *separation* concept, not perfect fidelity to vLLM's actual metrics format. The test feeds a *fixture* with known queue time and processing time. So I have freedom to design the fixture format and the parser, as long as:\n- The parser produces two columns: one for queue wait, one for prefill/processing.\n- The two columns are derived from *different* sources (not the same value twice).\n- The test can inject known values and verify separation.\n\nBut at the same time, I want the client to be a genuine, honest client that would work against real vLLM. So the parser should parse Prometheus exposition format from `/metrics` and extract queue-time and prefill-time histograms.\n\nLet me design it as follows:\n\n**Metrics scraping:** The client scrapes `/metrics` before sending the requests (baseline) and after they complete. It parses both scrapes and computes per-request deltas:\n- Queue wait = (sum_after(queue histogram) - sum_before(queue histogram)) / num_new_requests\n- Prefill/processing = (sum_after(prefill histogram) - sum_before(prefill histogram)) / num_new_requests\n\nHmm, but this gets complicated because we have to match up which requests are \"new.\" Let me simplify.\n\nActually — a cleaner approach for the bench: scrape metrics before each request (or batch) and after, and attribute the deltas.\n\nBut honestly, for the acceptance criteria, what matters is that the parser function exists and correctly separates two injected values. Let me make the parser robust and well-designed while keeping the core testable.\n\nLet me reconsider. Perhaps the cleanest design is:\n\nThe client's core parse function parses a Prometheus metrics payload (text in exposition format) and returns a structured snapshot: for each named histogram, the `_sum` and `_count`. Then a separate function computes the delta between two snapshots to derive per-request queue and prefill times.\n\nThe test can then:\n1. Construct two fixture payloads (before/after) with known `_sum` values for the queue-time histogram and the prefill-time histogram.\n2. Call the parser / delta function.\n3. Assert that the queue column and the prefill column have the expected distinct values.\n\nThis cleanly tests \"the two columns separate them.\"\n\nActually wait, let me re-read the task wording again very carefully:\n\n> tests/test_bench_client.py: feed the client's parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time, and assert that the two columns separate them.\n\nSo: feed the parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time. Assert that the two columns separate them.\n\nSo the fixture has a known queue time and a known processing time, and the parser should produce two columns (queue, processing) with those two distinct values. The test asserts that the queue column == known queue time and the processing column == known processing time, and that they're different from each other (and, importantly, that the processing column is not equal to the ttft / first-token column).\n\nAnd spec claim 6.2 says \"the client unit test with injected queuing.\" So the fixture injects queuing.\n\nOK, here's my plan for the parser design:\n\nThe client scrapes `/metrics` and parses it into a `MetricsSnapshot` object keyed by metric name -> {sum, count, buckets}. Then a function `split_first_token(before, after, n_requests)` computes:\n- `queue_wait_s` = delta_sum(queue_metric) / delta_count(queue_metric) (or / n_requests)\n- `prefill_s` = delta_sum(prefill_metric) / delta_count(prefill_metric)\n\nAnd the crucial invariant: `prefill_s` is derived from the prefill metric, NOT from the ttft series. And the ttft series is measured client-side.\n\nLet me also think about this: the reference defect was `prefill_mean_s = statistics.mean(ttfts)`. Our version must not do that. Our `prefill_mean_s` (or whatever we call it) must come from the server metrics' prefill histogram delta, while `ttft_mean_s` comes from the client-side first-token timestamps. And we also emit `queue_wait_mean_s` from the queue histogram delta.\n\nSo three distinct numbers:\n- `ttft_mean_s` — client-side, first-token minus request-start.\n- `queue_wait_mean_s` — server-side, from queue-time histogram delta.\n- `prefill_mean_s` — server-side, from prefill-time (prompt processing) histogram delta.\n\nAnd the invariant to test: `queue_wait_mean_s != prefill_mean_s` (they're separate), and `prefill_mean_s != ttft_mean_s` (prefill is not a duplicate of ttft).\n\nOK, let me design the metrics parsing. vLLM's `/metrics` is in Prometheus exposition format. Example:\n\n```\n# HELP vllm:request_queue_time_seconds Histogram of time spent in the queue\n# TYPE vllm:request_queue_time_seconds histogram\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_bucket{le=\"0.001\"} 0.0\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_bucket{le=\"0.01\"} 1.0\n...\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_sum 12.5\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_count 40.0\n```\n\nSo I need to parse `_sum` and `_count` lines.\n\nMetric names in vLLM: let me recall precisely. Actually, I should be careful about exact metric names. Let me think about what vLLM actually exposes.\n\nFrom vLLM source (`vllm/engine/metrics.py`), the stats logger exposes:\n- `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`\n- `vllm:time_per_output_token_seconds`\n- `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_prompt_tokens`\n- `vllm:request_generation_tokens`\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` (in newer versions)\n- `vllm:request_decode_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_success_total`\n- `vllm:num_requests_waiting`\n- `vllm:num_requests_running`\n- `vllm:num_requests_swapped`\n\nActually, the exact set depends on the vLLM version. This campaign uses vLLM 0.25.1 (forked), so let's check what metrics that version exposes. But we can't actually run the server here. For robustness, I'll parse whatever histogram is present, keyed by name, with configurable metric names.\n\nTo be safe and honest, I'll:\n1. Define the metric names we expect as constants, with fallbacks.\n2. The parser extracts `_sum` and `_count` for any metric name.\n3. The \"split\" logic computes queue wait from the queue-time metric and prefill from the prefill-time metric. If the prefill-time metric is absent, we can fall back to inference time minus decode time, but that gets complicated.\n\nActually, for the purposes of this task and the test, let me make the parser general (parse any histogram sum/count) and have the split logic reference specific metric names. The test will inject a fixture with those specific metric names.\n\nLet me reconsider: should I make the client robust to the case where the metrics endpoint doesn't expose a prefill breakdown? Spec says the client \"scrapes the server's metrics endpoint to separate scheduler queue wait from prompt processing.\" So the design assumes the metrics endpoint provides enough information to separate them. vLLM does expose `request_queue_time_seconds`. For prompt processing, in newer vLLM there's `request_prefill_time_seconds`.\n\nLet me verify: does vLLM 0.25.x expose `request_prefill_time_seconds`? I think recent vLLM has, behind `--otlp-traces-endpoint` or in metrics... Actually, let me reconsider.\n\nHmm, I recall vLLM's metrics have these \"request timing\" histograms, controlled by a config. Let me not over-think the exact upstream name; I'll make it configurable and document clearly. The key deliverable is the separation and the test.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole approach once more. There might be a simpler, cleaner interpretation that better matches \"feed the parser a fixture with known queue time and processing time.\"\n\nPerhaps the fixture is not a Prometheus payload but a structured representation. But the task says \"scrapes the server's metrics endpoint,\" so the natural format is Prometheus exposition text. The test fixture would be a sample of Prometheus text with known `_sum` values.\n\nLet me go with the Prometheus exposition format for the fixture. That's the most honest and matches \"scrapes the server's metrics endpoint.\"\n\nOK, let me design the code.\n\n### bench/fn-stream-client.py\n\nLet me structure it:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"fn-stream-client.py — honest streaming benchmark client for flashnext.\n\n... docstring explaining the separation doctrine ...\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport csv\nimport hashlib\nimport io\nimport json\nimport sys\nimport time\nimport urllib.request\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional\n\n# --- Prometheus metrics parsing ---\n\n@dataclass\nclass HistogramReading:\n    total_sum: float\n    count: float\n\n@dataclass\nclass MetricsSnapshot:\n    histograms: Dict[str, HistogramReading]\n    ...\n\ndef parse_prometheus(text: str) -> MetricsSnapshot:\n    \"\"\"Parse Prometheus exposition text into histogram sum/count readings.\"\"\"\n    ...\n\ndef scrape(url) -> MetricsSnapshot:\n    ...\n\n# Metric names\nQUEUE_METRIC = \"vllm:request_queue_time_seconds\"\nPREFILL_METRIC = \"vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds\"\nTTFT_METRIC = \"vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds\"\n\ndef split_first_token(before, after, num_requests) -> (queue_wait, prefill):\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, but I need to be careful: the number of requests for averaging. If I take the delta of `_sum` and `_count`, the average is delta_sum / delta_count. That's cleaner than dividing by num_requests (which might differ from delta_count if there's pre-existing load). Let me use delta_sum / delta_count.\n\nBut the test fixture: the test injects known queue time and processing time. If I use delta_sum / delta_count, the test needs to set up a before and after snapshot. Let me think about what's cleanest for the test.\n\nOption A: The parser function takes a single metrics payload that already contains per-request queue and prefill (as if the server reports per-request). But Prometheus doesn't work that way.\n\nOption B: The split function takes before and after snapshots. The test constructs two payloads.\n\nOption C: The parser has a lower-level function that, given a snapshot, returns the `_sum` and `_count` for a named metric, and a higher-level `per_request_mean(snapshot, name)` or a delta-based one.\n\nLet me make it testable at multiple levels:\n- `parse_prometheus(text) -> MetricsSnapshot` — parses text.\n- `MetricsSnapshot.hist(name) -> Optional[HistogramReading]` — gets a reading.\n- `split_first_token(before: MetricsSnapshot, after: MetricsSnapshot) -> FirstTokenSplit` — computes queue_wait_s and prefill_s from deltas.\n\nThe test will:\n1. Build a `before` payload with queue_sum=0, queue_count=0, prefill_sum=0, prefill_count=0.\n2. Build an `after` payload with queue_sum = KNOWN_QUEUE_TOTAL, queue_count = N, prefill_sum = KNOWN_PREFILL_TOTAL, prefill_count = N.\n3. Call split, and assert that queue_wait_s ≈ KNOWN_QUEUE_TOTAL/N and prefill_s ≈ KNOWN_PREFILL_TOTAL/N, and that they differ.\n\nThat's clean. Let me also make sure the test asserts the key anti-defect property: that prefill is not equal to ttft. But ttft is measured client-side, not from metrics. So maybe the test should also assert that the prefill column is not derived from the ttft series.\n\nHmm, to really nail down the \"does not duplicate first-token\" property, I could have the test also construct a scenario where ttft (client-side) is a known value different from prefill. But ttft is measured during an actual request, which requires a live server. In a unit test, I can't easily run a live request.\n\nAlternative: the test could verify the *structural* property — that the prefill column and the ttft column come from different code paths / sources. But that's hard to assert directly.\n\nLet me focus on what's cleanly testable: the separation of queue and prefill (the two server-side columns), and that the prefill parser reads from the prefill metric (not the ttft metric). I can assert that if I inject different values into the prefill metric and the ttft metric, the prefill column reflects the prefill metric and NOT the ttft metric. That directly proves \"prefill is not a duplicate of first-token.\"\n\nSo the test:\n- Fixture with:\n  - queue metric: sum = Q_TOTAL, count = N\n  - prefill metric: sum = P_TOTAL, count = N\n  - ttft metric: sum = T_TOTAL, count = N (T_TOTAL != P_TOTAL)\n- Assert:\n  - queue_wait == Q_TOTAL / N\n  - prefill == P_TOTAL / N\n  - prefill != T_TOTAL / N  (proving prefill is not read from ttft)\n  - prefill != queue_wait (proving separation)\n\nThat's a strong, honest test.\n\nActually, to keep it simple and focused, let me have `split_first_token` return a dataclass with `queue_wait_s`, `prefill_s`, and also `queue_n`, `prefill_n`. The test asserts on those.\n\nLet me also reconsider: do I want the client to actually perform HTTP requests and SSE parsing? For py_compile and a focused unit test, the HTTP parts just need to be syntactically valid and importable. But to be a genuine harness, it should do real streaming. Let me implement a real streaming client with urllib, so it's actually usable, while keeping the parsing logic isolated and unit-testable.\n\nBut SSE streaming with urllib is a bit awkward. Let me implement it cleanly. Actually, I could also support a non-streaming mode. But the task says \"streaming client\" and \"per-request time-to-first-token.\" So streaming (SSE) is the way.\n\nLet me implement:\n- `stream_completion(url, payload, ...)`: opens a POST with stream=True, reads SSE lines, timestamps the first token, collects token IDs, computes sha256 fingerprint.\n\nFor SSE parsing: read lines, look for `data: {...}` lines, parse JSON, extract token text and id. Timestamp first non-empty token.\n\nFor token IDs: OpenAI completions streaming returns `choices[0].logprobs` if requested, or `choices[0].token_id`? Actually, the completions API (non-chat) returns `choices[].text`. To get token IDs, we can request `logprobs` or use `echo`. Hmm. Actually, vLLM's completions API in streaming mode returns chunks with `choices[0].text`. Token IDs are not directly in the standard response unless we request logprobs.\n\nBut the task says \"sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token IDs.\" So we need token IDs. vLLM's `/v1/completions` supports returning `prompt_token_ids` in some modes, and logprobs give token info. Actually, there's a cleaner way: vLLM supports `logprobs` in completions, which gives per-token info. But that's per-token logprobs, not raw IDs.\n\nHmm, actually, vLLM's OpenAI completions API has a non-standard field. Let me recall. When you set `logprobs=k`, each chunk's choice has `logprobs: {tokens: [...], token_logprobs: [...], top_logprobs: [...], text_offset: [...]}`. The `tokens` list has token *strings*, not IDs.\n\nAlternatively, vLLM returns `prompt_token_ids` if you... no.\n\nActually, there's a simpler interpretation: \"sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token IDs\" — we can fingerprint the completion deterministically. If we can't easily get raw token IDs from the OpenAI API, we can fingerprint the concatenated token text (or the logprobs tokens). But the task explicitly says \"token IDs.\"\n\nLet me reconsider. vLLM's OpenAI-compatible completions endpoint does actually support returning token IDs in some configurations. Actually, I recall vLLM has an extra field. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the cleanest is: request with `logprobs=1` and `echo=False`, and use the returned token strings from logprobs as a proxy for the token sequence, then fingerprint that. But the task says token IDs specifically.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the client should hit vLLM's native `/generate` or use the `prompt_token_ids` input. Actually, for fingerprinting purposes, what matters is a deterministic hash of the completion. Let me design it so the fingerprint is computed over the token IDs if available, else over the token text, and document clearly.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider — maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me look at whether there's a reference in the repo for how token IDs are obtained. Let me check the container/host tooling and any existing bench-like scripts.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The repo doesn't have bench/ yet. Let me look at what other scripts exist, especially scripts/receipts-verify.py (style reference) and existing tests, to match the house style. Let me also check host/ and scripts/.\n\nLet me look at those now.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I now have the full picture of the reference defect (§4.4: `prefill_mean_s` duplicated `ttft_mean_s`). Let me study the house style: the cited style reference, existing tests, and surrounding tooling.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_cf583753df124541871b8603","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/scripts/receipts-verify.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":22151,"output":5899,"cacheRead":13312,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":5787,"totalTokens":41362,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990059209,"responseId":"chatcmpl-ac943386-c42a-44b6-b8e6-2df178dc6ad9","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_cf583753df124541871b8603","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Validate every overnight receipt under results/receipts/.\n\nA receipt is one JSON object: {\"step\": str, \"status\": \"pass\"|\"fail\",\n\"ts\": str, \"data\": {...}}. Missing receipts are legal (the step has not\nrun); a present receipt must be well-formed, and hard bounds are enforced\nper step. Any violation exits 2 — this is the ladder gate that makes the\novernight claims graded rather than narrated.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport pathlib\nimport sys\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nRECEIPTS = ROOT / \"results\" / \"receipts\"\n\n# Per-step hard bounds: step-name prefix -> checker(data) -> error or None.\ndef _residency(d):\n    for rank, gib in (d.get(\"gtt_gib_per_rank\") or {}).items():\n        if gib > 80:\n            return f\"rank {rank} GTT {gib} GiB exceeds the 80 GiB bound\"\n    if d.get(\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\", 0) != 0:\n        return \"table has GPU-resident bytes; the mmap path is not engaged\"\n    if not d.get(\"read_after_warmed_decode\", False):\n        return \"residency was not read after a warmed decode\"\n    return None\n\ndef _tp2(d):\n    if not d.get(\"byte_identical_repeat\", False):\n        return \"greedy repeat was not byte-identical\"\n    return None\n\ndef _weights(d):\n    if d.get(\"shards\", 0) < 131:\n        return f\"only {d.get('shards')} shards staged\"\n    return None\n\ndef _bench(d):\n    if d.get(\"loads_per_arm\", 0) < 3:\n        return \"fewer than 3 loads per arm\"\n    if not d.get(\"counterbalanced\", False):\n        return \"arms were not counterbalanced\"\n    return None\n\ndef _context(d):\n    ratio = d.get(\"decode_ratio_vs_short_context\")\n    if ratio is not None and ratio < 0.9:\n        return f\"full-context decode ratio {ratio} below the 0.9 bound\"\n    return None\n\ndef _smoke(d):\n    ap = (d.get(\"aperture\") or {}).get(\"ttm_pages_limit\")\n    if ap is not None and ap != 33554432:\n        return f\"aperture {ap} diverges from the 33554432-page ceiling\"\n    return None\n\nBOUNDS = {\n    \"residency\": _residency,\n    \"tp2\": _tp2,\n    \"weights\": _weights,\n    \"bench\": _bench,\n    \"context\": _context,\n    \"smoke\": _smoke,\n}\n\ndef _handoff_patch_ok() -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"When the catalog patch exists it must parse as a git patch.\"\"\"\n    import subprocess\n    patch = ROOT / \"handoff\" / \"catalog-row.patch\"\n    if not patch.is_file():\n        return None\n    proc = subprocess.run([\"git\", \"apply\", \"--stat\", str(patch)],\n                          capture_output=True, text=True)\n    if proc.returncode != 0:\n        return f\"handoff/catalog-row.patch does not parse: {proc.stderr.strip()}\"\n    return None\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    bad = 0\n    err = _handoff_patch_ok()\n    if err:\n        print(f\"receipts-verify: {err}\")\n        bad += 1\n    if not RECEIPTS.is_dir():\n        print(\"receipts-verify: no receipts directory; nothing ran\")\n        return 2 if bad else 0\n    seen = 0\n    for path in sorted(RECEIPTS.glob(\"*.json\")):\n        seen += 1\n        try:\n            r = json.loads(path.read_text())\n        except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n            print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: invalid JSON: {e}\")\n            bad += 1\n            continue\n        for key in (\"step\", \"status\", \"ts\"):\n            if key not in r:\n                print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: missing '{key}'\")\n                bad += 1\n        if r.get(\"status\") == \"fail\":\n            print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: step '{r.get('step')}' FAILED\")\n            bad += 1\n        for prefix, check in BOUNDS.items():\n            if str(r.get(\"step\", \"\")).startswith(prefix):\n                err = check(r.get(\"data\") or {})\n                if err:\n                    print(f\"receipts-verify: {path.name}: {err}\")\n                    bad += 1\n    print(f\"receipts-verify: {seen} receipts checked, {bad} violations\")\n    return 2 if bad else 0\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990187003},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, let's look at the existing tests, understand the test style, and see how they're structured (in particular, `test_repo_invariants.py` might have hints about bench, and `test_host_tooling.py` will tell us about the host tooling patterns).","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a5265c751abc466fb8fb4bfa","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/tests/test_repo_invariants.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":8712,"output":120,"cacheRead":33792,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":56,"totalTokens":42624,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990187009,"responseId":"chatcmpl-df9f0765-50fc-46d2-84a8-eefa269f107f","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_a5265c751abc466fb8fb4bfa","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"\"\"Repo packaging invariants — the offline half of the discipline.\n\nModeled on ds4-vllm's test_patchset_packaging.py (Apache-2.0), extended:\nnew-file line counts are audited too (the upstream suite skipped them and\n3 of its 12 MANIFEST counts drifted).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport pathlib\nimport re\nimport subprocess\nimport unittest\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n\n\nclass RepoInvariants(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_license_is_apache2(self):\n        text = (ROOT / \"LICENSE\").read_text()\n        self.assertIn(\"Apache License\", text)\n        self.assertIn(\"Version 2.0\", text)\n\n    def test_no_gpl_text_in_tree(self):\n        needle = \"GNU GENERAL \" + \"PUBLIC LICENSE\"  # avoid self-match\n        for path in ROOT.rglob(\"*\"):\n            if path.is_dir() or \".git\" in path.parts \\\n                    or \"__pycache__\" in path.parts:\n                continue\n            # Evidence dossiers may QUOTE license headers they inspected.\n            if \"evidence\" in path.parts or path.name == \"test_repo_invariants.py\":\n                continue\n            if path.suffix in {\".safetensors\", \".gguf\"}:\n                continue\n            try:\n                text = path.read_text(errors=\"ignore\")\n            except Exception:\n                continue\n            self.assertNotIn(needle, text, f\"GPL text found in {path}\")\n\n    def test_no_secret_material(self):\n        patterns = [re.compile(p) for p in\n                    (r\"BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY\", r\"hf_[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}\",\n                     r\"ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{30,}\", r\"AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}\")]\n        for path in ROOT.rglob(\"*\"):\n            if path.is_dir() or \".git\" in path.parts \\\n                    or \"__pycache__\" in path.parts:\n                continue\n            try:\n                text = path.read_text(errors=\"ignore\")\n            except Exception:\n                continue\n            for pat in patterns:\n                self.assertIsNone(pat.search(text),\n                                  f\"secret-like material in {path}: {pat.pattern}\")\n\n    def test_spec_artifacts_exist(self):\n        self.assertTrue((ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/spec.md\").is_file())\n        trace = json.loads((ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/trace.json\").read_text())\n        self.assertEqual(trace[\"schemaVersion\"], 1)\n        self.assertTrue(\n            (ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/contracts/trace.schema.json\").is_file())\n\n    def test_evidence_dossiers_present(self):\n        dossiers = list((ROOT / \"specs/flashnext/evidence\").glob(\"*.md\"))\n        self.assertGreaterEqual(len(dossiers), 7)\n\n    def test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene(self):\n        wl_path = ROOT / \"silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json\"\n        wl = json.loads(wl_path.read_text())\n        self.assertEqual(wl[\"schemaVersion\"], 1)\n        self.assertTrue(wl[\"tasks\"])\n        raw = wl_path.read_text()\n        # Runtime hub downloads are forbidden estate-wide.\n        self.assertNotIn(\"-hf \", raw)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"hf download\", raw)\n        # Model-family names never enter worklist bytes (lint L16 mirror).\n        for banned in (\"qwen\", \"llama\", \"deepseek\", \"claude\", \"opus\"):\n            self.assertNotIn(banned, raw.lower(),\n                             f\"banned token '{banned}' in worklist bytes\")\n        # Every implementation task carries executable acceptance.\n        for task in wl[\"tasks\"]:\n            if task.get(\"kind\") == \"implementation\":\n                self.assertTrue(task.get(\"acceptanceCriteria\"),\n                                f\"{task['id']} has no acceptance criteria\")\n\n    def test_scripts_are_syntax_clean(self):\n        for sh in (ROOT / \"scripts\").glob(\"*.sh\"):\n            subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-n\", str(sh)], check=True)\n        for sh in (ROOT / \"host\").rglob(\"*.sh\"):\n            subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-n\", str(sh)], check=True)\n        for py in (ROOT / \"scripts\").glob(\"*.py\"):\n            subprocess.run([\"python3\", \"-m\", \"py_compile\", str(py)],\n                           check=True)\n\n    def test_receipts_verify_runs_clean(self):\n        proc = subprocess.run(\n            [\"python3\", str(ROOT / \"scripts/receipts-verify.py\")],\n            capture_output=True, text=True)\n        self.assertIn(proc.returncode, (0,),\n                      f\"receipts-verify failed:\\n{proc.stdout}{proc.stderr}\")\n\n    def test_notices_cover_the_import_manifest(self):\n        notices = (ROOT / \"THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md\").read_text()\n        for needle in (\"vllm-project/vllm\", \"AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm\",\n                       \"amd-strix-halo-vllm-toolboxes\", \"nix-strix-halo\"):\n            self.assertIn(needle, notices)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990192135},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting. Note `test_scripts_are_syntax_clean`: this checks `scripts/*.sh`, `host/**/*.sh`, and `scripts/*.py`. It doesn't check `bench/` yet. And `test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene` checks the banned tokens — model family names like \"qwen\", \"llama\", \"deepseek\", \"claude\", \"opus\" must not be included in the worklist byte strings. That said, this applies to the worklist file, not to my bench files. But wait — there's also `test_no_secret_material`, and there might be other tests that scan the whole tree. Let me check whether there are any tests that scan the entire tree for something I might trip over.\n\n`test_no_gpl_text_in_tree` — scans everything for GPL text. My files are fine.\n`test_no_secret_material` — scans for secret-like stuff. My files are fine.\n\nNote: there's a lint regarding model family names in the worklist byte strings, but that's specific to the worklist. However, to be safe, I should avoid model family names in my bench files too (F.5 states \"do not put model family names in spec or worklist byte strings\" — but that's about spec/worklist). For bench, I'll use environment variables or placeholders for the model path, and not hardcode the model name.\n\nAlright, let me look at the host tooling tests and the instruments tests to understand the style more, and check host/ and scripts/ to see what's available.\n\nActually, let me focus. What I need to understand is:\n1. How the bench matrix is expected to integrate with the pair service (host/).\n2. What environment variables/URLs to use.\n\nLet me check host/ and scripts/.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_71c4129596874839a9edeeee","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\necho \"=== host/ tree ===\"; find host -type f | head -50\necho \"=== scripts/ ===\"; ls -la scripts/\necho \"=== container/ ===\"; find container -type f | head -30\necho \"=== results/receipts ===\"; ls -la results/receipts/\necho \"=== docs/ ===\"; ls -la docs/"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1548,"output":518,"cacheRead":42240,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":375,"totalTokens":44306,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990192140,"responseId":"chatcmpl-3cc7a49e-7be5-4dbb-a783-319baaed5c23","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_71c4129596874839a9edeeee","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== host/ tree ===\nhost/fn-cluster-up.sh\nhost/fn-env.sh\nhost/systemd/flashnext-pair.service\nhost/fn-preflight.sh\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build-nixos.diff\nhost/rdma/SCOPING-REPORT.md\nhost/rdma/fetch-and-build.sh\nhost/rdma/attended-bringup.md\nhost/rdma/ab-protocol.md\nhost/fn-cluster-down.sh\n=== scripts/ ===\ntotal 60\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 tom users  4096 Aug 29 09:53 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 tom users  4096 Aug 29 09:53 ..\n-rwxr-xr-x  1 tom users  6887 Aug 29 09:53 build-engine.sh\n-rwxr-xr-x  1 tom users  3879 Aug 29 09:53 mirror-substrate.sh\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users  3856 Aug 29 09:53 receipts-verify.py\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users  2502 Aug 29 09:53 run-smoke.sh\n-rwxr-xr-x  1 tom users 16651 Aug 29 09:53 run-tp2.sh\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users   995 Aug 29 09:53 stage-weights-both.sh\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users  2249 Aug 29 09:53 stage-weights.sh\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users  3002 Aug 29 09:53 verify-fork.sh\n=== container/ ===\ncontainer/Containerfile\ncontainer/rootfs/fn_synctrace.py\ncontainer/rootfs/fn_offload_batch.py\ncontainer/rootfs/fn_expert_union.py\ncontainer/rootfs/.keep\ncontainer/build.sh\n=== results/receipts ===\ntotal 20\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 tom users 4096 Aug 29 09:53 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 tom users 4096 Aug 29 09:53 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users  388 Aug 29 09:53 build.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users    0 Aug 29 09:53 .gitkeep\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users  139 Aug 29 09:53 weights-coordinator.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 tom users  134 Aug 29 09:53 weights-worker.json\n=== docs/ ===\ntotal 24\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 tom users  4096 Aug 29 09:53 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 tom users  4096 Aug 29 09:53 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users  2255 Aug 29 09:53 CONTINGENCY-sampler-cu-remaining-1024-launches.patch\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users 10417 Aug 29 09:53 GATE0.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 tom users     0 Aug 29 09:53 .gitkeep\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990204821},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me look at the pair service and the host tooling to understand how the bench matrix would interact with the running service — what URL/port, env conventions, etc. Let me read `host/fn-env.sh`, `host/systemd/flashnext-pair.service`, and `scripts/run-tp2.sh` (which likely shows how a first-light request is made against the pair). These will tell me the endpoint and conventions.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7a117f8ac75c482386d1300b","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/host/fn-env.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2139,"output":151,"cacheRead":43008,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":88,"totalTokens":45298,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990204825,"responseId":"chatcmpl-83ab837b-bc4c-4ede-8c36-c047ffff6261","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7a117f8ac75c482386d1300b","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-env.sh — the flashnext pair-service env doctrine.\n#\n# Sourced by every rank-side actor: the ray head, the ray worker, `vllm\n# serve`, and the bring-up/teardown/preflight scripts. Both TP ranks MUST see\n# the same doctrine — fn-preflight.sh byte-diffs the exported env across the\n# pair before first light, and VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY below\n# is what carries FN_* across ray to the worker rank (not in ray's default\n# copy prefixes; without it the two TP ranks diverge).\n#\n# Discipline lifted from the ds4-vllm estate's host/ds4-cluster-env.sh\n# (specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md §5) and the fleet's\n# lowlat-cluster doctrine (specs/flashnext/evidence/dotfiles-observed.md §1–2).\n# Safe to source repeatedly; every default is override-able via the environment.\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# HARD RULE — spec F.9: NEVER export an environment default the engine reads\n# through an is-set probe. VLLM_USE_DEEP_GEMM, VLLM_MOE_USE_DEEP_GEMM and the\n# VLLM_ROCM_USE_AITER* family are read via is_set() probes inside the fork:\n# EXPORTING THE DEFAULT DIVERTS THE ORACLE INTO A HARD RAISE. If a knob here\n# looks like \"just pinning the default\", it is not that — leave it unset.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# --- the FN_ configuration surface (propagated to the worker rank by ray) --\nexport FN_STATE_DIR=\"${FN_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/flashnext}\"\nexport FN_MODEL_DIR=\"${FN_MODEL_DIR:-/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8}\"\nexport FN_SERVED_NAME=\"${FN_SERVED_NAME:-flashnext}\"\nexport FN_PORT=\"${FN_PORT:-1234}\"\nexport FN_RAY_PORT=\"${FN_RAY_PORT:-6379}\"\nexport FN_MAX_CTX=\"${FN_MAX_CTX:-262144}\"\nexport FN_GPU_UTIL=\"${FN_GPU_UTIL:-0.83}\"\nexport FN_IMAGE=\"${FN_IMAGE:-flashnext:dev}\"\nexport FN_CONTAINER=\"${FN_CONTAINER:-flashnext-pair}\"\n# Worker-side actions ride the ethernet wire on the STABLE fleet identity,\n# never a 10.99.0.x Thunderbolt rail — the rails carry tensors only\n# (dotfiles-observed.md §2.3: admin traffic prefers the 5GbE cable).\nexport FN_WORKER_HOST=\"${FN_WORKER_HOST:-10.99.9.2}\"\nexport FN_HEAD_IP=\"${FN_HEAD_IP:-10.99.9.1}\"\n# The fleet latency budget from modules/lowlat-cluster.nix: 200 us sits well\n# above the measured held figure and well below the unheld one.\nexport FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US=\"${FN_LATENCY_BUDGET_US:-200}\"\n\n# --- determinism -------------------------------------------------------------\n# vLLM derives NONE_HASH (the prefix-cache chain seed) from PYTHONHASHSEED:\n# without a fixed value identical token content produces different block\n# filenames every run and the disk cache can never hit across a restart.\n# The greedy byte-compare in run-tp2.sh leans on this too.\nexport PYTHONHASHSEED=0\n\n# --- allocator ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# GC is off by default on a UMA box; 0.85 of ~124 GiB means reclaim starts at\n# ~105 GiB. expandable_segments is the fragmentation half. Safe with\n# --enforce-eager (no graph capture), which is what first light runs.\nexport PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF=\"${PYTORCH_HIP_ALLOC_CONF:-expandable_segments:True,garbage_collection_threshold:0.85}\"\n\n# --- HSA ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n# TheRock ROCm busy-polls (rocr InterruptSignal::WaitRelaxed) otherwise, i.e.\n# ~2-3 cores spinning during inference and CPU thermal throttling on a pair\n# whose PM QoS is held for latency. Set FN_HSA_INTERRUPT=0 to revert to spin.\nexport HSA_ENABLE_INTERRUPT=\"${FN_HSA_INTERRUPT:-1}\"\n\n# --- compiler caches under the STATE directory, never tmpfs ------------------\n# The defaults land under /tmp (tmpfs), and the first bring-up after a boot\n# then recompiles every kernel — ~25 min CPU-bound in LLVM before the API can\n# answer. The state directory is on-disk on both nodes.\nmkdir -p \"$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor\" \"$FN_STATE_DIR/triton\" 2>/dev/null || true\nexport TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR=\"${TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/torchinductor}\"\nexport TRITON_CACHE_DIR=\"${TRITON_CACHE_DIR:-$FN_STATE_DIR/triton}\"\n\n# --- NCCL: the transport-of-record block --------------------------------------\n# Socket transport on both rails is the overnight transport of record (spec\n# ruling P7); the RDMA package under host/rdma is operator-attended only, and\n# only after a committed socket-transport benchmark is banked.\n#\n# NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME is COMPUTED from `ip -br addr`: only Thunderbolt rails\n# that actually carry a routable /30 IP qualify. Rail 0 (thunderbolt0,\n# 10.99.0.x) is guaranteed; rail 1 (thunderbolt1) is trained but\n# IP-unconfigured today (dotfiles modules/lowlat-cluster.nix parks it) and\n# MUST NOT be listed until it has a peer IP — a peerless link-local rail in\n# the list hangs RCCL bootstrap. We log which rails we chose rather than\n# hardcoding both.\nfn_choose_rails() {\n  local rail addr chosen=\"\"\n  for rail in thunderbolt0 thunderbolt1; do\n    if [ ! -e \"/sys/class/net/$rail\" ]; then\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail absent on this node; not listed\" >&2\n      continue\n    fi\n    addr=\"$(ip -br -4 addr show dev \"$rail\" 2>/dev/null \\\n      | grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' \\\n      | grep -Ev '^(169\\.254\\.|127\\.)' | head -n1 || true)\"\n    if [ -n \"$addr\" ]; then\n      chosen=\"${chosen:+$chosen,}$rail\"\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail LISTED in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME ($addr is routable)\" >&2\n    else\n      echo \"fn-env: rail $rail NOT listed (no routable peer IP; a peerless rail in NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME hangs RCCL bootstrap)\" >&2\n    fi\n  done\n  printf '%s' \"$chosen\"\n}\nexport NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=\"${NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME:-$(fn_choose_rails)}\"\nif [ -z \"$NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME\" ]; then\n  echo \"fn-env: FATAL: no Thunderbolt rail carries a routable IP; refusing to name a phantom transport\" >&2\n  return 1 2>/dev/null || exit 1\nfi\n\n# NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1 UNCONDITIONALLY. Since the pre-arm bake an ibverbs device\n# exists on the rails of BOTH nodes (usb4_rdma0 + usb4_rdma5, by design);\n# without this pin RCCL autodetects verbs and silently rides unproven RDMA.\n# Sockets stay the transport of record until the attended morning A/B lands a\n# counterbalanced verdict (host/rdma/ab-protocol.md). Do NOT conditionalize\n# this on device detection — the devices are present by design.\nexport NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1\n\n# Cold-kernel-cache bring-up is ~25 min of LLVM before the first collective;\n# the collective timeouts must survive that without a watchdog kill.\nexport TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC=\"${TORCH_NCCL_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_SEC:-2400}\"\nexport TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING=\"${TORCH_NCCL_ENABLE_MONITORING:-0}\"\nexport NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS=\"${NCCL_TIMEOUT_MS:-2400000}\"\n\n# --- ray ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Propagate FN_* to the worker rank through ray. ESSENTIAL: without this the\n# two TP ranks diverge on every FN_ knob above.\nexport VLLM_RAY_EXTRA_ENV_VAR_PREFIXES_TO_COPY=FN_\n# Ray and ROCr visible-device handling fight on multi-rank boxes; opt out.\nexport RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"${RAY_EXPERIMENTAL_NOSET_ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-1}\"\n# On a 128 GB unified-memory box the serve legitimately claims most memory;\n# ray's OOM monitor must not reap the rank. Refresh 0 disables the monitor.\nexport RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms=\"${RAY_memory_monitor_refresh_ms:-0}\"\n# `ray start` pre-starts one idle python worker per CPU (~40 MB each); on a\n# 32-thread box that is over a gigabyte vLLM never touches. Cap the pool\n# small; fn-cluster-up.sh passes this as --num-cpus on both nodes.\nexport RAY_NUM_CPUS=\"${RAY_NUM_CPUS:-4}\"\n\n# --- the table path ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Serve the engram table from NVMe via mmap: page-cache faults serve gathers,\n# zero table bytes GPU-resident (spec ruling P11's zero-GPU-residency bound\n# leans on this). VLLM_PLE_MMAP=0 is the fork's kill switch.\nexport VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1\n\n# --- estate hygiene -------------------------------------------------------------\n# Runtime hub downloads stay forbidden (spec F.6): the checkpoint is staged\n# library-to-node, and the engine must never reach for the hub at runtime.\nexport HF_HUB_OFFLINE=\"${HF_HUB_OFFLINE:-1}\"\nexport VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS=\"${VLLM_NO_USAGE_STATS:-1}\"\nexport VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK=\"${VLLM_DO_NOT_TRACK:-1}\"\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990209786},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is very helpful. Now I understand the env conventions:\n- `FN_PORT` defaults to 1234\n- `FN_SERVED_NAME` = flashnext\n- The endpoint is `http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT/v1/completions` presumably\n- The model dir is `FN_MODEL_DIR`\n\nLet me look at `scripts/run-tp2.sh` to see how a request is made against the pair (first light), which will inform my client's request shape and the metrics endpoint.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_37b119bbe75c48768e7dfd2d","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/scripts/run-tp2.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":2530,"output":178,"cacheRead":45056,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":114,"totalTokens":47764,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990209791,"responseId":"chatcmpl-55f0923d-2e6a-4d5a-b561-512ef40edc7f","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_37b119bbe75c48768e7dfd2d","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/run-tp2.sh — flashnext first-light runner on the pair.\n#\n# Sequence (spec claims 4.2–4.5):\n#   preflight -> pair up -> two greedy 300-token completions byte-compared\n#   (tp2.json) -> residency read after 50 warmed decode tokens (residency.json,\n#   ruling P11) -> fidelity baseline: fixed-prompt losses and frontier logits\n#   under results/ (fidelity.json) -> the 262144-context probe (context.json).\n#\n# Every receipt is one JSON object {\"step\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\"} exactly as\n# scripts/receipts-verify.py expects. A step that cannot complete writes a\n# fail receipt (graded, never silently skipped — spec F.12) and the runner\n# exits non-zero.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\" && pwd)\"\nHOST_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/host\"\nRECEIPTS=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/receipts\"\nFIDELITY_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/results/fidelity\"\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$HOST_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nmkdir -p \"$RECEIPTS\" \"$FIDELITY_DIR\"\nexport RECEIPTS\nTMP=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$TMP\"' EXIT\n\nlog() { echo \"run-tp2: $*\" >&2; }\n\n# --- preflight, then stand the pair -------------------------------------------\nlog \"preflight\"\nbash \"$HOST_DIR/fn-preflight.sh\"\n\nlog \"standing the pair service\"\nbash \"$HOST_DIR/fn-cluster-up.sh\"\n\nexport FN_API=\"http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT\"\n\n# --- step tp2: two greedy 300-token completions, byte-compared -----------------\nlog \"step tp2: greedy byte-compare\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nPROMPT = (\"The pair boots before the operator arrives, and the ledger \"\n          \"records what the night proved: \")\n\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n\ndef greedy_300():\n    return post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": PROMPT,\n                                    \"max_tokens\": 300, \"temperature\": 0})\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\ndata = {}\ntry:\n    a, b = greedy_300(), greedy_300()\n    ta = a[\"choices\"][0][\"text\"].encode()\n    tb = b[\"choices\"][0][\"text\"].encode()\n    data[\"byte_identical_repeat\"] = ta == tb\n    data[\"completion_bytes\"] = len(ta)\n    data[\"completion_tokens\"] = a[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"]\n    if ta != tb:\n        status = \"fail\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"error\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n    data[\"byte_identical_repeat\"] = False\n\n# Fold the preflight record into the tp2 receipt (U.2: held sub-budget round\n# trips on both ends ride with the tp2 claim).\ntry:\n    pre = json.load(open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"preflight.json\")))\n    data[\"preflight\"] = pre[\"data\"]\nexcept Exception as e:\n    data[\"preflight\"] = f\"unreadable: {e}\"\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"tp2\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": data},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"tp2.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: tp2 receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\n# --- step residency: read after 50 warmed decode tokens, per ruling P11 --------\nlog \"step residency: warmed decode, then per-rank readings\"\ncat > \"$TMP/rank_metrics.py\" <<'PY'\n\"\"\"Per-rank residency probes: GTT from sysfs, process RSS, table page-cache\nresidency. Stdlib only; runs on either node (local or over ssh stdin).\"\"\"\nimport fnmatch, glob, json, os, shutil, subprocess\n\ndef first_int(paths):\n    for p in paths:\n        try:\n            return int(open(p).read().strip())\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n    return None\n\ndef gtt():\n    # GTT usage first; on the unified-memory APU the VRAM reading is the\n    # fallback (patch 0004 re-points VRAM reporting at unified memory).\n    for name, label in ((\"mem_info_gtt_used\", \"gtt\"),\n                        (\"mem_info_vram_used\", \"vram\")):\n        v = first_int(sorted(glob.glob(\"/sys/class/drm/card*/device/\" + name)))\n        if v is not None:\n            return v, label\n    return None, None\n\ndef rss_bytes():\n    total = 0\n    for pid in os.listdir(\"/proc\"):\n        if not pid.isdigit():\n            continue\n        try:\n            cmd = open(\"/proc/%s/cmdline\" % pid, \"rb\").read().decode(errors=\"ignore\")\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        if \"vllm serve\" not in cmd and \"ray::\" not in cmd:\n            continue\n        try:\n            status = open(\"/proc/%s/status\" % pid).read()\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        for line in status.splitlines():\n            if line.startswith(\"VmRSS:\"):\n                total += int(line.split()[1]) * 1024\n    return total\n\ndef table_cache():\n    patterns = [p.strip().lower() for p in\n                os.environ.get(\"FN_TABLE_GLOB\", \"*ple*,*engram*,*ngram*\").split(\",\")\n                if p.strip()]\n    root = os.environ.get(\"FN_MODEL_DIR\", \"/var/lib/local-models/flashnext-fp8\")\n    matched = []\n    for dirpath, _dirs, files in os.walk(root):\n        for f in files:\n            if any(fnmatch.fnmatch(f.lower(), pat) for pat in patterns):\n                matched.append(os.path.join(dirpath, f))\n    fincore = shutil.which(\"fincore\")\n    pages, ok = 0, False\n    if fincore:\n        ok = True\n        for m in matched:\n            out = subprocess.run([fincore, m], capture_output=True, text=True,\n                                 check=False).stdout\n            for line in out.splitlines():\n                parts = line.split()\n                if len(parts) >= 2 and parts[1].isdigit():\n                    pages += int(parts[1])\n                    break\n    return {\"files_matched\": len(matched), \"files\": matched[:8],\n            \"cached_pages\": pages if ok else None,\n            \"tool\": \"fincore\" if ok else None}\n\ngtt_bytes, gtt_src = gtt()\nprint(json.dumps({\"gtt_bytes\": gtt_bytes, \"gtt_source\": gtt_src,\n                  \"rss_bytes\": rss_bytes(), \"table\": table_cache()}))\nPY\n\nexport FN_METRICS=\"$TMP/rank_metrics.py\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, subprocess, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nWORKER = os.environ[\"FN_WORKER_HOST\"]\nSERVE_LOG = os.path.join(os.environ[\"FN_STATE_DIR\"], \"serve.log\")\nMETRICS = os.environ[\"FN_METRICS\"]\nPAGE = 4096\n\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\ndata = {}\ntry:\n    # Compile pass, then a decode long enough to be called warmed (>50 tokens).\n    post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL,\n                             \"prompt\": \"Warm the decode path: \",\n                             \"max_tokens\": 16, \"temperature\": 0})\n    r = post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL,\n                                 \"prompt\": \"Warm the decode path further: \",\n                                 \"max_tokens\": 64, \"temperature\": 0})\n    warmed = int(r[\"usage\"][\"completion_tokens\"])\n    data[\"warmed_decode_tokens\"] = warmed\n    data[\"read_after_warmed_decode\"] = warmed >= 50\n    if warmed < 50:\n        status = \"fail\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"read_after_warmed_decode\"] = False\n    data[\"error_warmup\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\nmetrics_src = open(METRICS).read()\ndef probe(node):\n    if node == \"coordinator\":\n        out = subprocess.run([sys.executable, METRICS], capture_output=True,\n                             text=True, check=True).stdout\n    else:\n        out = subprocess.run([\"ssh\", WORKER, \"python3\", \"-\"],\n                             input=metrics_src.encode(), capture_output=True,\n                             text=True, check=True).stdout\n    return json.loads(out.strip().splitlines()[-1])\n\ntry:\n    per_rank = {n: probe(n) for n in (\"coordinator\", \"worker\")}\n    data[\"gtt_gib_per_rank\"] = {\n        n: round(m[\"gtt_bytes\"] / 2**30, 2)\n        for n, m in per_rank.items() if m[\"gtt_bytes\"] is not None}\n    data[\"gtt_source_per_rank\"] = {\n        n: m[\"gtt_source\"] for n, m in per_rank.items()}\n    data[\"rss_gib_per_rank\"] = {\n        n: round(m[\"rss_bytes\"] / 2**30, 2) for n, m in per_rank.items()}\n    data[\"table_page_cache\"] = {\n        n: {**per_rank[n][\"table\"],\n            \"cached_gib\": (None if per_rank[n][\"table\"][\"cached_pages\"] is None\n                           else round(per_rank[n][\"table\"][\"cached_pages\"] * PAGE / 2**30, 2))}\n        for n in per_rank}\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"error_metrics\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\n# The mmap engagement probe: zero table bytes GPU-resident only if the table\n# path actually engaged in this run's own serve log under VLLM_PLE_MMAP=1.\ntry:\n    log_text = open(SERVE_LOG, errors=\"ignore\").read().lower()\n    engaged = (\"ple_mmap\" in log_text\n               and os.environ.get(\"VLLM_PLE_MMAP\") == \"1\")\n    data[\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\"] = 0 if engaged else None\n    data[\"ple_mmap_log_lines\"] = log_text.count(\"ple_mmap\")\n    if not engaged:\n        status = \"fail\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"table_gpu_resident_bytes\"] = None\n    data[\"error_ple_probe\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"residency\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": data},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"residency.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: residency receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\n# --- step fidelity: fixed-prompt losses and frontier logits ---------------------\nlog \"step fidelity: reference losses and frontier logits\"\nexport FIDELITY_DIR\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport hashlib, json, os, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nOUT_DIR = os.environ[\"FIDELITY_DIR\"]\nPROMPTS = [\n    \"The fleet measures before it claims, because \",\n    \"A receipt is a fact the morning operator can check: \",\n    \"Two ranks agree byte for byte when \",\n]\n\ndef post(path, body, timeout=3600):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + path, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return json.loads(r.read())\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\nfiles, hashes, losses = [], {}, []\nfor i, prompt in enumerate(PROMPTS):\n    name = f\"fidelity-{i}.json\"\n    path = os.path.join(OUT_DIR, name)\n    try:\n        r = post(\"/v1/completions\", {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt,\n                                     \"max_tokens\": 64, \"temperature\": 0,\n                                     \"logprobs\": 5})\n        choice = r[\"choices\"][0]\n        token_logprobs = [x for x in choice[\"logprobs\"][\"token_logprobs\"]\n                          if x is not None]\n        record = {\n            \"model\": MODEL,\n            \"prompt\": prompt,\n            \"text\": choice[\"text\"],\n            \"tokens\": choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"],\n            # Reference loss: mean negative log-likelihood of the fixed\n            # continuation under the as-served stack.\n            \"loss_nll\": -sum(token_logprobs) / len(token_logprobs),\n            # Frontier logits: the top-5 at the final generated token — the\n            # yardstick later changes must not move without a reason.\n            \"frontier_logits\": {\n                \"token\": choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"][-1],\n                \"top5\": choice[\"logprobs\"][\"top_logprobs\"][-1],\n            },\n        }\n        losses.append(record[\"loss_nll\"])\n        with open(path, \"w\") as f:\n            json.dump(record, f, indent=1)\n    except Exception as e:\n        status = \"fail\"\n        record = {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt,\n                  \"error\": f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"}\n        with open(path, \"w\") as f:\n            json.dump(record, f, indent=1)\n    files.append(name)\n    hashes[name] = hashlib.sha256(open(path, \"rb\").read()).hexdigest()\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"fidelity\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": {\"files\": files, \"sha256\": hashes, \"losses_nll\": losses,\n                    \"prompts\": len(PROMPTS)}},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"fidelity.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: fidelity receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\n# --- step context: the 262144-context probe -------------------------------------\nlog \"step context: decode at full context vs short context\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, os, sys, time, urllib.request\n\nAPI = os.environ[\"FN_API\"]\nMODEL = os.environ[\"FN_SERVED_NAME\"]\nRECEIPTS = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nTARGET = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_CONTEXT_TARGET\", \"262144\"))\nDECODE = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_CTX_DECODE\", \"128\"))\n# Claim 4.5's given context depth. The prompt is synthesized from a fixed\n# paragraph: tokenization is engine-side, so we verify the actual prompt\n# tokens the server reports and re-pad once if we undershot.\nPARAGRAPH = (\"The ledger records what the night proved. Every receipt is a \"\n             \"fact the morning operator can check. The pair serves from its \"\n             \"own drives; the rails carry tensors and the wire carries \"\n             \"control. Nothing is narrated that was not measured. \")\n\ndef stream_completion(prompt, max_tokens, timeout=7200):\n    body = {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": prompt, \"max_tokens\": max_tokens,\n            \"temperature\": 0, \"stream\": True,\n            \"stream_options\": {\"include_usage\": True}}\n    req = urllib.request.Request(API + \"/v1/completions\",\n                                 data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    t_first = t_last = None\n    usage = None\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        for raw in r:\n            line = raw.decode(errors=\"ignore\").strip()\n            if not line.startswith(\"data:\"):\n                continue\n            payload = line[5:].strip()\n            if payload == \"[DONE]\":\n                break\n            chunk = json.loads(payload)\n            if chunk.get(\"usage\"):\n                usage = chunk[\"usage\"]\n            for choice in chunk.get(\"choices\") or []:\n                if choice.get(\"text\"):\n                    now = time.time()\n                    if t_first is None:\n                        t_first = now\n                    t_last = now\n    return usage, t_first, t_last\n\ndef decode_tps(prompt, max_tokens):\n    usage, t_first, t_last = stream_completion(prompt, max_tokens)\n    if not usage or t_first is None or t_last is None or t_last <= t_first:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"no usable decode window in the streamed response\")\n    tokens = usage[\"completion_tokens\"]\n    return (tokens - 1) / (t_last - t_first), usage[\"prompt_tokens\"]\n\nstatus = \"pass\"\ndata = {\"target_context\": TARGET}\ntry:\n    short_tps, short_prompt_tokens = decode_tps(\n        \"Measure the short-context decode: \" + PARAGRAPH, DECODE)\n    data[\"short_prompt_tokens\"] = short_prompt_tokens\n    data[\"short_decode_tps\"] = round(short_tps, 3)\n\n    est_chars_per_token = 4.2  # conservative: undershoot tokens, never the cap\n    long_prompt = PARAGRAPH * int(((TARGET - 2 * DECODE) * est_chars_per_token)\n                                  / len(PARAGRAPH) + 1)\n    long_tps, long_prompt_tokens = decode_tps(long_prompt, DECODE)\n    if long_prompt_tokens < TARGET - 1024:  # undershot: re-pad once, exactly\n        long_prompt = long_prompt * int((TARGET - DECODE) / max(long_prompt_tokens, 1)) + PARAGRAPH\n        long_tps, long_prompt_tokens = decode_tps(long_prompt, DECODE)\n    data[\"long_prompt_tokens\"] = long_prompt_tokens\n    data[\"long_decode_tps\"] = round(long_tps, 3)\n    data[\"decode_ratio_vs_short_context\"] = round(long_tps / short_tps, 4)\n    if long_prompt_tokens < TARGET - 1024:\n        status = \"fail\"\n        data[\"error\"] = f\"server accepted only {long_prompt_tokens} prompt tokens\"\nexcept Exception as e:\n    status = \"fail\"\n    data[\"error\"] = f\"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\"\n\njson.dump({\"step\": \"context\", \"status\": status, \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n           \"data\": data},\n          open(os.path.join(RECEIPTS, \"context.json\"), \"w\"), indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-tp2: context receipt status={status}\", file=sys.stderr)\nsys.exit(0 if status == \"pass\" else 1)\nPY\n\nlog \"first light complete; receipts: $RECEIPTS/{tp2,residency,fidelity,context}.json\"\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990215178},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is extremely useful. Now I understand the house style and conventions:\n\n1. API endpoint: `FN_API = http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT`, completions at `/v1/completions`, metrics presumably at `/metrics`.\n2. Model name: `FN_SERVED_NAME` (default \"flashnext\").\n3. Streaming SSE parsing pattern: read lines starting with `data:`, parse JSON, `stream_options: {\"include_usage\": True}` to get usage including prompt_tokens and completion_tokens.\n4. Timestamps with `time.time()` or `time.perf_counter()`.\n5. Receipts: `{\"step\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\"}` JSON.\n\nImportantly, vLLM's `/metrics` endpoint. Let me confirm: in run-tp2.sh the metrics are probed from the OS (sysfs), not from vLLM's /metrics. But my client needs to scrape vLLM's `/metrics` endpoint. vLLM exposes Prometheus metrics at `/metrics` by default (unless disabled). Good.\n\nNow, for token IDs: note that in run-tp2.sh's fidelity step, they request `logprobs: 5` and get `choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"]` — that's a list of token strings, not IDs. So to get token IDs, we'd need a different approach.\n\nHmm, but the task says \"sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token ids\". Let me think about how vLLM can return token IDs.\n\nOption 1: vLLM's `/v1/completions` doesn't return token IDs directly in the standard OpenAI response. But it can if... Actually, vLLM has an extension. Let me think.\n\nActually — there's a cleaner way. vLLM supports `prompt_token_ids` as input. And for output, if we want token IDs, we can use the `logprobs` field which returns token strings but not IDs.\n\nWait, actually vLLM DOES return token IDs in some cases. Let me recall the OpenAI completions response format from vLLM. The standard `CompletionResponse` has `choices: [{text, index, logprobs, finish_reason, stop_reason}]`. No token_ids.\n\nBut vLLM has a feature: if you set `echo` or use the raw engine, you get token IDs. Actually the cleanest: vLLM's serving completions supports a `logprobs` returning tokens as strings.\n\nHmm, but actually — I recall that newer vLLM versions added `prompt_token_ids` to the request and there might be token_ids in... Let me not rely on uncertain API details.\n\nLet me reconsider. The fingerprint needs to be over \"token ids\". Given the constraints and that this is a bench harness that will run against real vLLM, I should get token IDs reliably. \n\nThe most robust approach in vLLM: request with `logprobs` set, and the response includes `logprobs.tokens` (strings) and `logprobs.text_offset`. But not IDs.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether vLLM returns token IDs anywhere. \n\nThere's actually a vLLM-specific feature: the `/v1/completions` endpoint, when given `extra_body` or... no.\n\nLet me think about the deterministic fingerprint requirement from the steering. The steering says: \"if any token fingerprint diverges across runs of the same arm, the harness must byte-compare a serial replay\". So the fingerprint needs to be a deterministic function of the completion that would be stable across runs when the engine is deterministic (temperature=0, greedy). And it should catch QSA-gather wrong-output divergences.\n\nFor fingerprinting, what matters is:\n1. It's deterministic for identical outputs.\n2. It changes when the output changes.\n3. It's based on token IDs ideally.\n\nGiven vLLM returns token *strings* via logprobs, and those are deterministic for greedy decoding, fingerprinting over the token strings (or the completion text) would work for divergence detection. But the task explicitly says \"token ids\".\n\nLet me look at whether vLLM provides token IDs. Actually, I now recall: vLLM's `CompletionResponseStreamChoice` and `CompletionResponseChoice` do NOT include token_ids in the OpenAI-compatible layer. BUT — vLLM has a way to get them.\n\nActually, the cleanest robust method: use `logprobs` and hash the `tokens` list from logprobs. But that's token strings.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider — maybe I should request the raw token IDs through a different mechanism. Actually, you know what, let me reconsider the whole fingerprinting approach to be robust:\n\nI'll compute the fingerprint over the token IDs if the server provides them (some vLLM builds/configs include them), and fall back to the token strings from logprobs, and document clearly. But to be safe and match \"token ids\", let me see if there's a way.\n\nActually — I just recalled. vLLM DOES support returning `token_ids` if you send `prompt_token_ids` in the request OR through the `CompletionResponse`. Let me check... No.\n\nLet me take yet another approach. Let me look at what vLLM actually exposes. Since I can't run vLLM here, let me be pragmatic: I'll design the fingerprint to hash the completion's token sequence. To get token IDs, I'll request `logprobs` and also try to use the `completion_tokens` usage. But the most reliable \"token id\" source in vLLM's OpenAI API is actually...\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider. There IS a clean way to get token IDs from vLLM: set `\"logprobs\": 1` and the response's `logprobs` dict contains `\"tokens\"` (the token strings). But there's also, in some versions, a way via `echo`.\n\nOK you know what, I think the pragmatic and honest approach is:\n- Request with `logprobs` enabled.\n- Collect the token sequence. vLLM returns token strings in `logprobs.tokens`.\n- BUT the task wants token IDs. Let me check if vLLM returns them.\n\nLet me actually search my memory harder: In vLLM, the OpenAI completions response `CompletionResponseChoice` has these fields: `index`, `text`, `logprobs`, `finish_reason`, `stop_reason`, and in newer versions `prompt_token_ids` is NOT there. \n\nHmm. Actually, I found it: vLLM has a feature where if you pass `\"extra_body\": {\"return_token_ids\": true}` ... no that's not real either.\n\nLet me just be pragmatic and design it so that:\n1. We request `logprobs` (so we get per-token data).\n2. We build the token sequence from whatever the server returns. We'll prefer explicit token IDs if present (checking a few possible field locations), and otherwise derive a stable fingerprint from the logprobs token strings + text.\n3. The fingerprint is sha256 over the canonical token sequence.\n\nActually, I realize I'm overthinking. Let me reconsider what \"token ids\" means here and what's realistic. \n\nGiven the reference harness (nix-strix-halo) and vLLM, and that the campaign uses a vLLM fork, the most reliable way to get token IDs is... Actually, I'll make a decision: I'll request logprobs and reconstruct. But actually, the simplest robust thing that gives real token IDs:\n\nvLLM supports `prompt_token_ids` as INPUT. For OUTPUT token IDs, I recall vLLM added a field. Let me just handle it generically: parse each streaming chunk, and for the fingerprint accumulate `choice.get(\"token_id\")` if present, else the text. Actually no.\n\nLet me step back. The critical acceptance criterion is:\n```\npython3 -m py_compile bench/fn-stream-client.py && bash -n bench/run-matrix.sh && python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v\n```\n\nSo the test `tests.test_bench_client` must pass. I control what that test does. The test is about the *parser* separating queue and processing times — NOT about live token IDs. So the fingerprint logic just needs to be syntactically valid and reasonable; it doesn't need to be exercised by the unit test.\n\nSo I'll implement the fingerprint over token IDs with a robust extraction that:\n- Tries to get token IDs from the streaming chunks (vLLM may include them in `chunk[\"choices\"][0]` under various keys, or via logprobs).\n- Falls back to hashing the concatenated token text if IDs aren't available.\n- Documents the approach.\n\nActually, let me make the fingerprint genuinely about token IDs by leveraging vLLM's actual capability. Let me recall definitively: \n\nvLLM OpenAI completions API: When you set `logprobs`, each returned `LogProbs` object has: `text_offset`, `token_logprobs`, `tokens`, `top_logprobs`. The `tokens` are strings.\n\nThere's NO token ID in the OpenAI completions response in standard vLLM.\n\nHowever — vLLM has a separate thing. The `CompletionRequest` accepts `prompt_token_ids`. And there's actually a response field... \n\nOK let me just go with a clean, honest design: I'll get token IDs by requesting `logprobs` and using the token strings as the token sequence representation, OR better — I'll make the client able to fingerprint over whatever token-level data the server provides, and I'll name the function and document that it fingerprints the completion's token sequence (token ids when the server provides them, token strings otherwise). Given greedy decoding determinism, this catches divergence either way.\n\nHmm, but I want to honor \"token ids\" literally. Let me reconsider — actually there IS a clean way. Let me reconsider using vLLM's `/v1/completions` with `echo` and the fact that... no.\n\nActually — I'll do this: I'll make the fingerprint function accept a list of token ids (ints). The streaming collector will extract token ids. To actually get token ids from vLLM, I'll note that recent vLLM versions DO support returning them. Let me just check for the field in a few places and use logprobs token strings as the documented fallback, computing a stable fingerprint. I'll make the code try `choice.get(\"token_ids\")` / logprobs, and I'll keep it robust.\n\nWait, actually — the cleanest honest approach that definitely works: use vLLM's native tokenization by requesting with `logprobs` and hashing the `tokens` array (token strings). But let me reconsider once more if token ids are available.\n\nYou know, I've spent enough time. Let me make a robust decision:\n\n**Fingerprint design:** The client collects the completion tokens. It requests `logprobs=1` to force per-token metadata. It builds a canonical byte string from the token sequence:\n- If the server provides integer token ids (checked in the chunk's choice), use those.\n- Otherwise, use the logprobs `tokens` strings (which ARE the token pieces and are deterministic under greedy).\nThe sha256 of that canonical encoding is the fingerprint.\n\nI'll write a helper `completion_fingerprint(token_ids)` that hashes a list of ints, and a path that extracts ints. And I'll make the extraction try hard to get real ids. Since vLLM's completions streaming doesn't easily give ids, I'll ALSO support getting them via the non-streaming final usage... no.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider entirely. Let me reconsider: maybe the intended approach is simpler than I think. The task says \"a sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token ids.\" \n\nThe most robust way to get token IDs from vLLM is to make a request and read `prompt_token_ids` / completion token ids. Actually — vLLM DOES return these in the `CompletionResponse` under certain conditions. Let me recall the actual vLLM code.\n\nIn `vllm/entrypoints/openai/protocol.py`, `CompletionResponseChoice` has:\n```python\nclass CompletionResponseChoice(BaseModel):\n    index: int\n    text: str\n    logprobs: Optional[LogProbs] = None\n    finish_reason: ...\n    stop_reason: ...\n    prompt_token_ids: Optional[List[int]] = None  # added in some versions\n```\n\nHmm, actually I'm not 100% sure `prompt_token_ids` is in the response choice. \n\nLet me just make it robust and move on. I'll extract token ids defensively and fall back to text. This is a bench harness; the important thing is a deterministic, comparable fingerprint. I'll document clearly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider using a totally clean approach that I'm confident works with vLLM: **request token IDs via `logprobs` is strings, but I can get integer ids by using the tokenizer?** No, the client doesn't have the tokenizer.\n\nOK final decision: I'll implement the fingerprint to operate on token IDs, and I'll get token IDs from vLLM by using the completions endpoint's ability to return them. Let me check: actually, I'm now fairly confident that vLLM's `/v1/completions` does NOT return completion token IDs in the OpenAI-compatible response by default. \n\nGiven all this uncertainty, the honest and robust engineering choice is:\n- The client requests completions with `logprobs` so each token carries metadata.\n- It builds the fingerprint from the token sequence. It prefers integer token ids if the server provides them; otherwise it uses the logprobs token strings. Both are deterministic under greedy decoding and will catch the QSA divergence the steering cares about.\n- I'll clearly document this in the CSV header and code comments.\n\nLet me write the fingerprint to hash a normalized token sequence. I'll extract whatever token identifiers are available.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether I even need live token extraction to be perfect. The steering's QSA protection is about comparing fingerprints across runs and doing a serial replay. That logic lives in run-matrix.sh (or the client). The fingerprint just needs to be computable. For the unit test, I don't test the fingerprint. So I have latitude.\n\nLet me make the fingerprint extraction clean: I'll accumulate `token_id` from each streaming chunk if vLLM provides it in `choice`, else accumulate the `text` delta and hash the full text. I'll request `logprobs` to have per-token granularity. I'll document that the fingerprint is over the completion's token sequence.\n\nHmm, but let me try to actually get real token IDs. Let me reconsider: I'll add `\"extra_body\": {\"return_token_ids\": ...}` — no.\n\nLet me just go. I'll implement:\n- `collect_stream` returns `(token_ids: list[int] | None, text: str, usage, t_first, t_last)`.\n- token_ids extracted from chunks: vLLM streaming completion chunks, when `logprobs` requested, include `logprobs.tokens`. Some vLLM versions include the token id. I'll try `choice.get(\"token_id\")` and `chunk`-level. If none, token_ids stays None.\n- `fingerprint = sha256(canonical(token_ids if token_ids is not None else text_tokens))`.\n\nFor canonical encoding of a list of ints: join with commas, or struct.pack. I'll do a stable repr: for ints, `\",\".join(str(i))`; for text, the utf-8 bytes. Actually to make it truly \"token ids\", I'll hash the struct-packed ints.\n\nOK let me also reconsider: the task says \"sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token ids\" — so per completion (per request), compute sha256 of its token ids. In the CSV, each row is a request, and there's a column for the fingerprint. And in bench.json, there's a `fingerprints` aggregate.\n\nGood. Now let me also handle the concurrency requirement from the steering. The matrix runs arms at some concurrency. Each output row must record the concurrency (number of in-flight sequences). And if fingerprints diverge across the loads of an arm, do a serial replay (concurrency=1) and flag the arm suspect.\n\nLet me now design run-matrix.sh.\n\n### bench/run-matrix.sh design\n\nRequirements:\n- Counterbalanced interleaved arms A-B-B-A.\n- Three loads per arm.\n- Medians reported.\n- Depth series: 0, 10240, 102400 tokens.\n- Speculative decoding on and off arms.\n- Results committed under results/.\n- bench.json receipt: `{loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}`.\n- Steering: record concurrency per arm per row; on fingerprint divergence, serial replay + flag suspect.\n\nWait — \"depth series 0, 10240, 102400 tokens\" and \"speculative decoding on and off arms\". So the arms are combinations? Let me think about what an \"arm\" is.\n\nRuling P12: \"three loads per arm, interleaved arms, medians, token fingerprints; depth series 0, 10240, 102400; speculative on and off arms; rows committed under results/\".\n\nSo the matrix has:\n- A depth series (prompt depths): 0, 10240, 102400 tokens.\n- Speculative decoding: on and off.\n\nAn \"arm\" is a (speculative-on/off) configuration. The depth series is swept within each arm? Or are arms (depth × spec)? \n\nThe task says: \"depth series 0, 10240, 102400 tokens, speculative decoding on and off arms\". And \"arms interleaved A-B-B-A\". So there are two arms: A = speculative off, B = speculative on (or vice versa). Counterbalanced means we interleave them A-B-B-A rather than running all A then all B (to avoid time-drift bias). Three loads per arm.\n\nSo the structure:\n- Arms: spec_off (A), spec_on (B).\n- Counterbalanced order: A B B A (for 2 arms with... hmm, A-B-B-A is 4 slots but 2 arms × ? loads).\n\nWait, \"three loads per arm\" and \"interleaved arms A-B-B-A\". Let me reconsider. A-B-B-A is a counterbalancing pattern. With 2 arms and the pattern A B B A, that's 4 runs. But \"three loads per arm\" means 3 loads each = 6 total. Hmm, A-B-B-A is 4. Maybe the pattern is repeated or the \"A-B-B-A\" is illustrative of the interleaving style (not running all A first).\n\nActually, \"three loads per arm\" and \"arms interleaved A-B-B-A\". Let me reconsider: maybe there are more than 2 arms. Let me reconsider: arms could be the combination of (spec on/off). Actually maybe the depth series creates arms too.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider the receipt shape: `{loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}`. So `arms` is a list. `loads_per_arm` = 3. \n\nI think the cleanest interpretation:\n- The \"arms\" are the speculative configurations: spec_off and spec_on. Possibly also keyed by depth.\n- Actually, re-reading: \"depth series 0, 10240, 102400 tokens, speculative decoding on and off arms\". \n\nI think the design is: for each depth in the series, and for each spec arm, run loads. But the counterbalancing A-B-B-A refers to interleaving the two spec arms.\n\nLet me simplify to something coherent and defensible:\n\nArms = two speculative-decoding configurations:\n- `spec-off`\n- `spec-on`\n\nWithin each arm/load, we sweep the depth series (0, 10240, 102400) as rows. Each row records: arm, depth, concurrency, ttft, queue_wait, prefill, decode, fingerprint, etc.\n\nCounterbalancing: instead of running all spec-off loads then all spec-on loads, we interleave: load order is A B B A ... Actually with 3 loads per arm and 2 arms, that's 6 loads. A B B A B A? The \"A-B-B-A\" is the canonical counterbalanced 4-run pattern (Latin-square-ish) to ensure neither arm always runs first/last. For 3 loads we'd extend it. Let me use an explicit interleaved schedule and document it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The phrase \"arms interleaved A-B-B-A\" strongly suggests the schedule is literally A, B, B, A (a balanced alternation). With three loads per arm, we can't do exactly A-B-B-A (that's 2 of each = but B appears twice in a row). A-B-B-A gives A:2, B:2. For 3 loads per arm we need 6 slots. A balanced 6-slot schedule: A B A B A B (strict alternation) or A B B A A B, etc. \n\nHmm, but the receipt requires `counterbalanced:true`. The key property of counterbalancing: the arm order is balanced so that position effects (warmup, thermal drift) don't confound one arm. A-B-B-A is the classic \"reverse counterbalance\" (ABBA) used to control for linear drift. \n\nI think the safest interpretation that satisfies the wording: the schedule interleaves arms rather than blocking them. I'll generate an interleaved schedule (e.g., ABBA repeated/truncated to fill 3 loads per arm), and set `counterbalanced:true`. Since 3 loads per arm with 2 arms = 6 runs, I'll use order: A B B A A B (which is ABBA + AB), or better, I'll construct it as an explicit counterbalanced sequence and document it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the number of arms. What if the depth series is ALSO part of the arm definition? \"depth series 0, 10240, 102400 tokens, speculative decoding on and off arms\" — reading it as: the matrix sweeps a depth series {0, 10240, 102400} AND has speculative on/off arms. So arms = spec {off, on}, and within each we sweep depths. That gives rows = arms(2) × loads(3) × depths(3) = 18 rows. That seems like a lot but is a proper matrix.\n\nBut actually re-reading even more carefully: \"depth series 0, 10240, 102400 tokens, speculative decoding on and off arms, results committed under results/\". \n\nI'll design it as: \n- Two arms: spec_off, spec_on.\n- Three loads per arm (counterbalanced interleaving).\n- Each load sweeps the depth series (0, 10240, 102400), producing one row per (arm, load, depth).\n- Medians computed per (arm, depth) across the 3 loads.\n- Concurrency recorded per row.\n\nThat's clean and satisfies all requirements.\n\nBut wait — does speculative decoding need a server restart between arms? Speculative decoding on/off is a server config (`--speculative-config` or `--num-speculative-tokens`). Changing it requires restarting the serve process. That's heavy. But for the bench harness design, the arms represent different server configs. The run-matrix.sh would need to restart the server between spec arms, OR the spec arms are toggled via env.\n\nHmm, this is getting into real operational complexity. But since the acceptance criteria only require syntax-clean + the client unit test, the run-matrix.sh needs to be a plausible, well-structured script, not necessarily fully executable here (there's no live pair).\n\nBut I should make it genuinely functional and honest. Let me design it to:\n1. Source fn-env.sh.\n2. Define arms (spec off/on) with the corresponding serve config.\n3. For counterbalanced order, for each arm/load, bring the server to that arm's config (restart serve if needed), wait for readiness, then run the depth series via the client.\n4. Collect per-row CSV, compute medians, write bench.json.\n\nActually, restarting the full pair service between arms is very heavy and slow. Let me reconsider: speculative decoding in vLLM is configured at server startup. So spec-on and spec-off ARE different server configs. For a real bench, you'd restart. But that's expensive. \n\nGiven this is a harness that will run overnight, and the spec says \"speculative on and off arms\", I'll design the matrix to restart the serve between arm groups (or per load if needed). But to keep it manageable, I'll structure it so the arm config is applied, then all loads for that arm run... but that's NOT counterbalanced (blocking). \n\nThe counterbalancing requirement (A-B-B-A interleaving) means we must alternate arms, which means restarting the server between each load. That's the honest cost of counterbalancing speculative arms. I'll implement it that way but make the server-restart a pluggable function, and document that it restarts the serve process per arm transition.\n\nHmm, but that's really heavy (each restart is minutes with kernel compilation, though cached after first). Let me reconsider whether \"speculative on and off arms\" might instead be toggled without full restart.\n\nActually, vLLM speculative decoding is set via CLI args at startup: `--speculative-model`, `--num-speculative-tokens`, `--speculative-draft-tensor-parallel-size`, etc. There's no runtime toggle. So arms require restart. \n\nOK, I'll design the matrix script to restart the serve for each arm (not each load) but interleave at the load level... no, that contradicts. Let me think about what's realistic and honest.\n\nLet me reconsider the counterbalancing more carefully. The point of counterbalancing is to control for *time-varying confounds* (thermal, background load, cache warmth). If spec-on and spec-off require server restarts, then the restart itself is a confound, and you'd counterbalance the ORDER of the two arm-blocks. With 3 loads per arm, a reasonable counterbalanced design:\n- Run order: [spec_off load1, spec_on load1, spec_on load2, spec_off load2, spec_off load3, spec_on load3] — interleaved ABBAAB. But this requires a restart between every adjacent different-arm pair.\n\nAlternatively, counterbalance could mean: across the whole matrix, each arm appears in each position equally. \n\nI think I'm overanalyzing the operational reality. Let me focus on producing a well-structured, honest, syntax-clean script that:\n- Implements counterbalanced interleaving (A-B-B-A pattern) at the load level.\n- For each load, ensures the server is in the arm's config (restarting serve if the arm changed from the previous load).\n- Runs the depth series per load.\n- Records concurrency per row.\n- Computes medians.\n- Detects fingerprint divergence and does serial replay + flags suspect.\n- Writes CSV rows under results/ and bench.json receipt.\n\nI'll make the server management go through host/fn-cluster-up.sh / fn-cluster-down.sh or a serve-restart helper. Let me check fn-cluster-up.sh and fn-cluster-down.sh to see the interface.\n\nActually, let me reconsider scope. The task boundary says \"touch only bench/ and tests/\". So I cannot modify host/ scripts. I can *call* them though. Let me check their interface.\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe speculative on/off doesn't need full restart if I pass it via env and the serve reads it. But vLLM reads speculative config from CLI. So restart needed.\n\nLet me look at fn-cluster-up.sh and fn-preflight.sh to understand the bring-up interface.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"This gives me the house style and API conventions. Let me check the cluster up/down interface and the worklist so I know exactly what I may call and how the receipt/CSV conventions look.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0b3474c708c645bca17e954a","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/host/fn-cluster-up.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":5310,"output":6195,"cacheRead":47104,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6091,"totalTokens":58609,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990215183,"responseId":"chatcmpl-7c5a710f-c2ed-4a79-ac5f-2ca0a43796f0","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_0b3474c708c645bca17e954a","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# host/fn-cluster-up.sh — stand the flashnext pair service with one command.\n#\n# Order is load-bearing (the reap-then-gate doctrine of the ds4 estate's\n# ds4-cluster-restart.sh, specs/flashnext/evidence/ds4-vllm-manifest.md §5.5):\n#\n#   1. REAP stranded serve processes on BOTH nodes first, then GATE on zero\n#      residue. Stopping a supervising unit does not kill the `vllm serve`\n#      behind it; repeated restarts strand one husk each, holding the port.\n#   2. Containers on both nodes — the worker over ssh on the WIRE\n#      (10.99.9.2); the rails carry tensors only, never control traffic.\n#   3. Ray head on the coordinator, worker join; the worker python pool is\n#      capped small (RAY_NUM_CPUS, host/fn-env.sh).\n#   4. A hard two-GPU gate BEFORE serve — if ray never reports 2.0 GPU, we\n#      abort loud rather than serve at TP=1.\n#   5. The TP=2 eager serve, then a wait-for-reality API poll.\n#\n# Failure cannot strand ranks: flashnext-pair.service runs this as a oneshot\n# with ExecStopPost=fn-cluster-down.sh, so even a failed bring-up tears down.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nlog() { echo \"fn-cluster-up: $*\" >&2; }\nworker() { ssh \"$FN_WORKER_HOST\" \"$@\"; }\n\n# --- 1. reap stranded serve processes, then gate on zero residue -------------\n# The serve process tree is visible from the host /proc even when it runs\n# inside a podman container, so we reap host-side on both nodes. The bracket\n# in the pattern keeps pgrep from matching itself.\nreap_serve_node() {\n  local pids\n  pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n  if [ -n \"$pids\" ]; then\n    echo \"fn-cluster-up: reaping stranded serve pids: $(echo $pids | tr '\\n' ' ')\" >&2\n    kill -TERM $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n    sleep 3\n    pids=\"$(pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true)\"\n    [ -z \"$pids\" ] || kill -KILL $pids 2>/dev/null || true\n    sleep 1\n  fi\n  pgrep -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' | wc -l\n}\n\nlog \"reap: coordinator\"\nresidue=\"$(reap_serve_node)\"\nif [ \"$residue\" -ne 0 ]; then\n  log \"FATAL: $residue stranded serve process(es) on the coordinator after reap\"\n  exit 1\nfi\nlog \"reap: worker ($FN_WORKER_HOST over the wire)\"\nresidue=\"$(worker \"$(declare -f reap_serve_node); reap_serve_node\")\"\nif [ \"$residue\" -ne 0 ]; then\n  log \"FATAL: $residue stranded serve process(es) on the worker after reap\"\n  exit 1\nfi\nlog \"reap gate passed: zero residue on both nodes\"\n\n# --- 2. containers on both nodes ---------------------------------------------\n# The env file is built PER NODE by sourcing fn-env.sh there: the rail list\n# is computed from each node's own `ip -br addr`, never copied across.\nENV_FILTER='^(FN_|NCCL_|RAY_|TORCH_NCCL_|VLLM_|PYTHONHASHSEED=|HSA_|PYTORCH_HIP_|TORCHINDUCTOR_|TRITON_|HF_)'\n\nmkdir -p \"$FN_STATE_DIR\"\nLOCAL_ENV_FILE=\"$FN_STATE_DIR/container-env.list\"\n( set -a; source \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\" >/dev/null; env ) \\\n  | grep -E \"$ENV_FILTER\" | LC_ALL=C sort > \"$LOCAL_ENV_FILE\"\n\nREMOTE_TMP=\"$(worker 'mktemp -d')\"\nworker \"cat > '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh'\" < \"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\nworker \"mkdir -p '$FN_STATE_DIR' \\\n  && ( set -a; source '$REMOTE_TMP/fn-env.sh' >/dev/null; env ) \\\n     | grep -E '$ENV_FILTER' | LC_ALL=C sort > '$REMOTE_TMP/env.list'\"\n\nrun_container() {  # $1 = env-file path; runs on the local node\n  podman rm -f \"$FN_CONTAINER\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true\n  podman run -d --name \"$FN_CONTAINER\" \\\n    --network host --ipc host \\\n    --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n    --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n    --group-add keep-groups --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \\\n    -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n    -v \"$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR\" \\\n    --env-file \"$1\" \\\n    \"$FN_IMAGE\" sleep infinity >/dev/null\n}\n\nlog \"container: coordinator\"\nrun_container \"$LOCAL_ENV_FILE\"\nlog \"container: worker\"\nworker \"podman rm -f '$FN_CONTAINER' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true \\\n  && podman run -d --name '$FN_CONTAINER' \\\n    --network host --ipc host \\\n    --device /dev/kfd --device /dev/dri \\\n    --security-opt seccomp=unconfined \\\n    --group-add keep-groups --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 \\\n    -v /var/lib/local-models:/var/lib/local-models:ro \\\n    -v '$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR' \\\n    --env-file '$REMOTE_TMP/env.list' \\\n    '$FN_IMAGE' sleep infinity >/dev/null\"\n\n# --- 3. ray: distributed head on the coordinator, worker join -----------------\nlog \"ray head on the coordinator\"\npodman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray start --head \\\n  --port \"$FN_RAY_PORT\" \\\n  --num-cpus \"$RAY_NUM_CPUS\" \\\n  --include-dashboard=false >/dev/null\nlog \"ray join from the worker (dials the fleet identity $FN_HEAD_IP on the wire)\"\n# --include-dashboard is head-only: ray PANICs if it is passed to a worker.\nworker \"podman exec '$FN_CONTAINER' ray start \\\n  --address='$FN_HEAD_IP:$FN_RAY_PORT' \\\n  --num-cpus '$RAY_NUM_CPUS'\" >/dev/null\n\n# --- 4. hard two-GPU gate before serve ----------------------------------------\ngpus_total=\"\"\nfor _ in $(seq 1 120); do\n  gpus_total=\"$(podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status 2>/dev/null \\\n    | awk '$2 == \"GPU\" { split($1, a, \"/\"); print a[2]; exit }')\" || true\n  case \"$gpus_total\" in\n    2|2.0) break ;;\n  esac\n  sleep 2\ndone\ncase \"$gpus_total\" in\n  2|2.0) log \"two-GPU gate passed: ray reports $gpus_total GPU\" ;;\n  *)\n    log \"FATAL: ray never reported 2.0 GPU (last reading: '$gpus_total'); refusing to serve\"\n    podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" ray status >&2 || true\n    exit 1\n    ;;\nesac\n\n# --- 5. the serve: TP=2, eager -------------------------------------------------\n# Do not add comments inside the backslash-continued `vllm serve` command\n# below: a '#' there silently comments out every remaining argument, and\n# `bash -n` still reports the file as valid.\nlog \"serving $FN_MODEL_DIR as '$FN_SERVED_NAME' at TP=2 (eager)\"\npodman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR \\\n  --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME \\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 \\\n  --port $FN_PORT \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 2 \\\n  --distributed-executor-backend ray \\\n  --enforce-eager \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization $FN_GPU_UTIL \\\n  --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX \\\n  > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"\n\n# Wait for reality, not for the target's word (the fleet's library-reachable\n# pattern): poll the models endpoint until the served name answers.\nattempts=$(( ${FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT:-2700} / 20 ))\nready=0\nfor _ in $(seq 1 \"$attempts\"); do\n  if curl -fsS \"http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT/v1/models\" 2>/dev/null \\\n      | grep -q \"\\\"$FN_SERVED_NAME\\\"\"; then\n    ready=1\n    break\n  fi\n  sleep 20\ndone\nif [ \"$ready\" -ne 1 ]; then\n  log \"FATAL: serve did not answer on :$FN_PORT inside the timeout\"\n  tail -n 40 \"$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log\" >&2 || true\n  exit 1\nfi\nlog \"pair service is up: $FN_SERVED_NAME answering on :$FN_PORT\"\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990359658},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the bring-up. The serve is started inside a podman container with a specific `vllm serve` command. To change speculative decoding config, I'd need to modify the serve command and restart.\n\nFor the bench matrix, speculative on/off arms require different `vllm serve` invocations. The matrix script would need to restart serve with different args. Since I can't modify host/ scripts (boundary), I'll implement the serve restart within bench/run-matrix.sh itself, using podman exec similar to fn-cluster-up.sh. Actually, that duplicates logic. But the boundary says only touch bench/ and tests/, so I can't edit fn-cluster-up.sh. I can call it, but it doesn't support speculative config.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. The realistic approach: run-matrix.sh manages the serve process itself for the bench (reaping any existing serve, then starting serve with the arm's config). This is self-contained within bench/. It can reuse fn-env.sh (source it) for env doctrine, and use podman exec to manage the serve in the existing container.\n\nBut wait — is the container/ray even up when the bench runs? The bench matrix runs after first light (run-tp2.sh), so the pair service is up. For the bench, we might reuse the running serve for the baseline arm and restart for spec arms. \n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole operational model. This is getting complex. Let me re-read the task goal precisely:\n\n> run-matrix.sh: arms interleaved A-B-B-A, three loads per arm, medians reported, depth series 0, 10240, 102400 tokens, speculative decoding on and off arms, results committed under results/ and a bench.json receipt {loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}.\n\nSo run-matrix.sh is the orchestrator. It needs to:\n1. Define arms: spec_off, spec_on.\n2. Counterbalanced interleave A-B-B-A.\n3. 3 loads per arm.\n4. For each load, sweep depth series 0/10240/102400.\n5. Call the client (fn-stream-client.py) to measure.\n6. Compute medians.\n7. Write rows under results/.\n8. Write bench.json.\n\nFor speculative arms, the serve config differs. I'll implement serve restart per arm transition. Let me design a helper in run-matrix.sh that restarts the serve with a given spec config, using the existing container and ray cluster (which stay up). Actually, only the `vllm serve` process needs restart (ray head/worker and containers stay). That's more manageable.\n\nBut here's a subtlety: speculative decoding changes token outputs, so fingerprints differ between spec arms — that's expected and fine (fingerprints are compared within an arm's loads, not across arms).\n\nLet me now think about the concurrency and the serial-replay QSA protection. The steering says:\n1. Record concurrency per arm per output row.\n2. If token fingerprint diverges across runs of the same arm, byte-compare a serial (concurrency=1) replay before trusting numbers; flag arm suspect.\n\nSo the client runs at some concurrency (in-flight sequences). If concurrency > 1, the QSA gather can produce divergent outputs across the concurrent sequences. The harness must detect this: if the N loads of an arm produce different fingerprints, do a serial replay (concurrency=1) and byte-compare. If serial replay is clean/deterministic but concurrent runs diverged, that's the QSA signature — flag the arm suspect.\n\nHmm, but actually re-reading: \"if any token fingerprint diverges across runs of the same arm, the harness must byte-compare a serial (concurrency=1) replay of that arm before its numbers are trusted — flag the arm as suspect in the output rather than averaging over the divergence.\"\n\nSo the flow per arm:\n- Run 3 loads (at the arm's concurrency).\n- Collect fingerprints from the 3 loads.\n- If all fingerprints identical → arm is clean, trust numbers, report medians.\n- If fingerprints diverge → do a serial replay (concurrency=1) of that arm. Byte-compare. If serial is deterministic (matches one canonical fingerprint) but concurrent diverged → QSA signature → flag arm suspect, record the divergence, do NOT average over it (report medians but mark suspect, or withhold the averaged number).\n\nActually \"rather than averaging over the divergence\" — so if suspect, don't present the median as a trusted number; flag it.\n\nLet me design this:\n- Each arm produces a set of fingerprints across its loads.\n- The matrix checks fingerprint consistency per arm.\n- On divergence: serial replay (concurrency=1), collect a reference fingerprint; byte-compare the divergent runs' completions against the serial reference. Mark arm `suspect: true` with a `qsa_signature` note if serial-clean-but-concurrent-divergent.\n\nThis is getting complex for a shell script. Let me put the fingerprint-divergence logic partly in the client (which computes fingerprints) and the orchestration in the shell. Actually, I'll have the client emit fingerprints per request in the CSV, and the shell (or a small python aggregation step) computes medians, detects divergence, and does serial replay.\n\nLet me structure run-matrix.sh to:\n1. Source fn-env.sh.\n2. Set up results dir.\n3. Define the arm schedule (counterbalanced).\n4. For each arm transition, restart serve with the arm's spec config.\n5. For each load, run the client at the arm's concurrency across the depth series → append CSV rows.\n6. After all loads, run an aggregation step (python) that:\n   - Reads the CSV.\n   - Computes per-(arm,depth) medians.\n   - Detects fingerprint divergence per arm.\n   - For divergent arms, triggers serial replay via the client and byte-compares.\n   - Writes the final median table + bench.json.\n\nActually, to keep it clean, maybe the aggregation + serial replay + bench.json is best done in a python helper. But the task says the deliverables are bench/fn-stream-client.py and bench/run-matrix.sh and tests/test_bench_client.py. So the aggregation logic should live in run-matrix.sh (can embed python heredocs like run-tp2.sh does) or in the client itself.\n\nLet me have the client support a \"mode\" for running a single arm/load and emitting CSV, and the shell orchestrates the counterbalanced schedule, then does aggregation via an embedded python heredoc (consistent with run-tp2.sh style). The serial replay can be a client invocation at concurrency=1.\n\nLet me now think about the client's CLI interface.\n\n### bench/fn-stream-client.py CLI\n\n```\npython3 bench/fn-stream-client.py \\\n  --api http://127.0.0.1:1234 \\\n  --model flashnext \\\n  --depth 10240 \\\n  --concurrency 4 \\\n  --requests 8 \\\n  --max-tokens 128 \\\n  --csv results/bench/rows.csv \\\n  --arm spec-off \\\n  --load 1\n```\n\nThe client:\n1. Builds a prompt of the target depth (in tokens). Since tokenization is engine-side, we synthesize a long prompt and verify prompt_tokens via usage, re-padding if needed (like run-tp2.sh context step). For depth=0, use a minimal prompt.\n2. Scrapes /metrics before.\n3. Runs `--requests` completions at `--concurrency` (threading), streaming, measuring per-request TTFT client-side, collecting token ids and fingerprints.\n4. Scrapes /metrics after.\n5. Computes queue_wait and prefill from metrics deltas.\n6. Emits CSV rows with a header comment describing column semantics.\n7. Also can emit fingerprints to stdout or a file for the matrix to consume.\n\nConcurrency model: use `concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor` with `--concurrency` workers, a barrier to unleash them simultaneously (like the reference client's `barrier.wait()`), then each worker runs requests.\n\nNow, the metrics scraping for queue/prefill separation:\n\nThe client scrapes /metrics before and after the batch. From the deltas:\n- queue_wait per request = Δsum(queue_hist) / Δcount(queue_hist)\n- prefill per request = Δsum(prefill_hist) / Δcount(prefill_hist)\n\nThese are server-reported, distinct from client TTFT. This is the separation.\n\nNow, which metrics names? Let me define:\n- QUEUE_METRIC = \"vllm:request_queue_time_seconds\"\n- PREFILL_METRIC = \"vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds\"\n\nBut does vLLM 0.25.x expose `request_prefill_time_seconds`? Let me think. Actually, I'm not 100% sure. Let me make the client robust: it parses ALL histograms and looks for queue and prefill metrics by name, with a fallback chain. And I'll make the names configurable via constants. For the unit test, I control the fixture so I'll use the names the parser expects.\n\nActually, let me reconsider what vLLM exposes. Let me recall vLLM's metrics more carefully. From vLLM `vllm/engine/metrics.py` (StatLogger), the histograms created:\n- `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds`  ← this exists in newer vLLM (0.6+)\n- `vllm:request_decode_time_seconds`\n- `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`\n- `vllm:time_per_output_token_seconds`\n- `vllm:request_prompt_tokens`\n- `vllm:request_generation_tokens`\n- `vllm:request_success_total`\n\nYes, I'm fairly confident `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` and `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds` exist in modern vLLM. Good. And the relationship: \n- queue + prefill ≈ TTFT (server-side)\n- inference = prefill + decode (roughly)\n\nSo separating queue from prefill: both are exposed as separate histograms. \n\nLet me also handle the case where prefill isn't directly exposed but inference and decode are: prefill = inference - decode. But that's more fragile. I'll prefer direct prefill metric, fall back to inference-decode. Actually, let me keep it simpler and rely on the direct metrics, but make the parser generic enough. I'll implement a fallback: if prefill metric absent, try inference minus decode.\n\nHmm, let me not overengineer. I'll implement:\n- `QUEUE_METRICS = (\"vllm:request_queue_time_seconds\",)` \n- `PREFILL_METRICS = (\"vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds\",)` with fallback computed from inference - decode if absent.\n\nActually, to keep the unit test clean and the code honest, I'll define the primary metric names and parse those. Let me keep a small fallback for robustness but keep the main path clear.\n\nLet me now also reconsider: the CSV header comment must describe column semantics explicitly. Something like:\n```\n# fn-stream-client CSV — column semantics:\n#   arm            : bench arm label (spec-off / spec-on)\n#   load           : load index within the arm (1..loads_per_arm)\n#   depth_tokens   : target prompt depth in tokens\n#   concurrency    : in-flight sequences for this row\n#   request_index  : per-request index within the load\n#   ttft_s         : CLIENT-side time-to-first-token (request start -> first token)\n#   queue_wait_s   : SERVER-reported scheduler queue wait (metrics delta), NOT derived from ttft\n#   prefill_s      : SERVER-reported prompt processing (metrics delta), NOT derived from ttft\n#   decode_s       : client-side total minus ttft\n#   total_s        : client-side total request time\n#   prompt_tokens  : server-reported prompt token count\n#   completion_tokens : server-reported completion token count\n#   fingerprint    : sha256 of the completion's token ids\n# NOTE: prefill_s is a measurement from the server metrics endpoint, NOT a\n#       duplicate of ttft_s (contrast: reference harness vllm-stream-client.py\n#       shipped prefill_mean_s == ttft_mean_s, evidence/nix-strix-halo.md §4.4).\n```\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me design the parser for testability. I'll structure:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass HistReading:\n    total: float  # _sum\n    count: float  # _count\n\ndef parse_metrics(text) -> dict[str, HistReading]:\n    # returns {metric_name: HistReading} for histograms (sum+count)\n\ndef scrape_metrics(url) -> dict[str, HistReading]:\n    # HTTP GET url, parse\n\ndef first_token_split(before, after, queue_metric=..., prefill_metric=...) -> dict:\n    # returns {\"queue_wait_s\": ..., \"prefill_s\": ..., ...}\n```\n\nWait, but for the test, the fixture should have \"known queue and processing times\". Let me make the split function take before/after snapshots and compute per-request means. The test builds before (empty/zero) and after (with known sums/counts) snapshots, calls split, and asserts.\n\nLet me reconsider the exact metric parsing. Prometheus exposition:\n```\n# TYPE vllm:request_queue_time_seconds histogram\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_bucket{le=\"0.5\"} 3.0\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_sum 42.5\nvllm:request_queue_time_seconds_count 10.0\n```\n\nI need to parse `_sum` and `_count` lines. Note metric names contain colons. The line format is `<name>{labels} <value>` or `<name> <value>`. For `_sum` and `_count`, there are no labels typically. So I parse lines, and for lines matching `^(<metric>_sum)\\s+(<float>)`, record.\n\nLet me write parse_metrics to return a dict keyed by the base histogram name (strip `_sum`/`_count`/`_bucket`) → HistReading(total, count).\n\nLet me write:\n```python\ndef parse_metrics(text):\n    readings = {}\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        line = line.strip()\n        if not line or line.startswith(\"#\"):\n            continue\n        parts = line.split()\n        if len(parts) < 2:\n            continue\n        name_field, value = parts[0], parts[-1]\n        # strip labels\n        name = name_field.split(\"{\", 1)[0]\n        try:\n            val = float(value)\n        except ValueError:\n            continue\n        if name.endswith(\"_sum\"):\n            base = name[:-4]\n            readings.setdefault(base, HistReading(0.0, 0.0)).total = val\n        elif name.endswith(\"_count\"):\n            base = name[:-6]\n            readings.setdefault(base, HistReading(0.0, 0.0)).count = val\n        # ignore _bucket and others for our purposes\n    return readings\n```\n\nWait, `_count` suffix is 6 chars (\"_count\"). Yes. `_sum` is 4. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: some metrics are counters, not histograms, e.g. `vllm:num_requests_running` (gauge) or `vllm:request_success_total` (counter). Those don't have `_sum`/`_count`. We only care about histogram sum/count. Fine.\n\nAlso, metric names: vLLM uses `vllm:` prefix. In Prometheus exposition, colons are valid in metric names. Good.\n\nNow the split function:\n```python\ndef first_token_split(before, after, num_requests=None,\n                      queue_metric=QUEUE_METRIC, prefill_metric=PREFILL_METRIC):\n    def delta_mean(metric):\n        b = before.get(metric); a = after.get(metric)\n        if a is None:\n            return None, 0\n        dsum = a.total - (b.total if b else 0.0)\n        dcount = a.count - (b.count if b else 0.0)\n        if dcount <= 0:\n            return None, 0\n        return dsum / dcount, int(dcount)\n    queue_wait, q_n = delta_mean(queue_metric)\n    prefill, p_n = delta_mean(prefill_metric)\n    return SplitResult(queue_wait_s=queue_wait, prefill_s=prefill, ...)\n```\n\nHmm wait, but what if before is None (first scrape)? Let me make before optional (treat as zero). Actually the client always scrapes before and after. For the test, I'll pass both.\n\nNow, an important honesty point: the queue_wait and prefill computed this way are *means over all requests that completed between the two scrapes*, not necessarily just our batch. If the server is otherwise idle (bench is the only client), then the delta is exactly our batch. That's the bench assumption. I'll document that.\n\nLet me reconsider whether dividing by Δcount is right. Δcount = number of requests that had that metric recorded between scrapes = our batch size (assuming idle). Δsum = total queue time for those. So mean = Δsum/Δcount. Good. And I'll also record the observed Δcount so the matrix can sanity-check it equals the expected request count.\n\nNow, the fingerprint. Let me design token-id extraction.\n\nFor streaming completions with logprobs, vLLM chunks look like:\n```json\n{\"id\":\"...\",\"object\":\"text_completion\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"text\":\" tok\",\"logprobs\":{\"tokens\":[\" tok\"],\"token_logprobs\":[-0.1],\"top_logprobs\":[...],\"text_offset\":[...]},\"finish_reason\":null}]}\n```\n\nSo `choice[\"logprobs\"][\"tokens\"]` gives token strings per chunk. But NOT ids.\n\nTo get token ids, I recall vLLM actually does include them now. Let me think... Actually, in vLLM, when you request completions, you can get token IDs by... hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. There's actually a clean way: vLLM's `CompletionResponse` in some versions includes `prompt_token_ids` in the choice. But completion token ids... \n\nOK, I'll make a pragmatic decision that's honest: The fingerprint is computed over the completion's token sequence. I'll get token identifiers from the response with a priority:\n1. If the chunk choice carries integer token ids (key `token_ids` or similar), use them.\n2. Otherwise, use the logprobs `tokens` strings.\n3. Otherwise, use the raw text.\n\nAnd I'll compute sha256 over a canonical serialization. I'll document that under greedy decoding this is deterministic and catches the QSA divergence.\n\nBut the task literally says \"token ids\". Let me try harder to get real token ids. \n\nActually — I just realized there's a clean and reliable way to get token IDs from vLLM: request `echo=False` and `logprobs`, then the response's logprobs contains tokens. But for IDs, vLLM's OpenAI layer... \n\nYou know, let me look at this from a different angle. vLLM's `/v1/completions` request accepts `prompt_token_ids` (list of ints) as an alternative to `prompt` (string). And I recall that recent vLLM added `CompletionResponseChoice.prompt_token_ids`. For completion tokens, there isn't a standard field.\n\nGiven the difficulty, and that the fingerprint's PURPOSE is divergence detection (per steering), I'll make the fingerprint robust and deterministic using whatever token-level signal the server provides, preferring integer ids. I'll write the extraction to look for ids and fall back to logprobs tokens. This is honest engineering: the code TRIES to use token ids, documents the fallback.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe I'm wrong and vLLM DOES return token ids. Let me just write the client to handle a `token_id` field in the chunk if present, and I'll make the fingerprint function work on a list of ints (the canonical case). For the fallback, I'll tokenize by logprobs tokens. \n\nHmm, I want to get this right. Let me think about whether there's a deterministic token-id source.\n\nAlternative clean approach: Use vLLM's `/tokenize` endpoint to get prompt token ids, and for completion, request with `logprobs=1` and `echo`... no.\n\nActually — the cleanest reliable source of completion token IDs in vLLM is to use the NON-streaming response with `logprobs` — but even there, logprobs gives token strings.\n\nWait, I recall now: vLLM's logprobs in the OpenAI completions response, the `LogProbs` model has `tokens: List[str]`, `token_logprobs`, `top_logprobs`, `text_offset`. No ids. BUT, there was a feature added where you can get content including token ids via... the `CompletionLogProbs`... no.\n\nOK. Decision: I'll make the fingerprint from the completion token sequence, using integer token IDs when available and logprobs token strings otherwise. I'll structure the code so the \"token ids\" path is primary and clearly documented, with the string fallback. For the bench's divergence-detection purpose, both work. This is the honest, robust choice. I'll note in a comment that vLLM's OpenAI completions layer reports per-token strings (not ids) unless the fork adds an ids field, so we hash whichever token-level identifier the server returns, which is deterministic under greedy sampling.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider — actually I want to double check by considering that the campaign's vLLM fork might expose token ids. But I can't know. Let me just be robust.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the fingerprint source ONE more time and settle it with the most robust vLLM-compatible method:\n\nI'll request completions with `\"logprobs\": 1` and `\"echo\": false`. From the streaming (or final) response, I collect `logprobs.tokens` (list of token strings). To turn this into a stable fingerprint equivalent to token ids, I hash the sequence. Since tokenization is deterministic, identical completions yield identical token sequences and identical fingerprints; QSA divergence changes tokens → changes fingerprint. \n\nBut to honor \"token ids\" literally, I'll ALSO check for `token_id`/`token_ids` fields. And I'll build the fingerprint over ints when present.\n\nLet me just write it robustly and move on. I've spent enough analysis.\n\nLet me now also think: the fingerprint is \"of every completion's token ids\" — so each completion (each request) gets one fingerprint. In the CSV, each request row has its fingerprint. Good.\n\nNow let me think about concurrency and the barrier. I'll use threading with a barrier:\n```python\nbarrier = threading.Barrier(concurrency)\ndef worker(i):\n    barrier.wait()\n    ... run request, measure ...\n```\nWith `--requests` total and `--concurrency` workers, I'll dispatch requests to a thread pool of `concurrency` workers, all released by a barrier at the start. Actually, if requests > concurrency, the barrier model is more complex. Let me keep it simple: the barrier synchronizes the first `concurrency` tasks. Actually, let me use a simpler model: launch `concurrency` threads, each pulls from a queue of `requests` items. The barrier releases all threads simultaneously for the first wave. Subsequent requests per thread run back-to-back. This measures sustained concurrency.\n\nActually, for a clean \"in-flight = concurrency\" measurement, I'll launch exactly `concurrency` threads, each running `requests/concurrency` sequential requests (or distribute). The barrier ensures they start together. That gives concurrency in-flight = concurrency.\n\nLet me keep the concurrency handling straightforward and well-documented.\n\nLet me now also handle depth: I need to synthesize prompts of ~depth tokens. Like run-tp2.sh, I'll build from a fixed paragraph, estimate chars/token, pad, and verify via usage prompt_tokens, re-pad once if undershot. For depth=0, use a minimal single-token-ish prompt (but can't be truly 0; I'll use the smallest reasonable and note the actual prompt_tokens). Actually \"depth 0\" probably means no long context — a short baseline prompt. I'll use a minimal prompt and record actual prompt_tokens. The row's depth_tokens column records the *target* depth, and prompt_tokens records actual.\n\nNow let me also reconsider the \"prefill number is a measurement, not a proxy\" requirement from deliveredBehaviors: \"A client whose prefill number is a measurement, not a proxy\". This confirms prefill must come from the server metrics (a real measurement), not be a duplicate of ttft. Good, my design does this.\n\nAnd \"A matrix whose every number carries its protocol\" — each row/number carries its protocol metadata (arm, depth, concurrency, load, protocol/spec config). So the CSV and bench.json must carry the full protocol context for every number. I'll make sure each row has arm/load/depth/concurrency/spec config, and bench.json carries the full protocol.\n\nAlright. Let me also check the worklist to see if there's more detail on this task and the receipt schema, and check tests/test_instruments.py and test_host_tooling.py for style. Also check contracts/trace.schema.json maybe not needed. Let me look at the worklist task entry and one more test.\n\nLet me read the worklist and test_host_tooling.py.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me check the worklist task entry, an existing test for style, and the cluster-down/preflight interfaces.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_9e967aa5b6c3415e9809ee48","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\necho \"=== worklist bench-harness task ===\"\npython3 -c \"import json;wl=json.load(open('silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json'));[print(json.dumps(t,indent=1)) for t in wl['tasks'] if t['id']=='bench-harness']\"\necho \"=== all task ids ===\"\npython3 -c \"import json;wl=json.load(open('silent-factory-worklists/flashnext.json'));[print(t['id'],'|',t.get('kind'),'|',t.get('conflictDomains')) for t in wl['tasks']]\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":10096,"output":5830,"cacheRead":50688,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":5619,"totalTokens":66614,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990359663,"responseId":"chatcmpl-3bda04d5-a31e-471f-9787-54e0c41007cf","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_9e967aa5b6c3415e9809ee48","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== worklist bench-harness task ===\n{\n \"id\": \"bench-harness\",\n \"kind\": \"implementation\",\n \"title\": \"Measurement harness: honest client and counterbalanced matrix\",\n \"goal\": \"Write bench/fn-stream-client.py and bench/run-matrix.sh per spec claims 6.2 and 6.3 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6). The client measures per-request time-to-first-token client-side AND scrapes the server metrics endpoint to separate scheduler queue wait from prompt processing \\u2014 the reference harness's client published a prefill column that duplicated its first-token column (specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md section 4.4); ours must not. Emit CSV with explicit column semantics in a header comment, plus a sha256 fingerprint of every completion's token ids. run-matrix.sh: arms interleaved A-B-B-A, three loads per arm, medians reported, depth series 0, 10240, 102400 tokens, speculative decoding on and off arms, results committed under results/ and a bench.json receipt {loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}. Add tests/test_bench_client.py: feed the client parser a fixture with known queue and processing times and assert the two columns separate them. Boundaries: touch only bench/ and tests/.\",\n \"deliveredBehaviors\": [\n  \"A client whose prefill number is a measurement, not a proxy\",\n  \"A matrix whose every number carries its protocol\"\n ],\n \"readFirst\": {\n  \"specSections\": [\n   \"specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6\",\n   \"specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md\"\n  ],\n  \"styleReferences\": [\n   \"scripts/receipts-verify.py\"\n  ]\n },\n \"acceptanceCriteria\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"bench-honest-client\",\n   \"description\": \"Client compiles, separation test passes, matrix script is syntax-clean.\",\n   \"argv\": [\n    \"bash\",\n    \"-euc\",\n    \"python3 -m py_compile bench/fn-stream-client.py && bash -n bench/run-matrix.sh && python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v\"\n   ]\n  }\n ],\n \"dependencies\": [],\n \"conflictDomains\": [\n  \"bench\",\n  \"tests\"\n ]\n}\n=== all task ids ===\ncontainer-recipe | implementation | ['container', 'results/receipts']\ninstruments | implementation | ['container/rootfs', 'tests']\nhost-tooling | implementation | ['host', 'scripts/run-tp2.sh', 'tests']\nbench-harness | implementation | ['bench', 'tests']\nproxy-tooling | implementation | ['scripts/make-proxy.sh', 'scripts/run-proxy.sh', 'tests']\nmorning-ledger | implementation | ['docs']\ncatalog-handoff | implementation | ['handoff']\nrdma-package | implementation | ['host/rdma', 'tests']\ncp-weights | checkpoint | None\ncp-build | checkpoint | None\ncp-smoke | checkpoint | None\ncp-proxy | checkpoint | None\ncp-tp2 | checkpoint | None\ncp-bench | checkpoint | None\ncp-close | checkpoint | None\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990487280},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Note that multiple tasks touch `tests`. My conflictDomains are `[\"bench\", \"tests\"]`. Since other tasks (instruments, host-tooling, proxy-tooling, rdma-package) also write to tests/, each adds its own test file. I must add `tests/test_bench_client.py` and not clobber others. Since the worktree currently has tests/ with test_host_tooling.py, test_instruments.py, test_repo_invariants.py (from already-merged baseRev tasks), I'll add test_bench_client.py.\n\nWait — test_host_tooling.py and test_instruments.py exist in baseRev. That means those tasks (host-tooling, instruments) already landed in baseRev. Interesting. So the baseRev b474021 already includes host-tooling and instruments work. My job is just bench-harness: add bench/ and tests/test_bench_client.py.\n\nLet me verify the current tests don't reference bench (to make sure I'm not breaking test_repo_invariants which scans scripts/ and host/ but not bench/). Let me check test_host_tooling.py and test_instruments.py to ensure my additions don't break them. Actually, the acceptance only runs test_bench_client. But I should make sure my changes don't break the existing test suite (test_repo_invariants scans the tree). Let me make sure bench/*.py compiles and bench/*.sh is syntax clean in case any test scans bench. Currently test_repo_invariants.test_scripts_are_syntax_clean only scans scripts/ and host/, not bench/. So bench isn't scanned. Good, but I'll keep bench clean anyway.\n\nLet me also check test_instruments.py and test_host_tooling.py briefly to ensure no interaction, and importantly check whether test_repo_invariants scans bench or has a GPL/secret scan that my files must pass. The GPL scan and secret scan are tree-wide (rglob), so my bench files must not contain \"GNU GENERAL PUBLIC LICENSE\" or secret patterns. They won't.\n\nLet me quickly look at test_instruments.py and test_host_tooling.py to match style and ensure I understand the conventions (they might reference things in tests/).\n\nActually, let me focus. Let me check the contracts dir and whether there's a bench receipt schema expectation. The receipts-verify.py `_bench` checker expects: `loads_per_arm >= 3` and `counterbalanced == true`. So my bench.json receipt must have `data.loads_per_arm >= 3` and `data.counterbalanced == true`, with `step` starting with \"bench\" and `status`. \n\nWait — receipts-verify checks `str(r.get(\"step\",\"\")).startswith(prefix)`. For bench, prefix is \"bench\". So step must start with \"bench\". And the receipt top-level shape is `{\"step\",\"status\",\"ts\",\"data\"}`. The `_bench(d)` checker reads `d = r[\"data\"]`, needs `d[\"loads_per_arm\"] >= 3` and `d[\"counterbalanced\"] == true`.\n\nBut the task says the bench.json receipt is `{loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}`. That's the DATA portion. So the full receipt is:\n```json\n{\"step\":\"bench\", \"status\":\"pass\", \"ts\":\"...\", \"data\":{\"loads_per_arm\":3,\"counterbalanced\":true,\"arms\":[...],\"fingerprints\":{...}}}\n```\n\nWhere to put it? receipts-verify reads `results/receipts/*.json`. So the bench receipt should go to `results/receipts/bench.json`. But the task says \"results committed under results/ and a bench.json receipt\". Let me put:\n- CSV rows under `results/bench/` (e.g., `results/bench/matrix.csv` or per-arm).\n- The receipt at `results/receipts/bench.json` so receipts-verify validates it.\n\nActually the task says \"a bench.json receipt {loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}\". Let me place it at `results/receipts/bench.json` with the full receipt wrapper so receipts-verify's `_bench` gate passes (loads_per_arm>=3, counterbalanced true). That's the honest, graded approach — consistent with run-tp2.sh writing receipts to results/receipts/.\n\nNow let me double check: receipts-verify runs on `results/receipts/`. Currently there are build.json, weights-coordinator.json, weights-worker.json. Those have steps \"build\", \"weights\". Adding bench.json is fine. But note — the bench hasn't actually RUN (no live pair), so there's no real bench.json yet. run-matrix.sh creates it at runtime. I should NOT commit a fake bench.json (that would be a fabricated number — forbidden F.8: \"Do not commit a benchmark number from a single uncounterbalanced run\", and the whole ethos is no fabricated facts). So I commit the harness (bench/ + tests/) but NOT fake results. The results/ and bench.json are produced at runtime by run-matrix.sh.\n\nGood — so my commit adds:\n- bench/fn-stream-client.py\n- bench/run-matrix.sh\n- tests/test_bench_client.py\n- Possibly results/.gitkeep for results/bench/ (but results/ already has receipts/ with .gitkeep). I'll add a results/bench/.gitkeep so the dir exists. Actually, run-matrix.sh can mkdir -p. But committing a .gitkeep keeps the structure. Let me add results/bench/.gitkeep. That's under results/ which is NOT in my conflictDomains [bench, tests]. \n\nWait — conflictDomains are [\"bench\", \"tests\"]. The write boundary says \"files your change makes false must be inside these prefixes\". Adding results/bench/.gitkeep is under results/, which is outside bench/ and tests/. Is that allowed? The boundary says my writes must be inside conflictDomains prefixes. Hmm. But the task explicitly says \"results committed under results/\". \n\nLet me re-read: \"The task's conflictDomains [\\\"bench\\\",\\\"tests\\\"] are the binding write boundary: files your change makes false must be inside these prefixes; anything else is the operator's to grant. Every path touched by any task commit, including a path later deleted or renamed, must remain inside them.\"\n\nSo strictly, every path my commit touches must be inside bench/ or tests/. Writing results/bench/.gitkeep would violate that. But the task goal says \"results committed under results/\". There's tension.\n\nResolution: The conflictDomain write boundary is about what MY commit may touch. The results/ directory is populated at RUNTIME by run-matrix.sh, not by my commit. So my commit should only add bench/ and tests/ files. The runtime writes to results/ happen when the harness actually runs (not part of my commit). So I should NOT commit anything under results/. run-matrix.sh writes there at runtime, which is fine (that's not \"my commit touching\" those paths — the script runs later).\n\nBut wait, will run-matrix.sh even be run as part of acceptance? No — acceptance is just py_compile + bash -n + unittest. So results/ is never written during acceptance. Good. So I commit only bench/ and tests/ files. I will NOT add results/bench/.gitkeep (that would touch results/, outside boundary). run-matrix.sh does `mkdir -p` at runtime.\n\nHmm, but actually let me reconsider: is writing to results/ at runtime a \"commit\"? The harness runs overnight and its outputs get committed by the checkpoint task (cp-bench). That's a different task's job. My job is just to author the harness. So I keep my commit within bench/ and tests/. \n\nLet me also reconsider: does the matrix need to commit results itself? The task says \"results committed under results/\". In the overnight flow, run-matrix.sh writes results, and then the cp-bench checkpoint commits them. So run-matrix.sh just writes files; committing is the checkpoint's job. I'll have run-matrix.sh write to results/ and results/receipts/bench.json, and note that the checkpoint commits them. Actually, to be safe and self-contained, run-matrix.sh could `git add` the results, but committing is likely the checkpoint's role. I'll have run-matrix.sh write the files and let the checkpoint commit. I won't git-commit from run-matrix.sh (keep it clean; the deterministic campaign nodes own git operations per the brief: \"Do not push, merge... deterministic campaign nodes own those operations\"). Actually the brief says *I* shouldn't push/merge; but committing my own work on the branch is required (\"Commit the complete result on the assigned branch\"). For run-matrix.sh runtime results, those are committed by cp-bench. So run-matrix.sh just writes files.\n\nOK let me now also verify: the brief says \"Commit the complete result on the assigned branch.\" So I commit bench/ + tests/ on the branch. Good.\n\nNow, let me reconsider the test file. It must pass with `python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v`. This requires `tests/` to be importable as a package `tests.test_bench_client`. Is there a `tests/__init__.py`? Let me check. The existing tests are run how? Let me check if tests/ has __init__.py. From the ls earlier, tests/ has test_host_tooling.py, test_instruments.py, test_repo_invariants.py — no __init__.py listed. So `tests` is a namespace package (Python 3.3+ implicit namespace packages) — `python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client` works without __init__.py as long as run from repo root. Actually, for `unittest` discovery with dotted name `tests.test_bench_client`, Python needs to import `tests.test_bench_client`. With implicit namespace packages, `tests` (no __init__) is importable as a namespace package when CWD is on sys.path. `python3 -m unittest` adds CWD to sys.path. So `tests.test_bench_client` resolves. Good. The acceptance argv runs from the worktree root presumably. Let me make sure my test imports the client via a path that works.\n\nHow does my test import bench/fn-stream-client.py? The filename has a hyphen (`fn-stream-client.py`), so it's NOT a normal importable module name (`fn-stream-client` is invalid as an identifier). So I can't do `import bench.fn_stream_client`. I need to load it by path using importlib. \n\nOptions:\n1. In the test, use `importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` to load `bench/fn-stream-client.py`.\n2. Or add `bench/__init__.py` and reference... but hyphenated filename still can't be imported normally.\n\nLet me use importlib in the test to load the module from its file path. That's clean. Actually, wait — should I name the file with a hyphen? The task explicitly says `bench/fn-stream-client.py` (with hyphen). And `bench/run-matrix.sh`. So yes, hyphenated. The test must load it via importlib.\n\nLet me structure the test:\n```python\nimport importlib.util\nimport pathlib\nimport unittest\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nCLIENT = ROOT / \"bench\" / \"fn-stream-client.py\"\n\ndef load_client():\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"fn_stream_client\", CLIENT)\n    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n    return mod\n\nclass BenchClientSeparation(unittest.TestCase):\n    ...\n```\n\nThis loads the hyphenated file. Good.\n\nNow, py_compile on bench/fn-stream-client.py: `python3 -m py_compile bench/fn-stream-client.py` works regardless of hyphen (py_compile takes a path). Good.\n\nNow let me reconsider the metric names once more and make them constants the test can reference. I'll define in the client:\n```python\nQUEUE_TIME_METRIC = \"vllm:request_queue_time_seconds\"\nPREFILL_TIME_METRIC = \"vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds\"\nTTFT_METRIC = \"vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds\"\n```\n\nThe test fixture will use these exact names (imported from the module), inject known sums/counts, and assert separation. This tightly couples test to implementation (good — it proves the parser reads the right metrics).\n\nNow let me design the split function's return so the test can assert. Let me define a dataclass `FirstTokenSplit` with fields:\n- `queue_wait_s` (float | None)\n- `prefill_s` (float | None)\n- `queue_samples` (int)\n- `prefill_samples` (int)\n\nAnd a function:\n```python\ndef split_first_token(before: Mapping[str, HistReading], after: Mapping[str, HistReading],\n                      queue_metric=QUEUE_TIME_METRIC, prefill_metric=PREFILL_TIME_METRIC) -> FirstTokenSplit\n```\n\nThe test:\n```python\ndef test_queue_and_prefill_separate(self):\n    c = load_client()\n    before = {}  # empty baseline\n    # after: 4 requests, queue total 8.0s (2.0 each), prefill total 2.0s (0.5 each)\n    after = {\n        c.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC: c.HistReading(total=8.0, count=4),\n        c.PREFILL_TIME_METRIC: c.HistReading(total=2.0, count=4),\n        c.TTFT_METRIC: c.HistReading(total=20.0, count=4),  # ttft DIFFERENT from prefill\n    }\n    split = c.split_first_token(before, after)\n    self.assertAlmostEqual(split.queue_wait_s, 2.0)\n    self.assertAlmostEqual(split.prefill_s, 0.5)\n    # The two columns are distinct measurements...\n    self.assertNotAlmostEqual(split.queue_wait_s, split.prefill_s)\n    # ... and prefill is NOT a duplicate of the server TTFT column (the §4.4 defect)\n    ttft_mean = 20.0/4\n    self.assertNotAlmostEqual(split.prefill_s, ttft_mean)\n```\n\nThat proves separation AND that prefill isn't a ttft duplicate. \n\nBut wait, the test says \"feed the client parser a fixture with known queue and processing times\". My fixture feeds `split_first_token` (which operates on parsed snapshots). Should the test also exercise `parse_metrics` (the actual text parser)? To be thorough and match \"feed the client parser\", let me have the test feed a Prometheus TEXT fixture to `parse_metrics` and then `split_first_token`. That's more faithful to \"feed the parser a fixture\". Let me do both:\n- Build a Prometheus exposition text with known `_sum`/`_count` values.\n- `parse_metrics(text)` → snapshot.\n- `split_first_token(before_snapshot, after_snapshot)` → assert separation.\n\nLet me construct the fixture as realistic Prometheus text. That's the most honest \"feed the parser a fixture\".\n\nLet me design the fixture: a \"before\" scrape (zero/absent) and an \"after\" scrape with the batch's contributions. Actually, to be realistic, before has some baseline and after has baseline + batch. Let me make before have baseline sums and after have baseline + known increments, so the delta is the known value. This tests the delta logic too.\n\nE.g.:\n- before: queue_sum=100.0, queue_count=50; prefill_sum=25.0, prefill_count=50; ttft_sum=500.0, ttft_count=50.\n- after: queue_sum=108.0, queue_count=54; prefill_sum=27.0, prefill_count=54; ttft_sum=620.0, ttft_count=54.\n- Batch of 4 requests: queue Δ = 8.0/4 = 2.0 each; prefill Δ = 2.0/4 = 0.5 each; ttft Δ = 120/4 = 30 each.\n- Assert split.queue_wait_s == 2.0, split.prefill_s == 0.5, prefill != ttft (30), queue != prefill.\n\nThat's a clean fixture testing deltas. \n\nNow let me write realistic Prometheus text for these. Each histogram needs `_sum` and `_count` lines (buckets optional; parser ignores buckets). Let me include a few bucket lines for realism but parser ignores them.\n\nLet me now think about whether `parse_metrics` handles the `+Inf` bucket and labels. I'll include labels like `{le=\"0.5\"}` on buckets but parser skips `_bucket`. For sum/count, no labels. Good.\n\nLet me now write all the code. Let me also make the client runnable as a real harness (argparse, streaming, threading, metrics). It must py_compile. Let me be careful with syntax.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should parse_metrics ignore gauge/counter lines that don't end in _sum/_count? Yes, it only records _sum/_count. Good. But what about a counter like `vllm:request_success_total`? It's a single line `vllm:request_success_total 5.0` — no _sum/_count suffix, so ignored. Good. What about `vllm:num_requests_running 3.0` (gauge)? Ignored. Good.\n\nEdge: some histograms in Prometheus have `_sum` and `_count`. Good.\n\nNow, one concern: metric name with labels on sum/count. Usually none, but to be safe, I strip labels via `split(\"{\",1)[0]`. Good.\n\nLet me now write the client. Let me structure it clearly with a module docstring explaining the doctrine.\n\nLet me draft bench/fn-stream-client.py:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"fn-stream-client.py — the honest flashnext streaming benchmark client.\n\nSpec claims 6.2 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6): the bench client separates\nscheduler queue wait from prompt processing. The reference harness's client\n(nix-strix-halo lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py:297) shipped a `prefill_mean_s`\ncolumn that was character-identical to its `ttft_mean_s` column — a proxy\ndressed as a measurement (evidence/nix-strix-halo.md §4.4). This client does\nnot repeat that: prefill is read off the engine's /metrics endpoint as an\nindependent histogram, and the CSV header spells out which number came from\nwhich clock.\n\nThree clocks, three columns, never conflated:\n  ttft_s        client-side, request start -> first streamed token. Includes\n                queue wait + prefill + first decode + network + SSE framing.\n  queue_wait_s  server-side scheduler queue wait, scraped from /metrics.\n  prefill_s     server-side prompt processing, scraped from /metrics.\n\nOnly the last two are allowed to claim \"prefill\" or \"queue\"; ttft_s is the\nclient-observed first-token latency and nothing more.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport sys\nimport threading\nimport time\nimport urllib.request\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Mapping, Optional, Tuple\n```\n\nThen the metrics parsing, the split, the fingerprint, the streaming, the CLI.\n\nLet me be careful and complete. Let me write it all out.\n\nFor streaming with urllib, reading SSE: iterate over the response object line by line. urllib's HTTPResponse is iterable by lines. Good (run-tp2.sh does `for raw in r:`).\n\nFor concurrency: ThreadPoolExecutor. Each task runs one request. Use a barrier to release the first wave together. Let me implement:\n\n```python\ndef run_load(args) -> LoadResult:\n    # scrape before\n    before = scrape_metrics(metrics_url)\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    barrier = threading.Barrier(args.concurrency) if args.concurrency>1 else None\n    results = []\n    lock = threading.Lock()\n    def one(req_idx):\n        if barrier: \n            try: barrier.wait()\n            except threading.BrokenBarrierError: pass\n        rec = run_single_request(args, req_idx, prompt)\n        with lock: results.append(rec)\n    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=args.concurrency) as ex:\n        list(ex.map(one, range(args.requests)))\n    after = scrape_metrics(metrics_url)\n    split = split_first_token(before, after)\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, barrier with requests > concurrency: barrier is sized concurrency, but ex.map submits requests tasks; the first `concurrency` will hit barrier.wait() and release; subsequent tasks find the barrier already used... Actually threading.Barrier can be reused (it resets after releasing). With more tasks than barrier size, the barrier will release in waves of `concurrency`. That's fine actually — it means tasks proceed in synchronized waves of `concurrency`. But with a thread pool of `concurrency` workers, at most `concurrency` tasks run concurrently anyway. The barrier just syncs the start. Let me keep barrier size = concurrency and it works: pool has `concurrency` workers, first `concurrency` tasks each wait at barrier, all release together, then proceed. As each finishes, next task starts (no barrier wait needed if barrier already cycled). Actually Barrier.wait() blocks until `concurrency` parties call wait(). If only `concurrency` tasks total call it, one release. If more, they'd block waiting for the next wave. With requests > concurrency and a pool of concurrency workers: wave 1 = first concurrency tasks all call wait → released. Then they run. As they complete, new tasks start and call wait() — but they need concurrency parties again; if fewer than concurrency remain, they'd deadlock. \n\nTo avoid barrier deadlock, let me only barrier the very first wave, or just not use a barrier and rely on the thread pool to start all workers near-simultaneously. Simpler: use a threading.Event released once all workers are spawned. Let me use a simpler synchronization: a start Event. Workers wait on the event; once all workers are ready, main sets the event. But with a pool and map, I don't have explicit worker handles.\n\nSimplest robust approach: create `concurrency` threads explicitly, each processes a slice of the requests, all gated by a single Event. Let me do that:\n\n```python\nstart = threading.Event()\nchunks = [list of request indices split into concurrency groups]\nthreads = []\nfor i in range(concurrency):\n    t = threading.Thread(target=worker, args=(chunks[i],))\n    threads.append(t); t.start()\n# once all started, release\nstart.set()\nfor t in threads: t.join()\n```\n\nWhere worker does `start.wait()` then runs its slice sequentially. This gives concurrency in-flight = concurrency, all released simultaneously. Clean, no deadlock. Good.\n\nActually to ensure they're all actually waiting before release, I can use a barrier for readiness then an event, but a small race is acceptable. Let me use a Barrier for the release to be precise:\n\n```python\nbarrier = threading.Barrier(concurrency + 1)  # workers + main\ndef worker(chunk):\n    barrier.wait()  # wait for main to release\n    for idx in chunk: run request\n# spawn threads, then:\nbarrier.wait()  # main releases all\n```\n\nWith barrier size concurrency+1, all workers + main wait; when main calls wait(), all release together. Clean. If concurrency==1, barrier size 2 (worker+main) still works. Good, no deadlock as long as exactly concurrency workers are spawned. Let me use this.\n\nLet me now write run_single_request to do one streaming completion, measure ttft, collect tokens, compute fingerprint, get usage.\n\nFor prompt synthesis by depth: I'll write a helper `build_prompt(depth_tokens)` that returns a prompt string approximating depth tokens. Since tokenization is engine-side, I'll estimate and rely on the reported prompt_tokens. For depth=0, a short prompt. Let me follow run-tp2.sh's pad-and-verify approach but keep it simpler: synthesize a repeated paragraph, and record actual prompt_tokens from usage. The depth is a *target*; the row records both target depth and actual prompt_tokens.\n\nActually for the bench, depth is important (it's the independent variable). Let me implement pad-and-verify: request once with an estimated prompt, read prompt_tokens, and if it undershoots the target significantly, re-pad. But that doubles requests. Given the matrix already runs many requests, and depth accuracy matters, I'll implement a `make_prompt_for_depth` that estimates and a re-pad. But re-padding requires a probe request. Let me keep it: for each load, before the measured requests, do one probe request to calibrate the prompt length for the target depth, then use the calibrated prompt for the measured requests. Actually that complicates things. \n\nLet me simplify: I'll synthesize the prompt to approximately the target depth using a fixed chars-per-token estimate, and record the actual prompt_tokens per request. The matrix's depth series is about the *target*; the CSV carries both target and actual. This is honest (we report actual). For the depth=102400 case, the estimate must be good enough to fit in context. I'll use a conservative estimate and note actual tokens.\n\nHmm, but if I undershoot, the \"depth\" isn't really 10240. Let me do the probe-calibration to be honest about depth. Let me implement: `calibrate_prompt(api, model, depth)` does a minimal probe if depth>0, else returns a short prompt. Actually, let me follow run-tp2.sh's approach: build from a paragraph, estimate, then verify prompt_tokens in the actual measured requests and re-pad once. But re-padding mid-load changes the prompt between requests of the same load — bad for fingerprint consistency.\n\nBetter: calibrate ONCE per depth at matrix start (outside the measured loads), store the calibrated prompt per depth, reuse for all loads. This keeps fingerprints consistent within a depth across loads (given greedy decoding). Let me have run-matrix.sh do calibration, OR have the client support a `--calibrate` mode. \n\nActually, let me put calibration inside the client as a preliminary step when `--depth > 0`: before the measured batch, the client does up to 2 probe requests to converge the prompt to the target depth, then runs the measured batch with the converged prompt. The probe requests are separate from measured requests and their metrics are excluded (they happen before the \"before\" scrape). This keeps measured-load consistency.\n\nWait, but probes change prompt across attempts; once converged, the measured batch uses the final prompt. Good. And the before-scrape happens after calibration, so probe metrics don't pollute the split. Good.\n\nLet me implement calibration:\n```python\ndef converge_prompt(api, model, depth, max_tokens_probe=8):\n    if depth <= 0: return SHORT_PROMPT\n    para = \"...\" \n    est_cpt = 4.0\n    prompt = para * max(1, int(depth * est_cpt / len(para) * ... ))\n    for attempt in range(3):\n        usage = probe(api, model, prompt)  # non-stream, max_tokens=1\n        got = usage[\"prompt_tokens\"]\n        if abs(got - depth) <= max(64, depth*0.02): return prompt\n        # rescale\n        prompt = rescale(prompt, depth, got)\n    return prompt\n```\n\nThis is getting heavy but it's the honest way to hit a token depth. Let me include it but keep it reasonably simple. Actually, given the acceptance doesn't run this against a live server, I'll implement it correctly but it won't be exercised in tests.\n\nLet me simplify calibration to avoid over-engineering while staying honest: estimate, probe once, rescale once, probe again if needed. Good enough.\n\nNow, fingerprint extraction. Let me design run_single_request to collect tokens. With streaming + logprobs, each chunk gives `logprobs.tokens` (list, usually 1 element per chunk). I'll accumulate. Also try to get integer ids. Let me write:\n\n```python\ndef _extract_token_ids(chunk_choice) -> Optional[List[int]]:\n    # vLLM forks may expose integer ids; prefer them when present.\n    for key in (\"token_ids\", \"token_id\"):\n        v = chunk_choice.get(key)\n        if isinstance(v, int): return [v]\n        if isinstance(v, list) and all(isinstance(x,int) for x in v): return v\n    lp = chunk_choice.get(\"logprobs\") or {}\n    # some builds put ids under logprobs\n    ids = lp.get(\"token_ids\")\n    if isinstance(ids, list) and all(isinstance(x,int) for x in ids): return ids\n    return None\n```\n\nAnd token strings:\n```python\ndef _extract_token_strs(chunk_choice):\n    lp = chunk_choice.get(\"logprobs\") or {}\n    toks = lp.get(\"tokens\")\n    if isinstance(toks, list): return [str(t) for t in toks]\n    txt = chunk_choice.get(\"text\")\n    return [txt] if txt else []\n```\n\nThen accumulate into lists `token_ids` (if all chunks give ids) and `token_strs`. At the end, if token_ids collected for every token, fingerprint over ids; else over strs.\n\nFingerprint function:\n```python\ndef completion_fingerprint(token_ids):\n    # canonical sha256 over the token id sequence\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    for tid in token_ids:\n        h.update(struct.pack(\"<q\", tid))  # or variable\n    return h.hexdigest()\n```\nFor strings, encode utf-8 with separators. Let me make one function that takes a sequence and a kind. Simpler: \n```python\ndef fingerprint_of(sequence):\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    for item in sequence:\n        if isinstance(item, int):\n            h.update(b\"i:\" + str(item).encode() + b\";\")\n        else:\n            h.update(b\"s:\" + str(item).encode(\"utf-8\") + b\";\")\n    return h.hexdigest()\n```\nThis is deterministic and distinguishes ints from strings. Good.\n\nLet me make the fingerprint helper testable/pure too (not strictly needed but nice).\n\nNow usage: with `stream_options: {\"include_usage\": True}`, the final chunk has usage with prompt_tokens, completion_tokens. I'll capture it.\n\nLet me now also handle the metrics scraping function:\n```python\ndef scrape_metrics(metrics_url, timeout=30):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(metrics_url, headers={\"Accept\":\"text/plain\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:\n        return parse_metrics(r.read().decode(errors=\"ignore\"))\n```\n\nNow the CLI main:\n```python\ndef main(argv=None):\n    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(...)\n    p.add_argument(\"--api\", default from env FN_API or http://127.0.0.1:FN_PORT)\n    ...\n    args = p.parse_args(argv)\n    ...\n    run and write CSV\n```\n\nLet me define defaults from env to integrate with fn-env.sh.\n\nNow, the CSV writing: header comment lines starting with `#`, then a CSV header row, then data rows. One row per request. Include: arm, load, depth_target, depth_actual(prompt_tokens), concurrency, request_index, ttft_s, queue_wait_s, prefill_s, decode_s, total_s, completion_tokens, fingerprint, spec_config.\n\nBut queue_wait_s and prefill_s are per-LOAD (from metrics delta across the batch), not per-request. So all requests in a load share the same queue_wait_s and prefill_s. That's fine — I'll emit them on each row (they're the load-level server measurement), and document that. Or I could emit them once. To keep the CSV rectangular and per-request TTFT separate, I'll put queue/prefill on each row of the load (constant within load), and note in the header that they are load-level server metrics. This makes \"every number carries its protocol\" clear.\n\nHmm, but then per-request rows repeat queue/prefill. That's OK and honest. Alternatively, per-request queue/prefill isn't obtainable from aggregate metrics. I'll document it.\n\nActually, wait — can I get per-request queue/prefill? Only via the delta across the whole batch, which is a mean. So per-request granularity isn't available from /metrics histograms. I'll emit the load-level mean on each row and clearly document it. The per-request column that IS per-request is ttft_s and total_s and fingerprint.\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me think about the matrix script and how it calls the client. Per (arm, load, depth): invoke client with --arm, --load, --depth, --concurrency, --requests, --csv (append). Then aggregate.\n\nLet me define:\n- LOADS_PER_ARM=3\n- DEPTHS=(0 10240 102400)\n- ARMS: spec_off, spec_on\n- CONCURRENCY: an env knob FN_BENCH_CONCURRENCY (default e.g. 1 for safety? or >1). The steering wants concurrency recorded and QSA protection for concurrency>1. Let me default concurrency to something and make it configurable. For the QSA logic to matter, concurrency should be >1. But the serial replay handles divergence. Let me default FN_BENCH_CONCURRENCY=1 (safest, deterministic) but allow >1, and the harness records it and does the divergence/serial-replay handling regardless. Actually, the steering explicitly wants the harness hardened for concurrency>1. Let me default to a modest >1, say 4, but make it env-configurable. Hmm, but at concurrency>1 with QSA issues, outputs may diverge; the harness detects and flags. That's the point. Let me default FN_BENCH_CONCURRENCY=\"${FN_BENCH_CONCURRENCY:-1}\" and let the operator raise it; the harness handles both. Actually, to exercise the QSA path and honor the steer, I'll default to 1 but fully implement the divergence detection so that if the operator sets >1, it's protected. Let me make it configurable and default 1 (deterministic-first), documenting that raising it enables the QSA-detection path. Wait, but the steer says record concurrency per arm. Even at concurrency=1, we record it. Good.\n\nLet me reconsider the counterbalanced schedule. Arms: A=spec_off, B=spec_on. Counterbalanced interleaving with 3 loads each. I'll generate a schedule list of (arm, load) pairs in interleaved order. The A-B-B-A pattern: I'll build the sequence as ABBA repeated and take the first LOADS_PER_ARM*2 entries, ensuring each arm gets exactly LOADS_PER_ARM loads. \n\nFor 3 loads each (6 total), a counterbalanced sequence: A B B A A B. That's ABBA + AB. Each arm appears 3 times. Positions: A at 1,4,5; B at 2,3,6. Not perfectly balanced in position but interleaved (not blocked). Alternatively A B A B B A (A:1,3,6; B:2,4,5). \n\nThe key anti-property: not all A before all B. I'll generate an explicit interleaved schedule and set counterbalanced:true. Let me use a deterministic interleaving: alternate, and when one arm runs out, append the rest. For 3/3: A B A B A B (strict alternation) is the cleanest counterbalance. But the task literally says \"A-B-B-A\". Let me honor that by using a reverse-counterbalanced pattern. A-B-B-A is the classic ABBA reversal. For 3 loads, I'll do ABBA then continue with AB: A B B A A B. Let me just implement a function that produces an ABBA-style interleave and document it. \n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe \"A-B-B-A\" describes the pattern for TWO arms where each arm has multiple loads, and the idea is to mirror. With loads_per_arm=3, a balanced mirror is: A1 B1 B2 A2 A3 B3? Or the classic \"ABBA\" per pair of loads. Let me generate: for load i in 1..L, emit (A,i) then (B,i) for odd i, and (B,i) then (A,i) for even i — that's alternating order per load pair, which is true counterbalancing (each arm leads half the time). For L=3: i=1: A1 B1; i=2: B2 A2; i=3: A3 B3 → sequence A1 B1 B2 A2 A3 B3. That's exactly the \"A-B-B-A\" spirit (AB then BA then AB). This is proper counterbalancing. Let me use this. It yields sequence: A1, B1, B2, A2, A3, B3. Each arm 3 loads, interleaved, and the leading arm alternates. \n\nNow, speculative arms require serve restart between arm changes. In the sequence A1 B1 B2 A2 A3 B3, arm changes happen at every step (A→B→B→A→A→B). Changes: A→B (restart), B→B (no restart), B→A (restart), A→A (no), A→B (restart). So 3 restarts. Manageable.\n\nLet me implement a function `serve_arm` that ensures the serve is running with the given arm's speculative config, restarting if the current config differs. Track current arm in a variable.\n\nFor spec config: \n- spec_off: no speculative args.\n- spec_on: `--speculative-config`... vLLM uses `--speculative-model` and `--num-speculative-tokens` (older) or `--speculative-config '{...}'` (newer). The campaign uses vLLM 0.25.1 fork. Speculative config in 0.25 uses `--speculative-config` with a JSON/dict. But the exact draft model is workload-specific. Since this is a harness and the actual speculative config is operator-tuned (spec UNKNOWN-5: \"speculative-decode acceptance on real prompts and the draft-length optimum — recorded by the bench receipt; tuning is morning work\"), I'll make the speculative config an env knob `FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS` that the operator sets for the spec_on arm, and default it to something the fork supports. Actually, vLLM's own draft (n-gram or EAGLE) — for an FP8 MoE model, speculative might use `[ngram]` or a draft model. This is uncertain. Let me make it a configurable env var and document that the operator sets it (morning tuning per UNKNOWN-5). Default: use `--num-speculative-tokens 1 --speculative-model [ngram]`? Hmm, ngram speculative uses `--speculative-model [ngram]` with `--ngram-prompt-lookup-max` etc. in older vLLM; newer uses speculative-config. \n\nGiven uncertainty, I'll make SPEC_ON_ARGS an env-overridable string, default to a reasonable vLLM speculative invocation, and clearly document it's the operator-tuned knob (UNKNOWN-5). The harness structure is what matters; the exact speculative knobs are morning tuning. I'll set a sensible default and make it overridable.\n\nActually, to avoid fabricating a specific speculative mechanism that might be wrong, I'll define:\n```bash\nFN_BENCH_SPEC_ON_ARGS=\"${FN_BENCH_SPEC_ON_ARGS:---speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":8}'}\"\n```\nHmm, but quoting JSON in bash env is fragile. Let me make it simpler: \n```bash\nSPEC_ON_ARGS=\"${FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS:-}\"\n```\nand if empty, use a documented default. But if I can't be sure of the right args, maybe better to make spec_on configurable and REQUIRED to be set, else the spec_on arm is skipped with a typed note? No — that would be a silent skip (F.12). \n\nLet me reconsider. The harness must have spec on and off arms. I'll implement both, with the spec_on arm using `FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS` (env-provided extra vllm serve args). I'll provide a sensible default that matches modern vLLM (0.25) speculative-config syntax, and document it as the tuning knob per UNKNOWN-5. If the operator needs a different draft, they override. This is honest: the structure is fixed, the speculative knobs are explicitly a tuning surface.\n\nLet me use default: `--speculative-config {\"method\":\"ngram\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":8,\"prompt_lookup_max\":8}`. In bash, I'll store it as an array or a string. Let me build the serve command with an array of extra args. I'll define:\n```bash\nSPEC_ON_EXTRA=( --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":8,\"prompt_lookup_max\":8,\"prompt_lookup_min\":2}' )\n```\nArrays avoid quoting issues. Good. And allow override via env by eval? Overriding an array via env is awkward. Let me allow `FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS` as a string that, if set, replaces the default, parsed via read into an array. Simpler: \n```bash\nif [ -n \"${FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS:-}\" ]; then\n  read -r -a SPEC_ON_EXTRA <<< \"$FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS\"\nelse\n  SPEC_ON_EXTRA=( --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":8,\"prompt_lookup_max\":8,\"prompt_lookup_min\":2}' )\nfi\n```\nGood.\n\nNow serve restart: I need to kill the current serve and start a new one with the arm's args, inside the existing container (ray cluster stays up). Let me implement:\n```bash\nrestart_serve() {\n  # $@ = extra vllm args for this arm\n  # reap serve inside container\n  podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"pkill -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true\"\n  sleep 5\n  podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"pkill -9 -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true\" 2>/dev/null || true\n  # start serve\n  podman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME --host 0.0.0.0 --port $FN_PORT --tensor-parallel-size 2 --distributed-executor-backend ray --enforce-eager --gpu-memory-utilization $FN_GPU_UTIL --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX $* > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"\n  # wait for readiness\n  wait_ready\n}\n```\n\nBut wait — after killing serve, does the ray cluster inside the container survive? The serve process uses ray, but ray head/worker are separate processes. Killing just serve should leave ray up. But speculative restart might leave GPU memory allocated; pkill serve releases it. Should be OK. This mirrors fn-cluster-up's reap logic but scoped to the serve process only (not containers/ray).\n\nHmm, but there's risk: repeatedly restarting serve at TP=2 over ray is heavy and may strand resources. But this is the honest cost of counterbalanced speculative arms. I'll implement it carefully with a reap+gate like fn-cluster-up.\n\nActually, I realize restarting serve repeatedly is quite risky operationally and may not be what's intended. Let me reconsider: maybe the intended design is that the bench matrix runs the speculative arms as separate serve configs but the matrix is designed to minimize restarts, OR speculative on/off is achieved differently.\n\nGiven the uncertainty and that acceptance is only syntax+unit-test, let me implement a clean, well-documented serve-restart mechanism in run-matrix.sh, but structure it so it's robust. I'll include the reap-then-gate discipline from fn-cluster-up. This is the honest implementation. If the operator finds restart too heavy, they can adjust concurrency/arms via env.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole restart approach — maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me reconsider: the two arms (spec on/off) genuinely need different serve configs. There's no way around restart. So restart it is. I'll make a `serve_with_arm()` function.\n\nLet me also reconsider: do I even need to bring the pair up in run-matrix.sh, or is it already up (from first light)? The bench runs after first light (run-tp2.sh). So the pair is up with the baseline (non-spec) config. run-matrix.sh should:\n1. Source fn-env.sh.\n2. Verify the API is reachable (the pair is up). If not, optionally bring it up via fn-cluster-up.sh. Actually, to be self-contained, I'll check readiness and if not ready, call host/fn-cluster-up.sh. But that's a cross-boundary *call* (allowed, since I'm not modifying host/). Let me have run-matrix.sh source fn-env.sh and, if the API isn't answering, invoke host/fn-cluster-up.sh. That keeps it robust.\n\nHmm, but calling host/fn-cluster-up.sh brings up the baseline (non-spec) serve. Then my first arm (spec_off) can reuse it (no restart), and spec_on triggers a restart. Good.\n\nLet me structure:\n```bash\nsource fn-env.sh\nensure_pair_up   # checks API; if down, bash host/fn-cluster-up.sh\ncurrent_arm=\"\"    # tracks the serve config currently up\nfor (arm, load) in schedule:\n    if arm != current_arm: restart_serve_for_arm \"$arm\"; current_arm=$arm\n    for depth in DEPTHS:\n        run client: --arm $arm --load $load --depth $depth ...\ndone\naggregate\n```\n\nWait, but if the pair was just brought up by fn-cluster-up (baseline non-spec), and first arm is spec_off, I shouldn't restart (it's already spec_off). Let me set current_arm=\"spec_off\" if the pair was freshly up with baseline. But if the pair was already up from first light, it's also baseline (non-spec). So initial current_arm=\"spec_off\". Then for the first spec_off load, no restart. Good. I'll initialize current_arm=\"spec_off\" after ensuring the pair is up with baseline config.\n\nActually, there's subtlety: what if the pair was left in spec_on config from a prior interrupted run? To be safe, I'll always ensure the correct config per arm, but skip restart when current matches. I'll track current_arm as what run-matrix last set it to, initialized to spec_off assuming baseline. To be fully safe, I could force a known state at start. Let me initialize current_arm=\"\" and force a restart for the first arm? No — that wastes a restart for spec_off if already up. Let me initialize to \"spec_off\" (baseline assumption) and document it. If the operator suspects drift, they can FN_BENCH_FORCE_RESTART=1. Keep it simple: assume baseline = spec_off at start.\n\nHmm, actually to be robust and honest, let me NOT assume; let me just always ensure the arm config matches before each arm's first load, using a tracked current_arm. Initialize current_arm=\"spec_off\" because fn-cluster-up (and first light) run baseline non-spec. Document the assumption clearly.\n\nOK this is getting long but let me just write it well.\n\nNow aggregation + medians + divergence + bench.json. Let me do this in an embedded python heredoc in run-matrix.sh (consistent with run-tp2.sh style). The python reads the CSV, computes per-(arm,depth) medians of ttft, queue, prefill, decode; detects fingerprint divergence per arm (across loads, per depth? or per arm overall?); does serial replay for divergent arms; writes median CSV + bench.json.\n\nFingerprint divergence detection: per arm, collect the set of fingerprints for a given (depth, request) across the 3 loads. If a given request-index/depth yields different fingerprints across loads, that's divergence. Actually, fingerprints depend on the prompt and sampling. With greedy decoding (temperature=0), same prompt → same completion → same fingerprint across loads. If they diverge across loads at the same depth, that indicates nondeterminism (QSA at concurrency>1). \n\nBut the measured requests within a load: if concurrency>1, multiple sequences in flight can diverge from each other (QSA). The fingerprint per request captures each sequence's output. Across loads, the same request index should reproduce. Divergence across loads (same depth, same request index, different fingerprint) OR divergence among concurrent requests within a load that should be identical (if they use the same prompt) signals QSA.\n\nHmm, actually within a load, all requests at the same depth use the SAME prompt (to measure TTFT distribution). With greedy decoding, they should all produce the SAME completion and SAME fingerprint. If concurrency>1 causes QSA wrong-output, the concurrent sequences may produce DIFFERENT outputs for the same prompt → different fingerprints within the load. THAT's the QSA signature: same prompt, concurrent, divergent outputs.\n\nSo divergence detection: within a load at a given depth, if the requests (same prompt, greedy) produce >1 distinct fingerprint, that's intra-load divergence → QSA suspect. The serial replay (concurrency=1) of that arm+depth should produce a single consistent fingerprint; byte-compare the concurrent outputs against the serial reference.\n\nLet me implement: per (arm, depth), gather fingerprints across all loads and requests. If >1 distinct fingerprint → divergent. Then serial replay at concurrency=1 for that (arm,depth), get reference fingerprint and completion text, byte-compare against the divergent runs. Flag arm suspect.\n\nActually the steering says \"if any token fingerprint diverges across runs of the same arm\" → \"runs\" = loads. So across the 3 loads of an arm, if fingerprints differ, do serial replay. Let me implement per-arm divergence across loads (and within-load concurrency divergence). I'll keep it at the (arm, depth) granularity.\n\nThis is complex for a heredoc but doable. Let me write it carefully. Actually, let me have the client itself be able to detect and the shell orchestrate. But the serial replay requires re-invoking the client at concurrency=1. The shell can do that: if the aggregation detects divergence for an arm, it re-runs the client at concurrency=1 for that arm's depths and byte-compares. Let me structure the heredoc to output which arms are suspect and need serial replay, then the shell does the replay, then a final aggregation writes bench.json.\n\nLet me simplify the orchestration:\n1. Phase 1: run all loads (counterbalanced), appending CSV rows.\n2. Phase 2 (python heredoc): read CSV, compute medians, detect fingerprint divergence per (arm,depth). Emit a list of divergent (arm,depth) to a file, and write the median table.\n3. Phase 3 (shell): for each divergent (arm,depth), run serial replay (client concurrency=1), byte-compare, and annotate.\n4. Phase 4 (python heredoc): write bench.json with loads_per_arm, counterbalanced, arms, fingerprints, suspect flags.\n\nTo keep it manageable, let me fold phases 2-4 into fewer heredocs. I'll do:\n- Phase 2: median + divergence detection → writes medians CSV and a `divergent.txt`.\n- Phase 3: shell loop does serial replay for divergent arms, writing serial results to a file.\n- Phase 4: final bench.json assembly (python) incorporating suspect flags and fingerprints.\n\nLet me now also reconsider: the fingerprints field in bench.json. \"{loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}\". fingerprints could be a map from (arm, depth) → the canonical fingerprint(s), or per-arm. Let me make `fingerprints` a dict keyed by arm (or arm/depth) to the set of observed fingerprints and the canonical one. I'll structure it informatively.\n\nAlright, I have a clear enough plan. Let me also reconsider test_repo_invariants.test_scripts_are_syntax_clean — it doesn't scan bench/, but my run-matrix.sh should still be bash -n clean (acceptance requires it). And the client py_compile clean. And I should make sure my test file is well-formed.\n\nLet me also double check: does test_repo_invariants.test_scripts_are_syntax_clean scan host/ and scripts/ only? Yes. So bench/run-matrix.sh isn't scanned by it, but acceptance runs `bash -n bench/run-matrix.sh` directly. Good.\n\nLet me also make sure I don't break test_instruments.py or test_host_tooling.py. Those test host/ and container/ things, unrelated to bench/. My additions don't touch those. Good.\n\nOne more: the brief says \"inspect and preserve any task work already present in the assigned lane.\" There's no bench/ and no test_bench_client.py, so nothing to preserve. I'm building fresh. Good.\n\nLet me also check: is there a `results/bench` expected by any existing test? No. And receipts-verify's _bench gate only fires if a bench receipt exists; none exists yet (runtime). So receipts-verify currently passes (it checks existing receipts: build, weights). Adding my files doesn't add a receipt, so receipts-verify still passes. Good — test_repo_invariants.test_receipts_verify_runs_clean will still pass.\n\nWait, let me verify the current receipts pass receipts-verify. Let me check build.json and weights-*.json quickly to ensure the repo is currently green (so I don't inherit a broken state). Let me run the existing test suite to establish a baseline before I add anything.\n\nLet me run the current tests to see the baseline.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me establish the baseline: run the existing test suite and receipts-verify before adding anything.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_da15dab876ce460e9bbbf128","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\necho \"=== python version ===\"; python3 --version\necho \"=== receipts-verify baseline ===\"; python3 scripts/receipts-verify.py; echo \"rc=$?\"\necho \"=== existing tests baseline ===\"; python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v 2>&1 | tail -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":8208,"output":11890,"cacheRead":59136,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":11742,"totalTokens":79234,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990487284,"responseId":"chatcmpl-4132d201-4759-4078-a19a-e56a49b2b5b8","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_da15dab876ce460e9bbbf128","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"=== python version ===\nPython 3.14.6\n=== receipts-verify baseline ===\nreceipts-verify: 3 receipts checked, 0 violations\nrc=0\n=== existing tests baseline ===\ntest_fn_env_ib_disable_is_unconditional (test_host_tooling.HostTooling.test_fn_env_ib_disable_is_unconditional) ... ok\ntest_fn_env_never_exports_probe_read_defaults (test_host_tooling.HostTooling.test_fn_env_never_exports_probe_read_defaults) ... ok\ntest_fn_env_rails_are_computed_not_hardcoded (test_host_tooling.HostTooling.test_fn_env_rails_are_computed_not_hardcoded) ... ok\ntest_runner_writes_the_four_graded_receipts (test_host_tooling.HostTooling.test_runner_writes_the_four_graded_receipts) ... ok\ntest_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free (test_host_tooling.HostTooling.test_unit_file_is_teardown_safe_and_boot_free) ... ok\ntest_env_surface_is_renamed_to_fn (test_instruments.InstrumentPackaging.test_env_surface_is_renamed_to_fn) ... ok\ntest_every_module_carries_the_notice (test_instruments.InstrumentPackaging.test_every_module_carries_the_notice) ... ok\ntest_every_module_compiles (test_instruments.InstrumentPackaging.test_every_module_compiles) ... ok\ntest_no_bytecode_in_the_overlay (test_instruments.InstrumentPackaging.test_no_bytecode_in_the_overlay) ... ok\ntest_constants_match_the_reference (test_instruments.OffloadBudgetInvariants.test_constants_match_the_reference) ... ok\ntest_promote_frac_reads_the_fn_env (test_instruments.OffloadBudgetInvariants.test_promote_frac_reads_the_fn_env) ... ok\ntest_promote_is_counted_in_blocks (test_instruments.OffloadBudgetInvariants.test_promote_is_counted_in_blocks) ... ok\ntest_store_budget_arithmetic (test_instruments.OffloadBudgetInvariants.test_store_budget_arithmetic) ... ok\ntest_store_disabled_paths_return_none (test_instruments.OffloadBudgetInvariants.test_store_disabled_paths_return_none) ... ok\ntest_store_floor_is_the_max_group_size (test_instruments.OffloadBudgetInvariants.test_store_floor_is_the_max_group_size) ... ok\ntest_store_frac_reads_the_fn_env (test_instruments.OffloadBudgetInvariants.test_store_frac_reads_the_fn_env) ... ok\ntest_evidence_dossiers_present (test_repo_invariants.RepoInvariants.test_evidence_dossiers_present) ... ok\ntest_license_is_apache2 (test_repo_invariants.RepoInvariants.test_license_is_apache2) ... ok\ntest_no_gpl_text_in_tree (test_repo_invariants.RepoInvariants.test_no_gpl_text_in_tree) ... ok\ntest_no_secret_material (test_repo_invariants.RepoInvariants.test_no_secret_material) ... ok\ntest_notices_cover_the_import_manifest (test_repo_invariants.RepoInvariants.test_notices_cover_the_import_manifest) ... ok\ntest_receipts_verify_runs_clean (test_repo_invariants.RepoInvariants.test_receipts_verify_runs_clean) ... ok\ntest_scripts_are_syntax_clean (test_repo_invariants.RepoInvariants.test_scripts_are_syntax_clean) ... ok\ntest_spec_artifacts_exist (test_repo_invariants.RepoInvariants.test_spec_artifacts_exist) ... ok\ntest_worklist_shape_and_hygiene (test_repo_invariants.RepoInvariants.test_worklist_shape_and_hygiene) ... ok\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 29 tests in 0.098s\n\nOK\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990756848},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Baseline is green (29 tests pass, receipts-verify clean). Python 3.14.6. Good.\n\nNow let me check the test_host_tooling.py to see if there's a pattern for testing hyphenated scripts, and to match the style. Let me quickly glance.\n\nActually, let me look at test_host_tooling.py and test_instruments.py to see how they load scripts/modules, in case there's an established pattern I should mirror.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Baseline is green (29 tests). Let me check how existing tests load modules/scripts, to match house patterns.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_60ed6922164e42be910d6872","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/tests/test_instruments.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":13428,"output":184,"cacheRead":66560,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":97,"totalTokens":80172,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990756853,"responseId":"chatcmpl-de5d16ee-2e4d-49d4-b691-e909aeba9174","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_60ed6922164e42be910d6872","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"\"\"Instrument overlay tests — compile, notices, and budget invariants.\n\nThe offline half for container/rootfs/fn_*.py: py_compile each module (cfile\ninto a tempdir, so no bytecode lands in the overlay), check every adapted\nmodule carries its upstream notice header, and check fn_offload_batch's\nbudget arithmetic against the reference invariants (floor at the max group\nsize; promote counted in blocks). fn_offload_batch is torch-free by design,\nso that module alone is imported and exercised directly; the other two are\nchecked at the text/compile level so this suite runs on a host without\ntorch or vllm.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport importlib.util\nimport os\nimport pathlib\nimport py_compile\nimport subprocess\nimport tempfile\nimport unittest\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nROOTFS = ROOT / \"container\" / \"rootfs\"\n\n# name -> (upstream file the notice must cite, env vars the module must expose)\nMODULES = {\n    \"fn_synctrace.py\": (\"ds4_synctrace.py\", (\"FN_PROFILE\",)),\n    \"fn_offload_batch.py\": (\n        \"ds4_offload_batch.py\",\n        (\"FN_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC\", \"FN_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC\"),\n    ),\n    \"fn_expert_union.py\": (\n        \"ds4_expert_union.py\",\n        (\"FN_EXPERT_UNION\", \"FN_EU_START\", \"FN_EU_CALLS\", \"FN_EU_OUT\"),\n    ),\n}\n\n# Stock env names the rename must have retired; none may survive in an\n# adapted module (the notice headers cite file names like ds4_synctrace.py,\n# which do not contain these).\n_RETIRED = (\"DS4_PROFILE\", \"DS4_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC\",\n            \"DS4_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC\", \"DS4_EXPERT_UNION\", \"DS4_EU_START\",\n            \"DS4_EU_CALLS\", \"DS4_EU_OUT\")\n\n\ndef _load_offload_batch():\n    path = ROOTFS / \"fn_offload_batch.py\"\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"fn_offload_batch\", path)\n    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n    return mod\n\n\nclass InstrumentPackaging(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_every_module_compiles(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as td:\n            for name in MODULES:\n                path = ROOTFS / name\n                self.assertTrue(path.is_file(), f\"{path} missing from the overlay\")\n                py_compile.compile(str(path),\n                                   cfile=str(pathlib.Path(td) / (name + \"c\")),\n                                   doraise=True)\n\n    def test_every_module_carries_the_notice(self):\n        for name, (upstream, _) in MODULES.items():\n            text = (ROOTFS / name).read_text()\n            self.assertIn(\"Adapted from\", text, f\"{name} lacks an adaptation notice\")\n            self.assertIn(upstream, text, f\"{name} does not name {upstream}\")\n            self.assertIn(\"ds4-vllm\", text, f\"{name} does not name the upstream repo\")\n            self.assertIn(\"Apache License 2.0\", text, f\"{name} does not name the license\")\n\n    def test_env_surface_is_renamed_to_fn(self):\n        for name, (_, envs) in MODULES.items():\n            text = (ROOTFS / name).read_text()\n            for env in envs:\n                self.assertIn(env, text, f\"{name} does not expose {env}\")\n            for retired in _RETIRED:\n                self.assertNotIn(retired, text,\n                                 f\"{name} still carries the stock env {retired}\")\n\n    def test_no_bytecode_in_the_overlay(self):\n        # What matters is what is TRACKED: COPY bakes tracked files into the\n        # image, while stray local __pycache__ (e.g. from a bare py_compile)\n        # is gitignored and never ships.\n        out = subprocess.run([\"git\", \"ls-files\", str(ROOTFS)],\n                             capture_output=True, text=True,\n                             cwd=ROOT, check=True).stdout\n        for rel in out.splitlines():\n            self.assertFalse(rel.endswith(\".pyc\"), f\"bytecode tracked: {rel}\")\n            self.assertNotIn(\"__pycache__\", pathlib.PurePosixPath(rel).parts,\n                             f\"bytecode dir tracked: {rel}\")\n\n\nclass OffloadBudgetInvariants(unittest.TestCase):\n    \"\"\"fn_offload_batch arithmetic must match the reference invariants.\"\"\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        cls.mod = _load_offload_batch()\n\n    def test_constants_match_the_reference(self):\n        self.assertEqual(self.mod.FRAC_ENV, \"FN_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC\")\n        self.assertEqual(self.mod.PROMOTE_FRAC_ENV, \"FN_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC\")\n        self.assertEqual(self.mod.DEFAULT_FRAC, 0.25)\n        self.assertEqual(self.mod.DEFAULT_PROMOTE_FRAC, 0.5)\n\n    def test_store_budget_arithmetic(self):\n        # 0.25 of 256 blocks = 64 blocks; key rate 1/128 + 1/16 per token\n        # -> 64 / 0.0703125 = 910 tokens (floor does not bite).\n        self.assertEqual(\n            self.mod.resolve_store_batch_tokens(256, (128, 16), frac=0.25), 910)\n\n    def test_store_floor_is_the_max_group_size(self):\n        # The floor is load-bearing: at frac 0.001 the raw budget is 14 tokens,\n        # under the coarsest group's 128-token block; the result must clamp to\n        # exactly max(sizes) so no group's cursor can rewind.\n        budget = self.mod.resolve_store_batch_tokens(256, (128, 16), frac=0.001)\n        self.assertEqual(budget, max(128, 16))\n        self.assertGreaterEqual(budget, 128)\n\n    def test_store_disabled_paths_return_none(self):\n        r = self.mod.resolve_store_batch_tokens\n        self.assertIsNone(r(None, (128, 16), frac=0.25))\n        self.assertIsNone(r(0, (128, 16), frac=0.25))\n        self.assertIsNone(r(256, (), frac=0.25))\n        self.assertIsNone(r(256, (0,), frac=0.25))\n        # frac <= 0 is the kill switch: unbounded == stock behaviour.\n        self.assertIsNone(r(256, (128, 16), frac=0))\n        self.assertIsNone(r(256, (128, 16), frac=-1))\n\n    def test_store_frac_reads_the_fn_env(self):\n        with mock.patch.dict(os.environ, {self.mod.FRAC_ENV: \"0.5\"}):\n            # 128 blocks / 0.0703125 keys-per-token = 1820 tokens.\n            self.assertEqual(\n                self.mod.resolve_store_batch_tokens(256, (128, 16)), 1820)\n        # Garbage in the env falls back to the default, never raises.\n        with mock.patch.dict(os.environ, {self.mod.FRAC_ENV: \"not-a-number\"}):\n            self.assertEqual(\n                self.mod.resolve_store_batch_tokens(256, (128, 16)), 910)\n\n    def test_promote_is_counted_in_blocks(self):\n        p = self.mod.resolve_promote_block_budget\n        # Blocks, not tokens: only num_blocks and frac enter the arithmetic.\n        self.assertEqual(p(258, frac=0.5), 129)\n        self.assertEqual(p(258, frac=0.0001), 1)       # floor of 1 block\n        self.assertEqual(p(258, frac=2.0), 258)        # capped at the tier\n        # Kill switch and degenerate specs restore stock behaviour.\n        self.assertIsNone(p(258, frac=0))\n        self.assertIsNone(p(0))\n        self.assertIsNone(p(None))\n\n    def test_promote_frac_reads_the_fn_env(self):\n        with mock.patch.dict(os.environ, {self.mod.PROMOTE_FRAC_ENV: \"0.25\"}):\n            self.assertEqual(self.mod.resolve_promote_block_budget(256), 64)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990766916},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This confirms the house pattern for loading modules by path with importlib (`spec_from_file_location`). I'll mirror that in test_bench_client.py.\n\nNow I have a full understanding. Let me write the three files.\n\nLet me also double-check: the test file uses `importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` with a valid module name and the path. For my hyphenated file, I'll use module name \"fn_stream_client\" and path bench/fn-stream-client.py. Good.\n\nLet me now write bench/fn-stream-client.py. I'll make it complete and honest.\n\nLet me carefully write the code. I'll aim for clarity, correctness, and the house style (docstrings explaining doctrine, comments citing spec/evidence).\n\nKey design decisions recap:\n- Metric names as constants.\n- `parse_metrics(text) -> Dict[str, HistReading]`.\n- `split_first_token(before, after, ...) -> FirstTokenSplit` with queue_wait_s, prefill_s, samples.\n- Fingerprint over token ids (int preferred, string fallback).\n- Streaming client measuring client-side TTFT, total, decode.\n- Concurrency via threads + barrier.\n- CSV with header comment.\n- CLI with env defaults.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the split function signature for testability and honesty. The delta approach needs before and after. But there's a subtlety: the ttft metric on the server (`vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`) is ALSO scraped, and I want the test to prove prefill != ttft. So I'll also expose reading the ttft metric mean, but the split's `prefill_s` must NOT be derived from ttft. The test will inject a ttft histogram with a different value and assert split.prefill_s != ttft_mean. Good.\n\nLet me define the split to return queue_wait_s and prefill_s from queue and prefill metrics respectively. And separately, I can compute ttft_server_mean if needed, but the client's ttft is client-side measured, not from the ttft metric. Actually the client measures ttft client-side (per request). The server ttft metric is redundant. I'll keep the client's ttft from client measurement. But for the test's anti-duplication assertion, I'll include a ttft metric in the fixture and assert prefill != ttft metric mean. Let me expose a helper `hist_mean(snapshot, metric)` or just compute in test.\n\nLet me make `split_first_token` return a dataclass with queue_wait_s, prefill_s, queue_samples, prefill_samples, and ALSO `ttft_server_s` (from the ttft metric) so the CSV could include it, but I'll keep ttft client-side as primary. Actually, to avoid confusion, let me have the split focus on queue and prefill. I'll add a separate helper for reading any hist mean. The test can compute ttft mean via the helper.\n\nLet me define:\n```python\ndef hist_delta_mean(before, after, metric):\n    b = before.get(metric); a = after.get(metric)\n    if a is None: return (None, 0)\n    dsum = a.total - (b.total if b else 0.0)\n    dcount = a.count - (b.count if b else 0.0)\n    if dcount <= 0: return (None, 0)\n    return (dsum/dcount, int(dcount))\n```\n\nAnd split_first_token uses it for queue and prefill. The test can also call hist_delta_mean for ttft to show it differs.\n\nLet me write the dataclass:\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass HistReading:\n    total: float\n    count: float\n\n@dataclass\nclass FirstTokenSplit:\n    queue_wait_s: Optional[float]\n    prefill_s: Optional[float]\n    queue_samples: int\n    prefill_samples: int\n```\n\nNow, let me write parse_metrics robustly.\n\nLet me write the full file now.\n\nOne more consideration: the prompt for the fingerprint column semantics and the anti-duplication note. Good.\n\nLet me also make sure the client handles `--requests` and `--concurrency` sensibly, and that the measured batch excludes calibration probes.\n\nLet me also handle the case where metrics endpoint returns 404 or the histograms are absent (older engines). In that case, queue_wait_s/prefill_s = None, and I should record that honestly (not fabricate). The CSV carries empty for those. I'll emit \"NA\" or empty. Let me emit empty string for None and document.\n\nNow let me write everything. Let me start with the client.\n\nLet me think about the streaming request function carefully:\n\n```python\ndef stream_one_request(api, model, prompt, max_tokens, temperature, request_index):\n    url = api.rstrip(\"/\") + \"/v1/completions\"\n    body = {\n        \"model\": model,\n        \"prompt\": prompt,\n        \"max_tokens\": max_tokens,\n        \"temperature\": temperature,\n        \"stream\": True,\n        \"stream_options\": {\"include_usage\": True},\n        \"logprobs\": 1,\n    }\n    req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n                                 headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    t_start = time.perf_counter()\n    t_first = None\n    t_last = None\n    usage = None\n    token_ids = []\n    token_strs = []\n    ids_complete = True  # whether every token had an integer id\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=...) as r:\n        for raw in r:\n            line = raw.decode(errors=\"ignore\").strip()\n            if not line.startswith(\"data:\"):\n                continue\n            payload = line[5:].strip()\n            if payload == \"[DONE]\":\n                break\n            try:\n                chunk = json.loads(payload)\n            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n                continue\n            if chunk.get(\"usage\"):\n                usage = chunk[\"usage\"]\n            for choice in chunk.get(\"choices\") or []:\n                ids = _extract_token_ids(choice)\n                strs = _extract_token_strs(choice)\n                got_token = bool(strs) or bool(ids) or bool(choice.get(\"text\"))\n                if got_token:\n                    now = time.perf_counter()\n                    if t_first is None:\n                        t_first = now\n                    t_last = now\n                if ids:\n                    token_ids.extend(ids)\n                    token_strs.extend(strs if strs else [\"\"]*len(ids))\n                elif strs:\n                    ids_complete = False\n                    token_strs.extend(strs)\n                elif choice.get(\"text\"):\n                    ids_complete = False\n                    token_strs.append(choice[\"text\"])\n    total = time.perf_counter() - t_start\n    ttft = None if t_first is None else t_first - t_start\n    decode = None if ttft is None else max(0.0, total - ttft)\n    if token_ids and ids_complete:\n        fp = fingerprint_of(token_ids)\n    else:\n        fp = fingerprint_of(token_strs)\n    return RequestRecord(...)\n```\n\nHmm, the token tracking is getting messy. Let me simplify: I'll accumulate `ids` list and `strs` list in parallel as best-effort, and track `have_all_ids`. Actually, let me track two parallel accumulators and a flag:\n\n- `id_seq`: list of ints (only appended when we have an id for a token)\n- `str_seq`: list of strs\n- `all_ids`: bool, starts True; set False if we ever append a token without an id.\n\nFor each chunk's tokens:\n- Get ids list (maybe empty) and strs list.\n- The number of tokens in this chunk = max(len(ids), len(strs), 1 if text else 0).\n- This is fiddly. Let me simplify by iterating over the token entries.\n\nActually, logprobs.tokens is a list; usually one token per streaming chunk. Let me handle per-token:\n```python\nlp = choice.get(\"logprobs\") or {}\ntok_strs = lp.get(\"tokens\") or []\ntok_ids = lp.get(\"token_ids\") or []  # may be absent\n# If no logprobs, fall back to text\nif not tok_strs and choice.get(\"text\"):\n    tok_strs = [choice[\"text\"]]\nn = len(tok_strs)\nif n == 0 and not tok_ids:\n    # nothing token-level; maybe just finish chunk\n    continue  # but still might mark first token if text present\nfor i in range(max(n, len(tok_ids))):\n    s = tok_strs[i] if i < n else \"\"\n    tid = tok_ids[i] if i < len(tok_ids) else None\n    if isinstance(tid, int):\n        id_seq.append(tid)\n    else:\n        all_ids = False\n    str_seq.append(s)\n```\n\nHmm, but if I require logprobs=1 and the server returns logprobs.tokens, then tok_strs is populated per token and I can index. If token_ids also present, great. If not, all_ids becomes False and I use str_seq.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: with `logprobs: 1`, does the streaming chunk include logprobs? Yes, vLLM includes logprobs in streaming chunks when requested. Each chunk typically carries one token's logprobs.\n\nFor first-token timing, I mark t_first when any token (str or id or text) appears.\n\nLet me simplify token counting: the number of new tokens in a chunk = len(tok_strs) if tok_strs else (len(tok_ids) if tok_ids else (1 if text else 0)). Let me use that for marking first token and for iterating.\n\nLet me restructure cleanly:\n\n```python\nfor choice in chunk.get(\"choices\") or []:\n    lp = choice.get(\"logprobs\") or {}\n    tok_strs = lp.get(\"tokens\") or []\n    tok_ids = lp.get(\"token_ids\") or []\n    text = choice.get(\"text\") or \"\"\n    # Determine token-level entries for this chunk.\n    if tok_strs or tok_ids:\n        n = max(len(tok_strs), len(tok_ids))\n        for i in range(n):\n            s = tok_strs[i] if i < len(tok_strs) else \"\"\n            tid = tok_ids[i] if i < len(tok_ids) else None\n            record_token(s, tid)\n    elif text:\n        record_token(text, None)\n    else:\n        continue\n    # any token recorded => possibly first token\n```\n\nWhere record_token appends and marks first/last timestamps. Let me inline record_token logic:\n\n```python\ndef _note_token(...):\n    nonlocal t_first, t_last, all_ids\n    now = time.perf_counter()\n    if t_first is None: t_first = now\n    t_last = now\n    if isinstance(tid, int): id_seq.append(tid)\n    else: all_ids = False\n    str_seq.append(s)\n```\n\nSince it's inside a function, I'll just inline it (no nested function needed; use direct code).\n\nOK let me just write it directly in the loop. Let me finalize.\n\nFor fingerprint: \n```python\nseq = id_seq if (all_ids and id_seq) else str_seq\nfp = fingerprint_of(seq)\n```\n\nfingerprint_of handles ints and strs with type-tagged encoding.\n\nNow, let me also record prompt_tokens and completion_tokens from usage (present with include_usage). depth_actual = usage[\"prompt_tokens\"] if usage else None.\n\nLet me define RequestRecord as a dataclass or just a dict. I'll use a dict for simplicity in the worker, and build CSV rows from dicts.\n\nLet me now also think about the CSV columns and the run_load structure.\n\nrun_load(args, prompt, arm, load, depth, concurrency, requests) -> list of row dicts + split.\n\nLet me define the main flow:\n\n```python\ndef run(args):\n    api = args.api\n    metrics_url = args.metrics or (api.rstrip(\"/\") + \"/metrics\")\n    # converge prompt for depth\n    prompt = converge_prompt(api, args.model, args.depth, args.max_tokens_probe) if args.depth>0 else SHORT_PROMPT\n    # before scrape\n    try:\n        before = scrape_metrics(metrics_url)\n    except Exception:\n        before = {}\n    # run requests concurrently\n    records = run_concurrent(args, prompt)\n    # after scrape\n    try:\n        after = scrape_metrics(metrics_url)\n    except Exception:\n        after = {}\n    split = split_first_token(before, after)\n    # emit rows\n    rows = []\n    for i, rec in enumerate(records):\n        rows.append({\n            \"arm\": args.arm, \"load\": args.load, \n            \"depth_target\": args.depth,\n            \"depth_actual\": rec.get(\"prompt_tokens\"),\n            \"concurrency\": args.concurrency,\n            \"request_index\": i,\n            \"ttft_s\": fmt(rec[\"ttft\"]),\n            \"queue_wait_s\": fmt(split.queue_wait_s),\n            \"prefill_s\": fmt(split.prefill_s),\n            \"decode_s\": fmt(rec[\"decode\"]),\n            \"total_s\": fmt(rec[\"total\"]),\n            \"completion_tokens\": rec.get(\"completion_tokens\"),\n            \"fingerprint\": rec[\"fingerprint\"],\n            \"spec_config\": args.spec_label,\n        })\n    write_csv(args.csv, rows)\n    # also emit fingerprints (for the matrix) \n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, wait: the before/after scrape bracketing. The before scrape must happen after convergence (so convergence probes don't count). And it should happen right before the measured batch. Good, my flow does convergence first, then before scrape, then batch, then after scrape. But there's a subtlety: convergence does probe requests that go through the server and get counted in metrics; but since before-scrape is after convergence, those probes are in the \"before\" baseline and won't be in the delta. Good.\n\nBut also: the convergence probe requests change metrics; then before scrape captures that state; then measured batch; then after scrape. Delta = measured batch only. Good.\n\nNow, write_csv with header comment. Let me append to the file if it exists (matrix calls once per load, appending). Actually, the matrix calls the client per (arm, load, depth). If I write the header comment every call, it'd be repeated. Let me have the client write to stdout by default and append to --csv if given, writing the header comment only if the file is new/empty. Let me handle: if csv file doesn't exist or is empty, write header comment + CSV header; then append data rows. \n\nLet me implement write_csv(args.csv, rows, write_header_if_new=True).\n\nLet me define CSV_FIELDS list.\n\nNow let me also think: the client is called once per (arm, load, depth), and runs `--requests` concurrent requests at that depth. So each call produces `requests` rows. The matrix sweeps depths in separate calls. Good.\n\nNow the fingerprints output: the matrix needs fingerprints to detect divergence. They're in the CSV. The aggregation reads the CSV. Good. But the receipt's `fingerprints` field — I'll build it in aggregation from the CSV. Good.\n\nLet me now write the code files. Let me start with the client.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check the metric name for prefill in vLLM 0.25. Let me reconsider. I'm fairly confident about `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`. For prefill, let me reconsider whether it's `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds`. \n\nIn vLLM, the StatLogger creates these histograms (from memory of vllm/engine/metrics.py):\n- `self.histogram_time_to_first_token` → `vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_time_per_output_token` → `vllm:time_per_output_token_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_e2e_request_latency` → `vllm:e2e_request_latency_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_request_queue_time` → `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_request_inference_time` → `vllm:request_inference_time_seconds`\n- `self.histogram_request_prefill_time` → `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` (added ~0.6.0)\n- `self.histogram_request_decode_time` → `vllm:request_decode_time_seconds`\n\nYes, I'm confident `vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds` exists in modern vLLM. And `vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`. Good.\n\nFor robustness, I'll add a fallback: if prefill metric is absent but inference and decode present, prefill = inference - decode (per request mean). Let me implement that fallback in split_first_token. Actually, computing prefill = inference - decode from means: prefill_mean ≈ inference_mean - decode_mean. That's approximate but a reasonable fallback. Let me add it but keep the direct metric primary.\n\nHmm, but this fallback adds complexity to the test. Let me keep the test focused on the primary metrics and add the fallback as an optional code path (not tested, or lightly tested). Actually, to keep the acceptance test crisp and the code honest, let me implement the primary path cleanly and add the fallback. I'll test the primary path thoroughly and maybe add one test for the fallback. Let me keep the main test focused.\n\nLet me now write. I'll write the client file.\n\nLet me also make sure I handle `--spec-label` for the spec_config column. The matrix passes it. Default \"unspecified\".\n\nLet me write it all now.\n\nFor the prompt paragraph, I'll reuse a neutral text (no model names). Something like the ledger paragraph from run-tp2.sh. Let me use a neutral measurement-themed paragraph.\n\nLet me write bench/fn-stream-client.py.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Baseline is green and I have the house pattern (`importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` for path-loaded modules). I now have everything I need. Let me write the client first.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0d90d7aa83a945879398fbb4","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/bench/fn-stream-client.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"fn-stream-client.py — the honest flashnext streaming benchmark client.\n\nSpec claim 6.2 (specs/flashnext/spec.md#r6): the bench client must separate\nscheduler queue wait from prompt processing. The reference harness's client\n(nix-strix-halo ``lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py``) shipped a ``prefill_mean_s``\ncolumn that was character-identical to its ``ttft_mean_s`` column — a proxy\ndressed as a measurement (evidence/nix-strix-halo.md §4.4, confirmed at line\n297 of that file). This client does not repeat that: prefill is read off the\nengine's ``/metrics`` endpoint as an independent histogram, and the CSV header\nspells out which number came from which clock.\n\nThree clocks, three columns, never conflated:\n\n  ttft_s        CLIENT-side time to first token. The interval from the moment\n                this client releases a request until the first streamed token\n                arrives. It necessarily includes queue wait + prefill + the\n                first decode step + network + SSE framing. It is a real\n                measurement, but it is NOT prefill.\n\n  queue_wait_s  SERVER-side scheduler queue wait, scraped from the engine's\n                Prometheus ``/metrics`` endpoint as the delta of the\n                ``vllm:request_queue_time_seconds`` histogram across the batch.\n\n  prefill_s     SERVER-side prompt processing, scraped from ``/metrics`` as\n                the delta of the ``vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds``\n                histogram across the batch. Independent of both ttft_s and\n                queue_wait_s. This is the column the reference harness faked;\n                here it is a measurement.\n\nOnly the last two are allowed to claim \"queue\" or \"prefill\". ttft_s is the\nclient-observed first-token latency and nothing more. F.8 doctrine: no number\nleaves this client without its protocol attached, and no prefill figure is\never derived from a first-token figure.\n\nStdlib only — the harness must run on any node that can reach the pair's API,\nwith no third-party dependencies.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport threading\nimport time\nimport urllib.error\nimport urllib.request\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Mapping, Optional\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# The engine's Prometheus metric names. These are the load-bearing constants:\n# prefill and queue wait are read from these two histograms, never from the\n# first-token series. If the engine does not expose one, the corresponding\n# column is emitted empty — an honest absence, never a fabricated proxy.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQUEUE_TIME_METRIC = \"vllm:request_queue_time_seconds\"\nPREFILL_TIME_METRIC = \"vllm:request_prefill_time_seconds\"\nTTFT_METRIC = \"vllm:time_to_first_token_seconds\"\n# Fallback chain for prompt processing if the direct prefill histogram is\n# absent on a given engine build: prefill ~= inference - decode.\nINFERENCE_TIME_METRIC = \"vllm:request_inference_time_seconds\"\nDECODE_TIME_METRIC = \"vllm:request_decode_time_seconds\"\n\n# A short, neutral prompt for the depth-0 arm. No model-family names (F.5).\nSHORT_PROMPT = \"The ledger records what the night proved: \"\n\n# A fixed paragraph we repeat to synthesize a prompt of a target token depth.\n# Tokenization is engine-side, so converge_prompt() verifies the actual prompt\n# token count the server reports and rescales once if it undershot.\n_DEPTH_PARAGRAPH = (\n    \"The fleet measures before it claims. Every receipt is a fact the morning \"\n    \"operator can check. The pair serves from its own drives; the rails carry \"\n    \"tensors and the wire carries control. Nothing is narrated that was not \"\n    \"measured, and no column is published without its protocol attached. \"\n)\n\n# CSV column order. The header comment written above these names documents the\n# semantics of every column; a consumer must never have to guess which clock a\n# number came from.\nCSV_FIELDS = [\n    \"arm\",              # bench arm label (e.g. spec-off / spec-on)\n    \"load\",             # load index within the arm (1..loads_per_arm)\n    \"depth_target\",     # requested prompt depth in tokens\n    \"depth_actual\",     # server-reported prompt_tokens actually accepted\n    \"concurrency\",      # in-flight sequences for this row (steering §1)\n    \"request_index\",    # per-request index within the load\n    \"ttft_s\",           # CLIENT-side first-token latency\n    \"queue_wait_s\",     # SERVER-side scheduler queue wait (metrics delta)\n    \"prefill_s\",        # SERVER-side prompt processing (metrics delta)\n    \"decode_s\",         # CLIENT-side total minus ttft\n    \"total_s\",          # CLIENT-side total request wall time\n    \"completion_tokens\",  # server-reported completion token count\n    \"fingerprint\",      # sha256 over the completion's token ids\n    \"spec_config\",      # the speculative-decoding configuration of the arm\n]\n\nCSV_HEADER_COMMENT = \"\"\"\\\n# fn-stream-client CSV — column semantics (spec claim 6.2; protocol per row).\n#   arm               bench arm label (spec-off / spec-on).\n#   load              load index within the arm, 1..loads_per_arm.\n#   depth_target      requested prompt depth in tokens (the independent var).\n#   depth_actual      prompt_tokens the server actually accepted.\n#   concurrency       number of in-flight sequences when this row was measured.\n#   request_index     per-request index within the load.\n#   ttft_s            CLIENT-side time-to-first-token: request release -> first\n#                     streamed token. Includes queue + prefill + first decode +\n#                     network + SSE framing. It is NOT prefill.\n#   queue_wait_s      SERVER-side scheduler queue wait, delta of the\n#                     {q} histogram across this load's batch.\n#   prefill_s         SERVER-side prompt processing, delta of the\n#                     {p} histogram across this load's batch. Independent of\n#                     ttft_s: the reference harness duplicated ttft into this\n#                     column (evidence/nix-strix-halo.md §4.4); we do not.\n#   decode_s          CLIENT-side total minus ttft.\n#   total_s           CLIENT-side total request wall time.\n#   completion_tokens server-reported completion token count.\n#   fingerprint       sha256 over the completion's token ids (ints when the\n#                     engine provides them, token pieces otherwise).\n#   spec_config       speculative-decoding configuration active for the arm.\n# queue_wait_s and prefill_s are load-level server measurements shared by every\n# row of a load; ttft_s/total_s/decode_s/fingerprint are per-request.\n# Empty means \"the engine did not expose this metric\" — an honest absence.\n\"\"\".format(q=QUEUE_TIME_METRIC, p=PREFILL_TIME_METRIC)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Prometheus metrics parsing.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n@dataclass\nclass HistReading:\n    \"\"\"One histogram's cumulative ``_sum`` and ``_count`` at scrape time.\"\"\"\n    total: float\n    count: float\n\n\ndef parse_metrics(text: str) -> Dict[str, HistReading]:\n    \"\"\"Parse Prometheus exposition text into histogram ``_sum``/``_count``.\n\n    Returns a dict keyed by the histogram's base name (the metric name with any\n    ``_sum``/``_count`` suffix stripped) to its cumulative reading. Bucket\n    lines, gauges, and counters are ignored: the queue/prefill split only needs\n    the cumulative sums and counts.\n    \"\"\"\n    readings: Dict[str, HistReading] = {}\n    for raw in text.splitlines():\n        line = raw.strip()\n        if not line or line.startswith(\"#\"):\n            continue\n        parts = line.split()\n        if len(parts) < 2:\n            continue\n        name = parts[0].split(\"{\", 1)[0]\n        try:\n            value = float(parts[-1])\n        except ValueError:\n            continue\n        if name.endswith(\"_sum\"):\n            base = name[: -len(\"_sum\")]\n            readings.setdefault(base, HistReading(0.0, 0.0)).total = value\n        elif name.endswith(\"_count\"):\n            base = name[: -len(\"_count\")]\n            readings.setdefault(base, HistReading(0.0, 0.0)).count = value\n    return readings\n\n\ndef scrape_metrics(metrics_url: str, timeout: float = 30.0) -> Dict[str, HistReading]:\n    \"\"\"Scrape the engine ``/metrics`` endpoint and parse it.\"\"\"\n    req = urllib.request.Request(metrics_url, headers={\"Accept\": \"text/plain\"})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as resp:\n        return parse_metrics(resp.read().decode(errors=\"ignore\"))\n\n\ndef hist_delta_mean(before: Mapping[str, HistReading],\n                    after: Mapping[str, HistReading],\n                    metric: str) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Per-request mean of ``metric`` over the requests landed between scrapes.\n\n    Returns ``(mean_or_None, sample_count)``. ``None`` means the engine never\n    exposed the metric (honest absence) or no new sample landed.\n    \"\"\"\n    a = after.get(metric)\n    if a is None:\n        return (None, 0)\n    b = before.get(metric)\n    d_sum = a.total - (b.total if b is not None else 0.0)\n    d_count = a.count - (b.count if b is not None else 0.0)\n    if d_count <= 0:\n        return (None, 0)\n    return (d_sum / d_count, int(d_count))\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass FirstTokenSplit:\n    \"\"\"The separated first-token budget. queue_wait_s + prefill_s are the two\n    server-side components; neither is derived from the client TTFT series.\"\"\"\n    queue_wait_s: Optional[float]\n    prefill_s: Optional[float]\n    queue_samples: int\n    prefill_samples: int\n    prefill_source: str  # which metric / derivation produced prefill_s\n\n\ndef split_first_token(before: Mapping[str, HistReading],\n                      after: Mapping[str, HistReading],\n                      queue_metric: str = QUEUE_TIME_METRIC,\n                      prefill_metric: str = PREFILL_TIME_METRIC) -> FirstTokenSplit:\n    \"\"\"Separate scheduler queue wait from prompt processing.\n\n    Both components come from the engine's own histograms, scraped before and\n    after the batch — never from the client's first-token series. If the direct\n    prefill histogram is absent we fall back to ``inference - decode`` means;\n    if even that is unavailable, prefill is reported as an honest ``None``.\n    \"\"\"\n    queue_wait, queue_samples = hist_delta_mean(before, after, queue_metric)\n    prefill, prefill_samples = hist_delta_mean(before, after, prefill_metric)\n    source = prefill_metric\n    if prefill is None:\n        infer, _ = hist_delta_mean(before, after, INFERENCE_TIME_METRIC)\n        decode, _ = hist_delta_mean(before, after, DECODE_TIME_METRIC)\n        if infer is not None and decode is not None:\n            prefill = max(0.0, infer - decode)\n            prefill_samples = -1  # derived, not a direct sample count\n            source = f\"{INFERENCE_TIME_METRIC} - {DECODE_TIME_METRIC}\"\n    return FirstTokenSplit(queue_wait_s=queue_wait, prefill_s=prefill,\n                           queue_samples=queue_samples,\n                           prefill_samples=prefill_samples,\n                           prefill_source=source)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Completion fingerprinting.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef fingerprint_of(sequence) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Deterministic sha256 over a completion's token sequence.\n\n    Integer token ids and token strings are type-tagged so an id sequence can\n    never collide with a string sequence. Under greedy sampling an identical\n    completion reproduces an identical fingerprint; a divergent completion\n    (the QSA-gather signature the matrix hunts for) changes it.\n    \"\"\"\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    for item in sequence:\n        if isinstance(item, int):\n            h.update(b\"i:\")\n            h.update(str(item).encode(\"utf-8\"))\n        else:\n            h.update(b\"s:\")\n            h.update(str(item).encode(\"utf-8\"))\n        h.update(b\";\")\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Streaming request.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef _extract_chunk_tokens(choice: Mapping) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Pull (token_strings, token_ids) out of one streamed choice, best-effort.\n\n    vLLM's OpenAI layer reports per-token strings under ``logprobs.tokens`` and\n    only exposes integer ids when the as-served fork adds them; we prefer the\n    ids and fall back to the strings (deterministic under greedy sampling).\n    \"\"\"\n    lp = choice.get(\"logprobs\") or {}\n    tok_strs = lp.get(\"tokens\") or []\n    tok_ids = lp.get(\"token_ids\") or []\n    # Some builds surface ids directly on the choice.\n    if not tok_ids:\n        direct = choice.get(\"token_ids\")\n        if isinstance(direct, int):\n            tok_ids = [direct]\n        elif isinstance(direct, list):\n            tok_ids = [x for x in direct if isinstance(x, int)]\n    if not tok_strs and not tok_ids and choice.get(\"text\"):\n        tok_strs = [choice[\"text\"]]\n    return (tok_strs, tok_ids)\n\n\ndef stream_one_request(api: str, model: str, prompt: str, max_tokens: int,\n                       temperature: float, timeout: float) -> Dict:\n    \"\"\"Run one streaming completion and measure it client-side.\n\n    Returns a dict with client-side ttft/decode/total, the server-reported\n    token counts, and the completion fingerprint. Never scrapes /metrics: the\n    queue/prefill split is a load-level concern handled by the caller.\n    \"\"\"\n    url = api.rstrip(\"/\") + \"/v1/completions\"\n    body = {\n        \"model\": model,\n        \"prompt\": prompt,\n        \"max_tokens\": max_tokens,\n        \"temperature\": temperature,\n        \"stream\": True,\n        \"stream_options\": {\"include_usage\": True},\n        \"logprobs\": 1,\n    }\n    req = urllib.request.Request(\n        url, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n        headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n\n    id_seq: List[int] = []\n    str_seq: List[str] = []\n    all_ids = True\n    t_start = time.perf_counter()\n    t_first: Optional[float] = None\n    t_last: Optional[float] = None\n    usage: Optional[Dict] = None\n\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as resp:\n        for raw in resp:\n            line = raw.decode(errors=\"ignore\").strip()\n            if not line.startswith(\"data:\"):\n                continue\n            payload = line[len(\"data:\"):].strip()\n            if payload == \"[DONE]\":\n                break\n            try:\n                chunk = json.loads(payload)\n            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n                continue\n            if chunk.get(\"usage\"):\n                usage = chunk[\"usage\"]\n            for choice in chunk.get(\"choices\") or []:\n                tok_strs, tok_ids = _extract_chunk_tokens(choice)\n                n = max(len(tok_strs), len(tok_ids))\n                if n == 0:\n                    continue\n                now = time.perf_counter()\n                if t_first is None:\n                    t_first = now\n                t_last = now\n                for i in range(n):\n                    s = tok_strs[i] if i < len(tok_strs) else \"\"\n                    tid = tok_ids[i] if i < len(tok_ids) else None\n                    if isinstance(tid, int):\n                        id_seq.append(tid)\n                    else:\n                        all_ids = False\n                    str_seq.append(s)\n\n    total = time.perf_counter() - t_start\n    ttft = None if t_first is None else t_first - t_start\n    decode = None if ttft is None else max(0.0, total - ttft)\n    seq = id_seq if (all_ids and id_seq) else str_seq\n    return {\n        \"ttft\": ttft,\n        \"decode\": decode,\n        \"total\": total,\n        \"prompt_tokens\": (usage or {}).get(\"prompt_tokens\"),\n        \"completion_tokens\": (usage or {}).get(\"completion_tokens\"),\n        \"fingerprint\": fingerprint_of(seq),\n        \"token_ids\": id_seq if all_ids and id_seq else None,\n    }\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Concurrency.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef run_concurrent(api: str, model: str, prompt: str, max_tokens: int,\n                   temperature: float, concurrency: int, requests: int,\n                   timeout: float) -> List[Dict]:\n    \"\"\"Run ``requests`` completions at ``concurrency`` in-flight sequences.\n\n    A barrier releases every worker at once so the in-flight count is exactly\n    ``concurrency`` from the first instant; each worker then drains its slice\n    sequentially. This is what makes the number on the ``concurrency`` column\n    true, and what makes the QSA-gather divergence (steering §2) reachable.\n    \"\"\"\n    if concurrency < 1:\n        concurrency = 1\n    if requests < 1:\n        requests = 1\n    # Slice the request indices across the workers.\n    chunks: List[List[int]] = [[] for _ in range(concurrency)]\n    for idx in range(requests):\n        chunks[idx % concurrency].append(idx)\n\n    barrier = threading.Barrier(concurrency + 1)  # workers + releaser\n    results: List[Optional[Dict]] = [None] * requests\n    lock = threading.Lock()\n    errors: List[str] = []\n\n    def worker(chunk: List[int]) -> None:\n        try:\n            barrier.wait()\n        except threading.BrokenBarrierError:\n            return\n        for idx in chunk:\n            try:\n                rec = stream_one_request(api, model, prompt, max_tokens,\n                                         temperature, timeout)\n                with lock:\n                    results[idx] = rec\n            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - record, keep going\n                with lock:\n                    errors.append(f\"request {idx}: {e.__class__.__name__}: {e}\")\n\n    threads = [threading.Thread(target=worker, args=(c,), daemon=True)\n               for c in chunks]\n    for t in threads:\n        t.start()\n    barrier.wait()  # release all workers together\n    for t in threads:\n        t.join()\n\n    done = [r for r in results if r is not None]\n    if not done:\n        raise RuntimeError(\n            \"no request completed; first errors: \" + \"; \".join(errors[:3]))\n    if errors:\n        print(f\"fn-stream-client: {len(errors)}/{requests} requests errored \"\n              f\"(first: {errors[0]})\", file=sys.stderr)\n    return done\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Prompt depth convergence.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef _probe_prompt_tokens(api: str, model: str, prompt: str,\n                         timeout: float) -> Optional[int]:\n    \"\"\"Cheap non-streaming probe returning the server's prompt_tokens.\"\"\"\n    url = api.rstrip(\"/\") + \"/v1/completions\"\n    body = {\"model\": model, \"prompt\": prompt, \"max_tokens\": 1, \"temperature\": 0}\n    req = urllib.request.Request(\n        url, data=json.dumps(body).encode(),\n        headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\n    try:\n        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as resp:\n            return json.loads(resp.read())[\"usage\"][\"prompt_tokens\"]\n    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - convergence is best-effort\n        return None\n\n\ndef converge_prompt(api: str, model: str, depth: int, timeout: float,\n                    attempts: int = 3) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Build a prompt the server reports as ~``depth`` tokens.\n\n    Tokenization is engine-side, so we estimate, probe the actual prompt_tokens,\n    and rescale once if we undershot. The returned prompt is what the measured\n    batch uses; the CSV still records depth_actual per request, so an honest\n    residual undershoot is visible rather than hidden.\n    \"\"\"\n    if depth <= 0:\n        return SHORT_PROMPT\n    chars_per_token = 4.0\n    reps = max(1, int((depth * chars_per_token) / len(_DEPTH_PARAGRAPH)))\n    prompt = _DEPTH_PARAGRAPH * reps\n    for _ in range(attempts):\n        got = _probe_prompt_tokens(api, model, prompt, timeout)\n        if got is None or got <= 0:\n            break\n        if abs(got - depth) <= max(64, depth * 0.02):\n            return prompt\n        scale = depth / got\n        reps = max(1, int(reps * scale) + (1 if scale > 1 else 0))\n        prompt = _DEPTH_PARAGRAPH * reps\n    return prompt\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# CSV output.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef fmt(x) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Render a number or an honest absence.\"\"\"\n    if x is None:\n        return \"\"\n    if isinstance(x, float):\n        return f\"{x:.6f}\"\n    return str(x)\n\n\ndef write_csv(path: str, rows: List[Dict]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Append rows, writing the semantics header only into a fresh file.\"\"\"\n    import csv as _csv\n    fresh = not os.path.exists(path) or os.path.getsize(path) == 0\n    parent = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path))\n    if parent:\n        os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)\n    with open(path, \"a\", newline=\"\") as f:\n        if fresh:\n            f.write(CSV_HEADER_COMMENT)\n        w = _csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=CSV_FIELDS)\n        if fresh:\n            w.writeheader()\n        for row in rows:\n            w.writerow({k: row.get(k, \"\") for k in CSV_FIELDS})\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# CLI.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:\n    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        prog=\"fn-stream-client.py\",\n        description=\"Honest streaming bench client: separates queue wait from \"\n                    \"prefill (spec claim 6.2).\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--api\", default=os.environ.get(\n        \"FN_API\", f\"http://127.0.0.1:{os.environ.get('FN_PORT', '1234')}\"),\n        help=\"OpenAI-compatible API base URL\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--metrics\", default=None,\n                   help=\"Metrics endpoint (default: <api>/metrics)\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--model\", default=os.environ.get(\"FN_SERVED_NAME\", \"flashnext\"))\n    p.add_argument(\"--arm\", default=\"unspecified\", help=\"bench arm label\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--load\", type=int, default=1, help=\"load index in the arm\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--depth\", type=int, default=0,\n                   help=\"target prompt depth in tokens\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--concurrency\", type=int, default=int(os.environ.get(\n        \"FN_BENCH_CONCURRENCY\", \"1\")), help=\"in-flight sequences\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--requests\", type=int, default=int(os.environ.get(\n        \"FN_BENCH_REQUESTS\", \"8\")), help=\"completions to measure this call\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--max-tokens\", type=int, default=int(os.environ.get(\n        \"FN_BENCH_MAX_TOKENS\", \"128\")))\n    p.add_argument(\"--temperature\", type=float, default=0.0,\n                   help=\"greedy by default so fingerprints are reproducible\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--timeout\", type=float, default=7200.0)\n    p.add_argument(\"--spec-label\", default=os.environ.get(\n        \"FN_BENCH_SPEC_LABEL\", \"unspecified\"),\n                   help=\"speculative-decoding configuration of the arm\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--csv\", default=None,\n                   help=\"append rows here (stdout summary if omitted)\")\n    return p\n\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)\n    metrics_url = args.metrics or (args.api.rstrip(\"/\") + \"/metrics\")\n\n    prompt = converge_prompt(args.api, args.model, args.depth, args.timeout)\n\n    # Bracket the measured batch with metrics scrapes. Convergence probes ran\n    # BEFORE the first scrape, so the delta covers only the measured requests.\n    try:\n        before = scrape_metrics(metrics_url)\n    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - honest absence, not a crash\n        print(f\"fn-stream-client: pre-scrape failed ({e}); queue/prefill will \"\n              f\"be empty\", file=sys.stderr)\n        before = {}\n    records = run_concurrent(args.api, args.model, prompt, args.max_tokens,\n                             args.temperature, args.concurrency, args.requests,\n                             args.timeout)\n    try:\n        after = scrape_metrics(metrics_url)\n    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001\n        print(f\"fn-stream-client: post-scrape failed ({e}); queue/prefill will \"\n              f\"be empty\", file=sys.stderr)\n        after = {}\n\n    split = split_first_token(before, after)\n\n    rows = []\n    for i, rec in enumerate(records):\n        rows.append({\n            \"arm\": args.arm,\n            \"load\": args.load,\n            \"depth_target\": args.depth,\n            \"depth_actual\": rec.get(\"prompt_tokens\"),\n            \"concurrency\": args.concurrency,\n            \"request_index\": i,\n            \"ttft_s\": fmt(rec[\"ttft\"]),\n            \"queue_wait_s\": fmt(split.queue_wait_s),\n            \"prefill_s\": fmt(split.prefill_s),\n            \"decode_s\": fmt(rec[\"decode\"]),\n            \"total_s\": fmt(rec[\"total\"]),\n            \"completion_tokens\": rec.get(\"completion_tokens\"),\n            \"fingerprint\": rec[\"fingerprint\"],\n            \"spec_config\": args.spec_label,\n        })\n\n    if args.csv:\n        write_csv(args.csv, rows)\n    # Always emit the fingerprints + split on stdout for the matrix to consume.\n    out = {\n        \"arm\": args.arm,\n        \"load\": args.load,\n        \"depth_target\": args.depth,\n        \"concurrency\": args.concurrency,\n        \"requests\": len(rows),\n        \"queue_wait_s\": split.queue_wait_s,\n        \"prefill_s\": split.prefill_s,\n        \"prefill_source\": split.prefill_source,\n        \"queue_samples\": split.queue_samples,\n        \"prefill_samples\": split.prefill_samples,\n        \"fingerprints\": [r[\"fingerprint\"] for r in rows],\n    }\n    print(json.dumps(out))\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":6110,"output":10187,"cacheRead":76032,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":3930,"totalTokens":92329,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990766922,"responseId":"chatcmpl-9d91c96d-73fd-4927-af3a-bebf9b33cf63","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_0d90d7aa83a945879398fbb4","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 25790 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/bench/fn-stream-client.py"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787990960794},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright, let's write the test. The test does the following:\n1. Load the client module via importlib.\n2. Construct Prometheus fixtures with known queue, prefill, and ttft values (before/after snapshots).\n3. Assert that split_first_token separates queue and prefill.\n4. Assert that prefill is not a duplicate of ttft (regression for the §4.4 defect).\n5. Possibly test parse_metrics directly on exposition text.\n6. Test fingerprint determinism and divergence sensitivity.\n\nLet's write a fixture in realistic Prometheus exposition format. To exercise parse_metrics, build the before and after text payloads, and then call split.\n\nLet's design the fixtures:\n- before text: baseline values.\n- after text: baseline + batch.\n\nSet batch = 4 requests.\n- queue: before sum=100.0 count=50; after sum=108.0 count=54 → delta sum=8.0, count=4 → mean=2.0\n- prefill: before sum=25.0 count=50; after sum=27.0 count=54 → delta sum=2.0, count=4 → mean=0.5\n- ttft: before sum=500.0 count=50; after sum=620.0 count=54 → delta sum=120, count=4 → mean=30.0\n\nAssertions:\n- split.queue_wait_s ≈ 2.0\n- split.prefill_s ≈ 0.5\n- queue != prefill\n- prefill != ttft delta mean (30.0) — proves prefill is not ttft.\n- Also, assert prefill_source == PREFILL_TIME_METRIC (i.e., it came from the prefill histogram and not from ttft).\n\nFurthermore, also add a test that directly feeds parse_metrics. And a test for the fallback when the prefill metric is missing (prefill = inference - decode). Additionally, a test for fingerprint determinism/divergence.\n\nLet's also add a test asserting the CSV header's semantics note that prefill is not ttft — this might be overkill. Keep it focused and meaningful.\n\nFor a text-based test, let's write a helper to construct Prometheus text.\n\nLet's write the metrics fixture text generator:\n\n```python\ndef prom_text(vals):\n    # vals: dict metric -> (sum, count)\n    lines = []\n    for m, (s, c) in vals.items():\n        lines.append(f\"# TYPE {m} histogram\")\n        lines.append(f'{m}_bucket{{le=\"0.5\"}} {c}')\n        lines.append(f\"{m}_sum {s}\")\n        lines.append(f\"{m}_count {c}\")\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\"\n```\n\nThen before = prom_text({queue:(100,50), prefill:(25,50), ttft:(500,50)})\nafter = prom_text({queue:(108,54), prefill:(27,54), ttft:(620,54)})\n\nbefore_snap = mod.parse_metrics(before_text)\nafter_snap = mod.parse_metrics(after_text)\nsplit = mod.split_first_token(before_snap, after_snap)\n\nGood. Now let's write the test file.\n\nTest methods:\n1. test_parser_separates_queue_and_prefill — the main separation test.\n2. test_prefill_is_not_the_first_token_column — the §4.4 regression guard.\n3. test_prefill_fallback_inference_minus_decode — fallback.\n4. test_missing_metric_is_honest_absence — prefill is None when absent and no fallback either.\n5. test_fingerprint_deterministic_and_divergence_sensitive.\n6. test_csv_header_documents_separation — assert the header comment notes that prefill is not ttft, and that the CSV has distinct columns. (Optional but nice to have.)\n\nAdditionally, let's assert that py_compile succeeds (though the acceptance criteria do this separately). Not needed in the test.\n\nAlso, verify that the split returns prefill from the prefill metric and not from the ttft metric: assert split.prefill_source == mod.PREFILL_TIME_METRIC.\n\nLet's write the test.\n\nOne concern: the module name for spec_from_file_location — \"fn_stream_client\". Let's use that.\n\nAlso, the client imports things like `urllib.error` — no problem. There is top-level code (constants), but main() is not executed on import (it's guarded by `if __name__ == \"__main__\"`). Good, importing the module will not run main. \n\nLet's write tests/test_bench_client.py.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now the separation test. I'll load the hyphenated client via `importlib` (matching the house pattern in `test_instruments.py`) and feed it realistic Prometheus fixtures with known queue/prefill/TTFT values.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_41d835b675c847229379e2cd","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"\"\"\"Bench-client separation tests (spec claim 6.2).\n\nThe reference harness's client (nix-strix-halo lib/bench/vllm-stream-client.py)\nshipped ``prefill_mean_s`` character-identical to ``ttft_mean_s`` — a proxy\ndressed as a measurement (specs/flashnext/evidence/nix-strix-halo.md §4.4).\nThese tests feed the flashnext client's parser fixtures with known scheduler\nqueue and prompt-processing times and assert the two columns stay separate —\nand that neither is ever a duplicate of the first-token column.\n\nThe client is a hyphenated script (bench/fn-stream-client.py), so it is loaded\nby path with importlib, the same way test_instruments.py loads the overlay\nmodules. Nothing here needs a live engine: the parser and the split are pure.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport importlib.util\nimport pathlib\nimport unittest\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nCLIENT_PATH = ROOT / \"bench\" / \"fn-stream-client.py\"\n\n\ndef _load_client():\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"fn_stream_client\", CLIENT_PATH)\n    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n    return mod\n\n\ndef _prom_text(hists):\n    \"\"\"Render ``{metric: (sum, count)}`` as Prometheus exposition text.\"\"\"\n    lines = []\n    for metric, (total, count) in hists.items():\n        lines.append(f\"# TYPE {metric} histogram\")\n        lines.append(f'{metric}_bucket{{le=\"0.5\"}} {count}')\n        lines.append(f\"{metric}_sum {total}\")\n        lines.append(f\"{metric}_count {count}\")\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\"\n\n\nclass BenchClientSeparation(unittest.TestCase):\n    \"\"\"The prefill column is a measurement, never a first-token duplicate.\"\"\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        cls.mod = _load_client()\n\n    def _fixtures(self):\n        \"\"\"A batch of 4 requests lands between the two scrapes.\n\n        Known per-request ground truth:\n          queue wait  = (108 - 100) / 4 = 2.0 s\n          prefill     = (27  - 25 ) / 4 = 0.5 s\n          server ttft = (620 - 500) / 4 = 30.0 s   (deliberately DIFFERENT,\n                                                    to prove prefill != ttft)\n        \"\"\"\n        q, p, t = (self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC, self.mod.PREFILL_TIME_METRIC,\n                   self.mod.TTFT_METRIC)\n        before = _prom_text({q: (100.0, 50), p: (25.0, 50), t: (500.0, 50)})\n        after = _prom_text({q: (108.0, 54), p: (27.0, 54), t: (620.0, 54)})\n        return before, after\n\n    def test_parser_separates_queue_and_prefill(self):\n        before_text, after_text = self._fixtures()\n        before = self.mod.parse_metrics(before_text)\n        after = self.mod.parse_metrics(after_text)\n        split = self.mod.split_first_token(before, after)\n\n        # Each component recovers its own injected value…\n        self.assertAlmostEqual(split.queue_wait_s, 2.0)\n        self.assertAlmostEqual(split.prefill_s, 0.5)\n        # …and the two columns are genuinely distinct measurements.\n        self.assertNotAlmostEqual(split.queue_wait_s, split.prefill_s)\n        # Both drew from direct samples of their own histograms.\n        self.assertEqual(split.queue_samples, 4)\n        self.assertEqual(split.prefill_samples, 4)\n        self.assertEqual(split.prefill_source, self.mod.PREFILL_TIME_METRIC)\n\n    def test_prefill_is_not_the_first_token_column(self):\n        # The §4.4 regression guard: prefill must track the prefill histogram,\n        # NOT the time-to-first-token series (which here is 30.0 per request).\n        before_text, after_text = self._fixtures()\n        before = self.mod.parse_metrics(before_text)\n        after = self.mod.parse_metrics(after_text)\n        split = self.mod.split_first_token(before, after)\n\n        ttft_mean, _ = self.mod.hist_delta_mean(\n            before, after, self.mod.TTFT_METRIC)\n        self.assertAlmostEqual(ttft_mean, 30.0)\n        self.assertNotAlmostEqual(split.prefill_s, ttft_mean)\n        self.assertNotEqual(split.prefill_source, self.mod.TTFT_METRIC)\n\n    def test_prefill_fallback_is_inference_minus_decode(self):\n        # If the engine build lacks the direct prefill histogram, fall back to\n        # inference - decode — still a server-side measurement, still not ttft.\n        q = self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC\n        inf, dec = (self.mod.INFERENCE_TIME_METRIC, self.mod.DECODE_TIME_METRIC)\n        before = _prom_text({q: (100.0, 50), inf: (60.0, 50), dec: (50.0, 50)})\n        after = _prom_text({q: (108.0, 54), inf: (68.0, 54), dec: (54.0, 54)})\n        split = self.mod.split_first_token(\n            self.mod.parse_metrics(before), self.mod.parse_metrics(after))\n        # prefill = (68-60)/4 - (54-50)/4 = 2.0 - 1.0 = 1.0\n        self.assertAlmostEqual(split.prefill_s, 1.0)\n        self.assertAlmostEqual(split.queue_wait_s, 2.0)\n        self.assertIn(self.mod.INFERENCE_TIME_METRIC, split.prefill_source)\n\n    def test_absent_metrics_are_an_honest_absence(self):\n        # No queue/prefill/inference metrics at all -> None, never a fabricated\n        # proxy from the first-token series.\n        t = self.mod.TTFT_METRIC\n        before = _prom_text({t: (500.0, 50)})\n        after = _prom_text({t: (620.0, 54)})\n        split = self.mod.split_first_token(\n            self.mod.parse_metrics(before), self.mod.parse_metrics(after))\n        self.assertIsNone(split.queue_wait_s)\n        self.assertIsNone(split.prefill_s)\n\n    def test_parse_metrics_ignores_gauges_and_buckets(self):\n        text = (\n            \"# TYPE vllm:num_requests_running gauge\\n\"\n            \"vllm:num_requests_running 3.0\\n\"\n            \"# TYPE \" + self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC + \" histogram\\n\"\n            + self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC + '_bucket{le=\"0.5\"} 2.0\\n'\n            + self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC + \"_sum 7.5\\n\"\n            + self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC + \"_count 3.0\\n\"\n        )\n        snap = self.mod.parse_metrics(text)\n        self.assertIn(self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC, snap)\n        self.assertAlmostEqual(snap[self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC].total, 7.5)\n        self.assertAlmostEqual(snap[self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC].count, 3.0)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"vllm:num_requests_running\", snap)\n\n\nclass BenchClientFingerprint(unittest.TestCase):\n    \"\"\"The completion fingerprint must be reproducible and divergence-sensitive.\"\"\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        cls.mod = _load_client()\n\n    def test_fingerprint_is_deterministic(self):\n        ids = [1, 2, 3, 58, 9001]\n        self.assertEqual(self.mod.fingerprint_of(ids),\n                         self.mod.fingerprint_of(list(ids)))\n\n    def test_fingerprint_detects_divergence(self):\n        # The QSA-gather signature: same prompt, one completion differs by a\n        # single token id. The fingerprint must change.\n        a = [1, 2, 3, 58]\n        b = [1, 2, 3, 59]\n        self.assertNotEqual(self.mod.fingerprint_of(a),\n                            self.mod.fingerprint_of(b))\n\n    def test_fingerprint_never_collides_ids_with_strings(self):\n        # Type-tagging: an id sequence cannot collide with a string sequence\n        # that happens to spell the same digits.\n        self.assertNotEqual(self.mod.fingerprint_of([1, 2]),\n                            self.mod.fingerprint_of([\"1\", \"2\"]))\n\n\nclass BenchClientCSVContract(unittest.TestCase):\n    \"\"\"Every number carries its protocol: the CSV header says which clock is\n    which, and the prefill column is declared independent of the ttft column.\"\"\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        cls.mod = _load_client()\n\n    def test_csv_has_distinct_ttft_queue_prefill_columns(self):\n        fields = self.mod.CSV_FIELDS\n        self.assertIn(\"ttft_s\", fields)\n        self.assertIn(\"queue_wait_s\", fields)\n        self.assertIn(\"prefill_s\", fields)\n        self.assertIn(\"concurrency\", fields)   # steering §1: per-row concurrency\n        self.assertIn(\"fingerprint\", fields)\n        self.assertEqual(len(fields), len(set(fields)))\n\n    def test_csv_header_denies_the_reference_defect(self):\n        header = self.mod.CSV_HEADER_COMMENT\n        self.assertIn(\"queue_wait_s\", header)\n        self.assertIn(\"prefill_s\", header)\n        # The header must state prefill is NOT derived from the first-token\n        # column, and it must name the metrics the split reads from.\n        self.assertIn(\"NOT prefill\", header)\n        self.assertIn(self.mod.QUEUE_TIME_METRIC, header)\n        self.assertIn(self.mod.PREFILL_TIME_METRIC, header)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n","path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/tests/test_bench_client.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":10474,"output":3404,"cacheRead":81920,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1024,"totalTokens":95798,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787990960799,"responseId":"chatcmpl-157b8523-e49e-405f-9f1d-702049a81a06","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_41d835b675c847229379e2cd","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 8394 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/tests/test_bench_client.py"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991026026},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright, before writing the matrix script, let's ru