init, repr, eq, order, unsafe_hash,\n                          frozen, match_args, kw_only, slots,\n                          weakref_slot)\n  File \"/nix/store/avhl4dazg7n1day3i4fsa1ivivrslsrm-python3-3.14.6-env/lib/python3.14/dataclasses.py\", line 1055, in _process_class\n    and _is_type(type, cls, dataclasses, dataclasses.KW_ONLY,\n        ~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n                 _is_kw_only))):\n                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^\n  File \"/nix/store/avhl4dazg7n1day3i4fsa1ivivrslsrm-python3-3.14.6-env/lib/python3.14/dataclasses.py\", line 814, in _is_type\n    ns = sys.modules.get(cls.__module__).__dict__\n         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\nAttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute '__dict__'. Did you mean: '__dir__'?\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 0 tests in 0.021s\n\nFAILED (errors=3)\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991031792},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The dataclass processing fails because the module isn't registered in sys.modules when exec_module runs. The dataclass decorator in Python 3.14 looks up `sys.modules[cls.__module__]`. Since we load via importlib without registering, `cls.__module__` is \"fn_stream_client\" but it's not in sys.modules.\n\nTwo fixes:\n1. In the test loader, register the module in sys.modules before exec_module. But that changes the test loader; other tests (test_instruments) load modules with dataclasses? Let me check — test_instruments loads fn_offload_batch which probably doesn't use dataclasses, so it didn't hit this. \n2. Avoid dataclasses in the client, or register the module.\n\nThe cleanest fix that keeps the client robust regardless of how it's loaded: register the module in sys.modules in the test loader. But if someone else loads the client differently, dataclasses would still fail. Actually, the client is meant to be run as a script (`python3 bench/fn-stream-client.py`), where `__name__ == \"__main__\"` and it IS in sys.modules. The dataclass issue only arises when loaded via importlib without registration.\n\nThe standard fix in the loader: add `sys.modules[spec.name] = mod` before `spec.loader.exec_module(mod)`. Let me check how test_instruments does it — it does NOT register. But fn_offload_batch doesn't use dataclasses, so no problem.\n\nI have two options:\nA. Fix the test loader to register the module in sys.modules.\nB. Avoid dataclasses in the client (use plain classes or namedtuple).\n\nOption A is cleaner and keeps the client idiomatic. But wait — the acceptance command is `python3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v`, which runs my test loader. If I register in sys.modules there, it works. Let me do that. But to be safe and match the house pattern, let me check: the house pattern (test_instruments._load_offload_batch) does NOT register. To be consistent AND correct, I should register. Let me update my loader to register the module.\n\nActually, the most robust approach: register the module in sys.modules in the loader. Let me update _load_client. This is the recommended importlib pattern anyway.\n\nLet me update the test loader:\n```python\ndef _load_client():\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"fn_stream_client\", CLIENT_PATH)\n    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n    sys.modules[spec.name] = mod\n    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n    return mod\n```\n\nNeed `import sys` in the test. Let me add it.\n\nAlternatively, I could avoid dataclasses. But dataclasses are idiomatic and the client is correct; the loader just needs to register. Let me go with registering in the loader — it's the correct importlib usage.\n\nLet me update the test file.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: is it better to make the client not depend on sys.modules registration? A dataclass defined in a module loaded via importlib without registration fails on Python 3.14. This is a real robustness concern. But the client's primary use is as a script. For test-loading, registering is standard. I'll register in the loader. That's the accepted fix.\n\nLet me update the test loader and add `import sys`.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"The dataclass needs the module registered in `sys.modules` when loaded via importlib on Python 3.14. Let me register it in the loader (the standard importlib pattern).\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ac1c86e45cf045188f73fd41","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/tests/test_bench_client.py","edits":[{"oldText":"import importlib.util\nimport pathlib\nimport unittest","newText":"import importlib.util\nimport pathlib\nimport sys\nimport unittest"},{"oldText":"def _load_client():\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"fn_stream_client\", CLIENT_PATH)\n    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n    return mod","newText":"def _load_client():\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"fn_stream_client\", CLIENT_PATH)\n    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n    # Register before exec so dataclasses/typing can resolve the module (py3.14).\n    sys.modules[spec.name] = mod\n    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n    return mod"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1386,"output":1019,"cacheRead":95232,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":723,"totalTokens":97637,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991031797,"responseId":"chatcmpl-452d450a-47a8-48a0-ab4b-33921c15f922","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ac1c86e45cf045188f73fd41","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 2 block(s) in /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/tests/test_bench_client.py."}],"details":{"diff":"     ...\n  13 \"\"\"\n  14 \n  15 import importlib.util\n  16 import pathlib\n+ 17 import sys\n  17 import unittest\n  18 \n  19 ROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n  20 CLIENT_PATH = ROOT / \"bench\" / \"fn-stream-client.py\"\n     ...\n  22 \n  23 def _load_client():\n  24     spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"fn_stream_client\", CLIENT_PATH)\n  25     mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n+ 27     # Register before exec so dataclasses/typing can resolve the module (py3.14).\n+ 28     sys.modules[spec.name] = mod\n  26     spec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n  27     return mod\n  28 \n  29 \n     ...","patch":"--- /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/tests/test_bench_client.py\n+++ /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/tests/test_bench_client.py\n@@ -13,8 +13,9 @@\n \"\"\"\n \n import importlib.util\n import pathlib\n+import sys\n import unittest\n \n ROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n CLIENT_PATH = ROOT / \"bench\" / \"fn-stream-client.py\"\n@@ -22,8 +23,10 @@\n \n def _load_client():\n     spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"fn_stream_client\", CLIENT_PATH)\n     mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n+    # Register before exec so dataclasses/typing can resolve the module (py3.14).\n+    sys.modules[spec.name] = mod\n     spec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n     return mod\n \n \n","firstChangedLine":17},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991054101},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Also, to prevent the dataclass from failing under other loading methods, we should add the same registration protection to the client itself. Actually, let's consider whether we should make the client more robust. The failure only occurs when loaded via importlib without registration. The acceptance tests are now registered. When run as a script, there's no problem. So there's no issue.\n\nBut wait — what if py_compile is run and something imports it? No, that's not the case. OK.\n\nLet's rerun the tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a61f455e28e440579868f202","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness\npython3 -m unittest tests.test_bench_client -v 2>&1 | tail -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1442,"output":193,"cacheRead":96256,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":109,"totalTokens":97891,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991054107,"responseId":"chatcmpl-ea824f99-0076-40e0-987e-5380e1b3c500","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_a61f455e28e440579868f202","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"test_csv_has_distinct_ttft_queue_prefill_columns (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientCSVContract.test_csv_has_distinct_ttft_queue_prefill_columns) ... ok\ntest_csv_header_denies_the_reference_defect (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientCSVContract.test_csv_header_denies_the_reference_defect) ... ok\ntest_fingerprint_detects_divergence (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientFingerprint.test_fingerprint_detects_divergence) ... ok\ntest_fingerprint_is_deterministic (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientFingerprint.test_fingerprint_is_deterministic) ... ok\ntest_fingerprint_never_collides_ids_with_strings (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientFingerprint.test_fingerprint_never_collides_ids_with_strings) ... ok\ntest_absent_metrics_are_an_honest_absence (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientSeparation.test_absent_metrics_are_an_honest_absence) ... ok\ntest_parse_metrics_ignores_gauges_and_buckets (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientSeparation.test_parse_metrics_ignores_gauges_and_buckets) ... ok\ntest_parser_separates_queue_and_prefill (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientSeparation.test_parser_separates_queue_and_prefill) ... ok\ntest_prefill_fallback_is_inference_minus_decode (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientSeparation.test_prefill_fallback_is_inference_minus_decode) ... ok\ntest_prefill_is_not_the_first_token_column (tests.test_bench_client.BenchClientSeparation.test_prefill_is_not_the_first_token_column) ... ok\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 10 tests in 0.023s\n\nOK\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991061135},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All 10 tests pass. Now let me write the matrix script `bench/run-matrix.sh`.\n\nRequirements recap:\n- Source fn-env.sh for env doctrine.\n- Counterbalanced interleaved arms A-B-B-A (spec_off / spec_on).\n- Three loads per arm.\n- Medians reported.\n- Depth series 0, 10240, 102400 tokens.\n- Speculative on/off arms.\n- Results committed under results/.\n- bench.json receipt: {loads_per_arm, counterbalanced:true, arms, fingerprints}.\n- Steering: record concurrency per arm per output row (client does this), and on fingerprint divergence, serial replay + flag suspect.\n\nLet me design the script carefully.\n\nStructure:\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# header explaining doctrine\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\" && pwd)\"\nHOST_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/host\"\nCLIENT=\"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-stream-client.py\"\nRESULTS=\"$REPO_ROOT/results\"\nBENCH_DIR=\"$RESULTS/bench\"\nRECEIPTS=\"$RESULTS/receipts\"\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$HOST_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nmkdir -p \"$BENCH_DIR\" \"$RECEIPTS\"\n```\n\nConfig knobs:\n```bash\nLOADS_PER_ARM=\"${FN_BENCH_LOADS_PER_ARM:-3}\"\nDEPTHS=(0 10240 102400)\nCONCURRENCY=\"${FN_BENCH_CONCURRENCY:-1}\"\nREQUESTS_PER_LOAD=\"${FN_BENCH_REQUESTS:-8}\"\nMAX_TOKENS=\"${FN_BENCH_MAX_TOKENS:-128}\"\nAPI=\"${FN_API:-http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT}\"\nMODEL=\"$FN_SERVED_NAME\"\n```\n\nArms:\n```bash\nARMS=(spec_off spec_on)\nSPEC_OFF_LABEL=\"spec-off\"\nSPEC_ON_LABEL=\"spec-on\"\n# speculative args for spec_on (UNKNOWN-5: tuning is morning work)\nif [ -n \"${FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS:-}\" ]; then\n  read -r -a SPEC_ON_EXTRA <<< \"$FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS\"\nelse\n  SPEC_ON_EXTRA=( --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":8,\"prompt_lookup_max\":8,\"prompt_lookup_min\":2}' )\nfi\n```\n\nCounterbalanced schedule: I'll build arrays SCHED_ARM and SCHED_LOAD. For loads_per_arm L, generate interleaved ABBA-style:\n```bash\nSCHED_ARM=(); SCHED_LOAD=()\nfor ((i=1; i<=LOADS_PER_ARM; i++)); do\n  if (( i % 2 == 1 )); then\n    SCHED_ARM+=(spec_off spec_on)\n    SCHED_LOAD+=(\"$i\" \"$i\")\n  else\n    SCHED_ARM+=(spec_on spec_off)\n    SCHED_LOAD+=(\"$i\" \"$i\")\n  fi\ndone\n```\nFor L=3: i=1: off,on; i=2: on,off; i=3: off,on → sequence off,on,on,off,off,on. That's the ABBA pattern (A B B A A B). Each arm 3 loads.\n\nWait let me double check: i=1 (odd): A B → off,on. i=2 (even): B A → on,off. i=3 (odd): A B → off,on. Total: off,on, on,off, off,on = [off, on, on, off, off, on]. Counts: off appears at positions 1,4,5 = 3; on at 2,3,6 = 3. Good. Interleaved, not blocked. And it literally contains \"A-B-B-A\" (positions 1-4: off,on,on,off = A,B,B,A). \n\nNow serve management. The pair should already be up (baseline spec_off) from first light. Let me ensure it's up, then track current_arm.\n\n```bash\nensure_pair_up() {\n  if curl -fsS \"$API/v1/models\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"\\\"$MODEL\\\"\"; then\n    return 0\n  fi\n  log \"pair not answering; standing it up via host/fn-cluster-up.sh\"\n  bash \"$HOST_DIR/fn-cluster-up.sh\"\n}\n```\n\nServe restart for an arm:\n```bash\nserve_arm() {\n  local arm=\"$1\"\n  if [ \"$CURRENT_ARM\" = \"$arm\" ]; then return 0; fi\n  log \"reconfiguring serve for arm $arm\"\n  # reap serve inside the container\n  podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"pkill -TERM -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true\"\n  sleep 4\n  podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"pkill -KILL -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' 2>/dev/null || true\"\n  # assemble extra args\n  local -a extra=()\n  case \"$arm\" in\n    spec_on) extra=( \"${SPEC_ON_EXTRA[@]}\" ) ;;\n    *) extra=() ;;\n  esac\n  podman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR \\\n    --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME \\\n    --host 0.0.0.0 --port $FN_PORT \\\n    --tensor-parallel-size 2 \\\n    --distributed-executor-backend ray \\\n    --enforce-eager \\\n    --gpu-memory-utilization $FN_GPU_UTIL \\\n    --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX \\\n    ${extra[*]} \\\n    > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"\n  wait_ready\n  CURRENT_ARM=\"$arm\"\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, the \"Do not add comments inside backslash-continued vllm serve\" warning from fn-cluster-up applies. I'll avoid comments there.\n\nAlso `${extra[*]}` — when extra is empty, it expands to nothing; fine. But with set -u, `\"${SPEC_ON_EXTRA[@]}\"` on an empty array... SPEC_ON_EXTRA is always non-empty (has default). For spec_off, extra=() then `${extra[*]}` under set -u with empty array: `${extra[*]}` on empty array is OK (expands to empty), but `\"${extra[@]}\"` under set -u errors on empty in older bash; `${extra[*]}` unquoted is fine. Let me use `${extra[*]:-}` to be safe. Actually `${extra[*]}` unquoted on empty array expands to nothing and is fine even with set -u (the `:-` isn't needed for `[*]` but let me add safety). Let me use `${extra[*]+\"${extra[*]}\"}` pattern, or simpler: build the full command string. Let me use an array for the whole serve command to avoid quoting issues.\n\nActually, passing the JSON speculative-config through bash is tricky. Let me build the serve command as a string and pass to bash -c. Let me construct:\n\n```bash\nlocal serve_cmd=\"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME --host 0.0.0.0 --port $FN_PORT --tensor-parallel-size 2 --distributed-executor-backend ray --enforce-eager --gpu-memory-utilization $FN_GPU_UTIL --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX\"\nif [ \"$arm\" = \"spec_on\" ]; then\n  serve_cmd=\"$serve_cmd ${SPEC_ON_EXTRA[*]}\"\nfi\npodman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"$serve_cmd > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"\n```\n\nWait, the redirection: `bash -c \"$serve_cmd > log 2>&1\"` — the `> log` applies to the command inside bash -c. Let me put redirect inside: `\"$serve_cmd > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"`. That works: bash -c runs `exec vllm serve ... > log 2>&1`. Good.\n\nSPEC_ON_EXTRA[*] with the JSON value containing spaces: `--speculative-config '{\"method\":...}'` — the array element is `{\"method\":...}` (single element). `${SPEC_ON_EXTRA[*]}` joins with space → `--speculative-config {\"method\":...}`. Inside the double-quoted serve_cmd string, the JSON has spaces, so when bash -c re-parses it, the JSON would be split on spaces → broken. This is a real quoting problem.\n\nTo avoid this, I should not embed a spaced JSON through multiple layers of shell. Options:\n1. Use a speculative config without spaces. But JSON inherently has spaces unless I strip them. I can write compact JSON without spaces: `{\"method\":\"ngram\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":8,\"prompt_lookup_max\":8,\"prompt_lookup_min\":2}` — no spaces! If I write the JSON without any spaces, it's a single token and survives shell splitting. Let me do that. The default SPEC_ON_EXTRA can be a compact no-space JSON. Then `${SPEC_ON_EXTRA[*]}` → `--speculative-config {\"method\":\"ngram\",...}` which is two tokens: `--speculative-config` and the JSON (no internal spaces) → survives. \n\nLet me make the default JSON compact (no spaces). And document that FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS, if provided, should be a single-token-safe value or the operator should quote carefully. Actually, since the operator provides FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS as a string that we `read -r -a` into an array and then join with `[*]`, any spaces within an element would break. Let me instead require FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS to be the raw extra args and NOT re-split — but we need it as part of the serve command string anyway.\n\nSimplest robust approach: represent SPEC_ON_EXTRA as a single string (not array) that's already space-safe (compact JSON, no spaces in values). Let me define:\n```bash\nSPEC_ON_EXTRA=\"${FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS:---speculative-config {\\\"method\\\":\\\"ngram\\\",\\\"num_speculative_tokens\\\":8,\\\"prompt_lookup_max\\\":8,\\\"prompt_lookup_min\\\":2}}\"\n```\nHmm, the JSON has no spaces so when appended to serve_cmd and re-parsed by bash -c, `{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}` is one word. Good. But wait, does vLLM accept `--speculative-config {\"method\":...}` without quoting? The JSON is one shell word (no spaces), so bash passes it as a single argv to vllm. vLLM then parses it as JSON. Good, as long as no shell metacharacters in the JSON get interpreted. `{`, `}`, `\"`, `:` — inside bash -c's double-quoted string, the `\"` would be interpreted! Because serve_cmd is built with double quotes and contains literal `\"`. Let me think.\n\nWhen I do `serve_cmd=\"... --speculative-config {\\\"method\\\":\\\"ngram\\\",...}\"`, the serve_cmd variable contains `--speculative-config {\"method\":\"ngram\",...}` (with real double quotes). Then `podman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"$serve_cmd > log 2>&1\"` — the outer `\"$serve_cmd ...\"` passes the content to bash -c. bash -c then parses `exec vllm serve ... --speculative-config {\"method\":\"ngram\",...} > log 2>&1`. In this re-parse, `{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}` — the double quotes here are shell quotes, so bash would interpret them: `{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}` → the quotes group, resulting in the word `{method:ngram,...}` (quotes removed!). That corrupts the JSON.\n\nThis is a quoting nightmare through two shell layers. Let me avoid embedding JSON through bash -c.\n\nBetter approach: write the speculative config to a file or env var and have the serve read it, OR pass it via a single-quoted heredoc that avoids re-interpretation.\n\nCleaner: use `podman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c '...'` with single quotes won't work for variable interpolation.\n\nAlternative: Instead of building the serve command inline, let me write a small serve-launcher that reads the arm from an env file. Actually, let me reconsider — maybe I can pass the speculative args via a file that bash -c cats.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me step back and reconsider whether speculative-config with JSON is even the right mechanism, or whether I should use a simpler speculative toggle.\n\nActually, for the purpose of this harness (which is graded on syntax + the client test, not on actually running), I should make the serve-restart mechanism clean and plausible without getting lost in JSON quoting. Let me use a more robust approach: write the serve command to a script file in FN_STATE_DIR and execute that. This avoids multi-layer quoting.\n\nLet me do:\n```bash\nserve_arm() {\n  local arm=\"$1\"\n  [ \"$CURRENT_ARM\" = \"$arm\" ] && return 0\n  log \"reconfiguring serve for arm '$arm'\"\n  # reap\n  podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"pkill -TERM -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true\"\n  sleep 4\n  podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"pkill -KILL -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true\" 2>/dev/null || true\n\n  local spec_args=\"\"\n  [ \"$arm\" = \"spec_on\" ] && spec_args=\"$SPEC_ON_EXTRA\"\n\n  # Write a launcher into the shared state dir (bind-mounted into the container)\n  cat > \"$FN_STATE_DIR/fn-bench-serve.sh\" <<EOF\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nexec vllm serve \"$FN_MODEL_DIR\" \\\\\n  --served-model-name \"$FN_SERVED_NAME\" \\\\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 \\\\\n  --port \"$FN_PORT\" \\\\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 2 \\\\\n  --distributed-executor-backend ray \\\\\n  --enforce-eager \\\\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization \"$FN_GPU_UTIL\" \\\\\n  --max-model-len \"$FN_MAX_CTX\" \\\\\n  $spec_args \\\\\n  > \"$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log\" 2>&1\nEOF\n  podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" chmod +x \"$FN_STATE_DIR/fn-bench-serve.sh\"\n  podman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" \"$FN_STATE_DIR/fn-bench-serve.sh\"\n  wait_ready\n  CURRENT_ARM=\"$arm\"\n}\n```\n\nBut `$spec_args` inside the heredoc EOF (unquoted) gets expanded at heredoc-write time. If spec_args contains the JSON with spaces, it's written literally into the script file (good — no shell re-interpretation at write time since it's just string interpolation into the file). Then when the script runs, `exec vllm serve ... $spec_args ...` — wait, in the generated script, `$spec_args` is already substituted (it's the literal JSON text). So the generated script line reads:\n```\n  --speculative-config {\"method\":\"ngram\",...} \\\n```\nWhen bash executes this script, it parses `{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}` — again the double quotes are shell quotes → corrupted. Same problem.\n\nThe issue is the JSON contains double quotes which bash interprets. To pass JSON safely, it must be quoted when the final command runs. Let me single-quote it in the generated script. I can write the spec_args with single quotes around the JSON:\n\nIn the generated script:\n```\n  --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}' \\\n```\nSingle quotes protect the JSON from shell interpretation, and vllm receives `{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}` as one arg. \n\nSo SPEC_ON_EXTRA should be the string `--speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}'` INCLUDING the single quotes. Then when interpolated into the generated script, it's `--speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}'`, which bash parses correctly (single quotes protect the JSON). \n\nLet me define:\n```bash\nSPEC_ON_EXTRA=\"${FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS:---speculative-config '{\\\"method\\\":\\\"ngram\\\",\\\"num_speculative_tokens\\\":8,\\\"prompt_lookup_max\\\":8,\\\"prompt_lookup_min\\\":2}'}\"\n```\nWait, in bash, to have the variable contain literal single quotes and double quotes: \n```bash\nSPEC_ON_EXTRA='--speculative-config '\\''{\"method\":\"ngram\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":8,\"prompt_lookup_max\":8,\"prompt_lookup_min\":2}'\\'''\n```\nThis is the classic single-quote-escaping. Ugly but works. Alternatively use $'...' or double quotes with escaped inner doubles:\n```bash\nSPEC_ON_EXTRA=\"--speculative-config '{\\\"method\\\":\\\"ngram\\\",\\\"num_speculative_tokens\\\":8,\\\"prompt_lookup_max\\\":8,\\\"prompt_lookup_min\\\":2}'\"\n```\nInside double quotes, `\\\"` becomes `\"`, and `'` is literal. So SPEC_ON_EXTRA = `--speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}'`. Good. That contains single quotes around the JSON.\n\nThen in the heredoc (unquoted EOF), `$spec_args` interpolates to `--speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}'`. Written into the script file literally. When the script runs, bash sees `--speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}'` → single quotes protect → vllm gets the JSON as one arg. \n\nBut wait — heredoc with unquoted EOF also does variable expansion AND interprets backslashes/`$`. The `$spec_args` expands. But does the heredoc interpret the single quotes or backslashes in the expanded value? No — heredoc expansion substitutes the variable's value literally (no further quote processing of the substituted text). So the single quotes and double quotes in the value are written as-is. Good.\n\nActually, there's a subtlety: in an unquoted heredoc, backslash and `$` and backtick are special, but only in the heredoc BODY, not in the values substituted by variable expansion. Variable expansion inserts the value verbatim. So `$spec_args` value is inserted verbatim. Good.\n\nOK this approach works but the heredoc + generated script adds complexity. Let me simplify further: instead of generating a script file, I can pass the serve command to `bash -c` but with the spec args properly single-quoted. The problem earlier was double-quote interpretation. If SPEC_ON_EXTRA contains single quotes around the JSON, and I build serve_cmd as a string and pass to `bash -c \"$serve_cmd\"`, then inside bash -c, the single quotes in serve_cmd would protect the JSON. Let me verify:\n\nserve_cmd = `exec vllm serve ... --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}' > log 2>&1`\n(where the JSON part has single quotes from SPEC_ON_EXTRA)\n\nThen `podman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"$serve_cmd\"`. The outer double quotes pass serve_cmd's content to bash -c. bash -c parses: `exec vllm serve ... --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}' > log 2>&1`. The single quotes protect the JSON → vllm gets it as one arg. The `> log 2>&1` redirect works. \n\nBut wait: the outer `\"$serve_cmd\"` — when I write `bash -c \"$serve_cmd\"`, the shell expands $serve_cmd within double quotes and passes the resulting string as ONE argument to bash -c. Then bash -c parses that string. In that string, the single quotes are present and protect the JSON. \n\nBut there's the concern: does the value of serve_cmd contain any double quotes that the OUTER shell (the `\"$serve_cmd\"` expansion) would interpret? No — variable expansion inside double quotes does NOT re-interpret the value's quotes. The value is inserted literally as the argument. So any double/single quotes in serve_cmd's value are passed to bash -c verbatim, and bash -c then interprets them. Since I've single-quoted the JSON in serve_cmd, bash -c handles it correctly. But if serve_cmd contained literal double quotes (from the JSON without single-quote protection), bash -c would strip them. By using single quotes around the JSON, I avoid that.\n\nSo the approach: SPEC_ON_EXTRA = `--speculative-config '{\"method\":\"ngram\",...}'` (with single quotes). Build serve_cmd with it. Pass via `bash -c \"$serve_cmd\"`. This works and avoids the generated-script file.\n\nWait, but there's still the outer layer: `podman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"$serve_cmd\"`. Actually, podman exec passes `bash`, `-c`, and the serve_cmd as argv to the container. So inside the container, bash -c receives serve_cmd as the command string. Good, no additional shell layer. So only ONE re-parse (by bash -c inside the container). The single quotes protect the JSON. \n\nLet me go with this (no generated script file). Let me verify the serve_cmd construction handles empty spec_args (spec_off). When spec_args is empty, serve_cmd has no speculative args. Good.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the redirection. `serve_cmd=\"$base $spec_args > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"`. When bash -c parses, `> '...'` single-quoted path works. Good. Let me include that.\n\nLet me also make sure FN_STATE_DIR is writable and visible in the container — yes, fn-cluster-up bind-mounts `$FN_STATE_DIR:$FN_STATE_DIR`. Good.\n\nNow wait_ready:\n```bash\nwait_ready() {\n  local attempts=$(( ${FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT:-2700} / 20 ))\n  local ready=0 i\n  for ((i=0; i<attempts; i++)); do\n    if curl -fsS \"$API/v1/models\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"\\\"$MODEL\\\"\"; then\n      ready=1; break\n    fi\n    sleep 20\n  done\n  if [ \"$ready\" -ne 1 ]; then\n    log \"FATAL: serve did not answer for arm $CURRENT_ARM\"\n    tail -n 40 \"$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log\" >&2 || true\n    exit 1\n  fi\n}\n```\n\nNow the measurement loop. For each scheduled (arm, load), ensure serve_arm, then for each depth, run the client appending to the matrix CSV.\n\n```bash\nMATRIX_CSV=\"$BENCH_DIR/matrix.csv\"\n: > \"$MATRIX_CSV\"   # fresh each run (client writes header on fresh file)\n```\n\nWait, the client writes the header comment only on a fresh (empty/nonexistent) file. If I `: >` it, it's empty, first client call writes header. Subsequent calls append. Good. But if I `: >` truncate, then the first call sees empty → writes header. Good. Let me NOT pre-truncate with the client's header logic; just remove any old file: `rm -f \"$MATRIX_CSV\"`. Then first call creates fresh. Good.\n\nLoop:\n```bash\nfor idx in \"${!SCHED_ARM[@]}\"; do\n  arm=\"${SCHED_ARM[$idx]}\"\n  load=\"${SCHED_LOAD[$idx]}\"\n  serve_arm \"$arm\"\n  label=\"$(arm_label \"$arm\")\"   # spec-off / spec-on\n  for depth in \"${DEPTHS[@]}\"; do\n    log \"arm=$arm load=$load depth=$depth concurrency=$CONCURRENCY\"\n    python3 \"$CLIENT\" \\\n      --api \"$API\" --model \"$MODEL\" \\\n      --arm \"$label\" --load \"$load\" \\\n      --depth \"$depth\" \\\n      --concurrency \"$CONCURRENCY\" \\\n      --requests \"$REQUESTS_PER_LOAD\" \\\n      --max-tokens \"$MAX_TOKENS\" \\\n      --spec-label \"$label\" \\\n      --csv \"$MATRIX_CSV\" \\\n      >> \"$BENCH_DIR/load-summaries.jsonl\"\n  done\ndone\n```\n\nThe client prints a JSON summary (with fingerprints) to stdout; I capture into load-summaries.jsonl for the aggregation. Good.\n\narm_label:\n```bash\narm_label() { case \"$1\" in spec_on) echo \"spec-on\";; *) echo \"spec-off\";; esac; }\n```\n\nNow aggregation: medians + divergence + serial replay + bench.json. This is the complex part. Let me do it in an embedded python heredoc that reads MATRIX_CSV and load-summaries.jsonl, computes medians per (arm, depth), detects fingerprint divergence per (arm,depth) across loads, and writes:\n- median CSV: $BENCH_DIR/medians.csv\n- divergence report: $BENCH_DIR/divergent.json (list of (arm,depth) that diverged)\n\nThen shell does serial replay for divergent arms, then a final heredoc writes bench.json.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: to keep it coherent, let me have the python aggregation detect divergence and perform the serial replay ITSELF by invoking the client (subprocess) at concurrency=1. That keeps the logic in one place (python) rather than ping-ponging shell/python. The python can call the client via subprocess for serial replay. That's clean.\n\nLet me design the final aggregation heredoc:\n```python\nimport csv, json, os, statistics, subprocess, sys\nclient = os.environ[\"CLIENT\"]\nmatrix_csv = os.environ[\"MATRIX_CSV\"]\nbench_dir = os.environ[\"BENCH_DIR\"]\nreceipts = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\napi = os.environ[\"API\"]; model = os.environ[\"MODEL\"]\nloads_per_arm = int(os.environ[\"LOADS_PER_ARM\"])\n# read rows\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(open(matrix_csv)))  # but header comment lines start with '#' — DictReader would choke. Need to skip comment lines.\n```\n\nWait, the CSV has `#` comment lines before the header. csv.DictReader doesn't skip comments. I need to filter lines starting with `#` before passing to csv reader. Let me read lines, filter comments, then csv.DictReader on the filtered lines.\n\n```python\nlines = [l for l in open(matrix_csv) if not l.startswith(\"#\")]\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(lines))\n```\n\nThen compute per (arm, depth_target) the median of ttft_s, queue_wait_s, prefill_s, decode_s, total_s. And collect fingerprints per (arm, depth_target).\n\nMedian of numeric column: filter non-empty, convert to float, statistics.median.\n\nFingerprint divergence per (arm, depth): set of fingerprints across rows. If len(set) > 1 → divergent.\n\nFor divergent (arm, depth): serial replay — run the client at concurrency=1 for that arm/depth, get fingerprints (should all be identical if deterministic). Compare: the serial fingerprint is the reference. Byte-compare means compare the actual completions, but we only have fingerprints here. The steering says \"byte-compare a serial replay\". To byte-compare, we'd need the actual completion text/ids. The client only outputs fingerprints to stdout. Hmm. Let me have the serial replay write a small file with the completion text so we can byte-compare. \n\nActually, \"byte-compare a serial (concurrency=1) replay\" — the intent: run the same arm serially and check if the output is deterministic (single fingerprint). If serial is consistent but concurrent diverged, that's the QSA signature. The \"byte-compare\" is comparing the serial outputs against each other / against the concurrent ones. Since we have fingerprints, comparing fingerprints IS comparing the outputs byte-for-byte at the token level (fingerprint is over token ids). So: serial replay produces fingerprints; if all serial fingerprints are identical, serial is clean. Then we check whether the concurrent divergent fingerprints include the serial reference (i.e., some concurrent runs match serial, others don't → QSA wrong-output in some concurrent sequences).\n\nLet me implement: for a divergent (arm,depth), run serial replay (concurrency=1, same requests count), collect fingerprints. If serial fingerprints are all one value (clean), record `serial_clean=true` and the reference fingerprint. Flag arm suspect with qsa_signature = (concurrent diverged) AND (serial clean). The byte-compare is the fingerprint comparison.\n\nTo truly \"byte-compare\", let me also have the client, when run for serial replay, optionally dump the completion bytes. But the client currently only outputs fingerprints + summary. Let me add a way: the client could write completions to a file if --dump-completions is given. Hmm, but I already wrote the client. Let me add a `--dump` option? That requires editing the client. Alternatively, I can make the serial-replay comparison purely fingerprint-based, which is a byte-level comparison of the token sequence (since fingerprint is a cryptographic hash of the token ids — equal fingerprints ⇒ equal token sequences with overwhelming probability). That satisfies \"byte-compare\" in spirit (compare the token sequences via their content hash). \n\nActually, the steering says \"byte-compare a serial (concurrency=1) replay of that arm before its numbers are trusted\". The purpose is to verify determinism serially. Comparing fingerprints of the serial replay (all must be identical) + comparing them to the concurrent reference achieves this. I'll implement fingerprint-based comparison and document that the fingerprint is a content hash of the token ids, so fingerprint equality is byte-equality of the token sequence.\n\nLet me add a small enhancement: I'll have the serial replay capture the client's stdout JSON (which includes fingerprints) and check consistency. Good, no client change needed.\n\nLet me write the aggregation heredoc to:\n1. Load rows.\n2. Compute medians per (arm, depth) → write medians.csv.\n3. Detect divergence per (arm, depth) via fingerprint sets.\n4. For divergent ones, subprocess-run the client serially (concurrency=1) for that arm+depth, parse stdout JSON fingerprints, determine serial_clean and reference, mark suspect.\n5. Write bench.json receipt.\n6. Write divergent.json / suspect report.\n\nFor the serial replay subprocess, I need to pass the arm's spec config. But the client doesn't change server config — the server is already in the arm's config (serve_arm was called). But during aggregation, the server is in whatever the last arm's config was. For serial replay of a specific arm, I need the server in that arm's config. Hmm. This requires serve_arm before serial replay. But the aggregation is in python; serve_arm is bash. \n\nLet me restructure: do the serial replay in BASH (which has serve_arm), not in python. So:\n- Python aggregation #1: compute medians, detect divergence, write medians.csv and a divergent list file (arm,depth lines).\n- Bash: for each divergent (arm,depth), serve_arm to that arm, run client serial (concurrency=1), capture fingerprints, determine if serial-clean, record to a serial-replay file.\n- Python aggregation #2: combine medians + divergence + serial results → write bench.json.\n\nThis is more moving parts but respects that serve management is bash. Let me do that.\n\nActually, let me simplify the serial replay: since divergence is per (arm, depth), and serial replay needs the arm's serve config, I'll loop over divergent arms (unique arms), serve_arm once per arm, then replay all divergent depths for that arm serially.\n\nLet me define the flow concretely:\n\nPhase A (measurement) — already described.\n\nPhase B (medians + divergence detection), python heredoc #1:\n- reads MATRIX_CSV\n- writes $BENCH_DIR/medians.csv (arm, depth, concurrency, median_ttft, median_queue, median_prefill, median_decode, n_requests, distinct_fingerprints)\n- writes $BENCH_DIR/divergent.txt: lines \"arm<TAB>depth\" for (arm,depth) with >1 distinct fingerprint.\n- Also record per-arm fingerprint sets for the receipt later.\n\nPhase C (serial replay), bash:\n- read divergent.txt; for each unique arm, serve_arm; for each (arm,depth) in that arm, run client serial (concurrency=1, requests=REQUESTS_PER_LOAD), capture stdout JSON, extract fingerprints; if all equal → serial_clean=true; append to $BENCH_DIR/serial.jsonl a record {arm, depth, serial_clean, serial_fingerprints, reference}.\n\nPhase D (receipt), python heredoc #2:\n- reads medians.csv, divergent.txt, serial.jsonl, load-summaries.jsonl\n- builds bench.json receipt:\n  {step:\"bench\", status:\"pass\", ts, data:{\n    loads_per_arm: LOADS_PER_ARM,\n    counterbalanced: true,\n    arms: [ {arm, spec_config, depths:[...], loads: LOADS_PER_ARM} ],\n    fingerprints: { \"<arm>/<depth>\": {distinct: [...], count: n, serial_clean: bool|null, suspect: bool} },\n    medians_file: \"results/bench/medians.csv\",\n    concurrency: CONCURRENCY,\n    protocol: { depths: [...], loads_per_arm, counterbalanced: true, arms:[...], schedule: [...] },\n  }}\n- writes to $RECEIPTS/bench.json\n\nSuspect flag: an (arm,depth) is suspect if it diverged AND (serial_clean is true → QSA signature, OR serial also diverged → even worse). Per steering: \"Fingerprint divergence at concurrency>1 with a clean serial replay is the QSA-gather signature; record it, do not paper over it.\" So suspect=true when diverged. And if serial replay is clean, it's specifically the QSA signature. If serial replay ALSO diverges, that's nondeterminism beyond QSA — still suspect. Either way, flag suspect and don't average over divergence (i.e., in medians, maybe withhold or mark). \n\n\"rather than averaging over the divergence\" — so for suspect (arm,depth), I should not present the median as a clean number. Let me mark those medians as suspect in the medians.csv (add a `suspect` column) and in bench.json. The median is still computed (for the record) but flagged suspect so no one quotes it as clean. Actually \"do not average over the divergence\" might mean: don't report a median that mixes divergent outputs. Let me set suspect=true and keep the median but clearly flag it, and note in the receipt. That's honest: the number exists but is marked untrustworthy. Alternatively, I could blank the median for suspect cells. Let me keep the median but flag suspect prominently, and document. That satisfies \"flag the arm as suspect in the output rather than averaging over the divergence\" — the flag is the key; I won't let a suspect median be read as clean.\n\nHmm, \"rather than averaging over the divergence\" is fairly explicit: don't average. Let me be safe: for suspect (arm,depth), I'll report the median as usual BUT set a `suspect: true` and `trusted: false` flag, and additionally I could report the per-load values instead of a single blended median. To honor \"do not average over the divergence\", I'll set the median cell but mark trusted=false and include the divergent fingerprints. The reader must not use it. I think flagging is the pragmatic reading. But to be more faithful, let me NOT collapse divergent arms into a single median: instead mark suspect and withhold the trusted median (set median but trusted=false). I'll document clearly.\n\nLet me reconsider — I think the cleanest honest approach: for suspect cells, still compute median (it's a well-defined statistic) but attach `suspect=true` and `qsa_signature` where applicable, and the receipt's top-level status stays pass but data.suspect_arms lists them. The morning operator sees the flags. This is \"record it, do not paper over it.\" I'll go with flagging + listing suspect arms. I won't silently average and call it clean.\n\nOK. This is a lot. Let me write run-matrix.sh now, carefully. I'll keep the python heredocs focused.\n\nLet me also handle the schedule recording for the receipt (protocol). I'll write the schedule to a variable/file.\n\nLet me also make sure the script is `bash -n` clean. I'll be careful with heredocs and quoting.\n\nLet me reconsider the `set -euo pipefail` with arrays and optional expansions. Under `set -u`, referencing an empty array `\"${arr[@]}\"` errors in bash < 4.4; modern bash (4.4+) treats `\"${arr[@]}\"` of empty array as nothing without error. The system bash is likely modern. But to be safe, I'll use `${arr[@]+\"${arr[@]}\"}` where an array might be empty. SPEC_ON_EXTRA is never empty. DEPTHS never empty. SCHED arrays never empty. So mostly fine.\n\nFor the `extra` in serve_arm, I'm using SPEC_ON_EXTRA string directly, not an array. Good.\n\nLet me write the script.\n\nFor arm_label and spec config label: spec-off and spec-on.\n\nLet me also record concurrency in every client call (the client does that via --concurrency, writing the concurrency column). Good — steering §1 satisfied.\n\nLet me also make sure I record the schedule order (the ABBA counterbalancing) in the receipt as proof of counterbalancing.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nOne more: the depths include 102400 which is large. converge_prompt probes to build a ~102400-token prompt. That's a big prompt but within max-model-len 262144. The probe requests for convergence use max_tokens=1. Fine.\n\nAlso, at depth 102400 with concurrency>1, KV memory could be an issue, but that's a runtime concern, not acceptance.\n\nLet me write run-matrix.sh.\n\nI need to be careful about the python heredocs reading env. I'll export needed vars.\n\nLet me draft it fully.\n\nActually, for Phase B/C/D, let me reduce complexity by doing divergence detection and serial replay orchestration, but keep the receipt assembly straightforward.\n\nLet me write the file now.\n\nRegarding the `# Do not add comments inside backslash-continued vllm serve` — I'm building serve_cmd as a variable, not a backslash-continued command, so comments are fine elsewhere. But I must be careful not to put `#` inside the serve_cmd string. I won't.\n\nLet me write.\n\nLet me also think about `wait_ready` and `serve_arm` interaction with the FIRST arm. Initially CURRENT_ARM=\"\". First scheduled arm is spec_off. serve_arm(spec_off) sees CURRENT_ARM != spec_off, so it reaps and restarts serve as spec_off. But the pair is already up as spec_off (baseline) from first light. Restarting is wasteful but correct. To avoid the first unnecessary restart, I'll initialize CURRENT_ARM=\"spec_off\" (assuming baseline from first light / fn-cluster-up). But if the pair wasn't up and ensure_pair_up brought it up via fn-cluster-up (baseline spec_off), then CURRENT_ARM=\"spec_off\" is correct. If the pair was already up from first light (baseline), also spec_off. So initializing CURRENT_ARM=\"spec_off\" is right in both cases. Then the first spec_off load reuses the running serve (no restart). Good. I'll set CURRENT_ARM=\"spec_off\" after ensure_pair_up.\n\nBut there's a risk: if a previous bench run left the serve in spec_on config, CURRENT_ARM=\"spec_off\" assumption is wrong. To be safe, I could force a reconfiguration at start. But that costs a restart. Let me add an optional FN_BENCH_FORCE_RECONFIG=1 to force. Default: assume baseline spec_off. I'll document the assumption. Actually, to be robust and avoid wrong-assumption bugs, let me just force the first arm's config always (set CURRENT_ARM=\"\" so the first serve_arm triggers). The cost is one restart at the start if the first arm is spec_off and it's already spec_off. That's a few minutes. Given correctness > a one-time restart, and to avoid assuming state, let me initialize CURRENT_ARM=\"\" (unknown) and let serve_arm establish it. Hmm, but that wastes a restart when it's already correct.\n\nCompromise: I'll check if the API is up and if so trust it's baseline spec_off (set CURRENT_ARM=\"spec_off\"), since ensure_pair_up only brings up baseline. If a prior run left it in spec_on, the operator should FN_BENCH_FORCE_RECONFIG. Let me set CURRENT_ARM=\"spec_off\" and support FN_BENCH_FORCE_RECONFIG=1 to reset to \"\". I'll document. Good enough and honest.\n\nActually, simpler and safer: set CURRENT_ARM=\"\" always, but make serve_arm skip restart if the arm matches a freshly-brought-up baseline. Ugh. Let me just go with CURRENT_ARM=\"spec_off\" default + FN_BENCH_FORCE_RECONFIG override. Fine.\n\nLet me finalize and write.\n\nLet me write the script.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 10 tests pass and the client compiles. Now the matrix script — the counterbalanced orchestrator with the steering's QSA-divergence protection.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_d7577d98cecf42c38e3706a7","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# bench/run-matrix.sh — the counterbalanced flashnext bench matrix.\n#\n# Spec claim 6.3 + ruling P12 (specs/flashnext/spec.md): three loads per arm,\n# interleaved (counterbalanced) arms, medians, token fingerprints, depth series\n# 0 / 10240 / 102400, speculative-decoding on and off arms, rows committed\n# under results/, and a bench.json receipt the receipts gate grades.\n#\n# Doctrine this matrix is built to make true:\n#   * F.8 — no benchmark number from a single uncounterbalanced run. The two\n#     speculative arms are interleaved A-B-B-A (the leading arm alternates per\n#     load), never blocked, so a time-drift confound cannot masquerade as an\n#     arm effect.\n#   * \"A matrix whose every number carries its protocol\" — every CSV row names\n#     its arm, load, depth, and in-flight concurrency; the receipt re-states the\n#     whole schedule.\n#   * Steering (flashnext#4): gfx1151 multi-sequence gathers can produce wrong\n#     outputs. So (1) every row records its concurrency, and (2) if a given\n#     (arm, depth) yields divergent completion fingerprints across its loads,\n#     the harness replays that arm serially (concurrency=1), byte-compares the\n#     token sequences via their fingerprints, and flags the arm SUSPECT instead\n#     of averaging over the divergence. Divergent-at-concurrency>1 with a clean\n#     serial replay is the QSA-gather signature: it is recorded, never papered\n#     over.\n#\n# The client (bench/fn-stream-client.py) supplies per-request TTFT and the\n# separated queue/prefill columns; this script owns the arm schedule, the serve\n# reconfiguration between speculative arms, the median reduction, and the\n# receipt.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSCRIPT_DIR=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\nREPO_ROOT=\"$(cd \"$SCRIPT_DIR/..\" && pwd)\"\nHOST_DIR=\"$REPO_ROOT/host\"\nCLIENT=\"$SCRIPT_DIR/fn-stream-client.py\"\nRESULTS=\"$REPO_ROOT/results\"\nBENCH_DIR=\"$RESULTS/bench\"\nRECEIPTS=\"$RESULTS/receipts\"\n\n# shellcheck source=host/fn-env.sh\nsource \"$HOST_DIR/fn-env.sh\"\n\nlog() { echo \"run-matrix: $*\" >&2; }\n\n# --- protocol knobs (all override-able via the environment) ------------------\nLOADS_PER_ARM=\"${FN_BENCH_LOADS_PER_ARM:-3}\"\nCONCURRENCY=\"${FN_BENCH_CONCURRENCY:-1}\"\nREQUESTS_PER_LOAD=\"${FN_BENCH_REQUESTS:-8}\"\nMAX_TOKENS=\"${FN_BENCH_MAX_TOKENS:-128}\"\nDEPTHS=(0 10240 102400)\nAPI=\"${FN_API:-http://127.0.0.1:$FN_PORT}\"\nMODEL=\"${FN_SERVED_NAME:-flashnext}\"\n\n# Speculative arm's extra `vllm serve` arguments. UNKNOWN-5 makes the draft and\n# its length a morning-tuning surface, so this is an operator knob with a\n# conservative n-gram default. The JSON is written WITHOUT whitespace and kept\n# single-quoted so it survives the `bash -c` re-parse in the container as one\n# argument.\nSPEC_ON_EXTRA=\"${FN_BENCH_SPEC_ARGS:---speculative-config '{\\\"method\\\":\\\"ngram\\\",\\\"num_speculative_tokens\\\":8,\\\"prompt_lookup_max\\\":8,\\\"prompt_lookup_min\\\":2}'}\"\n\nmkdir -p \"$BENCH_DIR\" \"$RECEIPTS\"\nMATRIX_CSV=\"$BENCH_DIR/matrix.csv\"\nMEDIANS_CSV=\"$BENCH_DIR/medians.csv\"\nDIVERGENT_TXT=\"$BENCH_DIR/divergent.txt\"\nSERIAL_JSONL=\"$BENCH_DIR/serial-replay.jsonl\"\nSUMMARIES_JSONL=\"$BENCH_DIR/load-summaries.jsonl\"\nrm -f \"$MATRIX_CSV\" \"$MEDIANS_CSV\" \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\" \"$SERIAL_JSONL\" \"$SUMMARIES_JSONL\"\n\n# --- the counterbalanced schedule: arms interleaved A-B-B-A -------------------\n# For each load index i, odd i leads arm A then B, even i leads B then A. With\n# three loads the run order is A B B A A B — the leading arm alternates, which\n# is the counterbalance; positions 1..4 are literally A-B-B-A.\nSCHED_ARM=()\nSCHED_LOAD=()\nfor ((i = 1; i <= LOADS_PER_ARM; i++)); do\n  if (( i % 2 == 1 )); then\n    SCHED_ARM+=(spec_off spec_on); SCHED_LOAD+=(\"$i\" \"$i\")\n  else\n    SCHED_ARM+=(spec_on spec_off); SCHED_LOAD+=(\"$i\" \"$i\")\n  fi\ndone\nSCHEDULE_DESC=\"\"\nfor idx in \"${!SCHED_ARM[@]}\"; do\n  SCHEDULE_DESC+=\"${SCHED_ARM[$idx]}:${SCHED_LOAD[$idx]} \"\ndone\nlog \"counterbalanced schedule: ${SCHEDULE_DESC% }\"\n\narm_label() { case \"$1\" in spec_on) echo \"spec-on\" ;; *) echo \"spec-off\" ;; esac; }\n\n# --- make sure the pair is answering -----------------------------------------\nensure_pair_up() {\n  if curl -fsS \"$API/v1/models\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"\\\"$MODEL\\\"\"; then\n    log \"pair already answering '$MODEL' on $API\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n  log \"pair not answering; standing it up via host/fn-cluster-up.sh\"\n  bash \"$HOST_DIR/fn-cluster-up.sh\"\n}\n\n# --- serve reconfiguration between speculative arms --------------------------\nCURRENT_ARM=\"\"\nif [ \"${FN_BENCH_FORCE_RECONFIG:-0}\" != \"1\" ]; then\n  # fn-cluster-up / first light serve the baseline (non-speculative) config,\n  # so a freshly-answered pair is arm spec_off. Set FN_BENCH_FORCE_RECONFIG=1\n  # if a prior run may have left the serve in a different arm.\n  CURRENT_ARM=\"spec_off\"\nfi\n\nwait_ready() {\n  local attempts=$(( ${FN_SERVE_TIMEOUT:-2700} / 20 )) ready=0 i\n  for ((i = 0; i < attempts; i++)); do\n    if curl -fsS \"$API/v1/models\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"\\\"$MODEL\\\"\"; then\n      ready=1; break\n    fi\n    sleep 20\n  done\n  if [ \"$ready\" -ne 1 ]; then\n    log \"FATAL: serve did not answer for arm '$CURRENT_ARM' inside the timeout\"\n    tail -n 40 \"$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log\" >&2 || true\n    exit 1\n  fi\n}\n\nserve_arm() {\n  local arm=\"$1\"\n  if [ \"$CURRENT_ARM\" = \"$arm\" ]; then\n    return 0\n  fi\n  log \"reconfiguring serve for arm '$arm'\"\n  podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"pkill -TERM -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true\"\n  sleep 4\n  podman exec \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"pkill -KILL -f 'bin/[v]llm serve' || true\" 2>/dev/null || true\n\n  local spec_args=\"\"\n  if [ \"$arm\" = \"spec_on\" ]; then\n    spec_args=\"$SPEC_ON_EXTRA\"\n  fi\n  local serve_cmd=\"exec vllm serve $FN_MODEL_DIR\"\n  serve_cmd+=\" --served-model-name $FN_SERVED_NAME\"\n  serve_cmd+=\" --host 0.0.0.0 --port $FN_PORT\"\n  serve_cmd+=\" --tensor-parallel-size 2\"\n  serve_cmd+=\" --distributed-executor-backend ray\"\n  serve_cmd+=\" --enforce-eager\"\n  serve_cmd+=\" --gpu-memory-utilization $FN_GPU_UTIL\"\n  serve_cmd+=\" --max-model-len $FN_MAX_CTX\"\n  if [ -n \"$spec_args\" ]; then\n    serve_cmd+=\" $spec_args\"\n  fi\n  serve_cmd+=\" > '$FN_STATE_DIR/serve.log' 2>&1\"\n  podman exec -d \"$FN_CONTAINER\" bash -c \"$serve_cmd\"\n\n  CURRENT_ARM=\"$arm\"\n  wait_ready\n  log \"arm '$arm' is serving\"\n}\n\n# --- one measured cell: the client at (arm, load, depth) ----------------------\nmeasure_cell() {  # $1=arm $2=load $3=depth\n  local arm=\"$1\" load=\"$2\" depth=\"$3\" label\n  label=\"$(arm_label \"$arm\")\"\n  log \"measure arm=$label load=$load depth=$depth concurrency=$CONCURRENCY\"\n  python3 \"$CLIENT\" \\\n    --api \"$API\" \\\n    --model \"$MODEL\" \\\n    --arm \"$label\" \\\n    --load \"$load\" \\\n    --depth \"$depth\" \\\n    --concurrency \"$CONCURRENCY\" \\\n    --requests \"$REQUESTS_PER_LOAD\" \\\n    --max-tokens \"$MAX_TOKENS\" \\\n    --spec-label \"$label\" \\\n    --csv \"$MATRIX_CSV\" \\\n    >> \"$SUMMARIES_JSONL\"\n}\n\n# =============================================================================\n# Phase A — the counterbalanced measurement sweep.\n# =============================================================================\nensure_pair_up\nfor idx in \"${!SCHED_ARM[@]}\"; do\n  arm=\"${SCHED_ARM[$idx]}\"\n  load=\"${SCHED_LOAD[$idx]}\"\n  serve_arm \"$arm\"\n  for depth in \"${DEPTHS[@]}\"; do\n    measure_cell \"$arm\" \"$load\" \"$depth\"\n  done\ndone\n\n# =============================================================================\n# Phase B — median reduction + fingerprint-divergence detection.\n# =============================================================================\nexport MATRIX_CSV MEDIANS_CSV DIVERGENT_CSV=\"$DIVERGENT_TXT\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport csv, os\nfrom statistics import median\n\nmatrix_csv = os.environ[\"MATRIX_CSV\"]\nmedians_csv = os.environ[\"MEDIANS_CSV\"]\ndivergent_txt = os.environ[\"DIVERGENT_CSV\"]\n\n# Strip the '#' column-semantics comment before handing lines to csv.\nwith open(matrix_csv) as f:\n    lines = [ln for ln in f if not ln.startswith(\"#\")]\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(lines))\n\ndef med(vals):\n    nums = [float(v) for v in vals if v not in (\"\", None)]\n    return median(nums) if nums else \"\"\n\ncells = {}\nfor r in rows:\n    key = (r[\"arm\"], r[\"depth_target\"])\n    cells.setdefault(key, []).append(r)\n\nmedian_fields = (\"ttft_s\", \"queue_wait_s\", \"prefill_s\", \"decode_s\", \"total_s\")\nwith open(medians_csv, \"w\", newline=\"\") as f:\n    w = csv.writer(f)\n    w.writerow([\"arm\", \"depth_target\", \"concurrency\", \"n_requests\",\n                *(\"median_\" + x for x in median_fields),\n                \"distinct_fingerprints\", \"suspect\"])\n    divergent = []\n    for (arm, depth), rs in sorted(cells.items()):\n        fp = set(r[\"fingerprint\"] for r in rs if r[\"fingerprint\"])\n        is_suspect = len(fp) > 1\n        if is_suspect:\n            divergent.append((arm, depth))\n        conc = rs[0][\"concurrency\"] if rs else \"\"\n        w.writerow([arm, depth, conc, len(rs),\n                    *(med([r[x] for r in rows if (r[\"arm\"], r[\"depth_target\"]) == (arm, depth)]) for x in median_fields),\n                    len(fp), \"yes\" if is_suspect else \"no\"])\n\nwith open(divergent_txt, \"w\") as f:\n    for arm, depth in divergent:\n        f.write(f\"{arm}\\t{depth}\\n\")\n\nprint(f\"run-matrix: reduced {len(rows)} rows into {len(cells)} (arm,depth) cells; \"\n      f\"{len(divergent)} divergent\", flush=True)\nPY\n\n# =============================================================================\n# Phase C — serial (concurrency=1) replay of every divergent arm.\n# Steering §2: fingerprint divergence at concurrency>1 with a clean serial\n# replay is the QSA-gather signature. Record it; never average over it.\n# =============================================================================\nif [ -s \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\" ]; then\n  log \"divergence detected; replaying divergent arms serially (concurrency=1)\"\n  # Replay each divergent (arm, depth) serially, grouped by arm so the serve is\n  # reconfigured at most once per arm.\n  for arm in spec_off spec_on; do\n    # Collect the divergent depths for this arm.\n    mapfile -t arm_depths < <(awk -F'\\t' -v a=\"$arm\" '$1==a {print $2}' \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\")\n    [ \"${#arm_depths[@]}\" -eq 0 ] && continue\n    serve_arm \"$arm\"\n    label=\"$(arm_label \"$arm\")\"\n    for depth in \"${arm_depths[@]}\"; do\n      log \"serial replay arm=$label depth=$depth\"\n      serial_json=\"$(python3 \"$CLIENT\" \\\n        --api \"$API\" --model \"$MODEL\" \\\n        --arm \"$label\" --load 0 \\\n        --depth \"$depth\" \\\n        --concurrency 1 \\\n        --requests \"$REQUESTS_PER_LOAD\" \\\n        --max-tokens \"$MAX_TOKENS\" \\\n        --spec-label \"$label\" \\\n        --csv \"$BENCH_DIR/serial-$arm-$depth.csv\")\"\n      printf '%s\\n' \"$serial_json\" \\\n        | python3 -c '\nimport json, sys\nrec = json.loads(sys.stdin.read())\nfps = rec.get(\"fingerprints\", [])\ndistinct = sorted(set(fps))\nprint(json.dumps({\n    \"arm\": rec.get(\"arm\"),\n    \"depth_target\": rec.get(\"depth_target\"),\n    \"concurrency\": rec.get(\"concurrency\"),\n    \"serial_clean\": len(distinct) <= 1,\n    \"serial_fingerprint\": distinct[0] if distinct else None,\n    \"serial_distinct_count\": len(distinct),\n}))' >> \"$SERIAL_JSONL\"\n    done\n  done\nelse\n  log \"no fingerprint divergence across loads; no serial replay needed\"\nfi\n\n# =============================================================================\n# Phase D — the bench.json receipt the receipts gate grades.\n# =============================================================================\nexport RECEIPTS BENCH_DIR SERIAL_JSONL SUMMARIES_JSONL DIVERGENT_TXT\nexport LOADS_PER_ARM CONCURRENCY SCHEDULE_DESC\nexport DEPTH_LIST=\"${DEPTHS[*]}\"\nexport ARM_LIST=\"spec_off spec_on\"\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport csv, json, os, time\n\nbench_dir = os.environ[\"BENCH_DIR\"]\nreceipts = os.environ[\"RECEIPTS\"]\nloads_per_arm = int(os.environ[\"LOADS_PER_ARM\"])\nconcurrency = int(os.environ[\"CONCURRENCY\"])\nschedule = os.environ[\"SCHEDULE_DESC\"].split()\ndepths = [int(x) for x in os.environ[\"DEPTH_LIST\"].split()]\narms = os.environ[\"ARM_LIST\"].split()\n\ndef read_jsonl(path):\n    if not os.path.exists(path):\n        return []\n    out = []\n    with open(path) as f:\n        for ln in f:\n            ln = ln.strip()\n            if ln:\n                out.append(json.loads(ln))\n    return out\n\nsummaries = read_jsonl(os.environ[\"SUMMARIES_JSONL\"])\nserials = read_jsonl(os.environ[\"SERIAL_JSONL\"])\nserial_by_key = {(s.get(\"arm\"), str(s.get(\"depth_target\"))): s for s in serials}\n\n# Per (arm, depth) fingerprint sets from the measured loads.\nfingerprints = {}\nsuspect_cells = []\nfor s in summaries:\n    key = f\"{s['arm']}/{s['depth_target']}\"\n    entry = fingerprints.setdefault(key, {\"fingerprints\": set(), \"loads\": set(),\n                                          \"concurrency\": s.get(\"concurrency\")})\n    entry[\"fingerprints\"].update(s.get(\"fingerprints\", []))\n    entry[\"loads\"].add(s.get(\"load\"))\n\nfor key, entry in fingerprints.items():\n    arm, depth = key.split(\"/\", 1)\n    distinct = sorted(entry[\"fingerprints\"])\n    divergent = len(distinct) > 1\n    serial = serial_by_key.get((arm, depth))\n    serial_clean = serial.get(\"serial_clean\") if serial else None\n    # Suspect when loads diverge. A clean serial replay alongside concurrent\n    # divergence is precisely the QSA-gather signature: recorded, not averaged.\n    suspect = divergent\n    qsa_signature = bool(divergent and serial_clean)\n    entry[\"distinct_count\"] = len(distinct)\n    entry[\"divergent\"] = divergent\n    entry[\"suspect\"] = suspect\n    entry[\"qsa_signature\"] = qsa_signature\n    entry[\"serial_clean\"] = serial_clean\n    entry[\"serial_fingerprint\"] = serial.get(\"serial_fingerprint\") if serial else None\n    entry[\"fingerprints\"] = distinct\n    entry[\"loads\"] = sorted(entry[\"loads\"])\n    if suspect:\n        suspect_cells.append(key)\n\nreceipt = {\n    \"step\": \"bench\",\n    \"status\": \"pass\",\n    \"ts\": time.strftime(\"%FT%T\"),\n    \"data\": {\n        \"loads_per_arm\": loads_per_arm,\n        \"counterbalanced\": True,\n        \"arms\": arms,\n        \"depth_series\": depths,\n        \"concurrency\": concurrency,\n        \"schedule\": schedule,\n        \"fingerprints\": fingerprints,\n        \"suspect_cells\": suspect_cells,\n        \"matrix_csv\": \"results/bench/matrix.csv\",\n        \"medians_csv\": \"results/bench/medians.csv\",\n        \"note\": (\"prefill is a server-metrics measurement, never a duplicate \"\n                 \"of the ttft column (spec 6.2; evidence/nix-strix-halo.md \"\n                 \"§4.4). Suspect cells diverged across loads and must not be \"\n                 \"quoted as clean numbers.\"),\n    },\n}\npath = os.path.join(receipts, \"bench.json\")\nwith open(path, \"w\") as f:\n    json.dump(receipt, f, indent=1)\nprint(f\"run-matrix: receipt written to {path} \"\n      f\"({len(fingerprints)} cells, {len(suspect_cells)} suspect)\", flush=True)\nPY\n\nlog \"matrix complete: rows=$MATRIX_CSV medians=$MEDIANS_CSV receipt=$RECEIPTS/bench.json\"\n","path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/bench/run-matrix.sh"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1109,"output":13325,"cacheRead":97152,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":8981,"totalTokens":111586,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787991061141,"responseId":"chatcmpl-5f31fd1f-e438-4615-ac06-51e999840598","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_d7577d98cecf42c38e3706a7","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 14674 bytes to /home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/bench/run-matrix.sh"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787991326472},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright, let's make it executable and run `bash -n` to check the syntax. Also, let me re-examine the median calculation logic in Phase B. There's a bug:\n\n```python\n*(med([r[x] for r in rows if (r[\"arm\"], r[\"depth_target\"]) == (arm, depth)]) for x in median_fields),\n```\n\nThis iterates over `rows` (all rows) for each field, filtering by (arm, depth). This works but is inefficient. Actually, we already have `rs` = the rows for this cell. Let's just use `rs` instead of re-filtering `rows`. Let's fix this to use `rs`:\n\n```python\n*(med([r[x] for r in rs]) for x in median_fields),\n```\n\nThis is cleaner and correct. Let's fix it.\n\nAlso, let me re-verify: `cells.setdefault(key, []).append(r)` — key is (arm, depth_target). depth_target is a string like \"0\", \"10240\", \"102400\". And we're comparing with `(r[\"arm\"], r[\"depth_target\"]) == (arm, depth)` — both strings, consistent. Good.\n\nLet's fix the median computation to use `rs`.\n\nAlso, in Phase B, we write divergent cells to divergent_txt. Phase C reads them. Good.\n\nLet's also re-verify the export of `DIVERGENT_CSV` in Phase B: I set `DIVERGENT_CSV=\"$DIVERGENT_TXT\"` in the export line. In Python it's read as `os.environ[\"DIVERGENT_CSV\"]`. Good.\n\nNext, `mapfile -t arm_depths < <(...)` — process substitution requires bash, but that's fine. Also, under `set -u`, if arm_depths is empty, `[ \"${#arm_depths[@]}\" -eq 0 ]` works fine. Good. However, if awk produces no output, `mapfile` produces an empty array. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle point: when mapfile assigns to an empty array, then under set -u, does `\"${#arm_depths[@]}\"` on an empty array work in bash 4.4+? It returns 0. Good.\n\nAlso in Phase C, `for depth in \"${arm_depths[@]}\"` on an empty array — it's guarded by the continue above. Good.\n\nNext, let's reconsider: in Phase C, we use `--csv \"$BENCH_DIR/serial-$arm-$depth.csv\"`. Here $arm is in a form like \"spec_off\" (because DIVERGENT_TXT records arm as arm_label? Wait, no). Let me verify: in measure_cell, we pass `--arm \"$label\"`, where label is \"spec-off\"/\"spec-on\". So the arm in the CSV is \"spec-off\"/\"spec-on\" (with a hyphen). Therefore in Phase B, `r[\"arm\"]` is \"spec-off\" etc. Divergent cells are written as `arm\\tdepth`, where arm is \"spec-off\"/\"spec-on\".\n\nIn Phase C, we loop with `for arm in spec_off spec_on` (with underscores!) and compare with `$1==a` against DIVERGENT_TXT, which contains \"spec-off\"/\"spec-on\" (with hyphens). Mismatch! awk's `$1==a` with a=\"spec_off\" won't match \"spec-off\".\n\nBug. Let's fix it: either loop with labels in Phase C (spec-off spec-on), or store arm as-is. DIVERGENT_TXT's arm comes from the CSV's \"arm\" column = label = \"spec-off\"/\"spec-on\". So in Phase C we should loop with `for arm in spec-off spec-on` (with hyphens). And `serve_arm \"$arm\"` — but serve_arm expects \"spec_off\"/\"spec_on\" (with underscores, because it checks `[ \"$arm\" = \"spec_on\" ]`). Ugh, inconsistent naming.\n\nLet's unify. Two naming schemes:\n- Internal arm IDs: spec_off / spec_on (with underscores) — used in SCHED_ARM and serve_arm.\n- Labels: spec-off / spec-on (with hyphens) — used in the CSV's \"arm\" column and in --arm.\n\nIn Phase C, we need to map the CSV's arm (hyphen-separated label) back to the internal arm (with underscore) for serve_arm. Let's handle it: read the hyphen-separated label from DIVERGENT_TXT, derive the internal arm from the label, call serve_arm with the internal arm, and run the client with the label.\n\nLet's redo Phase C: loop over the lines of DIVERGENT_TXT (label, depth). Group by label. For each unique label, derive the internal arm: if label == spec-on, arm=spec_on, else spec_off. serve_arm(arm). Then for each depth for that label, run the client with --arm label.\n\nLet's restructure Phase C to iterate directly over the unique labels in DIVERGENT_TXT.\n\nLet me rewrite Phase C:\n```bash\nif [ -s \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\" ]; then\n  log \"...\"\n  # unique labels preserving order\n  mapfile -t uniq_labels < <(cut -f1 \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\" | awk '!seen[$0]++')\n  for label in \"${uniq_labels[@]}\"; do\n    case \"$label\" in\n      spec-on) arm=\"spec_on\" ;;\n      *) arm=\"spec_off\" ;;\n    esac\n    mapfile -t label_depths < <(awk -F'\\t' -v a=\"$label\" '$1==a {print $2}' \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\")\n    serve_arm \"$arm\"\n    for depth in \"${label_depths[@]}\"; do\n      log \"serial replay arm=$label depth=$depth\"\n      serial_json=\"$(python3 \"$CLIENT\" ... --arm \"$label\" ...)\"\n      ...\n    done\n  done\nfi\n```\n\nThis is cleaner. Let's also fix the `serial-$arm-$depth.csv` filename to use the label: `serial-$label-$depth.csv`.\n\nLet me also re-verify the serial fingerprint comparison in Phase D. serial_by_key is keyed by (s.get(\"arm\"), str(s.get(\"depth_target\"))). The serial JSON from the client has \"arm\" (label) and \"depth_target\". And in Phase D, for fingerprints from summaries, key = f\"{s['arm']}/{s['depth_target']}\", with arm = label. And serial_by_key.get((arm, depth)), where arm = label (from key.split). depth = str of depth_target. In summaries, depth_target comes from the client JSON's `s['depth_target']`, which is an int (e.g., 10240). In the serial JSON, also int. We do str(s.get(\"depth_target\")) for the serial key. And `arm, depth = key.split(\"/\", 1)`, with depth = str(int) from the f-string. For example, key = \"spec-off/10240\", depth = \"10240\". serial_by_key's key = (label, str(10240)) = (\"spec-off\", \"10240\"). Match. Good.\n\nBut wait, in Phase B, divergent cells: key=(r[\"arm\"], r[\"depth_target\"]), and r[\"depth_target\"] is a CSV string like \"10240\". In Phase D, fingerprints come from SUMMARIES_JSONL (client stdout JSON). There, depth_target is int. Inconsistency between Phase B (CSV, strings) and Phase D (JSONL, int), but they're separate data flows. DIVERGENT_TXT is written in Phase B from the CSV's strings. Phase C reads DIVERGENT_TXT (labels + depths as strings). No problem.\n\nIn Phase D, we use only SUMMARIES_JSONL and SERIAL_JSONL, not the CSV. So depth_target is int throughout Phase D. Keys are consistent (both from JSONL). Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle point: the fingerprints in Phase D come from summaries (per-load stdout JSON). Each summary has a \"fingerprints\" list (for that load's requests at that depth). We aggregate them into a set per (arm, depth). If different loads produce different fingerprints, distinct > 1 → divergent. Good. That's the across-load divergence that the steering asks about.\n\nBut wait — there's also within-load divergence (multiple requests at the same depth in the same load producing different outputs due to concurrency). The fingerprints list within a single summary captures that: if concurrent requests within one load diverge, that summary's fingerprints list has > 1 distinct values, and the aggregated set reflects that. Good, both within-load and across-load divergence are captured in the aggregated set.\n\nAlright, let me fix the identified bugs:\n1. Phase B's median computation uses `rows` instead of `rs`. Fix to `rs`.\n2. Phase C naming: use labels and map to internal arms.\n\nLet me rewrite Phase B's medians and Phase C. Let's edit the file.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to use `rs` in Phase B's medians. `rs` is the list of rows for cell (arm, depth). `med([r[x] for r in rs])`. Yes. Let's fix it.\n\nLet's make these edits.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now make it executable and check syntax. But first let me fix two issues I spotted: the median re-filters `rows` instead of using the cell's rows, and Phase C mixes underscore/hyphen arm naming.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_c214a564758644b88a71b2ef","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/70bb1f7ebb6a/bench-harness/bench/run-matrix.sh","edits":[{"oldText":"        conc = rs[0][\"concurrency\"] if rs else \"\"\n        w.writerow([arm, depth, conc, len(rs),\n                    *(med([r[x] for r in rows if (r[\"arm\"], r[\"depth_target\"]) == (arm, depth)]) for x in median_fields),\n                    len(fp), \"yes\" if is_suspect else \"no\"])","newText":"        conc = rs[0][\"concurrency\"] if rs else \"\"\n        w.writerow([arm, depth, conc, len(rs),\n                    *(med([r[x] for r in rs]) for x in median_fields),\n                    len(fp), \"yes\" if is_suspect else \"no\"])"},{"oldText":"if [ -s \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\" ]; then\n  log \"divergence detected; replaying divergent arms serially (concurrency=1)\"\n  # Replay each divergent (arm, depth) serially, grouped by arm so the serve is\n  # reconfigured at most once per arm.\n  for arm in spec_off spec_on; do\n    # Collect the divergent depths for this arm.\n    mapfile -t arm_depths < <(awk -F'\\t' -v a=\"$arm\" '$1==a {print $2}' \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\")\n    [ \"${#arm_depths[@]}\" -eq 0 ] && continue\n    serve_arm \"$arm\"\n    label=\"$(arm_label \"$arm\")\"\n    for depth in \"${arm_depths[@]}\"; do\n      log \"serial replay arm=$label depth=$depth\"\n      serial_json=\"$(python3 \"$CLIENT\" \\\n        --api \"$API\" --model \"$MODEL\" \\\n        --arm \"$label\" --load 0 \\\n        --depth \"$depth\" \\\n        --concurrency 1 \\\n        --requests \"$REQUESTS_PER_LOAD\" \\\n        --max-tokens \"$MAX_TOKENS\" \\\n        --spec-label \"$label\" \\\n        --csv \"$BENCH_DIR/serial-$arm-$depth.csv\")\"","newText":"if [ -s \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\" ]; then\n  log \"divergence detected; replaying divergent arms serially (concurrency=1)\"\n  # Replay each divergent (arm, depth) serially, grouped by arm so the serve is\n  # reconfigured at most once per arm. DIVERGENT_TXT carries the CSV arm LABEL\n  # (spec-off / spec-on); map it back to the internal arm id for serve_arm.\n  mapfile -t uniq_labels < <(cut -f1 \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\" | awk '!seen[$0]++')\n  for label in \"${uniq_labels[@]}\"; do\n    case \"$label\" in\n      spec-on) arm=\"spec_on\" ;;\n      *) arm=\"spec_off\" ;;\n    esac\n    mapfile -t label_depths < <(awk -F'\\t' -v a=\"$label\" '$1==a {print $2}' \"$DIVERGENT_TXT\")\n    [ \"${#label_depths[@]}\" -eq 0 ] && continue\n    serve_arm \"$arm\"\n    for depth in \"${label_depths[@]}\"; do\n      log \"serial replay arm=$label depth=$depth\"\n      serial_json=\"$(python3 \"$CLIENT\" \\\n        --api \"$API\" --model \"$MODEL\" \\\n        --arm \"$label\" --load 0 \\\n        --depth \"$depth\" \\\n        --concurrency 1 \\\n        --requests \"$REQUESTS_PER_LOAD\" \\\n        --max-tokens \"$MAX_TOKENS\" \\\n        --spec-label \"$label\" \\\n        --csv \"$BENCH_DIR/serial-$label-$depth.csv\")\""}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":14370,"output":3043,"cacheRead":97280,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2044,"totalTokens":114693,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cach